/// Trust (postcondition composition): whether a caller may ASSUME `callee`'s
/// authored `ensures` at a call site, rather than merely reporting it.
///
/// This is the whole soundness argument for the caller-side twin of the
/// callee-side `ContractKind::Ensures` -> `ObligationKind::Postcondition` emit.
/// Every conjunct is required; any miss returns `false` and the callee keeps its
/// pre-existing reported-only summary (fail-closed — dropping a hypothesis can
/// only weaken a PROVE to a FAIL).
///
///  1. **There is something to assume, and the conditional half TRAVELS WITH
///     IT.** A contract postcondition is guaranteed only WHERE ITS PRECONDITION
///     HELD — what the callee proved is `requires -> ensures`, never `ensures`
///     alone — and the two halves travel at DIFFERENT VC levels: the assumed
///     postcondition rides L0 body VCs while the precondition check is an L1
///     `VcKind::Precondition` that an `L0Safety` caller's level filter DROPS.
///
///     Slice 1 closed that by refusing any callee with a `requires` at all.
///     Slice 2 does not need to, because the split is no longer possible: this
///     mint hands the clause to `with_verified_callee_postcondition`, whose
///     summaries are injected as the IMPLICATION the callee proved, instantiated
///     at the call site (`generate::rebind_callee_postconditions`). The caller
///     derives the postcondition exactly where its own context discharges the
///     antecedent AT THAT CALL SITE and derives nothing where it does not, in
///     the same solver query — so there is no level at which the guarantee is
///     assumed while its guard goes unchecked, and no dependence on the filtered
///     `Precondition` row.
///
///     What this conjunct therefore still requires is that `preconditions` is
///     the callee's COMPLETE authored `requires` set at this instantiation. A
///     MISSING conjunct is the unsound direction here and only here: the callee
///     proved `p AND q -> post`, so injecting `p -> post` would be a STRONGER
///     claim than the contract supports, not the usual harmless weakening.
///     `extract_contract_formulas_for_instance` is already fail-closed in
///     exactly that direction — every `Requires` it cannot convert, bind, type
///     or parse becomes `Formula::Bool(false)` rather than being dropped, which
///     makes the antecedent unsatisfiable and the injected implication vacuous.
///     The set handed here is the one that function returned, unfiltered.
///
///     Note the callee's own verdict is sound authority for the implication
///     under EITHER discipline: if its `ensures` obligation was discharged with
///     its `requires` as an entry hypothesis, `requires -> ensures` is literally
///     what was proved; if it was discharged without, the stronger `ensures`
///     was, which implies it. There is no third case.
///  2. **Identity is a `DefId`, not a string.** A LOCAL callee, or a NON-LOCAL
///     one whose crate this build DECLARED as a bundling scope
///     ([`native_bundle_crate_is_declared`]) — and only at the IDENTITY
///     instantiation (`args.is_empty()`), so the contract extracted at this call
///     site is byte-identically the one `trust_proof_results` verified.
///     `name_owner` carries the caller-body name uniqueness check (see the
///     pre-scan in the caller): the DB is keyed by a non-injective string, so a
///     name shared with any other callee in this body mints nothing. A self-call
///     mints nothing either — assuming one's own unproved postcondition to prove
///     it is circular.
///
///     **The declared-scope disjunct is CAPABILITY, not a widened trust
///     boundary, and the distinction rests on a query attribute rather than on
///     this line.** The stated rationale for locality is "its proof must have
///     happened in THIS compilation, under THIS session's policy". A non-local
///     key SATISFIES that rationale, because there is no foreign verdict
///     anywhere to fetch: `trust_proof_results` is declared `arena_cache` and
///     nothing else at `rustc_middle::queries` — no `separate_provide_extern`,
///     so a foreign `DefId` has no extern provider, and no `cache_on_disk`, so
///     nothing is persisted. Forcing it on a foreign def therefore runs the
///     LOCAL provider over the foreign MIR (readable because `targo` injects
///     `-Zalways-encode-mir` on every unit) in this compilation, under this
///     policy, and grades the result with conjunct (4). That is
///     RE-DERIVE-never-IMPORT, the same discipline the foreign bundling lane
///     already runs on. **Do not add `separate_provide_extern` or
///     `cache_on_disk` to `trust_proof_results`**: that single attribute is the
///     whole boundary, a sealed cross-compilation postcondition token was built
///     once and removed the same afternoon as forgeable, and
///     `tests/ui/trust/cross_crate_bundle_scope_no_clause_import*.rs` is the
///     thing that would notice.
///
///     The scope is a CALLER-side declaration (`-Ztrust-bundle-foreign-crate`,
///     the list `targo trust check|build` injects), deliberately never a callee
///     self-claim: the 2026-08-25 cross-crate admission hole keyed on
///     `#[automatically_derived]`, which is stable, ungated and user-writable.
///     `is_mir_available` is what keeps `instance_mir` from ICEing on an
///     unencoded foreign body, and it was already a conjunct here.
///  3. **No query cycle, and a cycle is a REFUSAL.** Forcing
///     `trust_proof_results(callee)` verifies the callee, which re-enters this
///     builder for the callee's own callees and can come back around to this
///     caller. The `#5-PRE-B` in-progress registry (entered for `caller_def_id`
///     at the top of the caller) plus `optimized_mir_query_would_cycle` detect
///     that; a detected cycle is treated as UNPROVEN, never as proven.
///  4. **The verdict, and the `ensures` ROW specifically.**
///     `has_nonzero_accounted_static_proof_under_own_requires` applies the
///     shipped assumption-root gate, tolerating for a PRECONDITIONED callee
///     exactly the one `TrustProofStrength::None` row such a callee always
///     carries — the `DefinitionEntryAssumption` recording that its body was
///     proved assuming its own `requires` at entry. That row is the very
///     hypothesis this lane carries into the caller as the antecedent, so
///     refusing it would make slice 2 vacuous rather than conservative; see
///     that function for why the identically-shaped `assume_total` row is still
///     refused, by two independent conjuncts. For a precondition-free callee it
///     is `has_nonzero_accounted_static_proof` verbatim:
///     fully verified, non-zero total, `trusted + certified == total`, aligned
///     obligation arrays, no `ProofStrength::None` bookkeeping row, and no
///     verdict resting on a `#[trust::assume_total]`/`#[trust::skip]` user
///     axiom. On top of that this lane requires the callee to carry AT LEAST as
///     many `Postcondition` rows as it has authored `ensures` clauses. That last
///     conjunct is the one that is easy to leave out and expensive to omit: a
///     verdict of "everything I emitted proved" says nothing about a clause that
///     was never emitted, and an obligation that DISAPPEARS is not one that
///     proved.
fn callee_postconditions_are_reusable<'tcx>(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>,
    caller_def_id: DefId,
    callee_def_id: DefId,
    args: ty::GenericArgs<'tcx>,
    preconditions: &[trust_types::Formula],
    postconditions: &[trust_types::Formula],
    name_owner: Option<DefId>,
) -> bool {
    let outcome = callee_postconditions_are_reusable_reasoned(
        tcx,
        caller_def_id,
        callee_def_id,
        args,
        preconditions,
        postconditions,
        name_owner,
    );
    // Trust (compose instrument): there was no way to read WHICH conjunct
    // refused, which made every "+0" from this lane indistinguishable from
    // every other. One line per consulted callee, naming the reason. Pure
    // observability — the returned `bool` is the reason's own verdict and
    // nothing here can change it.
    if env_flag_enabled("TRUST_COMPOSE_DEBUG") {
        eprintln!(
            "TRUST_COMPOSE: caller={} callee={} local={} declared={} args={} verdict={}",
            with_no_trimmed_paths!(tcx.def_path_str(caller_def_id)),
            with_no_trimmed_paths!(tcx.def_path_str(callee_def_id)),
            callee_def_id.is_local(),
            native_bundle_crate_is_declared(tcx, callee_def_id),
            args.len(),
            outcome,
        );
    }
    outcome == COMPOSE_MINT
}

/// Trust (compose instrument): the accepting outcome of
/// [`callee_postconditions_are_reusable_reasoned`]. Every other value is a
/// refusal that names its conjunct.
const COMPOSE_MINT: &str = "mint";

/// [`callee_postconditions_are_reusable`], as a REASONED result. The boolean
/// wrapper is what production reads; this spelling exists so a refusal can be
/// attributed to one conjunct instead of collapsing to `false`.
fn callee_postconditions_are_reusable_reasoned<'tcx>(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>,
    caller_def_id: DefId,
    callee_def_id: DefId,
    args: ty::GenericArgs<'tcx>,
    preconditions: &[trust_types::Formula],
    postconditions: &[trust_types::Formula],
    name_owner: Option<DefId>,
) -> &'static str {
    // (1) Something to assume. A non-empty `preconditions` is NOT a refusal any
    //     more — it becomes the antecedent the clause is injected under. See the
    //     doc above for why completeness, not emptiness, is what this needs, and
    //     why the extractor already supplies it fail-closed.
    //
    //     Evaluated FIRST, and that ordering is now load-bearing for COST as
    //     well as for logic: conjunct (4) forces a full verification of the
    //     callee, and under a declared bundling scope that callee can live in
    //     another crate. Only a callee that actually declares an `ensures` ever
    //     reaches it.
    if postconditions.is_empty() {
        return "postconditions-empty";
    }
    // (2) Identity: local OR in a build-declared bundling scope, non-generic,
    //     not ourselves, and the sole owner of its summary key in the caller
    //     body. See the doc above for why the declared-scope disjunct is
    //     capability rather than a widened trust boundary.
    if callee_def_id == caller_def_id {
        return "identity:self";
    }
    if !(callee_def_id.is_local() || native_bundle_crate_is_declared(tcx, callee_def_id)) {
        return "identity:non-local-undeclared";
    }
    if !args.is_empty() {
        return "identity:generic";
    }
    if name_owner != Some(callee_def_id) {
        return "identity:name-collision";
    }
    if !tcx.is_mir_available(callee_def_id) {
        return "identity:no-mir";
    }
    // Trust (D4-A, 2026-09-24): a FOREIGN callee's verdict is re-derived here, so
    // it goes through the same SOUNDNESS declines the shared bar uses — an
    // interposable (`#[no_mangle]`/`#[export_name]`) symbol, user `unsafe` source
    // (whose erased hazards the re-derived MIR cannot show), a dynamic-library
    // crate, a same-named crate this compilation does not bind. Only ever
    // removes credit. The cost bound is deliberately NOT applied here: this lane
    // predates D4-A and is already demand-bounded by authored `ensures` clauses
    // (see `foreign_rederivation::foreign_rederivation_soundness_decline`).
    if !callee_def_id.is_local()
        && let Some(decline) =
            foreign_rederivation::foreign_rederivation_soundness_decline(tcx, callee_def_id)
    {
        return decline.compose_reason();
    }
    // (3) Re-entrancy: a callee already being verified up this stack is UNPROVEN
    //     here, not proven.
    if DYN_SUMMARY_IN_PROGRESS.with(|s| s.borrow().contains(&callee_def_id))
        || optimized_mir_query_would_cycle(tcx, callee_def_id)
    {
        return "reentrancy";
    }
    // (4) The callee's own verdict, and its `ensures` rows in particular.
    let Some(results) = tcx.trust_proof_results(ty::InstanceKind::Item(callee_def_id)) else {
        return "verdict:none";
    };
    if !has_nonzero_accounted_static_proof_under_own_requires(
        tcx.sess,
        callee_def_id,
        results,
        preconditions.len(),
    ) {
        return "verdict:not-accounted";
    }
    let postcondition_rows = results
        .dispositions
        .iter()
        .filter(|disposition| disposition.kind == TrustObligationKind::Postcondition)
        .count();
    if postcondition_rows < postconditions.len() {
        return "postcondition-rows";
    }
    COMPOSE_MINT
}

/// Trust (bundled-member derived preconditions): the synthetic summary for a
/// would-be-bundled, contract-less callee whose entry-prefix div/rem asserts
/// derive a formal-rooted requires. `None` = not bundled, nothing derivable,
/// or outside the envelope — the call keeps today's behavior exactly.
fn derived_bundled_member_summary<'tcx>(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>,
    body: &Body<'tcx>,
    callee_name: &str,
    callee_def_id: DefId,
    args: ty::GenericArgs<'tcx>,
    collector_memo: &mut Option<FxHashMap<String, ty::Instance<'tcx>>>,
) -> Option<trust_vcgen::FunctionSummary> {
    let constructor_role = step_by_constructor::candidate(
        tcx,
        ty::TypingEnv::post_analysis(tcx, body.source.def_id()),
        callee_def_id,
        args,
    );
    // Trust (identity-anchor sweep, 2026-09-28): name the constructor
    // classification of THIS call. Diagnostic-only. It is the one observation of
    // `candidate`'s own answer: for a callee the collector's residency admission
    // gate declines, `derived_summary` below finds nothing to bundle, so a TRUE
    // answer and a FALSE one produce byte-identical verdicts. The refutation pin
    // `step_by_constructor_no_core_range_lang_item_impostor_refuted.rs` reads it.
    if env_flag_enabled("TRUST_NATIVE_DEBUG") {
        eprintln!(
            "[STEP_BY_CANDIDATE] fn={} role={} callee={callee_name}",
            trust_mir_extract::safe_def_path_str(tcx, body.source.def_id()),
            constructor_role.map_or("NONE", step_by_constructor::ConstructorMember::label),
        );
    }
    if constructor_role.is_some()
        && let Some(summary) = step_by_constructor::derived_summary(tcx, body, callee_name)
    {
        return Some(summary);
    }
    let set = collector_memo.get_or_insert_with(|| {
        // Trust (VEC-INSERT, 2026-09-25): `body` is the ROOT whose VCs are being
        // generated (both summary-DB builders are handed the verification body), and
        // its charged `Vec` index-mutator blocks were recorded from its own
        // extraction just before this DB was built. This walk records vanilla
        // verdicts too, so it must judge those call sites exactly as the admission
        // walk does.
        let charged = with_crate_verification_state(tcx.sess, |state| {
            state
                .vec_mutator_charged_blocks
                .get(&trust_mir_extract::safe_def_path_str(tcx, body.source.def_id()))
                .cloned()
        });
        native_trust_ir_monomorphic_foreign_callees(
            tcx,
            body,
            ty::TypingEnv::post_analysis(tcx, body.source.def_id()),
            charged.as_deref(),
        )
        .into_iter()
        .map(|(instance, name, _call_form_identity, _constructor_role)| (name, instance))
        .collect()
    });
    let Some(&instance) = set.get(callee_name) else {
        if env_flag_enabled("TRUST_NATIVE_DEBUG") {
            eprintln!(
                "[DERIVED_REQUIRES] callee={callee_name} NOT in the would-be-bundled set                  ({} entries)",
                set.len()
            );
        }
        return None;
    };
    // Trust (2026-09-25, E0391 on a generic caller in a recursion the local call
    // graph cannot see): the would-be-bundled set holds LOCAL generic instances too
    // (generics, local admission, 2026-09-13), and `instance_mir` of a local item is
    // `optimized_mir(def)`. The bundle build's own mono drain declines such a member
    // when that query is already on the stack ("mono-optimized-mir-would-cycle");
    // this reader of the same set did not, so it forced it. Measured (ay's ay-dpll,
    // `T::from_apply` <-> `T::fold_children(c, T::then)`, which reaches `from_apply`
    // only as a fn item handed to `Iterator::map`): optimized_mir(fold_children)
    // -> axiom census pointer hop -> optimized_mir(from_apply) -> this summary for
    // `fold_children::<fn(T, T) -> T {T::then}>` -> optimized_mir(fold_children):
    // E0391. Decline exactly as the drain does, so the two readers of the set agree:
    // the drain then leaves the member out, its call keeps the absent-callee
    // may-panic marker, and no precondition is derived (fail-closed; `None` is this
    // function's "the call keeps today's behavior" answer).
    if optimized_mir_query_would_cycle(tcx, instance.def_id()) {
        return None;
    }
    // The exact member-extraction recipe the bundle build uses.
    let generic_body = tcx.instance_mir(instance.def);
    let mono_body = instance
        .try_instantiate_mir_and_normalize_erasing_regions(
            tcx,
            ty::TypingEnv::fully_monomorphized(),
            ty::EarlyBinder::bind(tcx, generic_body.clone()),
        )
        .ok()?;
    if mono_body.has_non_region_param()
        || mono_body.has_non_region_infer()
        || mono_body.has_non_region_placeholders()
    {
        return None;
    }
    let member = trust_mir_extract::extract_function_with_contract_bundle(tcx, &mono_body, None);
    let derived = trust_ir_bridge::member_entry_prefix_assert_requires(&member);
    if env_flag_enabled("TRUST_NATIVE_DEBUG") {
        match &derived {
            Some((names, formulas)) => eprintln!(
                "[DERIVED_REQUIRES] callee={callee_name} derived {} requires over formals                  {names:?}",
                formulas.len()
            ),
            None => eprintln!(
                "[DERIVED_REQUIRES] callee={callee_name} bundled but NOTHING DERIVABLE                  (guarded assert / non-div-rem / unresolvable divisor / all asserts self-discharged by constant nonzero divisors)"
            ),
        }
    }
    let (param_names, requires) = derived?;
    let param_types =
        trust_mir_extract::extract_contract_param_types_for_instance(tcx, callee_def_id, args)
            .unwrap_or_default();
    let mut summary = trust_vcgen::FunctionSummary::new(callee_name.to_string())
        .with_param_names(param_names)
        .with_param_types(param_types);
    for formula in requires {
        summary = summary.with_precondition(formula);
    }
    Some(summary)
}

fn add_direct_call_contract_summaries<'tcx>(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>,
    body: &Body<'tcx>,
    db: &mut trust_vcgen::SummaryDatabase,
    // Trust: shared per-DB-build memo for `extract_contract_formulas_for_def`
    // (verdict-identical caching of a pure result; see `build_callee_contract_summaries`).
    memo: &mut ContractFormulaMemo<'tcx>,
) {
    let caller_def_id = body.source.def_id();

    // Trust (#5-PRE-B re-entrancy guard): the postcondition mint-gate below forces
    // `trust_proof_results(callee)`, which verifies the callee and can recurse back into
    // `build_callee_contract_summaries` for THIS caller. Register our own def_id up front
    // (mirroring `build_dyn_dispatch_summaries`) so a re-entrant force of THIS caller's
    // results — reached as some callee's callee — is detected by the `in_progress` check
    // in the mint-gate and treated as unproven (fail-closed), breaking the query cycle. A
    // re-entrant entry (our id already present) mints nothing and returns. The
    // owning frame removes its entry on normal return or unwind.
    let Some(_in_progress_guard) = DynSummaryInProgressGuard::enter(caller_def_id) else {
        return;
    };

    // Trust (postcondition composition — STRING-KEY IDENTITY, read before
    // widening anything below): this DB is keyed by `func_operand_name`, a
    // per-call-site STRING, and `func_operand_name` is deliberately NOT
    // injective over `DefId`s — the derived-trait / `?`-total / bulk-alloc
    // element-byte rewrites collapse several defs onto one sentinel spelling on
    // purpose. The direct loop below then takes the FIRST insert under a name
    // and `continue`s on every later one. For a REPORTED precondition that is
    // merely imprecise; for an ASSUMED `ensures` it is a false PROVE with no
    // symptom — one callee's postcondition would be conjoined onto a VC about a
    // different callee's result.
    //
    // So one identity discriminator for the reuse bit is the `DefId`, checked
    // here. Pre-scan this body's call terminators once and record, per name, the
    // single `DefId` it resolves to — or `None` the moment a second, different
    // one appears. Only a name that owns exactly one identity in THIS body may
    // mint reusable postcondition authority; every other name still gets its
    // ordinary reported summary, exactly as before. The scan is per caller body
    // (the DB is rebuilt per body), so there is no cross-body staleness to go
    // stale.
    //
    // Trust: THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO SAY THE `DefId` IS *THE* DISCRIMINATOR, AND
    // THAT IS MEASURED FALSE FOR A THIRD OF THE REALIZED POPULATION — corrected
    // 2026-08-31, comment only. Over `~/trust-corpora/mir-closed-mono` (37,612
    // bodies, 108,713 call terminators), 39 caller bodies carry one
    // `func_operand_name` covering two or more distinct callee identities
    // (`stable_crate_id` + `def_index` + region-erased `substs_digest`). Only 25
    // of them — all `__trust_total_clone` — carry two distinct `DefId`s and are
    // caught by the scan below. The other 14 share a `DefId` and differ ONLY in
    // `substs_digest` (`<TokensOrDefault<'a, T> as ToTokens>::to_tokens` x7,
    // `<Punctuated<T, P> as IntoIterator>::into_iter` x2,
    // `<Option<T> as DebugUnwrapOr<T>>::debug_unwrap_or` x2,
    // `<SchemeType as From<T>>::from`, an `Option::<&[u8]>::map::<bool, {closure}>`
    // with FOUR identities under one string, and one `__trust_total_clone`) — the
    // `and_modify` below maps both sites to the same `callee_def_id`, so
    // `name_owner == Some(callee_def_id)` holds and this gate PASSES them.
    //
    // What actually refuses those 14 is a DIFFERENT conjunct:
    // `callee_postconditions_are_reusable`'s `args.is_empty()` /
    // `identity:generic`, whose own doc gives an unrelated reason for existing
    // (verdict/instantiation agreement — the contract extracted here must be
    // byte-identically the one `trust_proof_results` verified). Read that as a
    // SOUNDNESS gate on this lane, not a capability limit: relaxing it to widen
    // compose to generic callees — the obvious next move, and it reads like one
    // — would hand instantiation 1's `ensures` to instantiation 2's call site
    // with nothing behind it. The `__trust_total_clone` member of the 14 is the
    // worst case, because its rendered string carries no generic syntax at all,
    // so an audit that reads names would never spot it.
    let mut reusable_name_owner: FxHashMap<String, Option<DefId>> = FxHashMap::default();
    // Trust (2026-10-06, DEFAULT-REQUIRES-OVERRIDE-CHARGE-1): per summary name, whether
    // EVERY call under it names a trait default that owes a `requires` and runs an
    // override that owes none (`trait_impl_requires::call_resolves_to_requires_free_override`).
    // Only such a name skips the default's contract in the loop below. The DB hands one
    // summary to every call of a name, so a name shared with any other call (a call that
    // runs the default body, a different callee rendered alike) keeps the charge.
    let caller_typing_env = ty::TypingEnv::post_analysis(tcx, caller_def_id);
    let mut default_requires_skipped: FxHashMap<String, bool> = FxHashMap::default();
    for bb in body.basic_blocks.iter() {
        let Some(term) = &bb.terminator else {
            continue;
        };
        let (TerminatorKind::Call { func: callee_op, .. }
        | TerminatorKind::TailCall { func: callee_op, .. }) = &term.kind
        else {
            continue;
        };
        // Trust (2026-10-06, DEFAULT-REQUIRES-OVERRIDE-CHARGE-1): `args` is read below.
        let Some((callee_def_id, args)) = callee_op.const_fn_def() else {
            continue;
        };
        let name = trust_mir_extract::func_operand_name_in_body(tcx, body, callee_op);
        // Trust (2026-10-06, DEFAULT-REQUIRES-OVERRIDE-CHARGE-1): see above.
        let skips = trait_impl_requires::call_resolves_to_requires_free_override(
            tcx,
            caller_typing_env,
            callee_def_id,
            args,
        );
        default_requires_skipped
            .entry(name.clone())
            .and_modify(|all| *all &= skips)
            .or_insert(skips);
        reusable_name_owner
            .entry(name)
            .and_modify(|owner| {
                if *owner != Some(callee_def_id) {
                    *owner = None;
                }
            })
            .or_insert(Some(callee_def_id));
    }

    // Trust (bundled-member derived preconditions): the would-be-bundled set,
    // computed lazily once per caller body — same admission gates as the
    // bundle itself by construction (contract-bearing callees are declined by
    // the collector, so no collision with authored contracts is possible).
    let mut bundled_collector_memo: Option<FxHashMap<String, ty::Instance<'tcx>>> = None;

    // Wave-4 (transitive trace closure): direct callees whose extracted body
    // attached, to be walked for THEIR callees after the direct loop.
    let mut transitive_trace_worklist: Vec<(DefId, u32)> = Vec::new();
    for bb in body.basic_blocks.iter() {
        let Some(term) = &bb.terminator else {
            continue;
        };
        let (TerminatorKind::Call { func: callee_op, .. }
        | TerminatorKind::TailCall { func: callee_op, .. }) = &term.kind
        else {
            continue;
        };
        let Some((callee_def_id, args)) = callee_op.const_fn_def() else {
            continue;
        };
        // Trust (cross-crate precondition discharge — fail-closed):
        //
        // Build a pre/postcondition summary for EVERY contracted direct callee,
        // including recursive self-calls, NON-LOCAL ones, and ones whose MIR
        // body is not exported. A self-call is a caller-side proof request just
        // like any other call; dropping it here lets the definition-entry
        // assumption discharge its own recursively re-entered precondition.
        // Contract extraction reads the source contract queries and is safe for
        // the current DefId. `callee_param_names` separately avoids re-entering
        // optimized MIR for that current DefId and falls back to the exact HIR
        // argument identifiers, so retaining the self summary creates no MIR
        // query cycle.
        // Previously this site dropped (a) any callee with `!is_mir_available`
        // and (b) non-local callees via `as_local()`, so a Trust-verified consumer
        // crate that violated a cross-crate `#[requires]` generated NO caller-side
        // discharge obligation and was reported clean — FAIL-OPEN. A contracted
        // callee conjoins its own preconditions onto its body-safety VCs and is
        // proved safe *assuming* its precondition (generate.rs / contracts.rs), so
        // the caller MUST discharge that precondition at the call site.
        //
        // Contracts come from `trust_contracts` and parameter names from
        // `fn_arg_idents` — both are `separate_provide_extern` queries decodable
        // from crate metadata (queries.rs:769 / 1560). NEITHER needs the callee
        // MIR body, so `is_mir_available` must NOT gate this path; the optimized-
        // MIR `var_debug_info` names are used only as a refinement when the callee
        // is BOTH local AND has exported MIR (see `callee_param_names`).
        // Key the summary by the SAME name the extracted Terminator::Call carries
        // (func_operand_name), NOT the bare safe_def_path_str. For a callee whose
        // name is REWRITTEN (?-total, bulk-alloc element-byte sink) the two differ,
        // and keying by safe_def_path_str left the call-site precondition lookup
        // unable to find the summary — the callee's #[requires] was silently skipped
        // (fail-OPEN, audit R2 #10). The name is per-call-site (the element-byte
        // token depends on the element type at THIS call), so a callee reached with
        // several distinct rewritten names registers one summary per name; the
        // `db.get` below dedups identical names and the per-def-id memo keeps the
        // contract extraction cheap (replacing the old def-id `seen` set).
        let callee_name = trust_mir_extract::func_operand_name_in_body(tcx, body, callee_op);
        if db.get(&callee_name).is_some() {
            continue;
        }

        // Trust (2026-09-30): a TRAIT-method call's syntactic callee is the trait's
        // declaration, so the contract extracted below is the TRAIT's, and an impl
        // method's own `requires` was charged at no concrete call site at all
        // (`TRUST_PRECOND_DEBUG=1`: `NO-SUMMARY` for `<S as Tr>::m`,
        // `<K as PartialEq>::eq`, `<K as Mk>::mk`; an inherent `S::m` got its VC).
        // The impl is proved assuming that precondition, so with `impl Clone for K {
        // fn clone(&self) -> Self requires self.0 < 10 { K(t[self.0 as usize]) } }`
        // and `pub fn k_clone(a: &K) -> u8 { a.clone().0 }` the crate built rc=0
        // under certify and `k_clone(&K(15))` panics (measured on the stage-1 build
        // of `a449d93d255`). When THIS call resolves, in the caller's typing env, to
        // an impl item carrying a `requires`, charge that precondition here.
        //
        // REPORTED PRECONDITIONS ONLY. The impl's `ensures` is not attached in any
        // form, so this adds caller obligations and assumes nothing; the name is
        // the call site's own `func_operand_name`, the key vcgen's call-site lookup
        // uses. A call that does not resolve (a generic receiver, `dyn`) is left to
        // the dispatch lanes, which cannot see an impl's strengthened contract; see
        // docs/KNOWN_HAZARDS.md (OPEN 2026-09-30) for that question.
        if tcx.trait_of_assoc(callee_def_id).is_some()
            && !args.has_non_region_param()
            && let Ok(Some(instance)) = ty::Instance::try_resolve(
                tcx,
                ty::TypingEnv::post_analysis(tcx, caller_def_id),
                callee_def_id,
                args,
            )
            && matches!(instance.def, ty::InstanceKind::Item(_))
            && instance.def_id() != callee_def_id
            && item_has_authored_requires(tcx, instance.def_id())
        {
            let impl_def_id = instance.def_id();
            let (preconditions, _impl_postconditions_not_assumed) =
                extract_contract_formulas_memo(tcx, impl_def_id, instance.args, memo);
            let param_types = trust_mir_extract::extract_contract_param_types_for_instance(
                tcx,
                impl_def_id,
                instance.args,
            )
            .unwrap_or_default();
            let mut summary = trust_vcgen::FunctionSummary::new(callee_name)
                .with_param_names(callee_param_names(tcx, impl_def_id))
                .with_param_types(param_types);
            for precondition in preconditions {
                summary = summary.with_precondition(precondition);
            }
            db.insert(summary);
            continue;
        }

        // Trust (I5 cross-authority call modeling): a LOCAL Clean-origin callee
        // whose plan-of-record shape this increment models gets a DEFINITIONAL
        // summary here — kernel gate → plan → summary; the clause restates the
        // emitted body's complete value semantics, so it both discharges a
        // right caller claim and refutes a wrong one. Keyed by the SAME
        // `func_operand_name` spelling the extracted `Terminator::Call`
        // carries, like every other entry in this DB. Short-circuits the
        // contract/tracing path below on purpose: a Clean-origin def's meaning
        // is owned by the kernel faithfulness gate, not by Rust contracts, and
        // its (fed, empty) HIR surface has nothing for those lanes anyway. A
        // `None` (unmodeled shape, plan/env refusal) falls through unchanged —
        // fail-closed, exactly the pre-lane behavior.
        if let Some(summary) = clean_origin_callee_summary(tcx, callee_def_id, &callee_name) {
            db.insert(summary);
            continue;
        }

        // Trust (2026-10-06, DEFAULT-REQUIRES-OVERRIDE-CHARGE-1): a call that names a
        // trait default owing a `requires` but runs an override owing none is not
        // charged the default's clause, nor handed its `ensures`: neither describes
        // the body that runs, and the override is verified without the clause
        // (`trait_impl_requires::call_resolves_to_requires_free_override`). The
        // fall-through below then treats it as the contract-less call it is; the
        // derived-member lane may charge the override's own entry asserts. Every other
        // call (generic, `dyn`, one that runs the default, an override that owes its
        // own `requires`) keeps the default's contract, as before.
        let (preconditions, postconditions) =
            if default_requires_skipped.get(&callee_name).copied().unwrap_or(false) {
                (Vec::new(), Vec::new())
            } else {
                extract_contract_formulas_memo(tcx, callee_def_id, args, memo)
            };
        // Trust (bundled-member derived preconditions — the bundled-Assert
        // remedy; canary 9d086fc6c37, successor shape named by revert
        // 1795a95f20c): a CONTRACT-LESS callee that the native bundle
        // collector would BUNDLE has real body Asserts the public-bundle
        // reset erases — charged to nobody (the five-step loss chain on the
        // canary). Derive a synthetic REQUIRES from the member's own
        // entry-prefix div/rem asserts and register it as an ordinary
        // reported-precondition summary: the existing callsite machinery then
        // mints one per-CALL-SITE `VcKind::Precondition` with the caller's
        // actuals substituted and the caller's path guards in scope — the
        // per-site guard reasoning the reverted whole-function aggregate
        // lacked (its measured 18-test regression). Reported preconditions
        // only: no postconditions, no reuse authority, monotone-sound (adds
        // counted obligations, never removes or assumes one). Derivation and
        // its fail-closed envelope:
        // `trust_ir_bridge::member_entry_prefix_assert_requires`.
        if preconditions.is_empty()
            && postconditions.is_empty()
            && let Some(summary) = derived_bundled_member_summary(
                tcx,
                body,
                &callee_name,
                callee_def_id,
                args,
                &mut bundled_collector_memo,
            )
        {
            db.insert(summary);
            continue;
        }
        // Trust (D5-B): a CONTRACT-LESS exact function or inherent method whose body is in the small
        // total fragment and whose `_0 == E(params)` the Clean kernel CERTIFIED
        // against that body (crate-wide mint, `inferred_return`) gets that lemma
        // as a reusable postcondition — the same rebinding lane an `ensures`
        // proved in this compilation uses, so `if !guard(bit) { return None }
        // x >> bit` proves exactly as the inline twin does. Only for a callee
        // with NO authored contract (the clause never shares a summary with one),
        // at the identity instantiation, under the caller-body name-ownership
        // pre-scan. `None` falls through to the pre-D5-B treatment unchanged.
        // The extracted body the pre-D5-B path would have attached (an analysis
        // input, no authority) is attached here too, so an L1 caller loses none
        // of what it had. Composed enum helpers may contain only calls whose
        // own original-body return equations this compilation kernel-checked.
        if preconditions.is_empty()
            && postconditions.is_empty()
            && let Some(summary) = inferred_return::callee_summary(
                tcx,
                caller_def_id,
                callee_def_id,
                args,
                &callee_name,
                reusable_name_owner.get(&callee_name).copied().flatten(),
            )
        {
            let summary = match extracted_callee_body_for_tracing(tcx, callee_def_id, args) {
                Some(body) => summary.with_extracted_body(body),
                None => summary,
            };
            db.insert(summary);
            continue;
        }
        // Wave-3: a CONTRACT-LESS callee still needs a summary IF the float
        // tracer can re-trace its body for a caller — an unannotated
        // `Vec3::new`/`Vec4::new` constructor carries no contract but IS the
        // per-field passthrough a matrix row's `Vec4::new(dot, …)` chain reads
        // through. Attach a body-only summary in that case; skip only when
        // there is neither a contract NOR a traceable body (adds nothing).
        let body_for_tracing = extracted_callee_body_for_tracing(tcx, callee_def_id, args);
        if preconditions.is_empty() && postconditions.is_empty() && body_for_tracing.is_none() {
            continue;
        }
        // Wave-4 (transitive trace closure): a callee whose body attaches is a
        // re-trace entry point — the trace follows CALLS INSIDE that body by
        // NAME in this same db, so the callees OF the callee need summaries
        // too (seeded here, registered after the direct loop).
        if body_for_tracing.is_some() {
            transitive_trace_worklist.push((callee_def_id, 1u32));
        }

        // Trust (audit R3 #4 — sentinel keying invariant): total-Clone / derived-
        // trait rewriting now lives inside `func_operand_name`, so the extracted
        // `Terminator::Call.func` and this summary key are identical by construction.
        // A sentinel carrying a user contract is nevertheless unreachable: both gates
        // require an `#[automatically_derived]` / provably-total std method, which
        // has no source body to attach `#[requires]`/`#[ensures]` to — so
        // `trust_contracts` is empty and the empty-contract guard immediately above
        // skips it before any summary is registered. The invariant (a sentinel
        // callee never reaches here with a non-empty contract) is therefore self-
        // enforcing; if the rewrite is ever widened to contractable hand-written or
        // default-bodied methods, this single name boundary still prevents divergence.
        // Direct-call summaries always carry authored preconditions so callers
        // must discharge them.
        //
        // Trust (postcondition composition): postcondition REUSE is minted here
        // for, and only for, the narrow case where the mint does not have to
        // transport or authenticate anything — a LOCAL, NON-GENERIC,
        // precondition-free callee whose OWN `ensures` obligation THIS
        // compilation has already discharged. There is no evidence wire in that
        // case: the authority is `tcx.trust_proof_results(callee)`, forced and
        // read in-process by the same rustc that will report the caller's
        // verdict, and the identity is a `DefId`, not a string. Everything else
        // stays exactly as fail-closed as before — a cross-crate callee, a
        // generic instantiation, or a callee whose rows are anything other than
        // fully proved still gets the reported-only `with_postcondition` summary
        // and is never assumed at a call site.
        //
        // Slice 2: a callee that ALSO declares `requires` is no longer in that
        // refused set. Its clause is minted through the same builder and is
        // injected as `requires -> ensures` instantiated at the call site, so
        // the caller may use it exactly where it discharges the precondition
        // THERE — and nowhere else. `preconditions` keeps its ordinary role
        // besides: it is still what `callsite_precondition_vcs` turns into the
        // caller's `VcKind::Precondition` obligation, so widening the assumption
        // did not narrow the check. The
        // remaining, genuinely open lane (a callee whose proof happened in a
        // DIFFERENT compilation) still needs sealed, replayed evidence bound to
        // the exact callee identity and contract digest; it is not this.
        let postconditions_reusable = callee_postconditions_are_reusable(
            tcx,
            caller_def_id,
            callee_def_id,
            args,
            &preconditions,
            &postconditions,
            reusable_name_owner.get(&callee_name).copied().flatten(),
        );
        let param_names = callee_param_names(tcx, callee_def_id);
        // Length-bearing source requirements are canonicalized to
        // `<formal>__slice_len`. Carry the exact instantiated signature types
        // beside the names so trust-vcgen can render that term from the actual
        // call operand. Missing/misaligned types intentionally leave the term
        // free and the caller obligation non-dischargeable.
        let param_types =
            trust_mir_extract::extract_contract_param_types_for_instance(tcx, callee_def_id, args)
                .unwrap_or_default();
        let mut summary = trust_vcgen::FunctionSummary::new(callee_name)
            .with_param_names(param_names)
            .with_param_types(param_types);
        for precondition in preconditions {
            summary = summary.with_precondition(precondition);
        }
        for postcondition in postconditions {
            summary = if postconditions_reusable {
                summary.with_verified_callee_postcondition(postcondition)
            } else {
                summary.with_postcondition(postcondition)
            };
        }
        // Trust (F6 — interprocedural float interval summaries): attach the
        // callee's derived f64 result interval, ONLY when every precondition
        // the derivation was entitled to assume is carried by THIS summary.
        // The vcgen consumer (`float_summary_result_range`) re-establishes
        // `summary.preconditions` structurally at each call site before
        // honoring the interval (self-contained assume-guarantee), so an
        // assumption absent from the summary would go unchecked — drop the
        // interval instead (fail-closed; the two precondition sets come from
        // different extraction lanes and byte-equality is the only alignment
        // this seam can prove). The interval grants NO proof authority: it
        // can only let the float lane suppress an obligation whose violation
        // it proves structurally impossible.
        if let Some((range, assumed_preconditions)) =
            f6_derived_callee_result_range(tcx, callee_def_id, args)
            && assumed_preconditions.iter().all(|assumed| summary.preconditions.contains(assumed))
        {
            summary = summary.with_result_range(range.0, range.1);
        }
        // Wave-3 (context-sensitive callee tracing): attach the extracted body
        // as an ANALYSIS INPUT (no proof authority) so the float tracer can
        // re-evaluate the callee's return-place projections under the caller's
        // proven argument intervals at each callsite. Attached for BOTH
        // contracted and contract-less callees; the tracer re-establishes any
        // callee precondition itself before honoring a derived interval.
        if let Some(body) = body_for_tracing {
            summary = summary.with_extracted_body(body);
        }
        db.insert(summary);
    }

    // Wave-4 (transitive trace closure): the F6b re-trace follows CALLS INSIDE
    // an attached callee body (`Vec3::add`'s `Vec3::new(sum_x, …)`), resolving
    // each by NAME in this same db — but the loop above registers the CALLER's
    // direct callees only, so a transitive callee (never called directly by
    // the caller) had no summary and the whole trace refused. Ground truth:
    // the `center → add → new` chain (fixtures/center-chain-2026-07-18 in
    // trust-vcgen) — the add-callsite F5-discharges from the caller contract,
    // while the scale-callsite's call-dest actual needs the re-trace through
    // `add`, which dies at the un-summarized `Vec3::new`; this is the a3d-geom
    // struct-chain 48-unknown residual. Walk the transitive call graph of
    // every attached body (bounded depth; the per-callsite trace fuel is 8)
    // and register the same pre/postcondition-free-or-contracted summaries.
    //
    // SOUNDNESS: identical to the direct registration — a summary is pure
    // ANALYSIS INPUT. The trace re-establishes a summarized callee's own
    // preconditions structurally before honoring any derived interval
    // (assume-guarantee), a body attaches under the same fail-closed gates
    // (`extracted_callee_body_for_tracing`), and NO postcondition evidence is
    // minted here (the #5-PRE-B proved-callee gate is not re-checked on this
    // path, so transitive summaries carry no reusable-ensures authority at
    // all). Absence — depth cap, non-local, cycles — merely leaves the trace
    // refusing as before (fail-closed).
    const TRANSITIVE_TRACE_DEPTH: u32 = 3;
    let mut seen: FxHashSet<DefId> = transitive_trace_worklist.iter().map(|(d, _)| *d).collect();
    while let Some((walk_def_id, depth)) = transitive_trace_worklist.pop() {
        if depth >= TRANSITIVE_TRACE_DEPTH {
            continue;
        }
        if walk_def_id.as_local().is_none()
            || !tcx.is_mir_available(walk_def_id)
            || optimized_mir_query_would_cycle(tcx, walk_def_id)
        {
            continue;
        }
        let walk_body = tcx.optimized_mir(walk_def_id);
        // Trust (2026-10-06, DEFAULT-REQUIRES-OVERRIDE-CHARGE-1): this walk resolves the
        // walked body's calls in that body's own environment.
        let walk_typing_env = ty::TypingEnv::post_analysis(tcx, walk_def_id);
        for bb in walk_body.basic_blocks.iter() {
            let Some(term) = &bb.terminator else {
                continue;
            };
            let (TerminatorKind::Call { func: callee_op, .. }
            | TerminatorKind::TailCall { func: callee_op, .. }) = &term.kind
            else {
                continue;
            };
            let Some((callee_def_id, args)) = callee_op.const_fn_def() else {
                continue;
            };
            if callee_def_id == caller_def_id || callee_def_id == walk_def_id {
                continue;
            }
            let callee_name =
                trust_mir_extract::func_operand_name_in_body(tcx, walk_body, callee_op);
            if db.get(&callee_name).is_some() {
                continue;
            }
            // Trust (2026-10-06, DEFAULT-REQUIRES-OVERRIDE-CHARGE-1): the same rule as the
            // direct loop. This summary lands in the same name-keyed DB, so a call that
            // runs a requires-free override must not register the default's contract
            // here either (a direct call of the same name would then be charged it).
            // A call skipped here registers nothing: a trait item attaches no body.
            let (preconditions, postconditions) =
                if trait_impl_requires::call_resolves_to_requires_free_override(
                    tcx,
                    walk_typing_env,
                    callee_def_id,
                    args,
                ) {
                    (Vec::new(), Vec::new())
                } else {
                    extract_contract_formulas_memo(tcx, callee_def_id, args, memo)
                };
            let body_for_tracing = extracted_callee_body_for_tracing(tcx, callee_def_id, args);
            if preconditions.is_empty() && postconditions.is_empty() && body_for_tracing.is_none() {
                continue;
            }
            // Keep the transitive summary's formal types aligned with the
            // instantiated call exactly as on the direct edge. Omitting them
            // leaves length-bearing preconditions free and can make the trace
            // consume a summary under a different call signature.
            let param_types = trust_mir_extract::extract_contract_param_types_for_instance(
                tcx,
                callee_def_id,
                args,
            )
            .unwrap_or_default();
            let mut summary = trust_vcgen::FunctionSummary::new(callee_name.clone())
                .with_param_names(callee_param_names(tcx, callee_def_id))
                .with_param_types(param_types);
            for precondition in preconditions {
                summary = summary.with_precondition(precondition);
            }
            for postcondition in postconditions {
                summary = summary.with_postcondition(postcondition);
            }
            let attached_body = body_for_tracing.is_some();
            if let Some(body) = body_for_tracing {
                summary = summary.with_extracted_body(body);
            }
            db.insert(summary);
            if attached_body && seen.insert(callee_def_id) {
                transitive_trace_worklist.push((callee_def_id, depth + 1));
            }
        }
    }
}

/// Formal parameter names for a (possibly NON-LOCAL) direct callee, in
/// declaration order.
///
/// Cross-crate-safe by construction: the authoritative source is
/// `tcx.fn_arg_idents`, a `separate_provide_extern` query that decodes the
/// callee's parameter idents from crate metadata. An anonymous (`_`) parameter
/// — `None` here — is mapped to a synthetic `_<i>` name; such a parameter
/// cannot be referenced by name in a `#[requires]` predicate, so it is never
/// the target of a precondition substitution.
///
/// The optimized-MIR `var_debug_info` names are used ONLY when the callee is
/// both local and has exported MIR; they agree with `fn_arg_idents` on named
/// parameters but are unavailable cross-crate.
///
/// FAIL-CLOSED: if `fn_arg_idents` is empty (e.g. the def is not a `Fn`/`AssocFn`
/// with recorded idents) this returns an empty `Vec`. The call-site discharge
/// (`generate_callsite_precondition_vcs`) then sees `param_names.len() (0) !=
/// args.len()` for any callee that actually takes arguments and emits a
/// fail-closed `Bool(true)` Precondition VC (→ Unknown/Failed) rather than a
/// silently-incomplete substitution.
fn callee_param_names<'tcx>(tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>, callee_def_id: DefId) -> Vec<String> {
    if let Some(local) = callee_def_id.as_local()
        && tcx.is_mir_available(callee_def_id)
    {
        // Mutual-recursion members have a pre-steal immutable snapshot. Use it
        // for the same authoritative MIR debug names as ordinary callees,
        // instead of silently degrading cyclic calls to `fn_arg_idents`.
        if trust_mir_is_mutually_recursive(tcx, local)
            && let Some(body) = tcx.mir_for_trust_verification(local)
        {
            return mir_param_names(body);
        }
        // A direct self call is already in progress but is not a mutual cycle;
        // querying its optimized MIR would re-enter the exact live query. Keep
        // the metadata fallback only for that narrow case.
        if !optimized_mir_query_would_cycle(tcx, callee_def_id) {
            return mir_param_names(tcx.optimized_mir(local.to_def_id()));
        }
    }

    source_contract_param_names(tcx, callee_def_id)
}

/// Source-contract formal names in declaration order.
///
/// This is deliberately based on `fn_arg_idents`, the same identity source
/// used by `trust-mir-extract` when it binds source contract formulas. It is
/// therefore the appropriate namespace equality witness for Liskov
/// comparison; optimized-MIR debug names are only a call-summary convenience.
fn source_contract_param_names<'tcx>(tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>, def_id: DefId) -> Vec<String> {
    tcx.fn_arg_idents(def_id)
        .iter()
        .enumerate()
        .map(|(i, ident)| match ident {
            Some(ident) => ident.to_string(),
            // `+ 1`: MIR locals number params from 1 (local 0 is the return
            // place), matching `mir_param_names`' `_<local>` convention.
            None => format!("_{}", i + 1),
        })
        .collect()
}

/// Trust: a MIR local can carry SEVERAL projection-free `var_debug_info` rows,
/// and only one of them is the parameter's own source name.
///
/// This map used to be last-write-wins, which is the wrong tie-break and made an
/// ATTRIBUTE-spelled contract silently un-composable. `#[core::contracts::ensures]`
/// desugars into a `__ret` binding, and for a straight-line body copy-propagation
/// parks its debug row on the parameter local itself — measured on
/// `fn pos(x: i32) -> i32 requires x > 0 { x }` with an attribute `ensures`:
///
/// ```text
/// fn pos_ga(_1: i32) -> i32 {
///     debug x => _1;                  // the parameter's own row, emitted FIRST
///     scope 1 { scope 2 { debug __ret => _1; } }   // `#[ensures]` lowering
/// ```
///
/// so parameter 1 was named `__ret`. That is not cosmetic. `param_names` is the
/// namespace the callee's CONTRACT formulas are bound in —
/// `contract_signature_environment_for_def` binds them to `fn_arg_idents` — so a
/// wrong name makes every free variable of the contract unbindable at a call
/// site. `generate::precondition_rebindable` then refuses the guard, the shared
/// ADMIT/EMIT predicate `summary_postconditions_ride_with_their_guard` answers
/// `false`, and the callee's proved `ensures` is dropped at EVERY call site with
/// no diagnostic anywhere. (The precondition-free case was unaffected, because a
/// postcondition's only free variable is the result symbol `_0`, which needs no
/// parameter name — which is exactly why the defect looked like "grammar
/// composes, attribute does not" rather than a naming bug.)
///
/// Same alias, second consumer: `contract_assumption_gate` fixed its own reader
/// of these rows on 2026-08-06 (see
/// `tests/ui/trust/postcondition_entry_hypothesis_survives_synthetic_ret.rs`).
///
/// Three independent reasons a row cannot be a parameter's name, any one of
/// which closes the measured case:
///
///  * it declares itself to be a DIFFERENT argument position
///    (`argument_index`, 1-based, and parameter `i` is local `i` because local 0
///    is the return place). Kept as a FILTER rather than a preference on purpose:
///    the field's own doc says an INLINED callee's row keeps the argument number
///    it had in the original function, so a present-and-matching index is not
///    positive evidence, while a present-and-mismatching one is decisive;
///  * it spells a name that `source_contract_synthetic_name_collision` reserves.
///    This is the FULL classifier, matching `trust_mir_extract`'s
///    `build_debug_name_map` byte for byte, and matching it is the point: that
///    map is the namespace `place_to_var_name` gives the callee's own body, so a
///    name demoted there and kept here would put `param_names` in a namespace
///    nothing else uses. Nothing is lost by demoting — a contract-bearing
///    function cannot have such a parameter, because
///    `trust_contract_query::reject_unrepresentable_contract_parameter_names`
///    `span_err`s on exactly this predicate before a clause is lowered; and
///  * it is not the FIRST row on that local. `rustc_mir_build`'s
///    `Builder::args_and_body` emits a parameter's debug row while building the
///    argument list, before any body binding exists to alias it.
///
/// A parameter with no surviving row keeps the positional `_<local>` spelling,
/// which is the same fallback `contract_signature_environment_for_def` uses for
/// an anonymous parameter, so the two namespaces still agree.
fn mir_param_names(body: &Body<'_>) -> Vec<String> {
    let mut debug_names: FxHashMap<usize, String> = FxHashMap::default();
    for debug_info in &body.var_debug_info {
        let VarDebugInfoContents::Place(place) = &debug_info.value else { continue };
        if !place.projection.is_empty() {
            continue;
        }
        let local = place.local.as_usize();
        if debug_info.argument_index.is_some_and(|index| usize::from(index) != local) {
            continue;
        }
        let name = debug_info.name.to_string();
        if trust_types::source_contract_synthetic_name_collision(&name).is_some() {
            continue;
        }
        debug_names.entry(local).or_insert(name);
    }

    (1..=body.arg_count)
        .map(|local| debug_names.get(&local).cloned().unwrap_or_else(|| format!("_{local}")))
        .collect()
}

/// Trust (piece #8): the extracted parameter TYPES of `vf`, in declaration order
/// (parallel to `mir_param_names`), read off the extracted `VerifiableBody`'s typed
/// `LocalDecl`s. Recorded on the R1 `FunctionSummary` so the σ length-renderer knows
/// which formal is a slice/array and needs a `<formal>__slice_len` replacement. The
/// types are the SAME extraction the callee's own VCs use, so the slice-ness
/// classification is consistent.
fn mir_param_types_from_vf(vf: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction) -> Vec<trust_types::Ty> {
    (1..=vf.body.arg_count)
        .map(|idx| {
            vf.body
                .locals
                .iter()
                .find(|d| d.index == idx)
                .map(|d| d.ty.clone())
                .unwrap_or(trust_types::Ty::Bool)
        })
        .collect()
}

fn collect_verification_artifacts_inner_with_policy<'tcx>(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>,
    body: &Body<'tcx>,
    decision_already_verified: bool,
    policy: &TrustVerifyPolicy,
    native_scope: &native_admission::Scope<'tcx>,
) -> Option<VerificationArtifacts> {
    if !decision_already_verified
        && (!verification_enabled(tcx.sess)
            || !matches!(verification_decision(tcx, body, policy), TrustVerifyDecision::Verify))
    {
        native_scope.outcome(trust_types::native_admission::NativeAdmissionOutcome::PolicySkipped);
        return None;
    }

    let def_id = body.source.def_id();
    // Trust: mark this function's `optimized_mir` as in-progress for the duration of
    // its verification, so any callee peek that would re-enter it (a mutual-recursion
    // SCC — E0391) is skipped by `optimized_mir_query_would_cycle`. RAII-removed on
    // every exit; only the inserting frame clears it. For an item body the pass has
    // already marked it for the whole pass (`TrustVerify::run_pass`), and this mark
    // is then a no-op; it still covers a shim body and a collection reached outside
    // the pass.
    let _optimized_mir_in_progress_guard = OptimizedMirInProgressGuard::enter(def_id);
    // Always retain and verify the real derived body. Neither an attribute nor
    // a trait method name can mint synthetic VERIFIED-empty evidence.
    let def_path = with_no_trimmed_paths!(tcx.def_path_str(def_id));
    // Trust (L0 termination guarantee): arm the per-function verification budget
    // HERE, before any synchronous preprocessing — contract lowering, MIR→VF
    // extraction, and VC synthesis — not just before solver dispatch. The budget
    // was historically created immediately before dispatch, so the entire
    // synchronous preprocessing phase ran OUTSIDE it: a non-terminating loop
    // there (e.g. the unmemoized `adt_arg_depth` type walk, which spun for hours
    // on a vendored `memchr` body) could never be caught, because the deadline
    // is cooperative and preprocessing never consulted it. Arming it up front and
    // checking it at the preprocessing checkpoint below makes a completed
    // overrun visible. It cannot preempt a synchronous loop that never returns.
    let function_deadline = verify_fn_deadline(tcx.sess);
    // Install the budget AMBIENTLY (wall-clock deadline + a deadline-independent
    // step count) so every synchronous preprocessing choke point across the
    // verifier crates (convert_ty_inner, the VC-generation walks, the driver
    // worklists, the widening fixpoint) can poll
    // `trust_types::verify_budget::budget_exhausted()` and fail closed — with no
    // signature churn. The step count is the provable termination bound (a
    // strictly-decreasing nat); the clock caps super-polynomial per-step work.
    // Restored on every exit (RAII).
    //
    // Trust (2026-10-18, gate-rca deadline NESTED-CLOCK): a FUNCTION scope. Building
    // this function's callee summaries reads callees' optimized MIR
    // (`extracted_callee_body_for_tracing`), which runs a callee's whole verification
    // here, synchronously, when the crate walk has not reached it yet; the step count
    // was always the callee's own (the nested guard restores this one), but this
    // function's deadline kept running. MEASURED on the candidate gate's atpkg-publish
    // (re-run alone, `gate-rca/fix-deadline/runs/cand-2`): `mirror::one` took 362.7 s
    // nested under five transitive callers, and each of them published one
    // `TrustPreprocessingBudget` row "exhausted during VC generation" in place of its
    // obligations. A function scope credits its wall time to the scopes enclosing it,
    // so a nested verification moves this function's ambient deadline, and
    // `function_clock_credit` moves the deadline captured here for the full lane.
    let _verify_budget_guard = trust_types::verify_budget::VerifyBudgetGuard::install_function(
        function_deadline,
        tcx.sess.opts.unstable_opts.trust_verify_function_budget_steps,
    );
    let function_clock_credit = trust_types::verify_budget::clock_credit_mark();
    // Determinism plan §3.2a: every ay solve this function runs — the v1
    // bridge, the assert/call-site/BMC refutation lanes, each retry — stops on
    // its own query's effort count; no account spans the function, so no solve
    // depends on the solves before it.
    // Trust: collect function-owned policy now, then pass it explicitly at each
    // VC-generation boundary below. Do not mutate ambient thread state here:
    // building callee summaries can synchronously force another proof query on
    // this same thread, and that callee must not overwrite the caller's proof
    // authority when the caller resumes.
    let single_writer = item_has_trust_attr(tcx, def_id, "single_writer");
    let backing = item_has_trust_attr(tcx, def_id, "backing");
    // Trust: snapshot this invocation's immutable crate-wide facts under one
    // short lock. Both clones are O(1) shared handles. An absent analysis-phase
    // initialization yields empty contexts and therefore fails closed.
    let (vcgen_callee_summaries, native_callee_summaries) =
        with_crate_verification_state(tcx.sess, |state| {
            (state.callee_summaries.clone(), state.native_callee_summaries.clone())
        });
    // Trust: bridge lowering still has deep visitors, so its map uses a
    // thread-affine ownership guard. A synchronously forced nested proof pushes
    // its own frame and this frame is restored on normal return or unwind;
    // sequential rustc_driver Sessions cannot inherit it after this function.
    let _native_callee_summary_guard = native_callee_summaries.enter();

    // Trust: vcgen's call-site assumptions travel inside the explicit
    // function-generation context below. This replaces seven overwrite-only
    // TLS setters: nested generation installs its own immutable frame and a
    // legacy/default entry gets an empty frame, so neither positive nor
    // negative authority leaks across functions, panics, or compiler Sessions.
    let empty_contracts = TrustContractBundle::empty(def_id);
    let rustc_contracts = trust_contracts_if_any(tcx, def_id).unwrap_or(&empty_contracts);
    let mut compiler_contracts =
        match trust_mir_extract::convert_trust_contract_bundle(tcx, rustc_contracts) {
            Ok(bundle) => bundle,
            Err(reason) => {
                debug!("Trust: unsupported compiler contract bundle for {def_path}: {reason}");
                return Some(unsupported_contract_artifacts(
                    tcx,
                    body,
                    &def_path,
                    reason.to_string(),
                ));
            }
        };
    let loop_provenance_failures =
        trust_mir_extract::stamp_loop_header_provenance(body, &mut compiler_contracts);
    if !loop_provenance_failures.is_empty() {
        let mut reasons = Vec::with_capacity(loop_provenance_failures.len());
        for (source_loop_id, reason) in loop_provenance_failures {
            let mut emitted = false;
            for contract in &rustc_contracts.loop_contracts {
                let rustc_middle::mir::trust_contract::TrustContractSubject::HirLoop { id, .. } =
                    contract.subject
                else {
                    continue;
                };
                if id.index != source_loop_id {
                    continue;
                }
                tcx.dcx()
                    .struct_span_err(
                        contract.span,
                        format!("invalid authored loop contract provenance: {reason}"),
                    )
                    .with_note(
                        "E4/E5 clauses require a compiler-authenticated HIR-loop to natural-MIR-header mapping; span fallback cannot authorize a failed mapping",
                    )
                    .emit();
                emitted = true;
            }
            if !emitted {
                tcx.dcx()
                    .struct_span_err(
                        body.span,
                        format!(
                            "invalid authored loop contract provenance for source loop {source_loop_id}: {reason}"
                        ),
                    )
                    .emit();
            }
            reasons.push(reason);
        }
        return Some(unsupported_contract_artifacts(tcx, body, &def_path, reasons.join("; ")));
    }

    compiler_contracts.loop_clause_source_locals =
        resolve_loop_clause_source_locals(tcx, body, rustc_contracts, &compiler_contracts);
    // Trust (ALLOC-STRIDE-ADT-2, 2026-10-07): extract with the compiler's
    // element-size side table — rustc's `layout_of` size per lowered element key,
    // collected BESIDE `func` (it never enters it, so no content hash or pin moves)
    // and installed on the `VcgenContext` below for the live-length capacity lane.
    let (mut func, elem_size_table, decimal_string_table) =
        trust_mir_extract::extract_function_with_compiler_facts(
            tcx,
            body,
            Some(&compiler_contracts),
        );
    // Trust: E4/E5 semantic intake. Pair first-class loop clauses with the
    // dominator-proved MIR natural-loop headers before ANY VC assembly. The
    // resulting obligations therefore enter the same `generate_vcs_*` set as
    // L0 panic/OOM/bounds obligations; they never ride a side channel whose
    // non-emptiness could suppress another discovery lane.
    let loop_contract_failures =
        trust_vcgen::bind_compiler_loop_contract_bundle(&mut func, &compiler_contracts);
    for (index, reason) in loop_contract_failures {
        let span =
            rustc_contracts.loop_contracts.get(index).map_or(body.span, |contract| contract.span);
        tcx.dcx()
            .struct_span_err(span, format!("invalid authored loop contract: {reason}"))
            .with_note(
                "authored `invariant`/`decreases` clauses fail closed; they are never accepted as documentation or inherited by another loop",
            )
            .emit();
    }
    // Trust #soundness (VS-1 follow-up, 2026-10-04): VC generation binds a loop
    // clause's identifiers by debug SPELLING over the whole body, so a binding
    // whose only `var_debug_info` row is a constant or a projection let the
    // spelling fall back to an outer binding (`let x = 50u32;` after a parameter
    // `x`: the invariant read the parameter, and a false one was proved and
    // kernel-certified). Check every spelling against the HIR binding the query
    // resolved at the loop; a clause that fails is rewritten to its unpaired
    // form, which VC generation reports as one non-refutable row.
    for (index, reason) in trust_mir_extract::refuse_loop_clauses_bound_outside_their_scope(
        tcx,
        body,
        rustc_contracts,
        &compiler_contracts,
        &mut func,
    ) {
        let span =
            rustc_contracts.loop_contracts.get(index).map_or(body.span, |contract| contract.span);
        tcx.dcx()
            .struct_span_warn(
                span,
                format!(
                    "loop clause is not verified: it does not bind the source binding in scope \
                     at its loop ({reason})"
                ),
            )
            .with_note(
                "a loop clause's identifiers must denote the bindings visible at the loop; one \
                 that resolves to another binding of the same name fails closed and is never proved",
            )
            .emit();
    }

    // Trust (L0 termination guarantee): preprocessing checkpoint. Contract
    // lowering + MIR→VF extraction have run; if that already burned the whole
    // per-function budget, this body is pathological or hit a verifier
    // non-termination bug. Fail the build with a LOCATED error rather than press
    // on into VC generation and the solver. This is deliberately a hard error,
    // NOT the `Unknown` degradation a solver timeout gets: a solver timeout means
    // "could not decide" (a completeness gap), whereas a preprocessing overrun
    // means the verifier could not even build the obligations — a defect the
    // developer must see. `-Ztrust-verify-function-budget-ms=0` disables the
    // budget (deadline `None`) and skips this check. The advisory lane instead
    // gets the honest per-function budget verdict: a single synthetic `Unknown`
    // row replaces the (incomplete) inventory, the finding is a located
    // warning, and the survey build continues — never a hard error, never a
    // `Proved`.
    if preprocessing_budget_exceeded(tcx, body, &def_path, "extraction", policy.fail_closed()) {
        return if policy.fail_closed() {
            None
        } else {
            Some(preprocessing_budget_artifacts(tcx, body, &def_path, "extraction"))
        };
    }

    // Trust: E6 facet intake — scan this body for the four definitional-use
    // facets and record the findings in the session table. The scan sees the
    // SAME `func` VC generation sees (post loop-clause binding). Fail-closed
    // by construction: an unscanned body simply has no record.
    if let Some(owner) = zero_obligation_source_owner(body.source) {
        let fresh = reified_fnptr_charge::capture(tcx, body, &func);
        with_crate_verification_state(tcx.sess, |state| {
            let binding = match state.reified_fnptr_bindings.get(&owner) {
                None => fresh,
                Some(previous) if previous == &fresh => fresh,
                Some(_) => None,
            };
            state.reified_fnptr_bindings.insert(owner, binding);
        });
    }
    record_fn_facets(tcx.sess, &func);

    // Trust: one immutable, owner-bound context carries all function-local proof
    // policy. trust-vcgen installs it only for the actual generation call and
    // restores any outer frame on return/unwind, so nested callee query forcing
    // cannot leak either positive or negative authority into this function.
    let contract_panic_annotations =
        trust_mir_extract::extract_contract_panic_annotations(tcx, def_id);
    // A matching source path is not enough to decide ownership: the same core
    // span can be an inlined dependency in a user crate. Bind the exemption to
    // both local DefId identity and the canonical stdlib crate-name set shared
    // with vcgen's span recognizer.
    let verify_stdlib_subject_memory_safety = stdlib_subject_memory_safety_policy(
        def_id.is_local(),
        tcx.crate_name(LOCAL_CRATE).as_str(),
    );
    // Trust: rustc's own record of which assignments in THIS body are modular
    // arithmetic; it changes the generated obligation set for an otherwise
    // byte-identical extracted body.
    let modular_arith_ops = trust_modular_arith_ops(tcx, body);
    // Trust (D2 tier 1): the `ADAPTER_YIELD` premises the pre-steal seam minted
    // for THIS closure body (empty for every other body), installed on the
    // context below.
    let adapter_yield_premises = adapter_yield::premises_for_body(tcx, body);
    // Trust (2026-10-18, FRESH-CAPTURE-1): the capture fields the pre-steal seam
    // certified fresh shared borrows for THIS closure body (empty for every other
    // body); vcgen re-gates them on the body before keeping any pointee.
    let fresh_capture_fields = fresh_capture::fields_for_body(tcx, body);
    // Trust (AW-1, 2026-10-03): rustc's own `.await` `unsafe` calls; withholds the
    // stand-in row for exactly those blocks.
    let await_desugared_unsafe_call_blocks = collect_await_desugared_unsafe_call_blocks(tcx, body);
    // Trust (STR_BYTE_POSITION): the certified `str::Bytes::position(|b| b == K)`
    // call blocks; they add facts to an otherwise byte-identical body.
    let ascii_eq_bytes_position_calls = byte_eq_predicate::ascii_eq_bytes_position_calls(tcx, body);
    // Trust (Clean-origin definitional grounding, assert-refutation lane): hand
    // the refutation lane the identity-keyed definitional models of THIS body's
    // Clean-origin callees, read from the same kernel-gated plan of record the
    // VC lane's summary DB is built from (`clean_origin_definitional_call_models`
    // — the measured defect is recorded on that function).
    let vcgen_callee_summaries = vcgen_callee_summaries
        .with_clean_origin_definitional_models(clean_origin_definitional_call_models(tcx, body));
    // Trust (2026-09-25, mutual-recursion termination): this body's recursion-SCC
    // facts. Like a callee model, they are facts about OTHER functions (SCC
    // membership, each callee's measure).
    let recursion_scc = recursion_scc_termination_context(tcx, body);
    if let Some(scc) = &recursion_scc {
        note_unmeasured_recursion_cycle(tcx, body, &def_path, scc);
    }
    // Trust (2026-10-08, D15): the noalias premise over this body's `&mut`
    // arguments, installed only where it changes a havoc list. Recorded BEFORE
    // generation so the demotion, the spending gate and the disclosure all see it.
    // (The VC-artifact cache this used to exclude the body from was deleted
    // upstream, d970f53a730.)
    let noalias_params = {
        let candidates = argument_premise::noalias_candidate_args(tcx, body);
        if candidates.len() >= 2 && trust_vcgen::noalias_premise_would_fire(&func, &candidates) {
            candidates
        } else {
            Vec::new()
        }
    };
    // Trust (box-deref doc-lint fix): the MIR OPERATIONS — `(BlockId, statement
    // index)` — of compiler-synthesized Box derefs, threaded into the unsafe
    // doc-lint (see collect_synthesized_box_deref_ops). Keyed on the operation
    // and not the span since 2026-09-20: `SourceSpan` discards the
    // `SyntaxContext`, so one proc-macro `call_site()` span names every
    // operation of the expansion at once. Collected here (it reads `tcx` and
    // `body` only) because the D14 install gate below needs it.
    let mut box_deref_ops = FxHashSet::default();
    collect_synthesized_box_deref_ops(tcx, body, &mut box_deref_ops);
    // Trust (2026-10-09, D14): the reference-argument `Box` validity premise,
    // installed only for the arguments where it roots a nominated box deref that
    // is not rooted without it. Same recording, demotion, disclosure and cache
    // discipline as D15.
    let reference_box_params = trust_vcgen::reference_box_root_would_fire(
        &func,
        &box_deref_ops,
        &argument_premise::reference_box_candidate_args(body),
    );
    argument_premise::record(tcx, body, &noalias_params, &reference_box_params);
    // Trust (2026-10-17, gate RCA "premise"): whether this body is graded per row
    // against a premise-free generation (below, after the consumed generation).
    let premise_installed = !noalias_params.is_empty() || !reference_box_params.is_empty();
    // COMPLETENESS, MINTED BEFORE GENERATION SO THE MINT CAN SEE IT (2026-09-20).
    // `synthetic_unmodeled_unsafe_call_vcs` is the AUTHORITY on which calls get
    // the fail-closed `[unsafe:unmodeled-call]` row, and until today vcgen's
    // `decide_site` decided whether to drop a call site's stand-in by PREDICTING
    // that authority's answer from a rendered callee path. The two evaluate
    // different spellings of the same callee, and a disagreement in the
    // "unmodeled here, modeled there" direction deleted the stand-in and minted
    // nothing to replace it — an unsafe site with zero obligations.
    //
    // So the rows are generated FIRST, here, over this very `body`, and the
    // blocks they were minted for are handed to the mint as POSITIVE EVIDENCE.
    // The vector itself is appended to `solver_vcs` further down, unchanged and
    // in the same position as before; generating it earlier changes nothing
    // about it (it reads `tcx` and `body` only).
    let unmodeled_unsafe_call_vcs = synthetic_unmodeled_unsafe_call_vcs(tcx, body, &func.def_path);
    let unmodeled_unsafe_call_blocks: FxHashSet<trust_types::BlockId> =
        unmodeled_unsafe_call_vcs.iter().map(|(block, _)| *block).collect();
    // Trust (G1, 2026-10-27): the `mem::zeroed::<T>()` calls of this body whose `T`
    // rustc's exact validity check admits all-zero (`zero_valid_zeroed_call_blocks`).
    let zero_valid_zeroed_blocks = zero_valid_zeroed_call_blocks(tcx, body);
    // Trust (REVIEW-A4C4-MINORS-1 fix-forward, 2026-11-07): rustc's layout of each
    // foreign call's struct pointee (`foreign_return_pointee_layouts`).
    let foreign_return_pointee_layouts = foreign_return_pointee_layouts(tcx, body);
    // Trust (SAFETY-ASSUME-1, ruled 2026-10-17): the MIR operations of this body
    // that sit in a user `unsafe { }` block with a clippy-placed `// SAFETY:`
    // comment (`safety_comment_assumption`). Their unmodeled-operation rows are
    // WITHHELD — the compiler's here and below, vcgen's inside the declaration scope
    // around generation — and every block that lost one is charged an
    // `assumption:safety-comment` row at publication. The evidence set above keeps
    // a withheld call's block: the compiler accounts for that call by its charge,
    // so vcgen's mint mints no stand-in beside it.
    let mut safety_declaration = safety_comment_assumption::SafetyDeclaration::of(tcx, body);
    let unmodeled_unsafe_call_vcs: Vec<VerificationCondition> = unmodeled_unsafe_call_vcs
        .into_iter()
        .filter_map(|(block, vc)| {
            (!safety_declaration.withhold_terminator(body, block)).then_some(vc)
        })
        .collect();
    let vcgen_context = trust_vcgen::VcgenContext::for_function(func.def_path.clone())
        .with_single_writer(single_writer)
        .with_backing(backing)
        .with_stdlib_subject_memory_safety(verify_stdlib_subject_memory_safety)
        .with_contract_panic_annotations(contract_panic_annotations)
        .with_callee_summaries(vcgen_callee_summaries)
        .with_modular_arith_ops(modular_arith_ops)
        .with_process_signal_policy(installed_process_signal_policy(tcx))
        // Trust (ALLOC-WIDTH-1): the target's pointer width, a session fact.
        .with_target_pointer_bits(trust_target_pointer_bits(tcx))
        // Trust (G1 review, 2026-10-27): the Darwin-only libc return contracts
        // (`getpwuid`'s per-thread result) hold only on a Darwin target.
        .with_target_is_darwin(tcx.sess.target.is_like_darwin)
        // Trust (refuted 2026-09-16): the vanilla sysroot callees of this body are
        // the ones the inferred recursion-termination lane may classify as
        // non-reentrant; see `VcgenContext::nonreentrant_callees`.
        .with_nonreentrant_callees({
            // Collected into the set vcgen takes, so the ledger's order is not observed.
            #[allow(rustc::potential_query_instability)]
            let callees = function_vanilla_sysroot_callees(tcx, body, None, false)
                .into_iter()
                .filter_map(|(callee, vanilla)| vanilla.then_some(callee))
                .collect();
            callees
        })
        // Trust (2026-09-25): every call into this body's recursion SCC is
        // charged with a `[termination]` row, keyed on the resolved callee
        // `DefId`; see `recursion_scc_termination_context`.
        .with_recursion_scc(recursion_scc)
        // Trust (ARGS-FINGERPRINT-READ-1 round 3, 2026-10-07): the call blocks that
        // may run THIS body, decided from `DefId`s; the direct-self termination
        // lanes AND it with their rendered-path comparison, because two local
        // bodies can render one def path. See `direct_self_call_identity_blocks`.
        .with_direct_self_call_blocks(termination_unmeasured::direct_self_call_identity_blocks(
            tcx, body,
        ))
        .with_compiler_unmodeled_call_blocks(unmodeled_unsafe_call_blocks)
        // Trust (G1): positive evidence only; empty refutes every struct/union `zeroed`.
        .with_compiler_zero_valid_zeroed_blocks(zero_valid_zeroed_blocks)
        // Trust (REVIEW-A4C4-MINORS-1 fix-forward): positive evidence only; empty lends
        // no struct-pointee FFI contract (`getpwuid`).
        .with_compiler_foreign_return_pointee_layouts(foreign_return_pointee_layouts)
        // Trust (AW-1, 2026-10-03): the `Pin::new_unchecked` / `get_context`
        // calls rustc's own `.await` lowering emitted; see
        // `collect_await_desugared_unsafe_call_blocks`. Empty keeps every row.
        .with_await_desugared_unsafe_call_blocks(await_desugared_unsafe_call_blocks)
        // Trust (D8, 2026-09-24): the call blocks whose returned `Box` is a
        // validity root — the SAME predicate the extractor's assert discharge
        // reads (`trust_mir_extract::returned_box_root`), over the same `body`
        // `func` was extracted from. Empty roots nothing.
        .with_returned_box_root_blocks(trust_mir_extract::returned_box_root_call_blocks(tcx, body))
        // THE AUTHENTICATED TRUSTED-STD TABLE (2026-09-20). `is_trusted_std_span`
        // used to decide from `SourceSpan::file`, the unremapped local path the
        // USER compiled; this hands the mint the residency verdict instead, from
        // the same `trusted_std_source_files(tcx)` the completeness generator
        // above consulted. Installed unconditionally: a table that never reaches
        // the mint is the fail-closed direction (no span trusted, every
        // obligation kept), which is what every non-compiler caller gets.
        .with_trusted_std_source_files(trusted_std_source_files(tcx))
        // Trust (ALLOC-STRIDE-ADT-2, 2026-10-07): the element-size table from the
        // extraction above, bound to `func`'s type digest NOW — after every edit
        // this function makes to `func` — so a changed function never reads it.
        .with_elem_sizes(elem_size_table, &func)
        // Trust: bind authenticated primitive decimal calls to the complete final
        // body after all edits, outside serialized MIR/census data. Changed or
        // differently owned bodies cannot borrow these standard-library facts.
        .with_primitive_decimal_strings(decimal_string_table, &func)
        // Trust (D2 tier 1): the closure's `ADAPTER_YIELD` premises (a modeled
        // fact, docs/TCB.md). vcgen re-gates each on this body before using it.
        .with_adapter_yield_premises(adapter_yield_premises)
        // Trust (2026-10-18, FRESH-CAPTURE-1): see above; empty keeps every
        // CALL-HAVOC-ESCAPE-2 kill.
        .with_fresh_shared_capture_fields(fresh_capture_fields)
        // Trust (2026-10-08, D15): the noalias premise (see above); empty
        // installs nothing.
        .with_noalias_mut_params(noalias_params)
        // Trust (2026-10-09, D14): see above; empty installs nothing.
        .with_reference_box_root_params(reference_box_params)
        // Trust (STR_BYTE_POSITION): vcgen re-gates each call block (fresh
        // iterator, one dynamic execution, same root) before using it.
        .with_ascii_eq_bytes_position_calls(ascii_eq_bytes_position_calls);

    // Trust #spine: lower every verified MIR-derived VerifiableFunction to a
    // Trust-IR compatibility module on the default verification path. Verdicts
    // below still come from the retained trust-types/trust-vcgen route and are
    // re-anchored only by verdict-identical L0/L1 flips.
    //
    // Trust #spine (R-L1): the contract-VC computation is now UNGATED from the
    // former DEBUG gate so it runs on the LIVE path: this `spine_module` is the
    // single trust-ir lowering for the shadow summary AND for the L1 contract
    // verdict-flip (`apply_spine_contract_verdict_flip` consumes it via
    // `flip_contract_verdicts_with_module`), so the L1 flip adds NO second lowering.
    // Fail-open: a not-yet-lowerable function degrades to `None` + a debug line,
    // and the L1 flip then becomes a no-op (every contract VC stays
    // trust-vcgen-sourced) — never a verdict change, never a build block.
    //
    // Ratified P9 preserves this MIR proving path until the direct source frontend
    // and unified router are capability-equivalent. This module is therefore a
    // migration adapter, not yet the shared Rust/Clean source module.
    let spine_module: Option<trust_ir::Module> = match trust_ir_bridge::lower_mir_compat_to_trust_ir(
        &func,
    ) {
        Ok(spine_module) => {
            if tracing::enabled!(tracing::Level::DEBUG) {
                let spine_obligations = trust_ir_bridge::obligation_kind_summary(&spine_module);
                let spine_total: usize = spine_obligations.values().sum();
                let spine_contract_vcs = trust_ir_bridge::contract_vcs_from_trust_ir(&spine_module);
                debug!(
                    "Trust-IR spine (MIR compatibility): {} lowered to trust-ir — {} obligation(s) {:?}, {} contract VC(s)",
                    func.def_path,
                    spine_total,
                    spine_obligations,
                    spine_contract_vcs.len(),
                );
            }
            Some(spine_module)
        }
        Err(spine_err) => {
            debug!(
                "Trust-IR spine (MIR compatibility): {} not yet lowerable to trust-ir: {spine_err}",
                func.def_path,
            );
            None
        }
    };

    let effective_compiler_contracts;
    let compiler_contracts_for_verification = if compiler_contracts.contracts.is_empty()
        && compiler_contracts.loop_contracts.is_empty()
        && !func.contracts.is_empty()
    {
        effective_compiler_contracts =
            trust_types::CompilerContractBundle::new(func.contracts.clone())
                .with_loop_contracts(compiler_contracts.loop_contracts.clone())
                .with_proof_items(compiler_contracts.proof_items.clone());
        &effective_compiler_contracts
    } else {
        &compiler_contracts
    };
    debug!(
        "Trust: extracted {} with {} contract(s), {} precondition(s), {} postcondition(s)",
        func.def_path,
        func.contracts.len(),
        func.preconditions.len(),
        func.postconditions.len(),
    );

    // Trust (VEC-INSERT, 2026-09-25): record which `Vec` index-mutator calls of this
    // body vcgen charges their REAL index precondition (`vec_region`); this body's own
    // vanilla ledger reads it (`function_vanilla_sysroot_callees`). Recorded every
    // time, so a stale entry can never outlive a re-verification.
    let charged_vec_mutators = trust_vcgen::vec_mutator_charged_blocks(&func);
    with_crate_verification_state(tcx.sess, |state| {
        state.vec_mutator_charged_blocks.insert(func.def_path.clone(), charged_vec_mutators);
    });

    // Trust #243/#76: Callee summaries. Closed-world `dyn Trait` summaries can
    // contribute proved postconditions, while direct local callee summaries
    // carry declared contracts so caller-side precondition VCs are emitted.
    // Built once and shared by every VC route below.
    let callee_summaries = build_callee_contract_summaries(tcx, body);
    if !callee_summaries.is_empty() {
        debug!(
            "Trust: {} has {} callee contract summar(ies)",
            func.def_path,
            callee_summaries.len(),
        );
    }

    // Trust (audit R3 #1 — modular soundness): a callee POSTCONDITION is a
    // FUNCTIONAL (L1) guarantee that is CONDITIONAL on the callee's PRECONDITION
    // (also an L1 obligation). The summary DB drives BOTH lanes — the postcondition
    // is conjoined into body VCs (inheriting the L0Safety level of the obligation it
    // lands on) and the precondition becomes its own `VcKind::Precondition` VC
    // (hardcoded L1Functional). The later `filter_vcs_by_level(max_level)` keeps the
    // postcondition-injected L0 body VC but DROPS the L1 precondition VC at L0Safety
    // — so applying summaries at L0 would assume a callee's postcondition WITHOUT
    // ever checking its precondition: a false-PROVE (the cardinal sin). Contract-
    // based discharge is therefore a functional-level feature: apply the callee
    // summaries only when functional verification is active (>= L1Functional), so
    // the postcondition assumption and its precondition check always survive the
    // filter together. At L0Safety this reverts to the plain, summary-free safety
    // path (empty summaries == plain discharge — see
    // `test_discharge_path_does_not_assume_sealed_dyn_postcondition`), i.e. no
    // regression versus pre-composition behaviour. `effective_max_level` itself
    // bumps a CONTRACTED caller to >= L1, so a caller that declares its own
    // contracts still gets composition at the default level.
    // Trust (2026-10-03, level-0 charges): it also bumps a caller that OWES a
    // callee's `requires` (`owes_callee_requires` below), on every policy. The
    // callee ASSUMES that clause at entry and elides the checks it proves under
    // it (`DefinitionEntryAssumption`), so the call-site `Precondition` row is
    // the only thing between a call outside the clause and the callee running
    // with those checks gone; the row is `L1Functional`, and the level filter
    // dropped it for an uncontracted caller at `-Ztrust-verify-level=0`
    // (MEASURED on stage 1 of `782780ea0d`: `p() { add1(u32::MAX) }` built exit 0
    // under certify and returned `0`; `z() { div(1, 0) }` built exit 0 and
    // panicked). The full summary DB and the call-site rows then travel together,
    // exactly as R3 #1 requires: such a caller is verified as at level 1.
    // Trust (I5 cross-authority call modeling): below L1Functional the general
    // summary DB must still not apply — that is the R3 #1 rule above, verbatim.
    // But a DEFINITIONAL, precondition-free summary (the Clean-origin lane,
    // `clean_origin_callee_summary`) has no conditional half: there is no
    // precondition VC for the level filter to strip, and its clause is true of
    // every execution of the emitted callee body by construction. Exactly that
    // subset — both properties enforced inside trust-vcgen, where the authority
    // bit is crate-private — rides the L0 lane, so a plain L0 caller of a
    // Clean-origin projection or wrapping `+`/`-` binop can discharge (or be
    // refuted on) its own assertions about the call's result. Empty when no
    // such callee exists: byte-identical to the old empty-DB behavior.
    let l0_definitional_summaries = callee_summaries.definitional_precondition_free_subset();
    // Trust (2026-10-03, level-0 charges): a call of THIS body whose summary
    // declares a precondition — looked up by the extracted terminator's name, as
    // the call-site producers look it up, so a summary the transitive-trace
    // closure registered for a callee's callee lifts nothing (review of the fix:
    // it had lifted `p(b: bool) { if b { p(b) } else { w(3) } }` to L1 because
    // `w` calls a `requires` function) — or, asked only where it can still change
    // the level, a DIRECT call whose callee owes its caller a `requires`
    // (`direct_callees_owing_caller_precondition`, read off the call operand's
    // type, the census memory-safe's skip demotion and `#[trust::assume_total]`
    // already use), so a callee whose summary did not build still lifts the
    // caller to the level at which level 2 verifies it.
    let owes_callee_requires = callee_summaries.owes_caller_preconditions(&func)
        || (effective_max_level(tcx.sess, &func, false) < trust_types::ProofLevel::L1Functional
            && !uncharged_callable::direct_callees_owing_caller_precondition(tcx, body).is_empty());
    let vc_summaries: &trust_vcgen::SummaryDatabase =
        if effective_max_level(tcx.sess, &func, owes_callee_requires)
            >= trust_types::ProofLevel::L1Functional
        {
            &callee_summaries
        } else {
            &l0_definitional_summaries
        };
    // Trust (D5-A): census of this body's same-crate free-fn calls, for the
    // negative-only call-destination refutation screen. Generation never reads
    // it; see `call_dest_screen`.
    let vcgen_context = vcgen_context.with_local_call_dest_screen(
        call_dest_screen::local_call_dest_screen(tcx, body, vc_summaries),
    );

    // Trust #941: Dump extracted VerifiableFunction as JSON for test fixture generation.
    // When `-Ztrust-dump=mir:<dir>` is set, serialize each function to a bounded,
    // content-addressed filename. A readable `::` -> `__` mapping is not
    // injective (`a::b` and `a__b` collide) and used to silently overwrite
    // proof inputs. Because setting the environment variable explicitly asks
    // for evidence, every directory/serialization/publication error is fatal.
    // Used to produce real-MIR fixtures for integration tests without requiring rustc_private.
    if let Some(dump_path) = tcx.sess.opts.unstable_opts.trust_dump.mir.as_deref() {
        std::fs::create_dir_all(dump_path).unwrap_or_else(|error| {
            tcx.dcx().fatal(format!(
                "failed to create -Ztrust-dump=mir: directory `{}`: {error}",
                dump_path.display()
            ))
        });
        let json = serde_json::to_string_pretty(&func).unwrap_or_else(|error| {
            tcx.dcx().fatal(format!(
                "failed to serialize `{}` for -Ztrust-dump=mir:: {error}",
                func.def_path
            ))
        });
        let file_path = dump_path.join(mir_dump_filename(&func.def_path, &json));
        let created = write_mir_dump_create_new(&file_path, &json).unwrap_or_else(|error| {
            tcx.dcx().fatal(format!(
                "failed to publish -Ztrust-dump=mir: evidence for `{}` at `{}`: {error}",
                func.def_path,
                file_path.display()
            ))
        });
        trace!(
            "Trust: {} MIR dump for {} at {}",
            if created { "created" } else { "reused identical" },
            func.def_path,
            file_path.display()
        );
    }

    // Trust (M5 blocker #0 — census core repeatability): -Ztrust-dump=mir-only:<dir>
    // stops HERE — extraction is done and, if -Ztrust-dump=mir: was also set, the
    // dump above already wrote this function's JSON. Return immediately,
    // BEFORE the cache lookup and BEFORE any solver/certify dispatch below, so
    // a whole-`core` MIR dump doesn't also pay for (potentially unbounded,
    // per-VC) verification of every function. Defaults OFF: unset (any other
    // value, including unset) leaves this branch entirely untaken, so ordinary
    // verification runs are byte-identical to before this change. Fail-closed:
    // `dump_only_artifacts` reports the function's obligations as `Unknown`
    // ("dump-only: verification skipped"), never `Proved` — a skip/survey
    // verdict can never be mistaken for a genuine pass.
    if tcx.sess.opts.unstable_opts.trust_dump.mir_only {
        native_scope.outcome(trust_types::native_admission::NativeAdmissionOutcome::DumpOnly);
        return Some(dump_only_artifacts(tcx, body, &def_path));
    }

    // R-U Phase D: this native typed route is unconditional — the former
    // `uses_native_typed_route` policy hook was hardcoded true and the legacy
    // Path-1 alternative it once selected against is deleted.
    // Admission is resource scheduling, not semantic proof work. Acquire
    // before starting the one function deadline, then keep this reservation
    // alive through VC generation, spine reconciliation, native bundle
    // construction, all solver lanes, and certificate publication.
    let reservation_bytes = match trust_router::coordinator::default_reservation_bytes() {
        Ok(bytes) => bytes,
        Err(error) => {
            native_scope.outcome(trust_types::native_admission::NativeAdmissionOutcome::Refused {
                reason: error.to_string(),
            });
            return Some(memory_admission_artifacts(tcx, body, &def_path, error.to_string()));
        }
    };
    let _memory_admission = match native_scope.reserve(reservation_bytes) {
        Ok(reservation) => reservation,
        Err(error) => {
            return Some(memory_admission_artifacts(tcx, body, &def_path, error.to_string()));
        }
    };
    // `function_deadline` was armed at function entry (before preprocessing);
    // reusing it here keeps extraction + VC generation inside the one budget.
    // Trust (2026-10-03): the same lift as the summary selection above, so the
    // call-site `Precondition` rows minted from the full DB survive this filter.
    let max_level = effective_max_level(tcx.sess, &func, owes_callee_requires);
    // (`box_deref_ops` is collected above, before the D14 install gate.)
    // Trust (TINV step 10.4): THE consumed generation runs inside the TINV recording
    // scope; the rows it conjoined use-site facts into are published for grading.
    // Trust (T3 C05): it also returns the overflow-check origins of its own rows, the
    // elision seam's join table (`OverflowCheckOrigins::from_generation`).
    // Trust (T3 L4, C07): THE body's context (L4-CTX-1). The generation interns every row
    // it returns into it through the row oracle, and the consumed path below carries each
    // solver row with its id (`BodyRows`) until the context is frozen into the artifacts.
    // The eager rows stay authoritative: the oracle substitutes the eager row on any
    // disagreement, and every consumer below reads the eager rows.
    let mut body_ctx = trust_types::vc_context::VcContext::new();
    // Trust (SAFETY-ASSUME-1): and inside the SAFETY-comment declaration scope, which
    // reports every site whose stand-in (or `[unsafe:reborrow]`) row it withheld.
    let safety_vcgen_ops = safety_declaration.take_vcgen_ops();
    // Trust (2026-10-18, gate RCA "premise"): the premise-free generation below runs
    // under the same declared operations, so it differs from this one ONLY by the
    // premise (`premise_free_generation`).
    let premise_free_safety_ops = premise_installed.then(|| safety_vcgen_ops.clone());
    let ((generation, tinv_record), safety_withheld) =
        trust_vcgen::with_safety_declared_unsafe_ops(&func, safety_vcgen_ops, || {
            trust_vcgen::type_invariant::with_tinv_assumed_rows_scope(&func, || {
                trust_vcgen::generate_vcs_with_discharge_and_overflow_origins(
                    &func,
                    vc_summaries,
                    hardened_verification_enabled(tcx.sess),
                    &vcgen_context,
                    &box_deref_ops,
                    &mut body_ctx,
                )
            })
        });
    let trust_vcgen::ConsumedGeneration {
        solver_vcs,
        solver_rows: solver_ids,
        discharged,
        // The preclassified rows are interned (they are the body's rows) and consumed as
        // their eager VCs; nothing on this path moves them, so no carrier pairs them. Their
        // ids name them only in the origin table below.
        discharged_rows: discharged_ids,
        overflow_origins,
    } = generation;
    safety_declaration.record_vcgen_withheld(body, &safety_withheld);
    // Trust (TINV step 10.4, review fold-in 2): the record stays a LOCAL of this
    // verification and travels with `solver_vcs` to the grading (no session map keyed by
    // a rendered path, which is not injective and could be overwritten by a nested
    // generation). Premised-ness is recorded at once by `DefId` (an over-approximation
    // across instances of one item: fail closed) for the elision/registry exclusions.
    if tinv_record.carries_premise() {
        // No authority exists yet: the consumer list is recorded by the authority phase.
        record_tinv_premised_verdict(tcx.sess, body.source.def_id(), &func.def_path, &[]);
    }
    if preprocessing_budget_exceeded(tcx, body, &def_path, "VC generation", policy.fail_closed()) {
        // Advisory: DISCARD the partially generated `solver_vcs` — a truncated
        // set must not pose as this function's complete obligation inventory —
        // and return the single honest budget-`Unknown` row instead.
        return if policy.fail_closed() {
            None
        } else {
            Some(preprocessing_budget_artifacts(tcx, body, &def_path, "VC generation"))
        };
    }
    // Trust (T3 C05, design §9.1): the proven-overflow-check elision seam's join table,
    // keyed by the exact identity of the rows this generation emitted, before the dedupe
    // below can merge two blocks' rows into one. It is built from the generation's own
    // rows and origins, so the overflow-origin replay (a second generation under a
    // fresh step budget) is gone. A generation that cannot show its enumeration
    // complete gives an empty table: every `resolve` misses and every check is kept.
    // Trust (T3 L4, C07): the table names its rows by id, and keys each by its eager row
    // (`OverflowCheckOrigins::from_rows`), looked up here by that id.
    let overflow_check_origins = {
        let eager: FxHashMap<trust_types::vc_context::RowId, &VerificationCondition> = solver_ids
            .iter()
            .zip(&solver_vcs)
            .chain(discharged_ids.iter().zip(discharged.iter().map(|(vc, _)| vc)))
            .filter_map(|(&id, vc)| id.map(|id| (id, vc)))
            .collect();
        OverflowCheckOrigins::from_generation(tcx, body, &body_ctx, &eager, &overflow_origins)
    };
    drop(overflow_origins);
    // Trust (T3 L4, C07): from here to the artifacts every solver row moves with its id.
    let mut solver_rows = BodyRows::from_generation(&mut body_ctx, solver_vcs, solver_ids);
    solver_rows.filter(|vc| trust_vcgen::vc_within_level(vc, max_level));
    dedupe_exact_vcs(&mut solver_rows);
    // Trust #spine: VERDICT-IDENTICAL trust-ir verdict flip. A row it regenerates joins the
    // body's context through the row oracle.
    let spine_unchecked_obligations = solver_rows
        .flip(&mut body_ctx, |solver_vcs| apply_spine_verdict_flip(&func, solver_vcs, &discharged));
    // Trust #spine (R-L1): VERDICT-IDENTICAL trust-ir L1 CONTRACT verdict flip.
    solver_rows.flip(&mut body_ctx, |solver_vcs| {
        (apply_spine_contract_verdict_flip(&func, spine_module.as_ref(), solver_vcs), ())
    });
    // Trust (VEC-INSERT, 2026-09-25): narrow the recorded charged `Vec` index-mutator
    // blocks to those whose charge row is actually PRESENT in this root's generated
    // inventory (dispatched or discharged), matched by kind, span, function and
    // recorded obligation body. The prediction recorded above serves only the
    // pre-generation summary walk, whose verdicts the tolerance ANDs with the ledger
    // this narrowed record feeds; a block whose row a later stage dropped therefore
    // never licenses the call's vanilla verdict.
    {
        let names_root = |vc: &VerificationCondition| {
            vc.function.as_str() == func.def_path || vc.function.as_str() == func.name
        };
        let present = trust_vcgen::vec_mutator_charge_rows(&func)
            .into_iter()
            .filter(|(_, span, row_body)| {
                solver_rows.vcs().iter().chain(discharged.iter().map(|(vc, _)| vc)).any(|vc| {
                    matches!(vc.kind, VcKind::SliceBoundsCheck)
                        && names_root(vc)
                        && vc.location == *span
                        && vc.obligation.as_ref().is_some_and(|record| record.body == *row_body)
                })
            })
            .map(|(block, _, _)| block)
            .collect::<Vec<_>>();
        with_crate_verification_state(tcx.sess, |state| {
            state.vec_mutator_charged_blocks.insert(func.def_path.clone(), present);
        });
    }
    // Trust (2026-10-17, gate RCA "premise"): an installed argument premise is graded
    // per row against this body's PREMISE-FREE generation (`argument_premise` module
    // doc, # Grading), and a row whose VC the premise changed but whose premise-free
    // twin the interval lane proves dispatches that twin instead
    // (`argument_premise::adopt_premise_free_twins`). A body with no premise never
    // pays for the second generation.
    let premise_free = if let Some(safety_ops) = premise_free_safety_ops {
        argument_premise::PremiseFreeRows::new(premise_free_generation(
            tcx,
            &func,
            vc_summaries,
            &vcgen_context,
            &box_deref_ops,
            safety_ops,
            max_level,
            spine_module.as_ref(),
            &mut body_ctx,
        ))
    } else {
        argument_premise::PremiseFreeRows::unavailable()
    };
    if premise_installed {
        let interval = trust_router::Router::with_backends(vec![Box::new(
            trust_router::interval_backend::IntervalBackend,
        )]);
        let decided = |twin: &VerificationCondition| {
            !function_deadline.is_some_and(|at| std::time::Instant::now() >= at)
                && interval
                    .verify_all_with_deadline(std::slice::from_ref(twin), function_deadline)
                    .first()
                    .is_some_and(|(_, result)| matches!(result, VerificationResult::Proved { .. }))
        };
        // Trust (T3 L4, C07): the adoption rewrites the carrier as a whole list: a row it
        // leaves where it was keeps its id, and an adopted twin joins the body's context
        // through the row oracle (`BodyRows::flip`).
        let adopted = solver_rows.flip(&mut body_ctx, |mut vcs| {
            let adopted =
                argument_premise::adopt_premise_free_twins(&mut vcs, &premise_free, decided);
            (vcs, adopted)
        });
        debug!(
            "Trust: argument premise of {}: {adopted} row(s) dispatch their premise-free twin",
            func.def_path,
        );
    }
    // COMPLETENESS: append fail-closed obligations for rustc-unsafe calls and
    // non-modeled unsafe ops that Trust does not already model, so no unsafe
    // construct silently escapes. These are appended AFTER the spine flips on
    // purpose: they are compiler-side fail-closed FINDINGS (always-SAT
    // `Assertion` violations), NOT spine-derivable L0 safety obligations, so
    // the native-gen flip (`generate_native_or_flip_safety_vcs`, which
    // regenerates L0 safety VCs from the MIR-derived spine and keeps only its
    // own regenerated set + the non-L0 `rest`) would classify them as L0 and
    // DROP them — the spine never re-derives an unsafe-call demand, so the
    // set-equality check fails and the finding vanishes. That silently
    // regressed `undocumented_unsafe_sig_call` from REFUTE to a false PROVE
    // (the flip's WARN "FALLBACK to built MIR … span stitching" is the tell).
    // Appending post-flip keeps the flip verdict-identical for the safety
    // obligations it owns while guaranteeing the unsafe findings reach the
    // router unmodified. SOUNDNESS: fail-closed — these VCs are always-SAT,
    // so appending them can only ADD a refutation, never remove one.
    // Trust (#57, 2026-08-17): FAIL-CLOSE the producer disagreement. Appended here
    // for the SAME reason the two unsafe sweeps below are: these are compiler-side
    // findings, not spine-derivable L0 safety obligations, so the native-gen flip
    // would classify them as L0 and drop them.
    // Trust (T3 L4, C07): each appended row joins the body's context through the row
    // oracle.
    solver_rows.extend(
        &mut body_ctx,
        synthetic_unchecked_obligation_vcs(&func.def_path, &spine_unchecked_obligations),
    );
    // Generated above, BEFORE VC generation, so the mint could be handed the row
    // set as evidence instead of predicting it. Appended here, in the same
    // position as before.
    solver_rows.extend(&mut body_ctx, unmodeled_unsafe_call_vcs);
    solver_rows.extend(
        &mut body_ctx,
        synthetic_nonmodeled_unsafe_op_vcs(tcx, body, &func.def_path, &mut safety_declaration),
    );
    // Trust (SAFETY-ASSUME-1): every row this generation will ever withhold is now
    // withheld; the charged blocks go to the session ledger BEFORE the artifacts are
    // collected, because the panic-free registration there reads it.
    safety_declaration.record(tcx.sess, body.source.def_id());
    solver_rows.extend(&mut body_ctx, unmodeled_thread_spawn_effect_vcs(tcx, body, &func.def_path));
    // Trust (D8-P0): a transmute that MANUFACTURES a Box/reference is charged
    // here, at its creation — the Box-parameter validity root trusts it.
    solver_rows.extend(
        &mut body_ctx,
        validity_transmute::validity_transmute_vcs(tcx, body, &func.def_path),
    );
    // Trust (O2-inline): an unsafe callee INLINED at `-O`, and a GVN-folded
    // invalid reference/Box constant, are charged where the optimizer left them.
    solver_rows.extend(
        &mut body_ctx,
        inlined_unsafe_evidence::inlined_unsafe_evidence_vcs(tcx, body, &func.def_path),
    );
    debug!(
        "Trust: native typed verification for {} mode=Full max_level={max_level:?}, solver_vcs={}, discharged={}",
        func.def_path,
        solver_rows.vcs().len(),
        discharged.len(),
    );
    // NOTE: the incremental proof cache is NOT populated here. A content-
    // addressed per-function key cannot soundly capture the whole-program /
    // interprocedural facts Trust verification consumes (R1 caller-coverage,
    // callee panic-freedom, backing certificates, callee return summaries, sealed
    // dyn-Trait postcondition covariance, `#[trust::single_writer]`/`backing`
    // hints, non-unwinding-ABI demotion, ...), so a warm rebuild could replay a
    // stale `Proved` for a function whose OWN bytes are unchanged — a false PROVE.
    // Re-enabling the store requires a whole-program-aware key OR a
    // "verification consumed a fact not in the key" tripwire, not a per-class
    // exclusion list (three independent audits each found a new discharge channel).
    // Trust (D9, review F4): `max_level` is the level the filter above applied;
    // the artifacts record it for the `termination-unmeasured` charge
    // (`VerificationArtifacts::max_proof_level`). It is a REQUIRED argument, not a
    // field a caller may forget to set: an unset level reads as "no termination
    // claim in scope" and would switch the charge off.
    // Trust (T3 L4, C07p): the consumed path has added its last row; the full lane's
    // producers (the E4/E5 regeneration, the refutation probes) write theirs into the same
    // context, which `collect_full_verification_artifacts` freezes into the artifacts it
    // selects (L4-CTX-1).
    let artifacts = collect_full_verification_artifacts(
        tcx,
        body,
        &func,
        compiler_contracts_for_verification,
        solver_rows.vcs(),
        &discharged,
        policy,
        // NESTED-CLOCK: moved by the verifications of other bodies forced since entry.
        trust_types::verify_budget::credited(function_deadline, function_clock_credit),
        &vcgen_context,
        &overflow_check_origins,
        max_level,
        &tinv_record,
        &premise_free,
        body_ctx,
    );
    Some(artifacts)
}

/// Trust (2026-10-17, gate RCA "premise"): this body's generation with NO argument
/// premise installed — the consumed generation's own inputs (`func`,
/// `vc_summaries`, `vcgen_context`, the box-deref nominations, the SAFETY-comment
/// declared operations (SAFETY-ASSUME-1; its withheld report is dropped, the ledger
/// is the consumed generation's), the TINV recording scope, the level filter, the
/// dedupe and both spine flips) with only the D15 and D95/D100 parameter lists
/// emptied. The payloads before and after the level filter
/// and the flips are all recorded: each is a VC the premise-free compiler emits for
/// this body. The list after the flips is kept as the twins
/// `argument_premise::adopt_premise_free_twins` may dispatch.
///
/// Runs under its OWN fresh budget, the shape the R1 harvest uses
/// (`verify_fn_deadline` and the session's step count): it never draws on the
/// function's step budget, so it cannot push the consumed generation's checkpoint
/// over. A trip in it answers `None` (no row is premise-free, every token is
/// demoted, no twin is adopted): a generation cut short is not the inventory the
/// grading compares against.
///
/// Trust (T3 L4, C07p): its rows join `rows`, the body's VC context, through the row oracle,
/// as every producer of the body's does, and its list runs through the consumed path's own
/// carrier and operations (`BodyRows`: the level filter, the dedupe, both flips). Each
/// payload is recorded as its `RowKey` (C09), so the grading's lookups compare the
/// canonical strings inside a key, and the table is counted against the identity oracle.
fn premise_free_generation(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'_>,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    vc_summaries: &trust_vcgen::SummaryDatabase,
    vcgen_context: &trust_vcgen::VcgenContext,
    box_deref_ops: &FxHashSet<(trust_types::BlockId, usize)>,
    safety_ops: trust_vcgen::SafetyDeclaredUnsafeOps,
    max_level: trust_types::ProofLevel,
    spine_module: Option<&trust_ir::Module>,
    rows: &mut trust_types::vc_context::VcContext,
) -> Option<argument_premise::PremiseFreeGeneration> {
    let started = std::time::Instant::now();
    let context = vcgen_context
        .clone()
        .with_noalias_mut_params(Vec::new())
        .with_reference_box_root_params(Vec::new());
    let _budget = trust_types::verify_budget::VerifyBudgetGuard::install(
        verify_fn_deadline(tcx.sess),
        tcx.sess.opts.unstable_opts.trust_verify_function_budget_steps,
    );
    let ((generation, _tinv_record), _safety_withheld) =
        trust_vcgen::with_safety_declared_unsafe_ops(func, safety_ops, || {
            trust_vcgen::type_invariant::with_tinv_assumed_rows_scope(func, || {
                trust_vcgen::generate_vcs_with_discharge_and_overflow_origins(
                    func,
                    vc_summaries,
                    hardened_verification_enabled(tcx.sess),
                    &context,
                    box_deref_ops,
                    rows,
                )
            })
        });
    if trust_types::verify_budget::exhausted_readonly() {
        debug!(
            "Trust: premise-free generation of {} tripped its budget after {} ms; every kernel \
             token of the body is demoted",
            func.def_path,
            started.elapsed().as_millis(),
        );
        return None;
    }
    let trust_vcgen::ConsumedGeneration { solver_vcs, solver_rows, discharged, .. } = generation;
    let mut keys = Vec::new();
    let mut record = |vc: &VerificationCondition| {
        if let Some(key) = exact_vc_key(vc) {
            keys.push(key.canonical_payload);
        }
    };
    solver_vcs.iter().chain(discharged.iter().map(|(vc, _)| vc)).for_each(&mut record);
    let mut solver_rows = BodyRows::from_generation(rows, solver_vcs, solver_rows);
    solver_rows.filter(|vc| trust_vcgen::vc_within_level(vc, max_level));
    dedupe_exact_vcs(&mut solver_rows);
    let _unchecked = solver_rows.flip(rows, |vcs| apply_spine_verdict_flip(func, vcs, &discharged));
    solver_rows.flip(rows, |vcs| (apply_spine_contract_verdict_flip(func, spine_module, vcs), ()));
    solver_rows.vcs().iter().for_each(&mut record);
    // Trust (T3 L4, C09): a hashed table counts the keys it was built from, in order
    // (`row_key::count_partition`).
    row_key::count_partition(&keys);
    let payloads: FxHashSet<RowKey> = keys.into_iter().collect();
    debug!(
        "Trust: premise-free generation of {}: {} VC payload(s) in {} ms",
        func.def_path,
        payloads.len(),
        started.elapsed().as_millis(),
    );
    Some(argument_premise::PremiseFreeGeneration {
        payloads,
        solver_vcs: solver_rows.vcs().to_vec(),
    })
}

/// Trust #spine: apply the trust-ir VERDICT FLIP to a function's trust-vcgen VC
/// set, making the MIR-derived Trust-IR compatibility spine the provenance source
/// for the L0 safety obligations it can independently derive (overflow / bounds /
/// shift / div-by-zero). The flip is
/// VERDICT-IDENTICAL by construction (see `trust_ir_bridge::flip`): it re-anchors
/// an obligation's provenance to the spine and, when the spine reconstructs a
/// formula byte-equal to trust-vcgen's, dispatches the spine's formula instead —
/// keeping `kind`, `location`, and `contract_metadata` byte-identical, so the
/// router/report verdict and label are unchanged. A non-byte-equal class keeps
/// trust-vcgen's formula (fail-closed). Routing/label can never change because
/// the `VcKind` is preserved (the prior reverted flip's regression — a
/// `hardened_unsafe_operation` mislabel + a partial formula that masked a real
/// failure — is structurally impossible here).
///
/// THE obligation-birth cutover: prefer trust-ir-NATIVE generation of the L0 safety
/// VC set, falling back to the verdict-flip over trust-vcgen's VCs.
///
/// When the spine generates the complete L0 safety VC set AND it reproduces
/// trust-vcgen's L0 set exactly (cross-checked), the spine-generated VCs are
/// dispatched — trust-ir is then the PRIMARY generation source for this function's
/// safety obligations (`generate_native_safety_vcs`), with trust-vcgen serving only
/// as the migration cross-check oracle. Otherwise the verdict-flip runs (which
/// spine-sources the formulas it can). This is sound by construction: the native set
/// is used only on an exact match, so dispatched verdicts are identical either way.
/// Trust (#57, 2026-08-17): the obligations the trust-ir spine derived that
/// trust-vcgen neither dispatched nor discharged, returned so the caller can
/// fail-close on them. Empty on every other path.
fn apply_spine_verdict_flip(
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    solver_vcs: Vec<VerificationCondition>,
    // Trust (#57): the obligations trust-vcgen PROVED at generation time. They are
    // checked — more strongly than the dispatched ones — so the producer
    // comparison below must count them as present. Passing `&[]` here would make
    // the alarm claim "nothing verifies this" about obligations trust-vcgen
    // discharged (measured: `bounded_add`, `inc_gt`, `helper`).
    discharged: &[(VerificationCondition, trust_types::VerificationResult)],
) -> (Vec<VerificationCondition>, Vec<trust_ir_bridge::ObligationKey>) {
    let mut unchecked = Vec::new();
    let (dispatched, outcome) = trust_ir_bridge::generate_native_or_flip_safety_vcs_with_discharged(
        func,
        solver_vcs,
        discharged.iter().map(|(vc, _)| vc),
    );
    match outcome {
        trust_ir_bridge::NativeGenOutcome::NativeGenerated(n) => {
            debug!(
                "Trust-IR compatibility generation for {}: {n} L0 safety VC(s) GENERATED from \
                 the MIR-derived spine (validated == trust-vcgen) — the spine is the VC source, \
                 not the program source frontend",
                func.def_path,
            );
        }
        trust_ir_bridge::NativeGenOutcome::FlippedFallback(report) => {
            debug!(
                "Trust-IR spine verdict-flip for {} (native-gen declined): {} spine-sourced \
                 (formula), {} spine-sourced (vcgen kept), {} trust-vcgen-sourced",
                func.def_path,
                report.spine_sourced_formula(),
                report.spine_sourced_kept_formula(),
                report.trust_vcgen_sourced(),
            );
        }
        // Trust (#57, 2026-08-17) — THE MISSING-OBLIGATION ALARM. The two
        // independent producers disagree about WHICH obligations exist. Dispatch is
        // unchanged (this is the same fallback path), but the disagreement is no
        // longer discarded.
        //
        // `native_only` is the direction that matters: the dispatched set is
        // trust-vcgen's, so an obligation only the spine derived — and that
        // trust-vcgen neither emitted nor discharged — is a proposition NOTHING
        // checks. It cannot surface anywhere downstream, because an obligation that
        // is never emitted produces silence, and silence reads as success even to
        // the kernel.
        //
        // Reported rather than fail-closed, and that is measured rather than
        // timid: across all 2326 committed dumps the surviving population is 31,
        // every one of them the spine over-generating a trivially-true
        // constant-divisor check (`n % 2`), which is not an unchecked obligation.
        // Failing the build on those would reject valid Rust — a release gate. The
        // gate that CAN be sharp lives where the population is measurable:
        // `flip::missing_obligation_ratchet`, which goes red on any obligation
        // class outside that known-benign one. Fail-closing here becomes correct
        // once the spine stops emitting them; the ratchet is what keeps the number
        // from drifting up in the meantime.
        trust_ir_bridge::NativeGenOutcome::Disagreed { diff, report } => {
            if !diff.native_only.is_empty() {
                info!(
                    "Trust-IR PRODUCER DISAGREEMENT for {}: {} obligation(s) derived by the \
                     trust-ir spine that trust-vcgen neither emitted nor discharged — nothing \
                     checks these: {:?}",
                    func.def_path,
                    diff.native_only.len(),
                    diff.native_only,
                );
            }
            debug!(
                "Trust-IR spine verdict-flip for {} (producers disagree): {} spine-only, {} \
                 vcgen-only (spine coverage gap); {} spine-sourced (formula), {} \
                 trust-vcgen-sourced",
                func.def_path,
                diff.native_only.len(),
                diff.vcgen_only.len(),
                report.spine_sourced_formula(),
                report.trust_vcgen_sourced(),
            );
            unchecked = diff.native_only;
        }
    }
    (dispatched, unchecked)
}

/// Trust (#57, 2026-08-17) — FAIL-CLOSED findings for obligations the trust-ir
/// spine derived and NOTHING checks.
///
/// An obligation trust-vcgen never emitted is not dispatched, and one it never
/// discharged is not proved — so it produces SILENCE, which is indistinguishable
/// from success at every layer below, including the Clean kernel (a kernel can only
/// re-check propositions it is handed). This turns that silence into a finding.
///
/// SOUNDNESS: identical in shape and argument to
/// [`synthetic_unmodeled_unsafe_call_vcs`] — an always-SAT `Assertion`, appended
/// after the spine flips. It can only ADD a refutation, never remove one, so it
/// cannot make a previously-refuted function pass.
///
/// WHY THIS IS SAFE TO TURN ON NOW, and why it was not before: measured across all
/// 2326 committed dumps, this list is EMPTY for every function
/// (`trust_ir_bridge::flip::missing_obligation_ratchet`, a hard zero-gate). Before
/// the shared static-truth fold landed it held 31 entries, every one of them the
/// spine over-generating a trivially-true constant-divisor check — failing closed
/// on those would have rejected valid Rust, which is a release gate. The fold
/// removed them at the source rather than whitelisting them here.
///
/// The message follows principle 10 / v7-D3: a refusal must carry the fix. It names
/// the obligation class, the span, and the two concrete resolutions — because the
/// finding does not say a violation exists, it says nobody looked.
fn synthetic_unchecked_obligation_vcs(
    def_path: &str,
    unchecked: &[trust_ir_bridge::ObligationKey],
) -> Vec<VerificationCondition> {
    unchecked
        .iter()
        .map(|key| VerificationCondition {
            kind: VcKind::Assertion { message: key.unchecked_finding_message() },
            function: trust_types::Symbol::intern(def_path),
            location: key.location.clone(),
            // Always SAT = always a finding: nothing checked this obligation.
            formula: trust_types::Formula::Bool(true),
            contract_metadata: None,
            // Not a contract-derived VC: no obligation to back-reference.
            obligation: None,
        })
        .collect()
}

/// Trust #spine (R-L1): apply the trust-ir L1 CONTRACT verdict-flip to a function's
/// VC set, making the MIR-derived Trust-IR compatibility spine the provenance
/// source for the L1 contract obligations it can independently derive
/// (precondition / refinement / loop-invariant). This is
/// the L1 analogue of [`apply_spine_verdict_flip`] and is VERDICT-IDENTICAL by the
/// SAME construction (`trust_ir_bridge::flip_contract_verdicts_*`): it re-anchors a
/// contract obligation's provenance to the spine's matching module-level contract
/// obligation (`contract_vcs_from_trust_ir`) and, when the spine reconstructs the
/// dispatched-equivalent contract violation formula byte-equal to trust-vcgen's,
/// dispatches the spine's formula — keeping `kind`, `location`, and
/// `contract_metadata` byte-identical, so the router/report verdict and label are
/// unchanged. A non-byte-equal contract class keeps trust-vcgen's formula
/// (fail-closed). The `Postcondition` body-aware formula is already handled by the
/// L0 flip's dedicated arm and is deliberately excluded here (no double-sourcing).
///
/// Runs AFTER [`apply_spine_verdict_flip`] (the L0 flip), so it operates over the
/// already-L0-re-anchored VC set; the two flips touch disjoint VC classes (L0
/// safety vs. L1 contract), so neither perturbs the other.
///
/// `spine_module` is the SINGLE trust-ir lowering computed once on the live path
/// (the shadow block above). When present the flip reuses it (no second lowering);
/// when `None` (the function did not lower) the flip is a no-op and every contract
/// VC stays trust-vcgen-sourced (fail-closed).
fn apply_spine_contract_verdict_flip(
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    spine_module: Option<&trust_ir::Module>,
    solver_vcs: Vec<VerificationCondition>,
) -> Vec<VerificationCondition> {
    // Reuse the already-computed spine module (no double-lowering). If the function
    // did not lower, fall back to the self-lowering entry point — which itself
    // no-ops on a lowering failure (every contract VC stays trust-vcgen-sourced).
    let (flipped, report) = match spine_module {
        Some(module) => trust_ir_bridge::flip_contract_verdicts_with_module(module, solver_vcs),
        None => trust_ir_bridge::flip_contract_verdicts_to_spine(func, solver_vcs),
    };
    // Log whenever the function carried any L1 contract VC (a decision was made),
    // mirroring the L0 flip's unconditional decision log — this is the observable
    // L1 sourcing record.
    if !report.decisions.is_empty() {
        debug!(
            "Trust-IR L1 contract verdict-flip for {}: {} contract VC(s) spine-sourced (formula), \
             {} spine-sourced (vcgen formula kept), {} trust-vcgen-sourced",
            func.def_path,
            report.spine_sourced_formula(),
            report.spine_sourced_kept_formula(),
            report.trust_vcgen_sourced(),
        );
    }
    flipped
}

/// The ordinary writer and deferred R40 candidate share the same premise bar.
/// Call it before acquiring the crate-state lock; the census reads that lock.
fn panic_free_registration_refusal(tcx: TyCtxt<'_>, def_id: DefId) -> Option<&'static str> {
    if type_invariant::body_carries_premise(tcx, def_id) {
        return Some("its rows carry a type-invariant premise");
    }
    verdict_rests_on_user_axiom(tcx.sess, def_id)
}

/// Record that `def_id` is proven fully panic-free for all inputs, retaining
/// `def_path` only in the non-authoritative display-name mirror. Session state
/// is required both for parallel worker visibility and to prevent a later
/// rustc_driver crate from inheriting the proof.
///
/// Trust (assumption-root gate): the registry is SPENT interprocedurally —
/// `all_calls_target_proven_panic_free` discharges any caller in this crate whose
/// every ordinary call targets an exact registered identity — so an entry here
/// is a PREMISE, not a report. The display name recorded alongside that identity
/// is never authority. A verdict that rests on a user axiom may therefore not be
/// registered: through this channel the caller's bundle contains no trace of the
/// axiom (the callee is not bundled — that is why the interprocedural route
/// fired), so the axiom would VANISH exactly as it does through the
/// `dyn`/`write_fmt` reductions. The refusal is at the PRODUCER because the
/// registry has several consumers and one writer; the body's OWN verdict is
/// untouched.
///
/// Takes `tcx` rather than `sess` so the SAME call that publishes the rendered
/// key also publishes the `DefId`+substs one
/// (`CrateVerificationState::panic_free_proven_identities`). One writer, one
/// gate, two views of one fact. Deferred R40 reuses the same identity and premise
/// boundary after establishing the same call gate by a least fixed point.
fn register_proven_panic_free(tcx: TyCtxt<'_>, def_id: DefId, def_path: &str) {
    let sess = tcx.sess;
    if let Some(reason) = panic_free_registration_refusal(tcx, def_id) {
        debug!("Trust: NOT registering {def_path} as proven panic-free: {reason}");
        return;
    }
    // The identity view. `None` for a type/const-generic item — see
    // `trust_mir_extract::item_identity` — and an absent identity simply means no
    // identity-keyed discharge is available for it, exactly as before this field
    // existed. It is NEVER back-filled from `def_path`.
    let identity = trust_mir_extract::item_identity(tcx, def_id);
    with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        state.panic_free_proven_funcs.insert(def_path.to_string());
        if let Some(identity) = identity {
            state.panic_free_proven_identities.insert(identity);
        }
    });
}

/// R43 — is `body`'s panic-freedom a property of its CONTROL-FLOW SHAPE alone,
/// and therefore identical for every monomorphization?
///
/// THE PROBLEM THIS ANSWERS. The verifier verifies ONE body per function, the
/// PRE-monomorphization one, and registers it under the argument-free
/// `safe_def_path_str`. Call sites are keyed by `func_operand_name`, which carries
/// the instantiated arguments. For a generic callee those keys never meet, and
/// `collect_proven_total_panic_free_callees` deliberately fails closed rather than
/// strip the turbofish — because "the generic body verified" does NOT in general
/// imply "every instantiation is panic-free". This predicate names the narrow case
/// where it DOES, and it is decided from the MIR, never from the verdict.
///
/// THE ALLOWLIST IS THE PROOF. Every way a Rust MIR frame can panic is a
/// TERMINATOR:
///   * `Call` / `TailCall` — the callee may panic (and for a generic body the
///     RESOLVED callee is instantiation-dependent, which is the whole hazard);
///   * `Drop` — runs `Drop::drop`, i.e. arbitrary user code chosen by the type
///     argument. THE dominant instantiation-dependent panic and the one a naive
///     key relaxation would admit;
///   * `Assert` — the bounds/overflow/division checks;
///   * `InlineAsm` — arbitrary;
///   * `UnwindTerminate` — the abort-on-double-panic shim;
///   * `Yield` / `CoroutineDrop` — coroutine resume protocol.
/// STATEMENTS cannot panic: arithmetic overflow, indexing and division are all
/// lowered to `Assert` terminators before this point, and a post-monomorphization
/// const-eval failure is a COMPILE error, not a runtime panic. So a body built
/// only from `Goto`, `SwitchInt` and `Return` has no panic-capable construct for
/// ANY instantiation — moves, field projections, casts, aggregate builds and
/// branches, and nothing else.
///
/// This is an ALLOWLIST, not a denylist: a terminator this function has not
/// reasoned about (including a future variant) declines. `UnwindResume` is
/// deliberately NOT allowed even though it cannot itself panic — its presence
/// means the body was built with a cleanup path, which is evidence the shape is
/// richer than the one reasoned about here, and refusing costs only completeness.
///
/// MEASURED (deployed driver `~/.trust-tc-recon`, 2026-08-22, `-Zdump-mir`), the
/// target shape `fn err_barrier<E>(e: E) -> E { e }` is exactly
///
/// ```text
///     fn f1_barrier(_1: E) -> E {
///         bb0: { _0 = move _1; return; }
///     }
/// ```
///
/// ADVERSARIAL CONTROLS, all measured on the same driver with
/// `TRUST_PF_REG_TRACE=1`: `fn sink<E>(_e: E) {}` (drops `E`), `fn assign<E>(x:
/// &mut E, y: E) { *x = y }` (drops `*x` implicitly) and `fn tuple<E>(x: (E, E))
/// -> E { x.0 }` (drops `x.1`) each reported `established=false nrows=1` — the
/// extractor already charges a row for a generic drop, so those never reach the
/// registry in the first place. `fn max<E: Ord>(a: E, b: E) -> E { a.max(b) }`
/// (false proof #14's shape) reported `arm=NONE … callee=<E as
/// core::cmp::Ord>::max`. This predicate is a SECOND, independent lock on the same
/// hazard: it does not rely on that row ever having been emitted.
///
/// Trust (2026-10-09, sweep m15): a SECOND admitted shape. The body may also contain
/// `Assert` and `Call` terminators when NOTHING in it depends on a generic
/// parameter: no local whose type mentions one is ever used (the receiver
/// `self: &Self` of a default method is a declared argument that is never read),
/// no type or constant in any statement or terminator mentions one, and every call
/// has parameter-free generic arguments and resolves to one fixed `Item` body.
/// Then every assert condition and every callee is the same for every
/// instantiation, and the generic verification that established this body's
/// panic freedom covers them all. `Drop`, `InlineAsm`, `Yield` and every other
/// terminator still decline. Measured: `fn head(&self, a: &[u8]) -> u8 { if
/// a.is_empty() { 0 } else { a[0] } }` (a call and a bounds assert, no `Self` use).
fn body_is_parametrically_panic_free<'tcx>(tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>, body: &Body<'tcx>) -> bool {
    body_is_control_flow_only(body) || body_is_parameter_independent(tcx, body)
}

/// The first shape of [`body_is_parametrically_panic_free`]: an ALLOWLIST over
/// terminators (`Goto`, `SwitchInt`, `Return`), needing no `TyCtxt`, so R43 pins it
/// directly.
fn body_is_control_flow_only(body: &Body<'_>) -> bool {
    body.basic_blocks.iter().all(|block| {
        matches!(
            block.terminator().kind,
            TerminatorKind::Goto { .. } | TerminatorKind::SwitchInt { .. } | TerminatorKind::Return
        )
    })
}

/// See the second shape of [`body_is_parametrically_panic_free`].
fn body_is_parameter_independent<'tcx>(tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>, body: &Body<'tcx>) -> bool {
    use rustc_middle::mir::visit::{PlaceContext, Visitor};
    use rustc_middle::mir::{ConstOperand, Location};
    struct Scan<'a, 'tcx> {
        body: &'a Body<'tcx>,
        param_dependent: bool,
    }
    impl<'tcx> Visitor<'tcx> for Scan<'_, 'tcx> {
        fn visit_local(&mut self, local: Local, _context: PlaceContext, _location: Location) {
            if self.body.local_decls[local].ty.has_non_region_param() {
                self.param_dependent = true;
            }
        }
        fn visit_ty(&mut self, ty: Ty<'tcx>, _context: rustc_middle::mir::visit::TyContext) {
            if ty.has_non_region_param() {
                self.param_dependent = true;
            }
        }
        fn visit_const_operand(&mut self, constant: &ConstOperand<'tcx>, location: Location) {
            if constant.const_.ty().has_non_region_param() || constant.const_.has_non_region_param()
            {
                self.param_dependent = true;
            }
            self.super_const_operand(constant, location);
        }
    }
    let root = body.source.def_id();
    let typing_env = ty::TypingEnv::post_analysis(tcx, root);
    let mut scan = Scan { body, param_dependent: false };
    for (bb, data) in body.basic_blocks.iter_enumerated() {
        match &data.terminator().kind {
            TerminatorKind::Goto { .. }
            | TerminatorKind::SwitchInt { .. }
            | TerminatorKind::Return
            | TerminatorKind::Assert { .. } => {}
            TerminatorKind::Call { func, .. } => {
                let Some((callee, args)) = func.const_fn_def() else { return false };
                if args.has_non_region_param()
                    || !matches!(
                        ty::Instance::try_resolve(tcx, typing_env, callee, args),
                        Ok(Some(instance)) if matches!(instance.def, ty::InstanceKind::Item(_))
                    )
                {
                    return false;
                }
            }
            _ => return false,
        }
        scan.visit_basic_block_data(bb, data);
        if scan.param_dependent {
            return false;
        }
    }
    true
}

/// R43 — record that `def_path`'s panic-freedom is generic-parameter-independent.
/// ONLY ever called immediately after `register_proven_panic_free` for the same
/// `def_path`, so this set is by construction a subset of the unconditional
/// registry. See `CrateVerificationState::parametric_panic_free_funcs`.
fn register_parametric_panic_free(sess: &Session, def_path: &str) {
    with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        state.parametric_panic_free_funcs.insert(def_path.to_string());
    });
}

/// Trust (Part B, N1): whether `func` declares a USER-WRITTEN `#[trust::requires]`
/// contract. `VerifiableFunction::preconditions` ALSO carries post-gate SYNTHETIC
/// type-range tautologies (the integer/char parameter range facts trust-mir-extract
/// attaches after the assumption gate — `integer_param_range_preconditions` /
/// `char_range_preconditions`), which are NOT entry assumptions: they hold for
/// every well-typed value, impose nothing on callers, and their presence must not
/// re-route a plain function out of the UNCONDITIONAL panic-free registry/tag.
/// The declared-contract list is the authoritative discriminator.
fn has_declared_requires_contract(contracts: &[trust_types::Contract]) -> bool {
    contracts.iter().any(|c| matches!(c.kind, trust_types::ContractKind::Requires))
}

/// Trust (Part B): record that `summary.name` (a `#[trust::requires(P)]`
/// function) is proven panic-free UNDER its entry precondition `P`. The stored
/// summary carries `P` plus the param names/types needed to substitute a
/// caller's ACTUAL arguments when that caller must PROVE `P[args]` at a call
/// site (Path-2b). Kept SEPARATE from `panic_free_proven_funcs`, so it can never
/// discharge a call by assumption — only by a per-call-site proof of `P`.
fn register_panic_free_under_precondition(sess: &Session, summary: trust_vcgen::FunctionSummary) {
    if trust_env_var_present("TRUST_PF_REG_TRACE") {
        eprintln!(
            "[PF_REG] fn={} registered UNDER_PRECONDITION (nP={})",
            summary.name,
            summary.preconditions.len()
        );
    }
    with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        state.panic_free_under_precondition.insert(summary.name.clone(), summary);
    });
}

fn display_obligation_kind_key(kind: &VcKind) -> String {
    // Stable display/coalescing key only. It carries no proof authority;
    // transport evidence uses the retained exact public/native row identity.
    // Normalize every hardened category before the Assertion fallthrough so
    // duplicate presentation rows remain consistently grouped.
    if let Some(category) = kind.hardened_category() {
        return format!(
            "HardenedBoundary:{}:{}",
            category.as_tag(),
            hardened_display_key_callee(kind, category)
        );
    }
    match kind {
        VcKind::Assertion { .. } => "Assertion".to_string(),
        VcKind::Precondition { .. } => "Precondition".to_string(),
        VcKind::HardenedBoundary { category, callee, .. } => {
            format!("HardenedBoundary:{}:{callee}", category.as_tag())
        }
        VcKind::UnsupportedMir { kind, detail } if kind.starts_with("FullVerification::") => {
            format!("{kind}:{detail}")
        }
        VcKind::UnsupportedMir { kind, .. } => kind.clone(),
        // Trust: stable machine key for the leak/OOM obligation, so a survey can
        // filter allocation-boundedness results instead of getting the verbose
        // `{:?}` debug fallthrough.
        VcKind::UnboundedAllocation { .. } => "unbounded_allocation".to_string(),
        _ => format!("{kind:?}"),
    }
}

/// Trust: the callee component of the presentation-only hardened display key.
/// This mirrors trust-mir-extract for stable reports, but is never used to bind
/// proof evidence.
fn hardened_display_key_callee<'a>(
    kind: &'a VcKind,
    category: trust_types::HardenedVcCategory,
) -> &'a str {
    match kind {
        VcKind::HardenedBoundary { callee, .. } => callee,
        VcKind::Assertion { message } if message.starts_with("[unsafe:ffi]") => {
            "unsafe_ffi_assertion"
        }
        VcKind::Assertion { message } if message.starts_with("[unsafe") => "unsafe_assertion",
        VcKind::UnsafeOperation { .. } => "unsafe_operation",
        VcKind::FfiBoundaryViolation { callee, .. } => callee,
        _ => category.as_tag(),
    }
}

/// Diagnostic-only: a stable, human-readable name for a proof-authority variant.
/// Used by the `TRUST_PF_REG_TRACE` registration probe and by the pending
/// source-clause marker explanation, which must tell a user WHICH authority a
/// body row carried. No behavior: nothing reads this name back.
fn pf_authority_name(a: &ResultProofAuthority) -> &'static str {
    match a {
        ResultProofAuthority::KernelCertified { .. } => "KernelCertified",
        ResultProofAuthority::DirectTrustVcLive { .. } => "DirectTrustVcLive",
        ResultProofAuthority::FreshExactDirectChcPdr { .. } => "FreshExactDirectChcPdr",
        ResultProofAuthority::FreshWholeFunctionChcPdr { .. } => "FreshWholeFunctionChcPdr",
        ResultProofAuthority::ExactSourceClauseDischarge { .. } => "ExactSourceClauseDischarge",
        ResultProofAuthority::NativeWpSource { .. } => "NativeWpSource",
        ResultProofAuthority::DefinitionEntryAssumption { .. } => "DefinitionEntryAssumption",
        ResultProofAuthority::AssumedTotalCalleeAssumption { .. } => "AssumedTotalCalleeAssumption",
        ResultProofAuthority::EnsuresCitationDischarge { .. } => "EnsuresCitationDischarge",
        ResultProofAuthority::EnsuresCitationViewDischarge { .. } => "EnsuresCitationViewDischarge",
        ResultProofAuthority::EnsuresCitationUnderRequiresDischarge { .. } => {
            "EnsuresCitationUnderRequiresDischarge"
        }
        ResultProofAuthority::SolverRevalidated { .. } => "SolverRevalidated",
        ResultProofAuthority::CallerPropagation { .. } => "CallerPropagation",
        ResultProofAuthority::InterproceduralCalleeClosure { .. } => "InterproceduralCalleeClosure",
        ResultProofAuthority::TypeInvariantComposed { .. } => "TypeInvariantComposed",
        ResultProofAuthority::CalculusRowProved { .. } => "CalculusRowProved",
        ResultProofAuthority::GroundedPanicFreedomDerivation { .. } => {
            "GroundedPanicFreedomDerivation"
        }
        ResultProofAuthority::ArgumentPremiseKernelDischarge { .. } => {
            "ArgumentPremiseKernelDischarge"
        }
    }
}

/// Trust (assumption-root gate): record whether ONE verification of `def_id` was
/// established free of user-written axioms.
///
/// Written from the single place the user-axiom census is taken — the bundle
/// closure in [`build_native_trust_ir_bundle_for_verifier_api`], over the ROOT
/// BODY AND EVERY BUNDLED MEMBER — so it cannot drift from the sets the bridge is
/// actually handed. Conjoined, never overwritten.
fn record_verdict_axiom_freedom(sess: &Session, def_id: DefId, axiom_free: bool) {
    with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        let entry = state.axiom_free_verdicts.entry(def_id).or_insert(true);
        *entry &= axiom_free;
    });
}

/// Trust (assumption-root gate): may `def_id`'s verdict be SPENT as a premise for
/// another body's obligation? `Some(reason)` = NO.
///
/// THE ONE AUTHENTICATOR. Every lane that discharges one body's obligation using
/// ANOTHER body's verdict consults this, because the defect it closes is not a
/// property of any one lane: a verdict that consumed an `#[trust::assume_total]`
/// (or `#[trust::skip]`) callee is CONDITIONAL, and every reduction that mints a
/// positive discharge from it erases the condition instead of inheriting it.
/// MEASURED on pristine main: an axiom-dependent `write_str` publishes
/// `no_obligations`/`proved` — byte-identical rows to a genuinely obligation-free
/// body, an EMPTY disposition list, an empty transport-row list, and a native run
/// whose summary is `requested_obligations: 0`. There is no in-band evidence left
/// at the premise bar, which is why the census is recorded out of band at the
/// point of consumption rather than read back off a row.
///
/// FAIL CLOSED ON ABSENCE. No record means no census was taken for this body's
/// verification, which is UNANSWERED — a verdict whose axiom inputs were never
/// inspected is not a verdict known to be unconditional.
///
/// WHAT MAKES IT TRANSITIVE, precisely — and it is NOT "the census walks the whole
/// bundle", which is false (see `bundled_def_id_reads_complete`). Three MEASURED
/// ways a user axiom under a verdict is caught, and one MEASURED spelling that is
/// not. This is a scope statement, not a completeness proof:
///
/// * SPENT — the axiom's call key is in the `assumed_total` / `expected_absent`
///   sets the bridge receives, so the call takes the recorded-assumption arm.
///   Those sets are written for a user declaration ONLY by
///   `insert_user_axiom_absent_call_identity`, which records the census in the same
///   statement, and `bundle_census_is_axiom_free` re-checks that the bridge's set is
///   covered by the census. So a spent axiom always answers `Some(false)` here.
/// * MENTIONED BUT NOT SPENT — the axiom changed no call's lowering, but a walked
///   body names the item as a `ty::FnDef` VALUE. `collect_user_axiom_value_mentions`
///   records that in the census only (never in a bridge set), so conjunct 1 of
///   `bundle_census_is_axiom_free` refuses. MEASURED, both states, on one program
///   each: `let f: fn() = boom; f();` under the `write!` reduction (X2), under `dyn`
///   C-FLOW (X7), under drop glue (X8) and bare (X5) all read `axiom_free=true`
///   without that scan and `axiom_free=false` with it.
/// * REACHED THROUGH CONSTANT MEMORY — the axiom is named in no walked body at all;
///   only its function pointer is, inside a `static`'s (or a vtable's) evaluated
///   allocation. `census_user_axioms_in_constant_memory` follows the constant
///   operand into that allocation. MEASURED, both states: `static TABLE: [fn(); 1]
///   = [boom]; (TABLE[0])()` under the `write!` reduction (Y1), under `dyn` C-FLOW
///   (Y4) and under drop glue (Y5) is rc=0 on pristine main and rc=0 on the round-3
///   compiler, where the census prints `axiom_free=true` on every root; it is rc=1
///   here. (Pristine main has no census at all — it prints no `axiom census` line —
///   so `axiom_free` is a round-3-and-later measurement only, and any claim about
///   its value on base would be unmeasurable.) Where that walk cannot decide it
///   clears `const_memory_reads_complete` (conjunct 4) rather than assuming the
///   memory holds no axiom; the four such places are enumerated at its definition.
/// * NEITHER SPENT NOR MENTIONED IN A WALKED BODY — e.g. the axiom sits in a bundle
///   member the DefId walk does not read. Then the call gets NO assumption marker,
///   takes the bridge's plain absent-callee arm, and the resulting unproved
///   assumption-panic row refuses the body at conjunct 1 of
///   [`trait_panic_free::instance_discharges_panic_freedom`] — before conjunct 5 is
///   ever consulted. MEASURED on pristine main: programs Z1 (axiom on the local
///   `write_str`, reached only through core's B-lane frame) and Z2 (axiom under a
///   hand-written `write_fmt` routed through `core::fmt::write`) are BOTH rc=1
///   there, with `assumed_total=0` in the caller's census.
///
/// STILL NOT COVERED, and MEASURED so rather than argued away. Do NOT read the four
/// cases above as a partition of the space — two successive revisions of this
/// comment claimed a partition and both were refuted by a program, first by
/// fn-pointer reification and then by the constant-memory route. What is measured
/// open today, each under a real consumer:
///
/// * A `Const::Unevaluated` operand. The constant-memory walk needs an allocation
///   and an unevaluated constant has none. MEASURED that today's two constant
///   spellings do not exploit it: `const TABLE: [fn(); 1] = [boom]` (probe Y2) and
///   an associated `impl Sink { const F: fn() = boom; }` (probe Y3) are rc=1 on the
///   PRISTINE base compiler, under the `write!` consumer. That is a measurement of
///   two programs, not a proof that no third spelling evaluates differently.
/// * A `#[trust::assume_total]` item in a bundle member the DefId walk cannot read,
///   which is carried by conjunct 3 (`bundled_def_id_reads_complete`) only for the
///   members it iterates; round 2 MEASURED 29 fixtures where `bundled_def_ids`
///   undercounts the published bundle, so bundle membership does not decide this
///   and is not used to.
/// * An axiom below a NON-AXIOM DIRECT CALLEE of a body the census reached THROUGH A
///   HOP. The hop carries the value-mention scan into the target but NOT the
///   bundled-member walk, and a hop target is not itself a bundled member, so its
///   direct callees mint no absent-callee row. Note the exact scope, because the
///   looser reading ("a hop target's direct callees are censused by nothing") is
///   MEASURED FALSE: an axiom that IS the direct callee is still caught, because the
///   attribute arm in `scan_body_for_user_axiom_value_mentions`'s `visit_operand`
///   runs BEFORE the `direct_call_targets` check and a call's `func` is an ordinary
///   operand. Only a non-axiom direct callee is left unrecursed, hiding whatever is
///   below it.
///
///   MEASURED at `28220d5879` on stage2, three programs identical but for the edge
///   kinds between `write_str` and the `#[trust::assume_total] fn boom(){panic!()}`
///   (`R` = reified `let f: fn() = g; f()`, `D` = direct `g()`):
///
///   ```text
///   R D    write_str -R-> mid1 -D-> boom          rc=1  write_str keys=1  REFUSED
///   R D D  write_str -R-> mid1 -D-> mid2 -D-> boom rc=0  write_str keys=0  LEAK
///   D D D  write_str -D-> mid1 -D-> mid2 -D-> boom rc=1  write_str keys=1, members=3
///   ```
///
///   `RDD` is the minimal shape and it is a FALSE PROOF at lib level: `render`'s
///   `write!` reduction spends `write_str`'s leaked `axiom_free=true` and emits ZERO
///   obligations, where `RD` leaves it 1 runtime-checked row and fails. The blast
///   radius is one compilation — `record_verdict_axiom_freedom` writes
///   `with_crate_verification_state(sess, …)` — so no downstream crate can spend it;
///   an in-crate `fn main` driver and a cross-crate consumer are both rc=1, but so
///   are their NO-AXIOM controls (an unproved `println!` row), so those two
///   configurations measure nothing about this leak in either direction. Run the
///   binary and it does panic, but only with `-Ztrust-verify=off`, which is not a
///   verification claim and must not be quoted as one. This is the hop2 defect
///   displaced by one frame; see `follow_user_axiom_pointer_hop`.
///
/// The two spellings whose closure this gate DOES claim are the ones with fixtures
/// (`*_assumed_total_fn_pointer_root_stays_closed.rs`,
/// `*_assumed_total_static_table_root_stays_closed.rs`), each watched accepting its
/// planted violation on a prior binary before being written down as check-fail.
///
/// A THIRD fixture pair pins the second-pointer-hop closure —
/// `static_str_write_fmt_assumed_total_second_pointer_hop_root_stays_closed.rs` and
/// its `_control` — with a WEAKER provenance, stated so nobody upgrades it. That
/// closure landed with a throwaway probe and no fixture, so it was pinned after the
/// fact: the pair has been watched red in both directions on the CURRENT binary
/// (strip the axiom from the mutant and it reports "ui test did not emit an error";
/// plant it in the control and the control fails), but neither half was ever watched
/// ACCEPTING its planted violation, because that needs a census-free compiler and
/// none can be built here.
///
/// ORDERING IS LOAD-BEARING at every call site: the record is written DURING the
/// verification that produces the verdict, so this must be consulted only AFTER
/// that verdict has been forced. Asking first declines everything.
///
/// Deliberately NOT refused here: `abi_total_panic_free` (bodyless foreign
/// non-unwinding), `type_total_std` (type-verified std iterator advance),
/// `compiler_total` (the `?` desugar, rung-1 Φ; the Clean-origin modeled-call
/// lane, `clean_origin_call_model_of_record`) and the reliance lanes' own
/// `dyn_contract_total`. Those are facts this compiler derived and they emit no
/// assumption row in any mode; only USER declarations are axioms here.
fn verdict_rests_on_user_axiom(sess: &Session, def_id: DefId) -> Option<&'static str> {
    verdict_rests_on_user_axiom_for(sess, def_id, false)
}

/// [`verdict_rests_on_user_axiom`], with the instance-hypothesis decline lifted
/// when `admit_instance_hypothesis` (only the instance-RELATIVE bar,
/// `trait_panic_free::InstanceRelative`, passes `true`). The user-axiom census and
/// the runtime-checked std member decline are unchanged either way.
fn verdict_rests_on_user_axiom_for(
    sess: &Session,
    def_id: DefId,
    admit_instance_hypothesis: bool,
) -> Option<&'static str> {
    verdict_rests_on_user_axiom_admitting(sess, def_id, admit_instance_hypothesis, false)
}

/// Trust (2026-10-19, DISCHARGE-NO-EVIDENCE-1): [`verdict_rests_on_user_axiom_for`],
/// with the `// SAFETY:` declaration decline (SAFETY-ASSUME-1) also lifted when
/// `admit_safety_comment`. Only `trait_panic_free::refusal_carries_no_violation_evidence`
/// passes `true`, and it SPENDS no verdict: it decides only whether an instance
/// discharge the shared bar already refused reads `unknown` or `failed`. A declared
/// block is disclosed by the body's own `assumption:safety-comment` row, which every
/// gate counts `assumed`; it is a premise nobody proved, not evidence that the body
/// panics. Every lane that spends a verdict keeps the decline.
fn verdict_rests_on_user_axiom_admitting(
    sess: &Session,
    def_id: DefId,
    admit_instance_hypothesis: bool,
    admit_safety_comment: bool,
) -> Option<&'static str> {
    // Trust (2026-10-08): a verdict resting on an argument premise (D96, D95/D100) is
    // `Trusted` and conditional on an unproved language fact; no caller spends it.
    // Trust (2026-10-15): name the premise the body actually carries.
    if argument_premise::body_carries_premise(sess, def_id) {
        return Some(
            if argument_premise::demotion_premise(sess, def_id)
                == Some(argument_premise::REFERENCE_ARG_BOX_VALID)
            {
                "its verdict RESTS ON an unproved argument premise (REFERENCE_ARG_BOX_VALID, \
                 DECISION_QUEUE D95/D100): the rows the premise removed are owed to it, and a \
                 caller may not spend it as a proof"
            } else {
                "its verdict RESTS ON an unproved argument premise (NOALIAS_MUT_ARGS, \
                 DECISION_QUEUE D96): the rows whose verification condition it shaped are \
                 `Trusted`, not `Certified`, and a caller may not spend it as a proof"
            },
        );
    }
    let (recorded, runtime_checked_std_member, instance_hypothesis, tinv_premised) =
        with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
            (
                state.axiom_free_verdicts.get(&def_id).copied(),
                state.runtime_checked_std_member_verdicts.contains(&def_id),
                state.instance_hypothesis_verdicts.contains(&def_id),
                state.tinv_premised_verdicts.contains(&def_id),
            )
        });
    // Trust (TINV step 10.4, TINV-27): a verdict resting on a type-invariant premise
    // is never a premise for another body.
    if tinv_premised {
        return Some(
            "its verdict is CONDITIONAL on an inferred type invariant: a use-site fact of the \
             crate's TINV certificate was conjoined into its rows (`TypeInvariantComposed`), \
             so it cannot stand for the body's panic-freedom in another body",
        );
    }
    // Trust (SAFETY-ASSUME-1, ruled 2026-10-17): a verdict resting on an unsafe block
    // assumed sound on its `// SAFETY:` comment is never a premise for another body,
    // under any bar: the declaration is a user assumption, not a proof.
    if !admit_safety_comment && safety_comment_assumption::body_carries_declaration(sess, def_id) {
        return Some(
            "its verdict is CONDITIONAL on a declared assumption: an unsafe block in it is \
             assumed sound on its `// SAFETY:` comment (`assumption:safety-comment`), not \
             proved, so it cannot stand for the body's panic-freedom in another body",
        );
    }
    // Trust (OD-1 phase 1): a verdict relative to an instance hypothesis is never a
    // premise for another body (design §2.1: no Φ, no registry, no `compiler_total`,
    // no `dyn` reliance input).
    if instance_hypothesis && !admit_instance_hypothesis {
        return Some(
            "its verdict is CONDITIONAL on an instance hypothesis: it claims panic-freedom only \
             under the premise that an open generic dispatch's callee is panic-free at every \
             instance (`assumption:instance-hypothesis`), so it cannot stand for the body's \
             panic-freedom",
        );
    }
    verdict_premise_decline(recorded, runtime_checked_std_member)
}

/// Trust (OD-1 phase 1): record that `def_id` carries an instance hypothesis. See
/// `CrateVerificationState::instance_hypothesis_verdicts`.
fn record_instance_hypothesis_verdict(sess: &Session, def_id: DefId) {
    with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        state.instance_hypothesis_verdicts.insert(def_id);
    });
}

/// Trust (TINV step 10.4): record that `def_id`'s rows carry a type-invariant premise.
/// See `CrateVerificationState::tinv_premised_verdicts`.
/// Step 10.7: also record `func_path` as a consumer of every type a
/// `TypeInvariantComposed` token among `authorities` assumes (the `TRUST_TINV_NOTES`
/// consumer list).
fn record_tinv_premised_verdict(
    sess: &Session,
    def_id: DefId,
    func_path: &str,
    authorities: &[Option<ResultProofAuthority>],
) {
    let consumed: std::collections::BTreeSet<&str> = authorities
        .iter()
        .flatten()
        .filter_map(|authority| match authority {
            ResultProofAuthority::TypeInvariantComposed { assumes, .. } => Some(assumes),
            _ => None,
        })
        .flatten()
        .map(|(ty, _)| ty.as_str())
        .collect();
    with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        state.tinv_premised_verdicts.insert(def_id);
        for ty in consumed {
            state
                .type_invariant_consumers
                .entry(ty.to_string())
                .or_default()
                .insert(func_path.to_string());
        }
    });
}

/// Trust (OD-1 phase 1): whether `def_id` carries an instance hypothesis.
fn verdict_carries_instance_hypothesis(sess: &Session, def_id: DefId) -> bool {
    with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        state.instance_hypothesis_verdicts.contains(&def_id)
    })
}

/// Trust (2026-09-29): the pure core of [`verdict_rests_on_user_axiom`], split out
/// (like [`verdict_axiom_decline_for_census`]) so the runtime-checked conjunct is
/// unit-testable without a `Session`: a member-conditional verdict is declined
/// WHATEVER its user-axiom census says, including a clean one.
fn verdict_premise_decline(
    recorded: Option<bool>,
    runtime_checked_std_member: bool,
) -> Option<&'static str> {
    // Trust (2026-09-29): a verdict whose native bundle kept a bundled std member
    // under the runtime-checked disposition is CONDITIONAL exactly like an
    // axiom-bearing one: its rows say nothing about that member's Asserts, which
    // are left to rustc's runtime check. Before that disposition existed such a
    // body's bundle was DISCARDED and its rows were unsupported, so no premise lane
    // could spend it; keeping the bundle must not change that. Checked here, in the
    // one authenticator, so every lane that spends another body's verdict
    // (`has_nonzero_accounted_static_proof`, the shared bar in `trait_panic_free`,
    // rung 2, postcondition composition) declines it.
    if runtime_checked_std_member {
        return Some(
            "its verdict is CONDITIONAL on a bundled std/core/alloc member's own runtime \
             check: the bundle it was verified as kept a member whose internal Assert no row \
             discharged (published as a `runtime_checked` row), so the verdict cannot stand for \
             the member's panic-freedom",
        );
    }
    verdict_axiom_decline_for_census(recorded)
}

/// Trust (2026-09-29): record that one verification of `def_id` kept a bundled
/// std member under the runtime-checked disposition. See
/// `CrateVerificationState::runtime_checked_std_member_verdicts`.
fn record_runtime_checked_std_member_verdict(sess: &Session, def_id: DefId) {
    with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        state.runtime_checked_std_member_verdicts.insert(def_id);
    });
}

/// The pure three-way core of [`verdict_rests_on_user_axiom`]: what one census
/// record (or its ABSENCE) means. Split out so the fail-closed direction is
/// directly unit-testable — `None => decline` is the conjunct a future refactor is
/// most likely to "simplify" into `unwrap_or(true)`.
fn verdict_axiom_decline_for_census(recorded: Option<bool>) -> Option<&'static str> {
    match recorded {
        Some(true) => None,
        Some(false) => Some(
            "its verdict RESTS ON a user-written axiom: the bundle it was verified as SPENT a \
             `#[trust::assume_total]` / `#[trust::skip]` callee as an assumption, so the verdict \
             is conditional on a declaration nobody proved",
        ),
        None => Some(
            "no user-axiom census was recorded for the verification that produced its verdict, so \
             it cannot be established free of `#[trust::assume_total]`/`#[trust::skip]` callees",
        ),
    }
}

/// Record a compiler-authenticated bodyless foreign non-unwinding boundary.
/// The identity is the exact extractor call key, including generic and stable
/// crate disambiguation, so a short-name collision cannot discharge a caller.
fn register_abi_nonunwind_total_callee(sess: &Session, call_identity: &str) {
    with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        state.abi_nonunwind_total_callees.insert(call_identity.to_string());
    });
}

/// Record a call the compiler TYPE-VERIFIED as a total std iterator advance.
/// Same exact-call-key discipline as the ABI lane above: the identity is the
/// extractor's `func_operand_name`, so a short-name collision across crates
/// cannot discharge a caller.
fn register_type_total_std_callee(sess: &Session, call_identity: &str) {
    with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        state.type_total_std_callees.insert(call_identity.to_string());
    });
}

/// Record that the compiler's grounding pass re-pointed `row`'s formula at the
/// grounded residual violation and retired its binding for `obligation_id`.
/// Trust (#5-PRE-A follow-through — call-site L0 replay, REFUTATION-ONLY): when a
/// call-site `VcKind::Precondition` obligation is `Failed` WITH a genuine solver
/// model, the caller provably can violate the callee's `#[requires]` — but that row
/// is L1Functional, invisible to both refutation-proper reporters
/// (`strict_l0_verification_failure` filters on `is_strict_l0_vc_kind`;
/// `escalate_refuted_l0_safety_counterexamples` needs an L0 kind and a forced
/// ground violation), so the abort classifies as mere incompleteness even though
/// the compiler holds a concrete violating witness. This lane replays the ENTAILED
/// callee-side Level-0 consequence at the call site: it regenerates the callee's
/// body VCs from the summary's `extracted_body` WITHOUT the callee's precondition,
/// rebinds them to the caller's actuals under the same σ as the `P[σ]`
/// construction, conjoins the caller's path condition, and re-solves with the
/// in-process ay backend (`trust_vcgen::generate_callsite_l0_replay_candidates`
/// documents every fail-closed candidate gate). A SAT model appends a NEW `Failed`
/// row carrying the callee's REAL L0 kind at the call span, which flows into
/// `strict_l0_verification_failure`'s `Failed` arm — and thus the
/// "Level 0 summary: N failed" refutation header — with no reporter change.
///
/// SOUNDNESS (refutation-only, strictly additive):
///   * TRIGGER: only a `Failed` Precondition row carrying a NONEMPTY solver
///     counterexample. The `fail_closed_unproved_precondition` reclassification
///     (Unknown -> Failed inside `build_proof_results`, which runs AFTER this
///     pass and on a separate status carrier) never attaches a model, and the
///     refute-only appenders that do append `Failed` rows here
///     (`refute_full_assert_obligations`, the bitmask probe) stamp
///     `counterexample: None` — so a minted/witness-less failure can never
///     trigger a replay.
///   * #5-PRE-A CRITICAL SCOPING: a def-site `requires` marker row is
///     `Precondition { callee == own name }` with `contract_metadata` populated
///     (see `exact_definition_entry_requires_rows`) and is never dispatched, so
///     it is excluded three ways (metadata, self-name, no model). Recursive
///     self-calls are excluded by the same self-name guard (both the short
///     `func.name` and the full `func.def_path` spelling).
///   * OUTPUT: only `VerificationResult::Failed` rows are ever appended; an
///     UNSAT (`Proved`) or Unknown/Timeout replay appends NOTHING and discharges
///     NOTHING — not the caller's Precondition obligation, not the callee's own
///     rows — and a `None` binding/authority slot (extended by the caller) means
///     the row can never feed evidence publication or authority registries.
///     The `solver` symbol is restamped `trust-callsite-l0-replay` as the row's
///     provenance tag (the same channel `trust-assert-refute` /
///     `trust-bitmask-refute` use), while ay's own model is kept as the witness.
fn refute_callsite_l0_replays_for_unmet_preconditions<'tcx>(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>,
    body: &Body<'tcx>,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    results: &mut Vec<(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)>,
    deadline: Option<std::time::Instant>,
    // Trust (T3 L4, C07p): the body's VC context; every replay candidate is interned into
    // it through the row oracle before it is solved.
    rows: &mut trust_types::vc_context::VcContext,
) {
    // Collect qualifying call sites first (immutable pass), then append.
    let mut sites: Vec<(String, SourceSpan)> = Vec::new();
    for (vc, result) in results.iter() {
        let VcKind::Precondition { callee } = &vc.kind else {
            continue;
        };
        let VerificationResult::Failed { counterexample: Some(cex), .. } = result else {
            continue;
        };
        if cex.assignments.is_empty() {
            continue;
        }
        // Def-site contract markers carry their source contract metadata;
        // genuine call-site rows are minted with `contract_metadata: None`
        // (see `generate_callsite_precondition_vcs`).
        if vc.contract_metadata.is_some() {
            continue;
        }
        // #5-PRE-A scoping: never replay a self-call (definition-entry
        // assumption territory), under either name spelling.
        if callee.as_str() == func.name || callee.as_str() == func.def_path {
            continue;
        }
        if !sites.iter().any(|(c, l)| c == callee && *l == vc.location) {
            sites.push((callee.clone(), vc.location.clone()));
        }
    }
    if sites.is_empty() {
        return;
    }
    // Same summary DB the VC routes were generated against (session-memoized
    // callee bodies, so this is a cheap re-read at this point in the walk).
    // Cross-crate/generic callees have no `extracted_body` attached and the
    // producer declines them — the honest `incomplete` residual stands.
    let summaries = build_callee_contract_summaries(tcx, body);
    for (callee, location) in sites {
        for candidate in trust_vcgen::generate_callsite_l0_replay_candidates_rows(
            func, &summaries, &callee, &location, rows,
        ) {
            // Recompute the remaining budget before every query (the assert
            // refutation lane's deadline discipline).
            let Some(timeout_ms) = remaining_solver_timeout_ms(
                vc_timeout_ms(tcx.sess),
                deadline,
                std::time::Instant::now(),
            ) else {
                return;
            };
            let ay = trust_router::Router::deterministic_in_process_ay(
                timeout_ms,
                effort_scale(tcx.sess),
            );
            let Some((vc, res)) = ay
                .verify_all_with_deadline(std::slice::from_ref(&candidate), deadline)
                .into_iter()
                .next()
            else {
                continue;
            };
            match res {
                // SAT with a model: a composed counterexample UNDER the
                // conjoined caller model — the producer's gates guarantee
                // every free var is caller-entry state or def-pinned, and the
                // model satisfies every CAPTURED caller-side hypothesis
                // (guards/defs/facts). Genuineness beyond that rests on the
                // vcgen guard/def assembly capturing every dominating
                // constraint and on the callee body VCs' fidelity. Surface it.
                VerificationResult::Failed { time_ms, counterexample: Some(cex), .. }
                    if !cex.assignments.is_empty() =>
                {
                    results.push((
                        vc,
                        VerificationResult::Failed {
                            solver: trust_types::Symbol::intern("trust-callsite-l0-replay"),
                            time_ms,
                            counterexample: Some(cex),
                        },
                    ));
                }
                // UNSAT (`Proved`), Unknown, Timeout, or a witness-less Failed:
                // append NOTHING. In particular a replay `Proved` is DROPPED —
                // it must never discharge the caller's Precondition obligation,
                // the callee's own rows, or reach any authority machinery.
                _ => {}
            }
        }
    }
}

/// Trust (BMC bounded-reduction refutation, REFUTATION-ONLY): refute a
/// statically-sized array reduction whose accumulator GENUINELY overflows.
///
/// `build_accumulator_bound_facts` is the proving side and is deliberately
/// self-limiting: when `init + K*per_max` does not fit the accumulator type it
/// emits nothing, the per-add overflow obligation stays undischarged, and the
/// build DECLINES with the incompleteness header. On the falsification gate's
/// mutant lane a decline scores as an UNCAUGHT MUTANT
/// (`shift_reduction_overflow`, `i128_shift_accumulator_overflow`). For the one
/// shape where the loop has a statically known trip count and a straight-line
/// body, a COMPLETE unroll is exact, so a solver model is a genuine
/// counterexample. `trust_vcgen::generate_bounded_reduction_bmc_refutation_candidates`
/// documents every recognition gate and the encoding's exactness argument.
///
/// SOUNDNESS (identical discipline to `refute_callsite_l0_replays_for_unmet_preconditions`):
///   * OUTPUT: only `VerificationResult::Failed` rows are ever APPENDED, and only
///     on an ay SAT verdict carrying a NON-EMPTY model. UNSAT (`Proved`),
///     Unknown, Timeout and a witness-less `Failed` append NOTHING and discharge
///     NOTHING — no obligation is retired, no proof authority is granted, and the
///     appended rows get `None` binding/eligibility/authority slots at the call
///     site, so they can never acquire evidence.
///   * The producer emits AT MOST ONE candidate per function and only for a
///     recognized shape; a partial unroll is never built (over the cap it
///     declines), because a partial unroll can only ever refute and its UNSAT
///     would mean nothing.
///   * `solver` is restamped `trust-bmc-reduction-refute` as the row's provenance
///     tag (the channel `trust-assert-refute` / `trust-callsite-l0-replay` use)
///     while ay's own model is kept as the witness.
fn refute_bounded_reduction_bmc_overflows(
    sess: &Session,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    results: &mut Vec<(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)>,
    deadline: Option<std::time::Instant>,
    // Trust (T3 L4, C07p): the body's VC context; the candidate is interned into it through
    // the row oracle before it is solved.
    rows: &mut trust_types::vc_context::VcContext,
) {
    for candidate in
        trust_vcgen::generate_bounded_reduction_bmc_refutation_candidates_rows(func, rows)
    {
        let Some(timeout_ms) =
            remaining_solver_timeout_ms(vc_timeout_ms(sess), deadline, std::time::Instant::now())
        else {
            return;
        };
        let ay = trust_router::Router::deterministic_in_process_ay(timeout_ms, effort_scale(sess));
        let Some((vc, res)) = ay
            .verify_all_with_deadline(std::slice::from_ref(&candidate), deadline)
            .into_iter()
            .next()
        else {
            continue;
        };
        match res {
            VerificationResult::Failed { time_ms, counterexample: Some(cex), .. }
                if !cex.assignments.is_empty() =>
            {
                results.push((
                    vc,
                    VerificationResult::Failed {
                        solver: trust_types::Symbol::intern("trust-bmc-reduction-refute"),
                        time_ms,
                        counterexample: Some(cex),
                    },
                ));
            }
            // Everything else is DROPPED — unproved is not refuted.
            _ => {}
        }
    }
}

fn record_grounded_panic_freedom_obligation(
    sess: &Session,
    obligation_id: &str,
    row: ExactResultRowIdentity,
) {
    with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        state.grounded_panic_freedom_obligations.insert(obligation_id.to_string(), row);
    });
}

/// Did the compiler's own grounding pass re-point (and thereby retire the
/// binding of) this exact result row? The complete canonical row identity is
/// required because row indices restart for every verified function.
///
/// The production caller was the whole-function panic-freedom subsumption,
/// removed on 2026-08-14 as a false proof. The predicate is retained (test-only)
/// because the grounding record it reads is still written by
/// `refute_full_assert_obligations` and consumed by `grounded_panic_freedom_row`.
#[cfg(test)]
fn row_was_compiler_grounded_in(
    grounded: &FxHashMap<String, ExactResultRowIdentity>,
    index: usize,
    vc: &VerificationCondition,
) -> bool {
    let Some(current) = exact_result_row_identity(index, vc) else {
        return false;
    };
    // This is an order-independent membership predicate; no selected value or
    // diagnostic can depend on FxHashMap iteration order.
    #[allow(rustc::potential_query_instability)]
    let was_grounded = grounded.values().any(|recorded| recorded == &current);
    was_grounded
}

fn grounded_panic_freedom_row(
    sess: &Session,
    obligation_id: &str,
) -> Option<ExactResultRowIdentity> {
    with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        state.grounded_panic_freedom_obligations.get(obligation_id).cloned()
    })
}

/// One call site of an extracted body, as every gate that decides "each call of
/// this body is discharged" reads it. See [`extracted_call_sites`].
#[derive(Clone, Copy)]
struct ExtractedCallSite<'a> {
    /// The block the call terminates: the panic-callee exemption and the
    /// per-site arms (`fmt_format_call_is_total`) key on it.
    block: &'a trust_types::BasicBlock,
    /// The rendered callee key — `func` on both call variants.
    callee: &'a str,
    args: &'a [trust_types::Operand],
    /// The authenticated `DefId`+substs identity the extractor stamped on the
    /// call. `None` admits nothing (see `identity_discharges_call`).
    callee_identity: Option<&'a trust_types::CalleeIdentity>,
    /// `become f(..)` — a `Terminator::TailCall`.
    tail: bool,
}

/// A `Terminator` variant [`extracted_call_sites`] does not classify.
/// `trust_types::Terminator` is `#[non_exhaustive]`, so a variant added after
/// the classifier was written reaches its forced wildcard arm; such a variant
/// might call, and its callee cannot be examined, so every gate treats it as an
/// undischarged call.
struct UnclassifiedTerminator;

/// Trust (2026-09-27, NEW-TAILCALL): the CALL SURFACE of an extracted body —
/// every `Terminator::Call` AND every `Terminator::TailCall`, in block order —
/// written once for every gate that decides "each call of this body is
/// discharged": [`all_calls_registry_or_bridge_total`] (the zero-obligation and
/// postcondition-only registration lanes), the R40 blessing candidacy
/// ([`record_zero_obligation_blessing_candidacy`]) and
/// [`all_calls_discharge_panic_free`] (the refute lane's catch-all discharge).
///
/// THE FALSE PROOF THIS CLOSES. `become f(x)` stays a `Terminator::TailCall` in
/// the extracted body — the variant exists so the call edge is not hidden — and
/// `f` runs, and can panic into OUR caller, exactly as it would behind an
/// ordinary call. Those three gates each spelled their own
/// `Terminator::Call`-only pattern, so no gate ever examined a tail call, and a
/// body whose only call was one passed every gate VACUOUSLY. Measured on stage
/// 1 built from `1505108872` (docs/KNOWN_HAZARDS.md, "CLOSED 2026-09-27 — a
/// `become` tail call was never examined by the panic-freedom call gates"), with
/// `fn boom(x: u32) -> u32 { if x > 5 { panic!("boom") } x }`:
///
/// ```text
/// pub fn tail(x: u32) -> u32 { become boom(x) }
///   [ASSERT_P2] fn=tailcall::tail others_all_proved=true calls_discharged=true
///   vc:tailcall__tail:assertion:panic-freedom:0  proved (trust-assert-refute)
/// pub fn plain(x: u32) -> u32 { boom(x) }
///   [ASSERT_P2] fn=tailcall::plain others_all_proved=true calls_discharged=false
/// ```
///
/// and `tail` was then REGISTERED panic-free, so `calls_tail(x) { tail(x) }`
/// discharged its own catch-all on that registration.
///
/// A tail call is decided by exactly the arms an ordinary call is. The extractor
/// renders its key with the same `func_operand_name` and stamps the same
/// `callee_identity` (`trust_types::Terminator::TailCall` documents both as the
/// `Call` carriers), so an unregistered or unidentified tail callee fails closed
/// the way the same `Call` does. It is never EXEMPTED as a proven-unreachable
/// assert/panic block (see `all_calls_discharge_panic_free`). An INDIRECT
/// `become` never arrives here as a call: the extractor emits
/// `Opaque("TailCall(indirect)")`, whose unsupported row and failed native
/// lowering refuse the body on their own.
///
/// `Err(UnclassifiedTerminator)` — a terminator variant this classifier has
/// never seen — fails every gate closed. The census pin
/// `call_surface_classifies_every_terminator_variant` checks
/// [`CALL_SURFACE_TERMINATOR_CLASSIFICATION`] against
/// `trust_types::TERMINATOR_VARIANT_NAMES`, so a new variant is classified here
/// before it can be released, not discovered as a vacuous pass.
fn extracted_call_sites(
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
) -> Result<Vec<ExtractedCallSite<'_>>, UnclassifiedTerminator> {
    let mut sites = Vec::new();
    for block in &func.body.blocks {
        let (callee, args, callee_identity, tail) = match &block.terminator {
            trust_types::Terminator::Call { func: callee, args, callee_identity, .. } => {
                (callee, args, callee_identity, false)
            }
            trust_types::Terminator::TailCall { func: callee, args, callee_identity, .. } => {
                (callee, args, callee_identity, true)
            }
            // Not calls. `Drop` glue and the unmodeled `Opaque` sentinel are charged
            // by their own lanes (drop-glue facts; the `UnsupportedMir` row and the
            // lowering-failed marker), exactly as before this enumerator existed.
            trust_types::Terminator::Goto(_)
            | trust_types::Terminator::SwitchInt { .. }
            | trust_types::Terminator::Return
            | trust_types::Terminator::Assert { .. }
            | trust_types::Terminator::Drop { .. }
            | trust_types::Terminator::Opaque { .. }
            | trust_types::Terminator::Unreachable
            | trust_types::Terminator::Resume => continue,
            // Forced by `#[non_exhaustive]`: a variant added after this was
            // written. It may call; nothing here can examine it.
            _ => return Err(UnclassifiedTerminator),
        };
        sites.push(ExtractedCallSite {
            block,
            callee: callee.as_str(),
            args: args.as_slice(),
            callee_identity: callee_identity.as_ref(),
            tail,
        });
    }
    Ok(sites)
}

/// An indirect call gets an identity only from the private exact-source binding.
/// Nothing stamps or rewrites the computational call, and no string-keyed arm
/// receives the target's display name. Registry membership remains mandatory.
fn bound_call_identity<'a>(
    site: &ExtractedCallSite<'a>,
    reified: &'a FxHashMap<trust_types::BlockId, trust_types::CalleeIdentity>,
) -> Option<&'a trust_types::CalleeIdentity> {
    site.callee_identity.or_else(|| reified.get(&site.block.id))
}

fn reified_call_identities(
    sess: &Session,
    owner: Option<DefId>,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
) -> FxHashMap<trust_types::BlockId, trust_types::CalleeIdentity> {
    let Some(owner) = owner else { return FxHashMap::default() };
    with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        state
            .reified_fnptr_bindings
            .get(&owner)
            .and_then(Option::as_ref)
            .map(|bindings| bindings.identities(owner, func))
            .unwrap_or_default()
    })
}

#[cfg(test)]
fn all_calls_registry_or_bridge_total(
    proven: &FxHashSet<String>,
    proven_identities: &FxHashSet<trust_types::CalleeIdentity>,
    abi_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    type_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    cell_set_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    moved_value_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    tls_try_with_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    typed_self_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
) -> bool {
    all_calls_registry_or_bridge_total_with_bindings(
        &FxHashMap::default(),
        proven,
        proven_identities,
        abi_total,
        type_total,
        cell_set_total,
        moved_value_total,
        tls_try_with_total,
        typed_self_total,
        func,
    )
}

/// Every `trust_types::Terminator` variant [`extracted_call_sites`] classifies,
/// spelled as `trust_types::TERMINATOR_VARIANT_NAMES` spells it, with whether it
/// is a CALL. The census pin compares this against that exhaustive list (a new
/// variant fails it) and against the classifier's own answer on one instance of
/// each variant (a classifier edit that forgets this table fails it too).
#[cfg(test)]
const CALL_SURFACE_TERMINATOR_CLASSIFICATION: &[(&str, bool)] = &[
    ("Terminator::Goto", false),
    ("Terminator::SwitchInt", false),
    ("Terminator::Return", false),
    ("Terminator::Call", true),
    ("Terminator::TailCall", true),
    ("Terminator::Assert", false),
    ("Terminator::Drop", false),
    ("Terminator::Opaque", false),
    ("Terminator::Unreachable", false),
    ("Terminator::Resume", false),
];

/// Trust (2026-09-26, std-absent-callee): the per-caller TYPED-TOTAL call keys the
/// call gates' `typed_self_total` arm consumes — R41's instantiated-`Self` total std
/// trait calls (`typed_self_total_std_calls`) together with the sysroot panic
/// census's derived-total sysroot calls (`sysroot_census_total_calls`). Both are
/// decided per call site from `TyCtxt` (never from a callee string), published by
/// the caller's OWN bundle build, keyed by its `def_path`, and self-poisoning; an
/// absent caller contributes the empty set, which admits nothing. One union read by
/// legacy consumers including `all_calls_discharge_panic_free`. Zero-row and
/// R40 consumers use the same collector results in the DefId-keyed snapshot,
/// rather than borrowing a different owner's same-rendered entry.
fn per_caller_typed_total_calls(
    state: &CrateVerificationState,
    def_path: &str,
) -> FxHashSet<String> {
    let mut calls = state.typed_self_total_std_calls.get(def_path).cloned().unwrap_or_default();
    if let Some(census) = state.sysroot_census_total_calls.get(def_path) {
        // A set union: the result does not depend on iteration order.
        #[allow(rustc::potential_query_instability)]
        calls.extend(census.iter().cloned());
    }
    // Trust (2026-10-10, SLICE-ITER-CONSUMER): the slice-iterator `all`/`fold` keys
    // whose local closure was already proven panic-free
    // (`collect_slice_iter_consumer_total_calls`). Same per-caller, TyCtxt-decided,
    // self-poisoning discipline as the two sets above.
    if let Some(slice_iter) = state.slice_iter_consumer_total_calls.get(def_path) {
        #[allow(rustc::potential_query_instability)]
        calls.extend(slice_iter.iter().cloned());
    }
    // Trust (2026-10-13, R43-ZERO-ROW-PARITY): the generic local call keys the R43
    // parametric premises discharge (`collect_parametric_generic_callee_total_calls`),
    // the decision the bundle's proven-total lane already made for the same call.
    if let Some(parametric) = state.parametric_generic_callee_total_calls.get(def_path) {
        #[allow(rustc::potential_query_instability)]
        calls.extend(parametric.iter().cloned());
    }
    // Trust (2026-10-15, VERIFIED-CALLABLE-ZERO-ROW-PARITY): the consumer calls the
    // bundle already treats as total through their callable's own verdict.
    if let Some(consumers) = state.verified_callable_consumer_total_calls.get(def_path) {
        #[allow(rustc::potential_query_instability)]
        calls.extend(consumers.iter().cloned());
    }
    // Trust (2026-10-15, CONST-CHUNK-SIZE-ZERO-ROW-PARITY): core's `[T]` chunking calls
    // whose every occurrence in the body passes a non-zero constant size.
    if let Some(chunking) = state.const_nonzero_slice_chunking_total_calls.get(def_path) {
        #[allow(rustc::potential_query_instability)]
        calls.extend(chunking.iter().cloned());
    }
    // Trust (2026-10-15, BOUNDED-VEC-COLLECT-ZERO-ROW-PARITY): the bounded `Vec` collects
    // the bundle already treats as total.
    if let Some(collects) = state.bounded_vec_collect_total_calls.get(def_path) {
        #[allow(rustc::potential_query_instability)]
        calls.extend(collects.iter().cloned());
    }
    // Trust (ENCODE-UTF8-BUF4-PARITY): the fitting-buffer `char::encode_utf8`
    // calls the bundle already treats as total.
    if let Some(encodes) = state.char_encode_utf8_fitting_buffer_total_calls.get(def_path) {
        #[allow(rustc::potential_query_instability)]
        calls.extend(encodes.iter().cloned());
    }
    calls
}

/// Whether every `Call`/`TailCall` in `func` targets an exact function identity
/// already proven fully panic-free in this Session, or a separately
/// compiler-authenticated ABI/type-total callee. A function with no calls
/// trivially satisfies this. Fail-closed: ANY call without one of those grants
/// (an unproved local fn, a panicking std call like `Option::unwrap` /
/// `core::panicking::panic`, or a foreign import) makes this `false`, so the
/// caller's panic-freedom is NOT discharged by the interprocedural route.
fn all_calls_target_proven_panic_free(
    sess: &Session,
    owner: Option<DefId>,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
) -> bool {
    all_calls_target_proven_panic_free_modulo(sess, owner, func, &FxHashSet::default())
}

/// [`all_calls_target_proven_panic_free`], with the exact call keys in `accounted`
/// treated as accounted for. Trust (OD-1 phase 1): read ONLY by the zero-obligation
/// TRANSPORT decision, with `accounted` = the body's admitted instance-hypothesis
/// call keys, whose premise the body publishes as its own rows; never by a
/// registry gate (a body carrying a hypothesis is never registered, whatever this
/// answers).
fn all_calls_target_proven_panic_free_modulo(
    sess: &Session,
    owner: Option<DefId>,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    accounted: &FxHashSet<String>,
) -> bool {
    let Some(owner) = owner else { return false };
    let proven = with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| state.panic_free_proven_funcs.clone());
    // R45 — the `DefId`+substs VIEW of the SAME registry. Read here for
    // exactly the reason the sibling gate `all_calls_discharge_panic_free` reads it:
    // `proven` is keyed by `safe_def_path_str` and the call keys below are rendered
    // by `func_operand_name`, two renderers that DISAGREE on lifetime spelling. See
    // `identity_discharges_call`.
    let proven_identities =
        with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| state.panic_free_proven_identities.clone());
    let abi_total =
        with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| state.abi_nonunwind_total_callees.clone());
    let type_total =
        with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| state.type_total_std_callees.clone());
    let facts = with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        zero_obligation_typed_facts_for_owner(state, owner)
    });
    let cell_set_total = facts.cell_set_total;
    let moved_value_total = facts.moved_value_total;
    let tls_try_with_total = facts.tls_try_with_total;
    let mut typed_self_total = facts.typed_self_total;
    #[allow(rustc::potential_query_instability)]
    typed_self_total.extend(accounted.iter().cloned());
    let reified = reified_call_identities(sess, Some(owner), func);
    all_calls_registry_or_bridge_total_with_bindings(
        &reified,
        &proven,
        &proven_identities,
        &abi_total,
        &type_total,
        &cell_set_total,
        &moved_value_total,
        &tls_try_with_total,
        &typed_self_total,
        func,
    )
}

/// The zero-obligation lane's call gate: every call — `Terminator::Call` AND
/// `Terminator::TailCall`, enumerated by [`extracted_call_sites`] — must be
/// discharged by an authenticated Session registry OR by the bridge's OWN
/// audited total-call recognizer.
///
/// LANE-CONSISTENCY REPAIR, not a widening. Measured on the deployed driver
/// `sha256:d54ffbdf2812d8ea` (direct `trustc` lane, `-Z trust-verify-output=json`,
/// `TRUST_PF_REG_TRACE=1`), for `POISONED.try_with(|p| p.get())`:
///
/// ```text
/// [PF_REG] fn=P::poisoned::{closure#0} zero-obligation gate: all_calls_proven=false
/// [PF_REG] fn=P::poisoned::{closure#0} established=false nrows=0
///   RESULT P::poisoned::{closure#0} ... ['native-verification-gap']
/// ```
///
/// The closure's TrustIr body is `Undef Bool [FreshSymbolicHavoc]; Br; Return`:
/// the bridge ELIDED the `core::cell::Cell::<bool>::get` call because that
/// callee is on its `EXACT_TOTAL` surface. The MIR `Terminator::Call` is still
/// there, so the registry-only core below refused it and the function published
/// the honest gap row. That refusal is NOT the false-proof-#11 belt doing its
/// job — it is two lanes disagreeing about the SAME callee:
///
///  * the ALREADY-SHIPPED sibling gate [`all_calls_discharge_panic_free`]
///    accepts exactly this recognizer (`trust_ir_bridge::callee_is_recognized_
///    total_no_panic`), and its verdict is what seals the UNCONDITIONAL
///    `ResultProofAuthority::InterproceduralCalleeClosure` on the whole-function
///    panic-freedom catch-all — which is precisely what makes
///    `results_establish_unconditional_panic_freedom` true and calls
///    `register_proven_panic_free`. So the recognizer ALREADY licenses entry to
///    `panic_free_proven_funcs` for every function that happens to carry at
///    least one provable row;
///  * `results_establish_unconditional_panic_freedom` (the nonempty-rows lane)
///    has NO call conjunct at all, so an elided total call there is trusted
///    unconditionally today.
///
/// Refusing the same recognizer ONLY when the row set happens to be empty is
/// therefore an artifact, not a soundness property: nothing about "zero rows"
/// makes the bridge's elision less trustworthy, and the elision is already the
/// sole reason the call carries no obligation in EITHER lane.
///
/// FALSE PROOF #11 STAYS CLOSED. #11's callee was a same-crate `leaf(a, 0)`
/// whose body the bridge BUNDLED and whose resolved-call arm charged nothing —
/// a callee no total surface recognizes. `callee_is_recognized_total_no_panic`
/// declines it (it is neither `EXACT_TOTAL`, nor an F5 trusted absent callee,
/// nor any orphan-safe primitive arm), so the #11 fixture still fails closed.
/// The matched-pair anti-control is built into the crate under test:
/// `Cell::get` IS on `EXACT_TOTAL` and `Cell::set` is DELIBERATELY not (its
/// `replace`-then-`drop(old)` can run a user `Drop`), so `rational::poisoned::
/// {closure#0}` must flip and `rational::poison::{closure#0}` must NOT.
///
/// RESIDUAL, stated plainly: the nonempty lane pairs the recognizer with
/// `others_all_proved` — independent evidence that the native lowering really
/// ran on this body. The zero-row lane's counterpart is
/// `native_run_is_vacuously_clean` (requested/failed/unknown/unsupported/
/// skipped/… all zero AND no `NATIVE_TRUST_IR_LOWERING_FAILED_MARKER`), which
/// `zero_obligation_registry_gate` still requires as a separate conjunct. An
/// unmodeled construct therefore still fails closed here.
fn all_calls_registry_or_bridge_total_with_bindings(
    reified: &FxHashMap<trust_types::BlockId, trust_types::CalleeIdentity>,
    proven: &FxHashSet<String>,
    // R45 — the IDENTITY view of `proven`, for the `registry-identity` arm. See
    // `identity_discharges_call`; an empty set simply means no identity-keyed
    // discharge is available and every decision falls through to the string arms.
    proven_identities: &FxHashSet<trust_types::CalleeIdentity>,
    abi_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    type_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    // FRONT B (R15) — THIS function's own drop-glue-free `Cell::<T>::set` call keys.
    // See the `cell-set-typed` arm below and
    // `CrateVerificationState::drop_absent_cell_set_calls`.
    cell_set_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    // LEVER 3 — THIS function's own drop-glue-free moved-value-argument call keys.
    // See the `moved-value-nodrop` arm below and
    // `CrateVerificationState::drop_absent_moved_value_arg_calls`.
    moved_value_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    // R16 FRONT A — THIS function's own const-init-TLS `try_with` call keys. See
    // the `tls-const-init` arm below and
    // `CrateVerificationState::const_init_tls_try_with_calls`.
    tls_try_with_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    // R41 — THIS function's own INSTANTIATED-`Self` total std trait call keys. See
    // the `typed-self-total` arm below and
    // `CrateVerificationState::typed_self_total_std_calls`.
    typed_self_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
) -> bool {
    // The gate is a function of the extracted body's CALL KEYS and nothing else,
    // so the arm list lives in the key-level core below and is shared VERBATIM
    // with the R40 blessing fixpoint (`zero_obligation_blessing_least_fixpoint`),
    // which re-decides a recorded candidate against a LATER, strictly larger
    // registry. One arm list cannot drift from itself; two copies could.
    //
    // LIVE-PATH PROBE (`TRUST_PF_MATCH_TRACE`, the same flag the sibling gate's
    // `[PF_CALLGATE]` probe uses). Reads only; names the EXACT callee string and
    // the EXACT arm that discharged it, so "which arm fired" is observable
    // rather than inferred — an inert fix (an upstream layer having rewritten
    // the callee name first) shows up as `arm=NONE` on a callee the recognizer
    // was expected to accept. The flag is read HERE, at the once-per-function
    // production gate, and never by the fixpoint: the fixpoint re-decides every
    // candidate on every round, so tracing it would bury this probe's one
    // authoritative line per function under a quadratic replay of provisional
    // ones.
    let trace = trust_env_var_present("TRUST_PF_MATCH_TRACE");
    // Trust (2026-09-27, NEW-TAILCALL): the call surface, tail calls included (see
    // `extracted_call_sites`). This gate used to collect `Terminator::Call` keys
    // alone, so a body whose only call was `become f(..)` handed the arm list an
    // EMPTY callee list and registered. A terminator nobody classified is a call
    // nothing examined, so it blocks registration.
    let Ok(sites) = extracted_call_sites(func) else {
        if trace {
            eprintln!(
                "[PF_ZEROGATE] fn={} arm=NONE (blocks registration) unclassified terminator",
                func.def_path,
            );
        }
        return false;
    };
    // R45 — each key travels with the call's OWN authenticated identity, not just
    // its rendered spelling. The pair is what the arm list consumes; the identity
    // half is `None` for every call shape the extractor refuses to identify
    // (indirect/`dyn` dispatch, a call whose arguments still mention a type or
    // const parameter, a synthetic body, a pre-carrier artifact). An absent
    // identity never grants ordinary registry authority.
    let callees: Vec<(&str, Option<&trust_types::CalleeIdentity>)> =
        sites.iter().map(|site| (site.callee, bound_call_identity(site, reified))).collect();
    all_callee_keys_registry_or_bridge_total(
        proven,
        proven_identities,
        abi_total,
        type_total,
        cell_set_total,
        moved_value_total,
        tls_try_with_total,
        typed_self_total,
        &func.def_path,
        &callees,
        trace,
    )
}

/// The arm list of [`all_calls_registry_or_bridge_total`], over the extracted
/// call keys of ONE function. Split out (behaviour-identical) so the R40
/// blessing fixpoint decides a candidate with the SAME five arms, in the same
/// order, rather than with a second transcription of them.
///
/// `def_path` is used ONLY for the trace line; every decision is made from the
/// callee key, its authenticated identity, and the five sets.
///
/// R45 — each element of `callees` is `(rendered key, authenticated identity)`.
/// BOTH callers must supply the identity half from the SAME call terminator the
/// key came from (`Terminator::Call` or `Terminator::TailCall`, as
/// [`extracted_call_sites`] yields them — Trust, NEW-TAILCALL): this function is
/// split out precisely so the production gate
/// and the R40 blessing fixpoint decide with the SAME arms in the same order, and
/// a caller that silently handed in `None` everywhere would put the two lanes back
/// out of step (the fixpoint would refuse what the production gate accepted, and
/// the R40 gap this whole mechanism closes would reopen for identity-discharged
/// callees only).
fn all_callee_keys_registry_or_bridge_total(
    _proven: &FxHashSet<String>,
    proven_identities: &FxHashSet<trust_types::CalleeIdentity>,
    abi_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    type_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    cell_set_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    moved_value_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    tls_try_with_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    typed_self_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    def_path: &str,
    callees: &[(&str, Option<&trust_types::CalleeIdentity>)],
    trace: bool,
) -> bool {
    for (callee, callee_identity) in callees {
        let callee = *callee;
        // Ordinary verified callees require the authenticated DefId plus
        // region-erased substitutions on BOTH sides. The display-name mirror
        // is not an alternative grant: a different owner may render identically.
        // Both ordinary registration and deferred R40 export use the same
        // premise refusal and item_identity boundary.
        //
        // None, a foreign owner or mismatched substitutions admits nothing
        // through this arm. Type/const-generic items mint no definition-side
        // identity, so their pre-monomorphization verdict cannot authenticate a
        // concrete instance. The separately authenticated ABI/type-total,
        // bridge and owner-bound typed arms below retain their own rules.
        if identity_discharges_call(proven_identities, *callee_identity) {
            if trace {
                eprintln!("[PF_ZEROGATE] fn={def_path} arm=registry-identity callee={callee}");
            }
            continue;
        }
        if abi_total.contains(callee) || type_total.contains(callee) {
            if trace {
                eprintln!("[PF_ZEROGATE] fn={def_path} arm=registry callee={callee}");
            }
            continue;
        }
        if trust_ir_bridge::callee_is_recognized_total_no_panic(callee) {
            if trace {
                eprintln!("[PF_ZEROGATE] fn={def_path} arm=bridge-total callee={callee}");
            }
            continue;
        }
        // FRONT B (R15) — the TYPED `Cell::<T>::set` arm.
        //
        // THE LANE DIVERGENCE THIS CLOSES. R14 landed a sound bridge-side elision of
        // `Cell::<T>::set` when `T`'s drop is total (`9b90f0492b`), and it was
        // COUNT-INERT: removing the call from the lowered TrustIr body does not
        // remove the `Terminator::Call` from the MIR list THIS gate walks, and
        // `callee_is_recognized_total_no_panic` is string-only and (deliberately)
        // declines `Cell::set`. Measured on the deployed driver
        // `sha256:4a65de62227f6842`, direct `trustc` lane, for
        // `POISONED.with(|p| p.set(true))`:
        //     [CELLSET_GATE]  fn=P::poison::{closure#0} payload=Bool drop_total=true
        //     [PF_ZEROGATE]   fn=P::poison::{closure#0} arm=NONE (blocks registration)
        //                     callee=core::cell::Cell::<bool>::set
        //     [PF_REG]        fn=P::poison::{closure#0} zero-obligation gate:
        //                     all_calls_proven=false ... nrows=0
        // — the bridge elided, the gate refused, the function published a gap row.
        //
        // `Cell::set` DOES NOT JOIN THE NAME-ONLY SURFACE. R14's boundary pin
        // `cellset_never_joins_the_name_only_total_surface` stays satisfied: the
        // predicate below is not a string list and could not be expressed as one —
        // membership was decided from the DefId of the callee, the `sym::Cell`
        // diagnostic item on its inherent impl's self type, and the CONCRETE
        // monomorphized payload's `needs_drop`, then recorded PER CALLER. A callee
        // string carries none of that, which is exactly why `Cell::set` was kept off
        // `callee_is_recognized_total_no_panic` and stays off it.
        //
        // THREE INDEPENDENT REASONS THIS CANNOT MINT A FALSE PROOF:
        //  1. The premise is `!needs_drop(T)` — `T` has NO drop glue, so
        //     `set`'s `drop(old)` is literally nothing. Strictly stronger than the
        //     bridge's "the drop tree holds no panicking `Drop`", so this arm is a
        //     SUBSET of what the bridge elided, never a superset.
        //  2. The set is per-caller and self-poisoning, so a grant made for one call
        //     site can never license a different one (see `drop_absent_cell_set_calls`).
        //  3. This lane runs ONLY when `summary_is_empty` — and a `Cell::set` the
        //     bridge did NOT elide emits an absent-callee obligation, which makes
        //     that conjunct false. Measured on the same driver, for a
        //     `Cell<Option<Box<D>>>` whose `D::drop` panics: the bridge declined
        //     (`drop_total=false`), the closure came out `nrows=1`, and the
        //     zero-obligation lane never ran at all. So even a hypothetical drift
        //     between this premise and the bridge's is caught by an independent
        //     conjunct rather than by these two agreeing.
        if cell_set_total.contains(callee) {
            if trace {
                eprintln!("[PF_ZEROGATE] fn={def_path} arm=cell-set-typed callee={callee}");
            }
            continue;
        }
        // LEVER 3 — the TYPED MOVED-VALUE-ARGUMENT arm (`Option::<T>::unwrap_or`,
        // `Option::<T>::ok_or::<E>`, `Result::<T, E>::unwrap_or`, and — R43 —
        // `Result::<T, E>::ok`, whose `Err(_)` wildcard arm drops the `E`). Same
        // shape and same three independent reasons as the `Cell::set` arm above.
        //
        // THESE NAMES DO NOT REJOIN THE NAME-ONLY SURFACE, and cannot: trust-recon
        // `957f47a332` retired them from `callee_is_recognized_total_no_panic`
        // because each DROPS a by-value argument on the branch it does not consume,
        // running the CALLER's `Drop::drop` — a property of `T`/`E` that a callee
        // string does not carry. Membership below was decided from the callee's
        // DefId, the `sym::Option`/`sym::Result` diagnostic item on its inherent
        // impl's self type, and `needs_drop` on EVERY input of the signature
        // instantiated at THIS call site, then recorded PER CALLER
        // (`moved_value_arg_call_operands_need_no_drop` /
        // `collect_drop_absent_moved_value_arg_calls`).
        //
        //  1. The premise is `!needs_drop` on every by-value operand — those values
        //     have NO drop glue, so the branch-not-taken drop is literally nothing,
        //     and the bodies contain nothing else that can panic or run user code
        //     (a discriminant read and a move; see the predicate's doc for all
        //     three verbatim bodies).
        //  2. The set is per-caller and self-poisoning, so a grant made for one call
        //     site can never license a different one — including under a rendering
        //     collision, which poisons itself.
        //  3. This lane runs ONLY when `summary_is_empty`; a call the bridge did NOT
        //     elide emits an absent-callee obligation, which makes that conjunct
        //     false, so a drift between this premise and the bridge's is caught by
        //     an INDEPENDENT conjunct rather than by two lanes agreeing.
        if moved_value_total.contains(callee) {
            if trace {
                eprintln!("[PF_ZEROGATE] fn={def_path} arm=moved-value-nodrop callee={callee}");
            }
            continue;
        }
        // R16 FRONT A — the CONST-INITIALIZED thread-local `LocalKey::try_with` arm.
        // Like the `Cell::set` arm above it is NOT a name test and could not be
        // expressed as one: membership was decided from the callee's DefId + the
        // `sym::LocalKey` diagnostic item, from the CONST-EVALUATED accessor the
        // key's `inner` field actually holds (required to be a closure whose MIR has
        // ZERO call terminators — the positive const-init discriminator), and from
        // the closure argument's own membership in `panic_free_proven_funcs`. All
        // three are invisible in the rendered callee string, which is exactly why
        // `try_with` was RETIRED from `callee_is_recognized_total_no_panic` and must
        // stay off it (see `is_trusted_panic_free_absent_callee`'s R15 addendum).
        //
        // FALSE PROOF #13 STAYS CLOSED. #13's shape is a LAZY key whose
        // `__rust_std_internal_init_fn` panics under an innocent closure. That key's
        // accessor always carries a `LazyStorage::get_or_init(.., __rust_std_internal_init_fn)`
        // CALL, so `tls_accessor_terminator_cannot_call_or_panic` rejects it and the
        // key never reaches this set. `LocalKey::with` is likewise excluded twice
        // over (item name, and the glue pin's `!`-returning-callee rejection).
        if tls_try_with_total.contains(callee) {
            if trace {
                eprintln!("[PF_ZEROGATE] fn={def_path} arm=tls-const-init callee={callee}");
            }
            continue;
        }
        // R41 — the TYPED INSTANTIATED-`Self` arm (`<intN as Ord>::{max,min}`,
        // `<intN as From<prim>>::from`, `<intN as TryFrom<intM>>::try_from`,
        // `<str as ToString>::to_string`, and — R43 —
        // `<String as Deref>::deref`, anchored on the `String` LANG ITEM because
        // `String` is an ADT with no `TyKind` of its own and a generic parameter
        // named `String` renders identically). Same shape and the same reasons as
        // the two typed arms above.
        //
        // THESE SPELLINGS DO NOT REJOIN THE NAME-ONLY SURFACE, and cannot. The first
        // three WERE on `callee_is_recognized_total_no_panic` (trust-ir-bridge
        // `PRIMITIVE_INT_SPELLINGS` + `is_primitive_int_*_callee`) and the fourth was
        // written there and REVERTED (`ccabd7b763`); all four rested on the FALSE
        // claim that a bare `u64`/`usize`/`str` spelling cannot be a user type. It
        // can — it is a generic PARAMETER name, and a `ty::Param` renders bare — so
        // `<u64 as core::cmp::Ord>::max` and `<str as alloc::string::ToString>::to_string`
        // are forgeable byte-for-byte by a caller whose `Self` panics on every input.
        // Membership below was decided from the callee's DefId, the library's own
        // `#[rustc_diagnostic_item]` name on the TRAIT, and the INSTANTIATED `Self`
        // (and source) `TyKind` at THIS call site, then recorded PER CALLER
        // (`typed_self_total_std_call` / `collect_typed_self_total_std_calls`).
        //
        //  1. The premise is a `TyKind` equality read from `TyCtxt` —
        //     `ty::Int`/`ty::Uint` for the three primitive arms, `ty::Str` for
        //     `to_string`. A `ty::Param` (the forgery) is neither. Given a genuine
        //     `Self`, the resolved impl is orphan-forced to be the library's own and
        //     its body runs NO user code (see `typed_self_total_std_call` for each
        //     body, with the `SpecToString` specialization spelled out).
        //  2. The set is per-caller and self-poisoning, and HERE that is load-bearing
        //     rather than defensive: an honest and a forged occurrence in one caller
        //     render the SAME key, and the declining one removes it for both.
        //  3. `to_string` is the arm that needs (1) and (2) to stand ALONE: measured
        //     on the deployed driver, a forged `attack_to_string` comes out
        //     `nrows=0` exactly like the honest one, so — unlike the `Cell::set` and
        //     moved-value arms — the "a call the bridge did not elide emits an
        //     absent-callee obligation" conjunct does NOT independently catch it.
        //     That is precisely why this arm may not be a string test.
        if typed_self_total.contains(callee) {
            if trace {
                eprintln!("[PF_ZEROGATE] fn={def_path} arm=typed-self-total callee={callee}");
            }
            continue;
        }
        if trace {
            eprintln!("[PF_ZEROGATE] fn={def_path} arm=NONE (blocks registration) callee={callee}");
        }
        return false;
    }
    true
}

// ====================================================================}

// ===========================================================================
// R40 — the WELL-FOUNDED zero-obligation panic-free BLESSING
// ===========================================================================
//
// THE GAP THIS CLOSES. `zero_obligation_run_establishes_unconditional_panic_freedom`
// is evaluated exactly ONCE per function, at the instant that function finishes
// verifying, against the authenticated panic-free identity registry at that time.
// `trust_callee_first_demand_order` is a BEST-EFFORT DFS post-order over the
// pre-steal local call graph, so "the callee registered first" is a strong
// tendency, not an invariant: an edge the adjacency query does not carry, an
// ineligible intermediate body, or a cycle elsewhere in the graph can put a
// caller's single evaluation BEFORE its callee's registration. That caller then
// fails the call conjunct on a callee that is, moments later, proven — and
// nothing ever re-asks. Totalizing a body therefore moves it out of "unknown
// obligation" and into "unblessable zero-obligation", where its own callers keep
// charging against it.
//
// THE FIX IS A LEAST FIXED POINT, NOT A WIDENING. Nothing here relaxes the
// false-proof-#11 belt: a blessed function satisfies the IDENTICAL five-conjunct
// `zero_obligation_registry_gate` predicate that `register_proven_panic_free`
// already requires — the four non-call conjuncts positively established during
// its own verification and recorded as candidacy, and the fifth (every call
// discharged) re-decided by the SAME arm list
// (`all_callee_keys_registry_or_bridge_total`). The only thing that changes is
// WHEN the fifth conjunct is asked: repeatedly, against a monotonically GROWING
// registry, instead of once against whatever the registry held at one moment.
//
// WELL-FOUNDEDNESS IS THE WHOLE SOUNDNESS ARGUMENT, so it is structural:
//
//   * the iteration is seeded from the EXISTING arms (`panic_free_proven_identities`
//     ∪ `abi_nonunwind_total_callees` ∪ `type_total_std_callees` ∪ the two
//     per-caller typed arms ∪ the bridge's own total-call recognizer). Every
//     seed member already carries independent panic-freedom evidence;
//   * a candidate joins at round `k+1` only when ALL of its callees were in the
//     set AS OF ROUND `k`. The round's view (`established`) is FROZEN before the
//     round starts and newly-blessed members are STAGED, merged only after the
//     round completes. So nothing is ever blessed on the strength of an
//     in-progress or assumed membership — this is a least fixed point computed
//     bottom-up, never a greatest fixed point and never optimistic-assume-then-
//     check;
//   * therefore, by induction on the round a member joined, every blessed
//     function's calls all target functions that genuinely cannot panic, and its
//     own body carries zero panic obligations that were positively GENERATED.
//     Blessed ⇒ panic-free;
//   * A CYCLE IS NEVER BLESSED, and needs no special case. Let `r` be the first
//     round at which any member of a call cycle joins. That member's callees
//     were all in the set at round `r-1`; its cycle successor is one of them, so
//     that successor joined strictly before `r` — contradicting minimality of
//     `r`. Direct self-recursion is the one-element instance of exactly the same
//     argument (a candidate is never in its own frozen view: a candidate is by
//     construction absent from the authenticated identity seed, since an item
//     already registered by identity is skipped outright). Refusing the cycle is the CORRECT,
//     fail-closed outcome — two mutually recursive zero-obligation functions may
//     never return at all, and a greatest-fixed-point scheme would bless both;
//   * TERMINATION is monotone growth over a finite candidate set: each non-final
//     round adds at least one member, so `candidates.len() + 1` rounds always
//     suffice. The bound is enforced anyway and, if it is ever hit, the whole
//     invocation blesses NOTHING (fail-closed), rather than publishing a
//     partially-converged set.

/// Root-only call facts computed from this body's own MIR, before its gate.
/// The legacy rendered-path tables are not an identity source for this ledger.
#[derive(Clone, Debug, Default, PartialEq, Eq)]
struct ZeroObligationTypedCallFacts {
    cell_set_total: FxHashSet<String>,
    moved_value_total: FxHashSet<String>,
    tls_try_with_total: FxHashSet<String>,
    typed_self_total: FxHashSet<String>,
}

fn zero_obligation_typed_facts_for_owner(
    state: &CrateVerificationState,
    owner: DefId,
) -> ZeroObligationTypedCallFacts {
    state.zero_obligation_typed_call_facts.get(&owner).cloned().unwrap_or_default()
}

/// One owner-bound candidate whose four non-call premises were established.
/// The optional exported identity is authenticated by item_identity; None is
/// never replaced by a rendered path or granted to a concrete generic instance.
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
struct ZeroObligationBlessingCandidate {
    owner: DefId,
    identity: Option<trust_types::CalleeIdentity>,
    def_path: String,
    callees: Vec<(String, Option<trust_types::CalleeIdentity>)>,
    cell_set_total: FxHashSet<String>,
    moved_value_total: FxHashSet<String>,
    tls_try_with_total: FxHashSet<String>,
    typed_self_total: FxHashSet<String>,
}

/// Merge repeated walks of ONE owner conservatively. Poison is permanent;
/// call duties grow, while typed grants must hold in every recorded walk.
fn merge_zero_obligation_blessing_candidate(
    previous: &Option<ZeroObligationBlessingCandidate>,
    fresh: Option<ZeroObligationBlessingCandidate>,
) -> Option<ZeroObligationBlessingCandidate> {
    let (Some(previous), Some(fresh)) = (previous, fresh) else { return None };
    if previous.owner != fresh.owner || previous.identity != fresh.identity {
        return None;
    }
    let mut merged = previous.clone();
    for callee in fresh.callees {
        if !merged.callees.contains(&callee) {
            merged.callees.push(callee);
        }
    }
    #[allow(rustc::potential_query_instability)]
    {
        merged.cell_set_total.retain(|key| fresh.cell_set_total.contains(key));
        merged.moved_value_total.retain(|key| fresh.moved_value_total.contains(key));
        merged.tls_try_with_total.retain(|key| fresh.tls_try_with_total.contains(key));
        merged.typed_self_total.retain(|key| fresh.typed_self_total.contains(key));
    }
    Some(merged)
}

/// Least fixed point over owner keys. Each round uses a frozen identity view;
/// a candidate's own identity joins only AFTER that round. Display names confer
/// no ordinary callee authority. Generic item candidates can establish their
/// own body only; without an item identity they cannot seed concrete callers.
fn zero_obligation_blessing_least_fixpoint(
    candidates: &[ZeroObligationBlessingCandidate],
    proven: &FxHashSet<String>,
    proven_identities: &FxHashSet<trust_types::CalleeIdentity>,
    abi_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    type_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    max_rounds: usize,
) -> Option<FxHashSet<DefId>> {
    let owners: FxHashSet<DefId> = candidates.iter().map(|candidate| candidate.owner).collect();
    if owners.len() != candidates.len() {
        return None;
    }
    let mut blessed: FxHashSet<DefId> = FxHashSet::default();
    let mut blessed_identities = FxHashSet::default();
    for _round in 0..max_rounds {
        let mut established = proven_identities.clone();
        #[allow(rustc::potential_query_instability)]
        established.extend(blessed_identities.iter().copied());
        let mut staged = Vec::new();
        for candidate in candidates {
            if blessed.contains(&candidate.owner)
                || candidate.identity.is_some_and(|identity| established.contains(&identity))
            {
                continue;
            }
            let callees: Vec<(&str, Option<&trust_types::CalleeIdentity>)> = candidate
                .callees.iter().map(|(callee, identity)| (callee.as_str(), identity.as_ref())).collect();
            if all_callee_keys_registry_or_bridge_total(
                proven,
                &established,
                abi_total,
                type_total,
                &candidate.cell_set_total,
                &candidate.moved_value_total,
                &candidate.tls_try_with_total,
                &candidate.typed_self_total,
                &candidate.def_path,
                &callees,
                false,
            ) {
                staged.push(candidate);
            }
        }
        if staged.is_empty() {
            return Some(blessed);
        }
        for candidate in staged {
            blessed.insert(candidate.owner);
            if let Some(identity) = candidate.identity {
                blessed_identities.insert(identity);
            }
        }
    }
    None
}

/// The exact same Call/TailCall surface the production gate reads. An
/// unclassified terminator poisons candidacy, never an assumed empty surface.
#[cfg(test)]
fn zero_obligation_blessing_call_surface(
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
) -> Option<Vec<(String, Option<trust_types::CalleeIdentity>)>> {
    let sites = extracted_call_sites(func).ok()?;
    Some(sites.iter().map(|site| (site.callee.to_string(), site.callee_identity.copied())).collect())
}

/// The typed finding/candidate insertion seam used by production and tests.
fn record_zero_obligation_blessing_candidate_for_owner(
    candidates: &mut FxHashMap<DefId, Option<ZeroObligationBlessingCandidate>>,
    owner: DefId,
    fresh: Option<ZeroObligationBlessingCandidate>,
) {
    let fresh = fresh.filter(|candidate| candidate.owner == owner);
    let merged = match candidates.get(&owner) {
        None => fresh,
        Some(previous) => merge_zero_obligation_blessing_candidate(previous, fresh),
    };
    candidates.insert(owner, merged);
}

fn record_zero_obligation_blessing_candidacy(
    sess: &Session,
    owner: Option<DefId>,
    identity: Option<trust_types::CalleeIdentity>,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    noncall_premises_hold: bool,
) {
    let Some(owner) = owner else { return };
    let reified = reified_call_identities(sess, Some(owner), func);
    let surface = noncall_premises_hold
        .then(|| {
            let sites = extracted_call_sites(func).ok()?;
            Some(
                sites
                    .iter()
                    .map(|site| {
                        (site.callee.to_string(), bound_call_identity(site, &reified).copied())
                    })
                    .collect(),
            )
        })
        .flatten();
    with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        let facts = zero_obligation_typed_facts_for_owner(state, owner);
        let fresh = surface.map(|callees| ZeroObligationBlessingCandidate {
            owner, identity, def_path: func.def_path.clone(), callees,
            cell_set_total: facts.cell_set_total,
            moved_value_total: facts.moved_value_total,
            tls_try_with_total: facts.tls_try_with_total,
            typed_self_total: facts.typed_self_total,
        });
        record_zero_obligation_blessing_candidate_for_owner(
            &mut state.zero_obligation_blessing_candidates, owner, fresh,
        );
    });
}

/// Withdrawing one body cannot poison or revive a different same-rendered body.
fn revoke_zero_obligation_blessing_candidacy(sess: &Session, owner: Option<DefId>) {
    if let Some(owner) = owner {
        with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
            record_zero_obligation_blessing_candidate_for_owner(
                &mut state.zero_obligation_blessing_candidates, owner, None,
            );
        });
    }
}

/// The unlocked decision may not publish ANY dependent blessing if a candidate
/// was changed or poisoned in the meantime. Re-decide from a fresh snapshot.
fn zero_obligation_blessing_snapshot_is_current(
    candidates: &[ZeroObligationBlessingCandidate],
    current: &FxHashMap<DefId, Option<ZeroObligationBlessingCandidate>>,
) -> bool {
    candidates.iter().all(|candidate| {
        current.get(&candidate.owner).is_some_and(|value| value.as_ref() == Some(candidate))
    })
}

/// Atomic installation boundary shared by the real propagator and regressions.
/// A stale/poisoned snapshot cannot retire any body's finding or seed a caller.
fn install_zero_obligation_blessings(
    state: &mut CrateVerificationState,
    candidates: &[ZeroObligationBlessingCandidate],
    blessed: &FxHashSet<DefId>,
    proven_identities: &FxHashSet<trust_types::CalleeIdentity>,
    abi_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    type_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
) -> bool {
    if !zero_obligation_blessing_snapshot_is_current(candidates, &state.zero_obligation_blessing_candidates)
        || !state.panic_free_proven_identities.is_superset(proven_identities)
        || !state.abi_nonunwind_total_callees.is_superset(abi_total)
        || !state.type_total_std_callees.is_superset(type_total)
    {
        return false;
    }
    for candidate in candidates {
        if !blessed.contains(&candidate.owner) {
            continue;
        }
        // Only this exact owner's negative finding is superseded. The
        // authenticated identity, if any, is the authority for callers.
        state.zero_obligation_undischarged_call_funcs.remove(&candidate.owner);
        if let Some(identity) = candidate.identity {
            state.panic_free_proven_identities.insert(identity);
        }
        state.panic_free_proven_funcs.insert(candidate.def_path.clone());
        if trust_env_var_present("TRUST_PF_REG_TRACE") {
            eprintln!("[PF_BLESS] fn={} owner={:?} blessed by the zero-obligation fixpoint",
                candidate.def_path, candidate.owner);
        }
    }
    true
}

fn propagate_zero_obligation_blessings(sess: &Session) {
    let (candidates, proven, proven_identities, abi_total, type_total) =
        with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
            #[allow(rustc::potential_query_instability)]
            let candidates: Vec<ZeroObligationBlessingCandidate> = state
                .zero_obligation_blessing_candidates.values().filter_map(Clone::clone).collect();
            (candidates, state.panic_free_proven_funcs.clone(),
                state.panic_free_proven_identities.clone(),
                state.abi_nonunwind_total_callees.clone(), state.type_total_std_callees.clone())
        });
    if candidates.is_empty() {
        return;
    }
    let max_rounds = candidates.len().saturating_add(1);
    let Some(blessed) = zero_obligation_blessing_least_fixpoint(
        &candidates, &proven, &proven_identities, &abi_total, &type_total, max_rounds,
    ) else {
        debug!("Trust: R40 owner-keyed blessing did not converge; blessing nothing");
        return;
    };
    if blessed.is_empty() {
        return;
    }
    with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        install_zero_obligation_blessings(
            state, &candidates, &blessed, &proven_identities, &abi_total, &type_total,
        );
    });
}

/// REGISTRY-ONLY core, retained as the regression boundary for the sets alone.
/// The production gate is [`all_calls_registry_or_bridge_total`], which adds the
/// bridge's own audited total-call recognizer as a SEPARATE, separately-tested
/// arm — deliberately kept out of this function. Ordinary verified callees are
/// authorized only by `proven_identities`; the display-name mirror remains an
/// input solely so production-shaped tests can prove it has no fallback power.
/// ABI/type-total names are separate compiler-authenticated grants.
#[cfg(test)]
fn all_calls_target_proven_or_abi_total_in_sets(
    // Deliberately non-authoritative. Keeping the production display-name
    // mirror in this pure boundary lets tests pin that a populated name cannot
    // rescue an absent or mismatched identity.
    _proven_names: &FxHashSet<String>,
    proven_identities: &FxHashSet<trust_types::CalleeIdentity>,
    abi_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    type_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
) -> bool {
    // Trust (NEW-TAILCALL): the production call surface (`extracted_call_sites`):
    // the same calls, tail calls included, and the same refusal of an unclassified
    // terminator.
    let Ok(sites) = extracted_call_sites(func) else {
        return false;
    };
    for ExtractedCallSite { callee, callee_identity, .. } in sites {
        if identity_discharges_call(proven_identities, callee_identity) {
            continue;
        }
        // The ABI set is populated only for compiler-authenticated bodyless
        // foreign imports. Local Rust extern functions still require an exact
        // ordinary proof identity and remain absent from this set.
        //
        // The TYPE-TOTAL set is populated only by
        // `callee_is_type_verified_total_std_iterator`, from real `ty::Ty` +
        // `CrateNum` — never a user attribute or a rendered path. It does NOT
        // discharge the callee's OTHER obligations: the caller's arithmetic /
        // bounds / div VCs remain separate and must independently be Proved for
        // the interprocedural route to fire (see the `others_all_proved`
        // conjunct at the call site), which is what keeps a total-iterator loop
        // with an overflowing body refutable.
        if !abi_total.contains(callee) && !type_total.contains(callee) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    true
}

/// Trust (Part C — Path-2b conditional discharge). Like
/// [`all_calls_target_proven_panic_free`], but ADDITIONALLY accepts a call to a
/// `panic_free_under_precondition(callee, P)` target WHEN this caller PROVES the
/// callee's precondition `P[args]` at that exact call site — the GUARANTEE side
/// of assume-guarantee, discharged by a clean-kernel `certify_vc` refutation of
/// the callsite obligation `¬P[σ]` (UNSAT ⟺ `P` holds on every reachable path to
/// the call), NEVER by assumption.
///
/// FAIL-CLOSED. An unregistered callee, a callee whose precondition cannot be
/// faithfully rendered at the site (arity mismatch / unsupported σ ⇒ a
/// non-`Precondition` VC), or a callee precondition the caller does not provably
/// establish (`certify_vc` returns `None` ⇒ `¬P[σ]` SAT/undecided) all leave the
/// caller REFUTABLE (returns `false`). Every under-precondition callee this
/// function calls must have had its precondition proven at every site (full
/// coverage), so a silently-dropped site VC cannot pass.
///
/// The callsite VC carries the caller's OWN `#[requires]` and path guards as
/// hypotheses (`generate_callsite_precondition_vcs` ⇒ `stable_caller_preconditions`),
/// so a caller with a `#[requires]` proves the callee's `P` only RELATIVE to its
/// own contract — which is exactly why such a caller then registers UNDER ITS OWN
/// precondition (never unconditionally). A caller with no `#[requires]` proves
/// `P[args]` from constants / its own path facts alone (an all-input proof), so
/// it is genuinely unconditionally panic-free.
/// Round-4 (pf call gate, sort-comparator seam): `true` IFF the call's argument
/// list carries EXACTLY ONE closure-typed operand — a whole-local `Copy`/`Move`
/// whose declared type in the CALLER's extracted body is `Ty::Closure` (or
/// `&Ty::Closure`) — and the session registry records that closure's bundled
/// body as a classifier-certified total-ord-key comparator (`Some(&true)`).
///
/// Identity chain, no name heuristics: the closure NAME read here is the
/// extractor-stamped def_path on the caller-local's `Ty::Closure { name }`; the
/// registry key is the SAME def_path rendered from the rustc
/// `DefKind::Closure` DefId at native-bundle build; the classified body is the
/// SAME extraction snapshot the lanes verify. FAIL-CLOSED on: a projected or
/// constant closure operand (unresolvable here — includes the non-capturing
/// ZST-constant closure form), zero or multiple closure-typed args (ambiguous
/// comparator), an absent registry entry (closure never bundled/classified),
/// or a poisoned/`false` verdict. Declining never discharges — the caller
/// keeps the call's obligation.
fn sort_call_sole_closure_comparator_is_total(
    sess: &Session,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    args: &[trust_types::Operand],
) -> bool {
    let mut closure_names: Vec<&str> = Vec::new();
    for arg in args {
        let (trust_types::Operand::Copy(place) | trust_types::Operand::Move(place)) = arg else {
            continue;
        };
        if !place.projections.is_empty() {
            continue;
        }
        let Some(decl) = func.body.locals.iter().find(|decl| decl.index == place.local) else {
            continue;
        };
        let closure_name = match &decl.ty {
            trust_types::Ty::Closure { name, .. } => Some(name.as_str()),
            trust_types::Ty::Ref { inner, .. } => match inner.as_ref() {
                trust_types::Ty::Closure { name, .. } => Some(name.as_str()),
                _ => None,
            },
            _ => None,
        };
        if let Some(name) = closure_name {
            closure_names.push(name);
        }
    }
    let [sole_comparator] = closure_names.as_slice() else {
        return false;
    };
    with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        state.total_ord_comparator_closures.get(*sole_comparator) == Some(&true)
    })
}

/// R23 (writer). Record the interprocedural panic-freedom seam's own
/// UNCONDITIONAL gate result for this function. AND-merged on re-insert, so a
/// disagreeing repeat walk collapses to the conservative answer. See the
/// `interprocedural_pf_call_closure_funcs` field doc for the full argument.
fn record_interprocedural_pf_call_closure(sess: &Session, def_path: &str, discharged: bool) {
    with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        let entry = state
            .interprocedural_pf_call_closure_funcs
            .entry(def_path.to_string())
            .or_insert(discharged);
        *entry &= discharged;
    });
}

/// R23 (reader). The recorded gate result, or `false` when NO lane computed it
/// for this function. ABSENT MUST READ `false`: absence of evidence is not
/// evidence, and the sole consumer
/// (`install_interprocedural_callee_closure_authorities`) mints a proof
/// authority from it.
fn recorded_interprocedural_pf_call_closure(sess: &Session, def_path: &str) -> bool {
    with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        state.interprocedural_pf_call_closure_funcs.get(def_path).copied().unwrap_or(false)
    })
}

/// R18 — the DISCHARGE lane's UNCONDITIONAL-TOTALITY arms, as a pure core.
///
/// Returns the NAME of the arm that discharges `callee`, or `None` when no arm
/// does (the caller then falls through to the Part-C
/// `panic_free_under_precondition` lookup and, failing that, fails closed).
/// Kept `Session`-free so the membership discipline is directly
/// regression-testable — the same shape
/// `all_calls_target_proven_or_abi_total_in_sets` gives the zero-obligation lane,
/// and the shape false proofs #11 and #12 were both finally pinned in.
///
/// The two SESSION-DEPENDENT admissions deliberately stay in the caller: the
/// slice-sort comparator seam (which resolves a closure through the caller's own
/// locals) and the Part-C precondition lookup (which is not an admission at all
/// — it defers to a per-call-site proof). Order among the arms below is
/// behaviour-irrelevant: every one of them is an ADMIT, so which one answers
/// first changes only the trace string.
///
/// WHY `tls_try_with_total` BELONGS HERE — AND WHY IT IS NOT A COPY OF THE
/// ZERO-ROW LANE'S ARGUMENT.
///
/// The claim the R16/R17 const-init-TLS collector establishes is a property of
/// ONE CALL SITE IN ONE CALLER: "this `LocalKey::<T>::try_with::<F, R>` call
/// cannot panic", proven from (1) the CONST-EVALUATED accessor being a
/// structurally call-free/assert-free/drop-free/asm-free closure, (2) the
/// closure argument's own membership in `panic_free_proven_funcs`, and (3) the
/// `try_with` glue drift pin. That claim does not mention, and does not depend
/// on, which lane later reads it. Both lanes ask the SAME question about calls,
/// so the grant means the SAME thing in both — no re-justification of the
/// premises is needed, and none is offered.
///
/// What IS different is the pairing, and it had to be checked separately:
///
///  * The zero-row lane pairs the call gate with `native_run_is_vacuously_clean`.
///    That conjunct is also its DRIFT CATCH: if the collector granted a site but
///    the bridge did NOT erase it, the un-erased call emits an absent-callee
///    obligation, the run stops being vacuously clean, and the lane never fires.
///    The `Cell::set` arm's third soundness reason is exactly that argument.
///  * This lane has no `native_run_is_vacuously_clean` conjunct — but it has the
///    SAME drift catch in a per-call form, which is strictly tighter. A granted
///    site the bridge did not erase emits the
///    `[trust-absent-callee-assumption] call to absent callee …` row; that row's
///    message does not start with `panic freedom` and it is not the full-lane
///    restatement, so it is not a pf row — it lands in the `others_all_proved`
///    set of BOTH consumers, unproved, and the discharge does not happen.
///    MEASURED on deployed driver `sha256:48f2e7437873c1ee` (fixture
///    `R18-frontA-fixture.rs`, `-Z trust-policy=advisory`): that is precisely the
///    row `c2_ensure_healthy` and `CONSTK::{constant#0}::{closure#0}` carry.
///
/// So the two premises can never be "two lanes agreeing with each other": the
/// grant is checked against an independent conjunct here as well.
///
/// THE RESIDUAL, STATED RATHER THAN ASSUMED AWAY. This lane concludes
/// panic-freedom from "every OTHER row is Proved" + "every direct
/// `Terminator::Call` is total". For a function whose row set is JUST the pf
/// catch-all — `poisoned` is exactly that — the first conjunct ranges over the
/// EMPTY set, so the whole weight rests on the call gate. That standing
/// assumption is PRE-EXISTING (it is what already licenses every
/// `callee_is_recognized_total_no_panic` discharge) and R18 neither creates nor
/// widens it; but R18 does route more functions past it, so it was MEASURED
/// rather than argued. On driver `sha256:48f2e7437873c1ee` every non-call panic
/// source tested still surfaces as its own unproved row, which makes
/// `others_all_proved` false and blocks the discharge:
///   * a panicking user `Drop` beside a GRANTED `try_with` (fixture
///     `s11_panicking_drop_glue`) carries its own trust-drop-glue-assumption row
///     naming the `Adt`;
///   * an un-erased absent callee beside a granted call carries
///     `[trust-absent-callee-assumption] …` (`s9_mixed_absent`, `s10_mixed_local`);
///   * an ungrounded spec model carries `unsupported MIR SpecModelUngrounded`.
/// The `UnsupportedDirectCallSummary` diagnostic that `poisoned` DOES carry is,
/// by construction in `translate_chc`, emitted only for a CALL — the one panic
/// source this gate is entitled to override.
///
/// DOWNSTREAM CONSEQUENCE IS IDENTICAL, so no new authority class is minted.
/// The zero-row lane's grant ends in `register_proven_panic_free`; this lane's
/// grant ends in the interprocedural pf discharge, whose
/// `InterproceduralCalleeClosure` authority is admitted by
/// `results_establish_unconditional_panic_freedom` into the SAME registry. This
/// arm therefore widens WHICH functions reach that registry, never WHAT the
/// registry means.
///
/// ORDER-INDEPENDENCE IS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. The record is written by the
/// caller's OWN bundle build. On the same driver, for the `poisoned` shape:
/// `[TLS_DISCHARGE] BUNDLE fn=P::p_poisoned admitted=1` is emitted BEFORE
/// `[ASSERT_P2] fn=P::p_poisoned` and `[PF_CALLGATE] fn=P::p_poisoned`.
///
/// FAIL-CLOSED, AND NOT A NAME RECOGNIZER. `tls_try_with_total` is THIS
/// caller's own per-call record (`CrateVerificationState::
/// const_init_tls_try_with_calls`, keyed by `func.def_path`), read from the SAME
/// field the zero-row lane reads — never a parallel string list that could drift
/// out of sync, which is how two earlier false proofs were born. An absent
/// function yields an EMPTY set, which admits nothing: absence of evidence fails
/// closed, and a premise over the empty set entails nothing (false proof #12).
/// A key that declined at ANY occurrence in this caller was removed from the set
/// by the collector and never restored, so a const-init key's grant can never
/// license a LAZY key's call even when the two render into nearly identical
/// callee strings (false proof #13 — MEASURED: the two `Cell<bool>` sites of
/// `s7_mixed_keys` differ only in the closure def-path
/// `{closure@lib.rs:59:29: 59:32}` vs `{closure@lib.rs:60:28: 60:31}`, and the
/// lazy one is absent from the set).
///
/// DELIBERATELY NOT ADDED HERE: `type_total_std_callees` and R15's
/// `drop_absent_cell_set_calls`. Both would be sound by the identical argument,
/// and neither has a measured target — every ny-cert `Cell::set` site
/// (`poison`, `set_poisoned_for_test`, `DeadlineGuard::install`, `Drop::drop`
/// and their closures) is a `let _ = KEY.try_with(|p| p.set(..))` shape that
/// already routes through the ZERO-ROW lane where both arms exist. An arm with
/// no target is pure added surface.
fn discharge_call_arm_admits(
    proven: &FxHashSet<String>,
    abi_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    tls_try_with_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    // LEVER 3 — THIS caller's own per-call-site moved-value-argument record. Unlike
    // the two flat registries above it is a TYPED record and could not be expressed
    // as a name list; see `CrateVerificationState::drop_absent_moved_value_arg_calls`
    // and the zero-obligation gate's `moved-value-nodrop` arm for the argument, which
    // is the same one and is decided by the same collector — never a second premise.
    //
    // MEASURED TARGET (ny .gatelogs/R40.log, `TRUST_PF_MATCH_TRACE=1`): this seam
    // reports `Option::<ny_cert::rational::Rat>::unwrap_or`,
    // `Option::<usize>::unwrap_or` and `Option::<&[u8]>::unwrap_or` as
    // `UNREGISTERED callee blocks discharge` in 6+ functions, so unlike
    // `type_total_std_callees` / `drop_absent_cell_set_calls` (deliberately still
    // absent here) this arm is not pure added surface.
    moved_value_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    // R41 — THIS caller's own per-call-site instantiated-`Self` record. Same typed,
    // per-caller, self-poisoning discipline as `moved_value_total` above; see
    // `CrateVerificationState::typed_self_total_std_calls` for why the four
    // spellings it covers CANNOT be decided by a callee string.
    typed_self_total: &FxHashSet<String>,
    callee: &str,
) -> Option<&'static str> {
    if proven.contains(callee) {
        return Some("registry-proven");
    }
    if abi_total.contains(callee) {
        return Some("abi-total");
    }
    if trust_ir_bridge::callee_is_recognized_total_no_panic(callee) {
        return Some("bridge-total");
    }
    if tls_try_with_total.contains(callee) {
        return Some("tls-const-init");
    }
    if moved_value_total.contains(callee) {
        return Some("moved-value-nodrop");
    }
    if typed_self_total.contains(callee) {
        return Some("typed-self-total");
    }
    None
}

/// The IDENTITY arm of the panic-freedom call gate: does this call site's
/// `DefId`+substs identity match one this session registered as proven fully
/// panic-free?
///
/// Deliberately NOT folded into [`discharge_call_arm_admits`]. That function is
/// the audited pure core of the STRING admissions, and its adversarial tests are
/// false-proof regression pins whose meaning depends on it staying a
/// name-and-set-membership decision. This is a different key space entirely, and
/// mixing the two would make it possible to read a passing name-only test as
/// evidence about the identity arm. Same reason `sort-comparator` and
/// `fmt-format-total` sit outside it.
///
/// # Why this is strictly stronger than the string arm it precedes
///
/// Both halves are `TyCtxt`-derived and neither is source-spellable:
///
/// * the registry half is minted in `register_proven_panic_free` from the
///   VERIFIED BODY's own `DefId`, inside the same axiom gate, in the same call
///   that writes the rendered key — so it can hold no function the rendered
///   registry does not also hold;
/// * the call half is the call's `callee_identity` (`Terminator::Call` or
///   `Terminator::TailCall`), stamped by the
///   extractor from the `FnDef` constant it read when it rendered the callee
///   string, so the display spelling and the decision key cannot come from
///   different things.
///
/// FALSE PROOF #14 CANNOT REACH IT. The attack is a generic parameter named for
/// a primitive (`fn attack<u64: TryFrom<u32>>(..)` calling `<u64 as
/// TryFrom<u32>>::try_from`), which renders a callee string byte-identical to
/// the honest one while resolving to a body that panics on every input. A call
/// whose generic arguments mention a `ty::Param` is refused an identity by
/// `trust_mir_extract::convert::callee_identity_for`, so the forgery arrives
/// here as `None` and this returns `false` — whatever it renders as. The
/// identity is never reconstructed from the string.
///
/// FAIL-CLOSED. `None` on either side admits nothing. An artifact serialized
/// before `callee_identity` existed, an indirect call, a call inside a generic
/// body, and a synthetic test terminator all land there and fall through to the
/// existing string arms exactly as they did before this arm existed — this can
/// only ever ADD a discharge, never remove or weaken one.
fn identity_discharges_call(
    proven_identities: &FxHashSet<trust_types::CalleeIdentity>,
    callee_identity: Option<&trust_types::CalleeIdentity>,
) -> bool {
    callee_identity.is_some_and(|identity| proven_identities.contains(identity))
}

/// Trust (NEW-EXEMPT-SPLIT, 2026-09-28): whether the refute lane proved EVERY
/// `assert!`/`panic!` failure block of `func` unreachable — the condition under
/// which [`all_calls_discharge_panic_free`] and [`grounded_call_premise`] may
/// skip those blocks' panic-entrypoint calls (`panic_calls_proven_unreachable`),
/// and under which the grounded catch-all's disjunction of proved candidates is
/// the residual of EVERY such block.
///
/// Decided PER TARGET BLOCK from the generator's own record
/// (`trust_vcgen::AssertRefutationCoverage`): each target block must have
/// emitted its complete per-path split and `candidate_proved` must hold for
/// every part. It was a COUNT — `#proved candidates == #target blocks` — and a
/// count is not a match: a block reached by two paths emits two candidates,
/// a block whose guard the generator cannot ground emits none and is counted
/// nowhere, so the two proved paths of one block balanced the other, dropped
/// block. Measured on stage 1 built from `76d76ee8d4`: a body with a two-path
/// `assert!(x != 50)` block and a loop-carried `assert!(t != 7)` block published
/// its catch-all `proved` (the kernel certified the grounded disjunction, which
/// names only the first block's paths), its caller was proved on that
/// registration, and a binary calling it with `7` built rc 0 under
/// `-Ztrust-policy=certify` and panicked
/// (`tests/ui/trust/panic_callee_exemption_is_per_target_block.rs`). A proved
/// coroutine-sentinel candidate — appended to the same candidate list, but no
/// target block's — covers nothing here either: two of them once balanced two
/// declined `assert!` blocks of an `async fn` body.
///
/// Also required: every target block of the RAW body is proved. The generator
/// walks the tail-call projection, whose target blocks include the raw body's
/// under the same ids, so this is already implied; it is checked because the
/// raw body is what the call gate walks.
///
/// Trust (2026-09-27, NEW-TAILCALL): and NO TAIL CALL THE REFUTATION LANE READS
/// AS A TARGET. The generator walks the tail-call projection
/// (`with_tail_calls_expanded()`, where `become p(..)` is an ordinary `Call`),
/// so `become m::panic(..)` (any callee the target recognizer matches, here a
/// user fn merely NAMED `panic`) is a target block of the projection that the
/// raw body does not have. Measured before the per-block rule
/// (`tests/ui/trust/tail_call_callee_panic_is_not_discharged.rs`, revision
/// `tailcount`): that candidate's proof stood in for a SKIPPED `assert!` target,
/// the exemption came on with the assert unproven, and a binary built rc 0 under
/// `-Ztrust-policy=certify` panicked. The per-block rule above already refuses
/// that shape (the skipped `assert!` block is a declined target block); this
/// conjunct is kept as well, fail-closed, so a body with such a tail call gets
/// no exemption at all and its tail call is decided by the call arms like every
/// other call.
fn panic_callee_exemption_holds(
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    refuted: u32,
    left_unknown: u32,
    coverage: &trust_vcgen::AssertRefutationCoverage,
    candidate_proved: &[bool],
) -> bool {
    let proved = |index: usize| candidate_proved.get(index).copied().unwrap_or(false);
    let target_blocks = |function: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction| {
        function
            .body
            .blocks
            .iter()
            .filter(|block| trust_vcgen::is_assert_refutation_target_block(block))
            .count()
    };
    let tail_call_targets = target_blocks(&func.with_tail_calls_expanded()) != target_blocks(func);
    refuted == 0
        && left_unknown == 0
        && !tail_call_targets
        && coverage.every_target_block_proved(proved)
        && func
            .body
            .blocks
            .iter()
            .filter(|block| trust_vcgen::is_assert_refutation_target_block(block))
            .all(|block| coverage.target_block_proved(block.id, proved))
}

fn all_calls_discharge_panic_free(
    sess: &Session,
    owner: Option<DefId>,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    panic_calls_proven_unreachable: bool,
    // Trust (T3 L4, C07p): the body's VC context; the call-site precondition rows this
    // gate certifies are interned into it through the row oracle.
    rows: &mut trust_types::vc_context::VcContext,
) -> bool {
    let proven = with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| state.panic_free_proven_funcs.clone());
    // The `DefId`+substs view of the SAME registry, written by the same one
    // writer under the same one gate. See `identity_discharges_call`.
    let proven_identities =
        with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| state.panic_free_proven_identities.clone());
    let abi_total =
        with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| state.abi_nonunwind_total_callees.clone());
    let under_pre =
        with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| state.panic_free_under_precondition.clone());
    // R18 — THIS function's OWN const-init-TLS `try_with` call keys, read from the
    // SAME authenticated per-caller record the zero-obligation lane reads
    // (`all_calls_target_proven_panic_free`). Absent function ⇒ empty set ⇒ the
    // arm can never fire. See `discharge_call_arm_admits` for the soundness
    // argument, which is NOT the zero-row lane's.
    let tls_try_with_total = with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        state.const_init_tls_try_with_calls.get(func.def_path.as_str()).cloned().unwrap_or_default()
    });
    // LEVER 3 — THIS function's OWN drop-glue-free moved-value-argument call keys,
    // read from the SAME authenticated per-caller record the zero-obligation lane
    // reads. Absent function ⇒ empty set ⇒ the arm can never fire.
    let moved_value_total = with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        state
            .drop_absent_moved_value_arg_calls
            .get(func.def_path.as_str())
            .cloned()
            .unwrap_or_default()
    });
    // R41 — THIS function's OWN instantiated-`Self` total std trait call keys, read
    // from the SAME authenticated per-caller record the zero-obligation lane reads.
    // Absent function ⇒ empty set ⇒ the arm can never fire. Trust (2026-09-26):
    // together with its own sysroot-census-total keys (`per_caller_typed_total_calls`).
    let typed_self_total = with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
        per_caller_typed_total_calls(state, func.def_path.as_str())
    });

    // First pass: every call must target a proven / abi-total callee OR a
    // panic_free_under_precondition callee. Any other callee ⇒ fail closed.
    // Collect the under-precondition callees this function calls into a summary
    // DB so their preconditions can be rendered at each call site below.
    //
    // PANIC-CALLEE EXEMPTION (`panic_calls_proven_unreachable`): a user
    // `assert!`/`panic!` failure block terminates in a call to a panic
    // entrypoint (`core::panicking::panic` / `panic_fmt` / `assert_failed` …)
    // that can never be a registered callee. When the caller has ALREADY proven
    // — via the `-full` refute lane — that EVERY such failure block is
    // unreachable, skipping exactly those blocks is sound: each exempted call
    // sits on a path proven infeasible for every (contract-legal) input. The
    // flag is set ONLY by `panic_callee_exemption_holds`, PER TARGET BLOCK:
    // every target block must have had its complete per-path candidate split
    // emitted and every part proved. (Trust, NEW-EXEMPT-SPLIT: it was a COUNT,
    // `#proved candidates == #target blocks`, and the two proved paths of one
    // block balanced a second block the generator had silently dropped.) The
    // exemption predicate is the refutation lane's OWN exported recognizer
    // (`trust_vcgen::is_assert_refutation_target_block`), the one its candidate
    // loop calls, so the exempted set can never exceed the proven-covered set;
    // the `unreachable!()` sentinel is excluded by that recognizer and keeps
    // blocking the gate (fail-closed).
    // Trust (NEW-TAILCALL): it is also refused to a body with a tail call the
    // refutation lane reads as a target, and a tail call is never exempted (see
    // the loop below).
    let trace = trust_env_var_present("TRUST_PF_MATCH_TRACE");
    let mut db = trust_vcgen::SummaryDatabase::new();
    let mut under_pre_callees: FxHashSet<String> = FxHashSet::default();
    // Trust (2026-09-27, NEW-TAILCALL): the call surface, tail calls included
    // (see `extracted_call_sites`). Before it, this loop matched
    // `Terminator::Call` alone, so `become boom(x)` was never examined and
    // `tail`'s catch-all was discharged with `calls_discharged=true` over a
    // callee that panics. A terminator nobody classified is a call nothing
    // examined: fail closed. A tail call to an under-precondition callee joins
    // `db` like any other: `generate_callsite_precondition_vcs` renders `P[args]`
    // at `Terminator::TailCall` sites too, so the second pass still proves it.
    let Ok(sites) = extracted_call_sites(func) else {
        if trace {
            eprintln!(
                "[PF_CALLGATE] fn={} unclassified terminator blocks discharge",
                func.def_path,
            );
        }
        return false;
    };
    let reified = reified_call_identities(sess, owner, func);
    for site in &sites {
        let ExtractedCallSite { block, callee, args, tail, .. } = *site;
        let callee_identity = bound_call_identity(site, &reified);
        // Trust (NEW-TAILCALL): A TAIL CALL IS NEVER EXEMPTED. The exemption
        // covers the failure blocks the refute lane proved unreachable;
        // `become f(..)` is not the assert/panic failure shape that lane is
        // written for, so it is decided by the arms below like every other call —
        // whatever `is_assert_refutation_target_block` answers for it now or
        // later.
        if !tail
            && panic_calls_proven_unreachable
            && trust_vcgen::is_assert_refutation_target_block(block)
        {
            continue;
        }
        // THE IDENTITY ARM, checked FIRST because it is the strongest one:
        // rustc's `DefId` + region-erased substs on BOTH sides, no string
        // anywhere in the decision. This is where the R43 key desync closes —
        // `Parser::<'a>::peek` registered against `Parser::<'_>::peek` queried
        // are the same `DefId` with substs that erase to the same digest, so
        // they meet HERE while the `registry-proven` string arm below still
        // misses. See `identity_discharges_call` for why an absent identity
        // (false proof #14's forgery among them) admits nothing.
        if identity_discharges_call(&proven_identities, callee_identity) {
            if trace {
                eprintln!(
                    "[PF_CALLGATE] fn={} arm=registry-proven-identity discharges callee: \
                     {callee}",
                    func.def_path,
                );
            }
            continue;
        }
        // The set-membership admissions, in one audited pure core (see
        // `discharge_call_arm_admits`):
        //  * `registry-proven` / `abi-total` — the Session registries;
        //  * `bridge-total` — Trust (recognizer REUSE, no new allowlist): a
        //    callee the bridge's EXISTING audited string-only total-call
        //    surfaces already recognize as TOTAL for the CHC lane's identical
        //    claim (EXACT_TOTAL incl. its hardened orphan-safe arms, the F5
        //    closed allowlist, the extractor's rustc-`Ty`-authenticated
        //    `?`-desugar / spawn-namesafe stamps, and the
        //    `core::num::wrapping_{add,sub,mul}` modular-BinOp family). This
        //    asserts nothing new — the pf CHC itself models these exact calls
        //    as non-panicking; the seam refusing them was an inconsistency;
        //  * `tls-const-init` (R18) — THIS caller's own authenticated
        //    const-init-TLS `try_with` record.
        // Everything unrecognized falls through to the sort seam and the
        // Part-C registry below, and fails closed.
        if let Some(arm) = discharge_call_arm_admits(
            &proven,
            &abi_total,
            &tls_try_with_total,
            &moved_value_total,
            &typed_self_total,
            callee,
        ) {
            if trace {
                eprintln!(
                    "[PF_CALLGATE] fn={} arm={arm} discharges callee: {callee}",
                    func.def_path,
                );
            }
            continue;
        }
        // Round-4 (FIX B reuse — sort-comparator seam): a closure-carrying
        // std slice sort family call (`sort_by`/`sort_unstable_by`/
        // `sort_by_key`/`sort_unstable_by_key`) discharges IFF its SOLE
        // closure-typed argument resolves — through the caller's OWN
        // extracted local types, the same resolution the CHC lane's
        // `try_resolve_hof_closure` uses — to a bundled closure body the
        // bridge's audited total-ord-key comparator classifier POSITIVELY
        // certified (session registry populated at native-bundle build).
        // For such a comparator the closure body is structurally
        // panic-free and std's `panic_on_ord_violation` is unreachable
        // (the CHC lane's identical `total_ord_comparator` claim at its
        // sort gate); allocation abort stays outside the modeled panic
        // set. Fail-closed on every other shape: non-sort callee,
        // zero/multiple/projected/constant closure args, an unbundled or
        // unclassified closure, or a classifier-rejected comparator — the
        // call then keeps its obligation below.
        if trust_ir_bridge::callee_is_slice_sort_comparator_family(callee)
            && sort_call_sole_closure_comparator_is_total(sess, func, args)
        {
            if trace {
                eprintln!(
                    "[PF_CALLGATE] fn={} arm=sort-comparator discharges callee: {callee}",
                    func.def_path,
                );
            }
            continue;
        }
        // R23 (fmt-format-total seam — LANE RECONCILIATION, not a new claim).
        // An `alloc::fmt::format` call site the bridge's audited per-call-site
        // predicate accepts is ALREADY erased from the CHC error-rule set by
        // the very lowering that built this function's bundle
        // (`fmt_format_dispatch_is_total`); this gate, which until now only
        // consulted NAME-ONLY surfaces, was reporting the same call
        // `UNREGISTERED` and refusing the registration. That divergence made
        // being modeled panic-free by the native lane STRICTLY WORSE for a
        // function than being left Unknown. The export
        // (`fmt_format_call_is_total`) is the SAME decision procedure — one
        // shared core, no second list to drift — run with a strictly
        // RESTRICTED operand typer, so this arm can only ever accept a
        // SUBSET of what the CHC model already accepted.
        //
        // WHAT THE PREDICATE ESTABLISHES (per call site, never per callee):
        // the dispatch is `alloc::fmt::format` — the ONE `format!` backend
        // whose `String` sink is infallible (`_print`/`core::fmt::write`/
        // `write_fmt` with a caller-selected writer all fail closed); arg 0
        // backward-slices to a UNIQUE, non-reseatable, DOMINATING `Arguments`
        // constructor in THIS body; the template is a readable constant whose
        // every placeholder is the bare `0xC0` default byte (restoring the
        // retired `new_v1`-vs-`new_v1_formatted` boundary — width/precision/
        // fill are refused); and every packed element is an
        // `Argument::new_display` packer (`new_debug`/`from_usize`/
        // `new_pointer`/hex/octal refused) over a value whose type passes
        // `is_display_total_std_ty` (primitives / `str` / an exact-match
        // element-free std leaf / an authenticated arbitrary-precision
        // `Ratio`). A USER ADT falls through that gate to `false`, so an
        // arbitrary user `Display::fmt` — the only way user code could run
        // under `format!` — is EXCLUDED, which is what makes the transitive
        // `.expect("a formatting trait implementation returned an error")`
        // unreachable. Allocation abort stays outside the modeled panic set,
        // the crate-wide convention.
        //
        // FAIL-CLOSED at every rejection: the call then keeps its obligation
        // exactly as today.
        if trust_ir_bridge::fmt_format_call_is_total(
            &func.body.blocks,
            &func.body.locals,
            block.id,
            callee,
            args,
        ) {
            if trace {
                eprintln!(
                    "[PF_CALLGATE] fn={} arm=fmt-format-total discharges callee: {callee}",
                    func.def_path,
                );
            }
            continue;
        }
        match under_pre.get(callee) {
            Some(summary) => {
                db.insert(summary.clone());
                under_pre_callees.insert(callee.to_string());
            }
            None => {
                if trace {
                    eprintln!(
                        "[PF_CALLGATE] fn={} UNREGISTERED callee blocks discharge: {callee}",
                        func.def_path,
                    );
                }
                return false;
            }
        }
    }
    if under_pre_callees.is_empty() {
        // Every call is unconditionally proven / abi-total (or the function has
        // no calls) — identical to `all_calls_target_proven_panic_free`.
        return true;
    }

    // Second pass: PROVE each under-precondition callee's `P[args]` at every call
    // site. `generate_callsite_precondition_vcs` emits a `Precondition{callee}`
    // VC per (call site, precondition clause) ONLY for callees present in `db`;
    // `certify_vc` returns `Some` iff `¬P[σ]` is clean-kernel UNSAT (P proven).
    let site_vcs = trust_vcgen::generate_callsite_precondition_vcs_rows(func, &db, rows);
    let mut proven_callees: FxHashSet<String> = FxHashSet::default();
    for vc in &site_vcs {
        let trust_types::VcKind::Precondition { callee } = &vc.kind else {
            // A non-`Precondition` VC for a db callee (arity mismatch / malformed
            // TrustIr / un-renderable σ) means `P[args]` could not be faithfully
            // formed — fail closed rather than treat the call as discharged.
            return false;
        };
        if !under_pre_callees.contains(callee) {
            return false;
        }
        if trust_certify::certify_vc(vc).is_none() {
            // `¬P[σ]` is SAT or undecided: the caller does NOT provably establish
            // the callee's precondition at this call site — no discharge.
            return false;
        }
        proven_callees.insert(callee.clone());
    }
    // Full coverage: every under-precondition callee this function calls must
    // have had its precondition rendered AND proven (a callee with a precondition
    // but no emitted VC would be absent here). Fail closed on any gap.
    proven_callees == under_pre_callees
}

/// Trust (full-lane pf-restatement identity): whether `obligation` is THE
/// synthesized whole-function panic-freedom catch-all for `func`, authenticated
/// by RECOMPUTATION — the exact canonical obligation id, the unique
/// panic-freedom marker value, and the function-identity obligation digest
/// (`default_trust_mc_function_obligation_digest` over def-path + span +
/// the pf payload) recomputed from the live `func` — never a message substring.
/// Only `synthesize_native_panic_freedom_to_verifier_api_obligations` mints
/// this id + marker + digest combination on a compiler-held obligation, so a
/// restatement of any OTHER obligation kind (postcondition / arithmetic /
/// bounds / a user assert's own Assertion row) cannot satisfy it.
///
/// This is deliberately the recomputed-authentication CORE of
/// `obligation_is_synthesized_whole_function_panic_freedom` WITHOUT that
/// template's dispatch-fragile tail (exact metadata-key allowlist, exactly-one
/// context with `formula_schema: None`): post-dispatch stamping (typed-CHC
/// lowering status, native transport keys, a context formula schema) can make
/// the strict template reject the very same compiler-held obligation — which is
/// exactly why the row falls back to its `UnsupportedMir` transport restatement
/// in the first place. The checks kept here are input-independent recomputations
/// no other obligation can satisfy.
fn api_obligation_is_panic_freedom_catchall_for(
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    obligation: &trust_verifier_api::TrustObligation,
) -> bool {
    let expected_obligation_id = format!(
        "vc:{}:assertion:panic-freedom:0",
        trust_types::canonical_artifact_id_component(&func.def_path)
    );
    let Ok(expected_vc_digest) = default_trust_mc_function_obligation_digest(
        func,
        &format!("panic-freedom:{}", func.def_path),
    ) else {
        return false;
    };
    // NOTE deliberately NOT checked: the whole-serialized-function source digest
    // (`compiler_function_source_digest_hex`). The `VerifiableFunction` value at
    // this seam is a LATER snapshot than the one the synthesis digested (the
    // pipeline attaches/rewrites formula-level data in between), so that digest
    // NEVER matches here — it is the exact clause that also defeats the strict
    // template recognizer at row-build time and produces the restatement
    // fallback this matcher exists for. Identity does not need it: the
    // obligation is COMPILER-HELD (the row binding's `canonical_obligation`,
    // sealed to this row), and the exact-id + unique-marker + recomputed
    // obligation-digest combination below is minted ONLY by
    // `synthesize_native_panic_freedom_to_verifier_api_obligations` for exactly
    // this function identity.
    let checks = [
        ("kind", obligation.kind == trust_verifier_api::ObligationKind::Assertion),
        ("id", obligation.obligation_id == expected_obligation_id),
        ("contract_id", obligation.contract_id.is_none()),
        ("proof_item_id", obligation.proof_item_id.is_none()),
        (
            "marker",
            unique_api_obligation_metadata_value(
                obligation,
                TRUST_MC_PANIC_FREEDOM_OBLIGATION_METADATA_KEY,
            ) == Some("enabled"),
        ),
        (
            "vc_kind",
            unique_api_obligation_metadata_value(obligation, "trust.vc.kind")
                == Some("panic_freedom"),
        ),
        (
            "vc_digest",
            unique_api_obligation_metadata_value(obligation, TRUST_VC_DIGEST_METADATA_KEY)
                == Some(expected_vc_digest.as_str()),
        ),
    ];
    // Diagnostic-only trace (TRUST_PF_MATCH_TRACE): reads only; names the first
    // failing clause so a template drift is observable without altering any verdict.
    if trust_env_var_present("TRUST_PF_MATCH_TRACE")
        && let Some((name, _)) = checks.iter().find(|(_, ok)| !ok)
    {
        eprintln!(
            "[PF_MATCH] fn={} obligation_id={} FAILED clause={name}",
            func.def_path, obligation.obligation_id,
        );
    }
    checks.iter().all(|(_, ok)| *ok)
}

/// Trust (full-lane pf-restatement row): whether results row `index` is the
/// whole-function panic-freedom catch-all in its full-lane TRANSPORT
/// RESTATEMENT shape — `VcKind::UnsupportedMir { kind: "FullVerification::Assertion" }`,
/// the fallback `full_verification_legacy_results_bound_with_fresh_vcs` mints
/// when the fresh-VC rekey and the Assertion special-case both decline.
/// Identity comes from the row's sealed binding: the binding must seal THIS
/// exact row (`matches_row`) and its COMPILER-HELD `canonical_obligation` must
/// be the authenticated pf catch-all for `func`
/// (`api_obligation_is_panic_freedom_catchall_for`) — never the row's display
/// strings, and never verifier-returned transport data.
fn is_full_lane_panic_freedom_restatement_row(
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    index: usize,
    vc: &VerificationCondition,
    binding: Option<&ResultObligationBinding>,
) -> bool {
    let trace = trust_env_var_present("TRUST_PF_MATCH_TRACE");
    if !matches!(
        &vc.kind,
        VcKind::UnsupportedMir { kind, .. } if kind == "FullVerification::Assertion"
    ) {
        return false;
    }
    let Some(binding) = binding else {
        if trace {
            eprintln!("[PF_MATCH] fn={} idx={index} restatement-shape NO-BINDING", func.def_path);
        }
        return false;
    };
    if !binding.matches_row(index, vc) {
        if trace {
            eprintln!(
                "[PF_MATCH] fn={} idx={index} restatement-shape BINDING-ROW-MISMATCH",
                func.def_path
            );
        }
        return false;
    }
    api_obligation_is_panic_freedom_catchall_for(func, &binding.canonical_obligation)
}

/// Trust: `-full`-only, refute-only user-`assert!` refutation.
///
/// `trust_vcgen::generate_full_assert_refutation_vcs` builds the path-feasibility
/// formula of each `assert!`/`panic!` failure block, emitting it ONLY when every
/// free variable is a function parameter — so the formula is a faithful predicate
/// over the free inputs (no computed intermediate is left unconstrained). An ay
/// SAT verdict on such a formula is therefore a GENUINE counterexample: a real
/// input drives execution into the panic. SYMMETRICALLY, an ay UNSAT verdict is a
/// GENUINE PROOF: no input reaches the panic, so the assert holds for all inputs.
/// We push each refuted obligation as `Failed` (folded into `summary.failed`, so
/// it aborts the strict build) and each proved obligation as `Proved` (folded into
/// `summary.proved`). Returns `(proved, refuted)`.
///
/// Soundness: the formula is grounded (free vars = parameters, intermediates
/// constrained by block defs), so SAT is a real counterexample and UNSAT is a real
/// ∀-inputs proof — no spurious SAT, so a guarded-safe assert like
/// `if ms < 32 { assert!(ms < 32) }` stays UNSAT and is never refuted. A `Proved`
/// here carries ay's strict-checked SmtBacked certificate (the deferred-trust
/// recovery certifies the BitVec/array equality of the panic path), so it is not a
/// bare solver-core unsat. `-full`-only (reached only from
/// `collect_full_verification_artifacts`; default mode never calls it, so a
/// reachable assert panic stays valid drop-in Rust).
fn refute_full_assert_obligations(
    sess: &Session,
    owner: Option<DefId>,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    results: &mut Vec<(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)>,
    mut result_bindings: Option<&mut [Option<ResultObligationBinding>]>,
    deadline: Option<std::time::Instant>,
    vcgen_context: &trust_vcgen::VcgenContext,
    // Native-run obligation ids with Proved evidence; their rows' bindings are
    // load-bearing for post-run validation, so the grounding rewrite retires
    // one only when the kernel certifies the grounded residual that replaces
    // it (see the certify-first guard in the grounding loop).
    natively_proved_obligation_ids: &FxHashSet<String>,
    // Trust (grounded catch-all premise floor): one record per catch-all the
    // intraprocedural discharge below GROUNDS, naming the sibling rows whose
    // `Proved` labels its gate consumed and whether its call gate discharged a
    // call. The authority phase grades the grounded row by the weakest of them
    // (`cap_grounded_panic_freedom_authorities`).
    grounded_catchalls: &mut Vec<GroundedCatchallPremises>,
    // Trust (T3 L4, C07p): the body's VC context. Every candidate and probe this lane
    // generates is interned into it through the row oracle.
    rows: &mut trust_types::vc_context::VcContext,
) -> (u32, u32) {
    // NOTE: do NOT early-return when `refutable` is empty — a function with no
    // assert/panic block of its own (e.g. a `fn main` whose only statement is a call)
    // still reaches the interprocedural panic-freedom discharge below, which proves
    // its catch-all when every callee is already proven panic-free.
    // Trust (NEW-EXEMPT-SPLIT): the candidates come with the target block each
    // belongs to, which is what `panic_callee_exemption_holds` decides on.
    let (mut refutable, refutation_coverage) =
        trust_vcgen::generate_full_assert_refutation_candidates_with_context_rows(
            func,
            vcgen_context,
            rows,
        )
        .into_parts();
    // Trust (coroutine resume-state contract): that generator walks only
    // `Terminator::Call` panic sites, so rustc's compiler-inserted
    // `ResumedAfter{Return,Panic,Drop}` sentinels — which are
    // `Terminator::Assert`s, never calls — produced ZERO candidates and the
    // solver was never asked about them. Append one real candidate per
    // sentinel: exact path feasibility ∧ the recognized entry precondition
    // `selector ∈ live_states`. UNSAT here is a genuine proof about the BODY
    // (no execution honoring that precondition reaches the sentinel) and
    // NOTHING more — the precondition itself is the caller's obligation, is
    // published as the `contract:coroutine-resume-state` row, and is charged to
    // every in-bundle poll/resume site; it is not discharged anywhere by this
    // call. Fail-closed: the generator declines (empty) on any shape it cannot
    // exactly constrain.
    let sentinels = trust_vcgen::coroutine_sentinel_refutation_vcs(func);
    trust_vcgen::intern_eager_rows(rows, &sentinels);
    refutable.extend(sentinels);
    let debug = env_flag_enabled("TRUST_ASSERT_DEBUG");
    let mut proved_vcs: Vec<(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)> = Vec::new();
    let mut refuted_vcs: Vec<(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)> = Vec::new();
    let mut left_unknown = 0u32;
    // Which candidates ay PROVED, by index into `refutable` — the per-block
    // exemption reads it against `refutation_coverage`.
    let mut candidate_proved = vec![false; refutable.len()];
    for (index, candidate) in refutable.iter().enumerate() {
        // Recompute immediately before every query. Reusing the remaining time
        // captured before the batch let a later AY solve overrun the function
        // deadline by almost a full earlier allowance.
        let Some(timeout_ms) =
            remaining_solver_timeout_ms(vc_timeout_ms(sess), deadline, std::time::Instant::now())
        else {
            left_unknown += u32::try_from(refutable.len() - index).unwrap_or(u32::MAX);
            break;
        };
        let ay = trust_router::Router::deterministic_in_process_ay(timeout_ms, effort_scale(sess));
        let Some((vc, res)) = ay
            .verify_all_with_deadline(std::slice::from_ref(candidate), deadline)
            .into_iter()
            .next()
        else {
            left_unknown += 1;
            continue;
        };
        match res {
            VerificationResult::Failed { .. } => {
                if debug {
                    eprintln!("[ASSERT_REFUTE] REFUTED {:?} @ {:?}", vc.kind, vc.location);
                }
                refuted_vcs.push((
                    vc,
                    VerificationResult::Failed {
                        solver: trust_types::Symbol::intern("trust-assert-refute"),
                        time_ms: 0,
                        counterexample: None,
                    },
                ));
            }
            // ay (or its bounded-finite exhaustive route) decided the panic-path
            // formula UNSAT: no input reaches the failure block, so the assert HOLDS
            // for all inputs.
            proved_res @ VerificationResult::Proved { .. } => {
                if debug {
                    eprintln!("[ASSERT_PROVE] PROVED {:?} @ {:?}", vc.kind, vc.location);
                }
                candidate_proved[index] = true;
                proved_vcs.push((vc, proved_res));
            }
            other => {
                if debug {
                    eprintln!("[ASSERT_OTHER] {:?} @ {:?} -> {:?}", vc.kind, vc.location, other);
                }
                left_unknown += 1;
            }
        }
    }

    // Trust: PER-PATH REFUTE-ONLY fallback (`trust_vcgen::per_path_refute`).
    //
    // The bidirectional lane above DECLINES any panic block whose feasibility
    // formula carries a loop-carried value: the CFG-wide intersection leaves
    // that value free, the formula is ungrounded, and the assert is skipped. So
    // `while i < 4 { assert!(n != i); … }` — which panics for every `n` in
    // `0..4` — produces no row at all.
    //
    // The per-path lane instead symbolically executes ENUMERATED ACYCLIC PATHS,
    // resolving every read to the value written on THAT path. That formula
    // UNDER-approximates, which flips the direction of every verdict:
    //
    //   * SAT is a WITNESS — a concrete input that drives execution down a
    //     concrete block sequence into the panic. Sound, and stronger than the
    //     usual SAT (nothing was over-approximated, so nothing can be spurious).
    //   * UNSAT is NOT A PROOF — the enumeration stops at the first repeated
    //     block, so every execution past a back edge is simply absent from the
    //     formula. Reading it as a proof would be a false proof of exactly the
    //     iterations the enumeration skipped.
    //
    // That asymmetry is NOT enforced by discipline here. `RefuteOnlyVc` never
    // yields an owned VC, its `refute_with` consumes the raw solver result
    // internally, and `RefuteOnlyOutcome::NoRefutation` is payload-free — a
    // solver `Proved` and a solver `Unknown` arrive as the byte-identical value.
    // There is no expression in this function that can turn a per-path query
    // into a `Proved` row.
    //
    // GATING: this runs only when the SOUND bidirectional lane decided NOTHING
    // for this function (no refutation, no proof). It therefore cannot perturb
    // any function that lane already decides — in particular it cannot touch the
    // grounded-catch-all discharge below, which fires only on `!proved_vcs
    // .is_empty()`. A refutation found here makes `refuted > 0`, which keeps the
    // whole-function panic-freedom catch-all open, exactly as a refutation from
    // the bidirectional lane does.
    if refuted_vcs.is_empty() && proved_vcs.is_empty() {
        let per_path =
            trust_vcgen::generate_per_path_assert_refutation_vcs_with_context(func, vcgen_context);
        // At most ONE refutation per panic block, mirroring the bidirectional
        // lane's one-row-per-block shape (the failed-rekey pairing downstream
        // matches refutation COUNT against native duplicate count per span).
        let mut refuted_blocks: FxHashSet<trust_types::BlockId> = FxHashSet::default();
        for candidate in per_path {
            let block = candidate.provenance().panic_block();
            if refuted_blocks.contains(&block) {
                continue;
            }
            let Some(timeout_ms) = remaining_solver_timeout_ms(
                vc_timeout_ms(sess),
                deadline,
                std::time::Instant::now(),
            ) else {
                break;
            };
            let location = candidate.location().clone();
            let outcome =
                candidate.refute_with(trust_types::Symbol::intern("trust-perpath-refute"), |q| {
                    // Trust (T3 L4, C07p): a `RefuteOnlyVc` yields its query only here, so
                    // the query joins the body's context here, before it is solved.
                    trust_vcgen::intern_eager_rows(rows, std::iter::once(q));
                    let ay = trust_router::Router::deterministic_in_process_ay(
                        timeout_ms,
                        effort_scale(sess),
                    );
                    ay.verify_all_with_deadline(std::slice::from_ref(q), deadline)
                        .into_iter()
                        .next()
                        .map(|(_, res)| res)
                });
            match outcome {
                trust_types::RefuteOnlyOutcome::Refuted { vc, result, provenance } => {
                    if debug {
                        eprintln!(
                            "[ASSERT_REFUTE] PER-PATH REFUTED {:?} @ {location:?} via {provenance:?}",
                            vc.kind
                        );
                    }
                    refuted_blocks.insert(block);
                    refuted_vcs.push((vc, result));
                }
                // NOT a proof and NOT an unknown: the absence of a witness on
                // the enumerated paths. It contributes nothing — in particular
                // it does not touch `left_unknown`, so a function this lane
                // cannot model is left exactly as the bidirectional lane left
                // it.
                trust_types::RefuteOnlyOutcome::NoRefutation => {}
            }
        }
    }

    // A genuine reachable assert/panic is a real violation: always surface it.
    let refuted = refuted_vcs.len() as u32;
    for entry in refuted_vcs {
        results.push(entry);
    }

    // Whether the refute path proved EVERY refutable assert/panic block unreachable
    // (none refuted, none left unknown, at least one proved). Only then is the
    // function's assert/panic surface fully discharged.
    let all_assert_panics_unreachable = refuted == 0 && left_unknown == 0 && !proved_vcs.is_empty();

    // STRICTER, per-block variant for the Path-2 panic-callee exemption: every
    // SYNTACTIC refutation-target failure block in the body must be PROVED
    // unreachable — its complete per-path split emitted, every part proved. The
    // candidate generator silently SKIPS a target block with no guard-path entry
    // or an ungrounded path formula (it is not counted in `left_unknown`), so
    // `all_assert_panics_unreachable` alone cannot certify full coverage.
    // Trust (NEW-EXEMPT-SPLIT): nor can a COUNT of proved candidates, which is
    // what this used to compare against the number of target blocks — a
    // block's path split and the coroutine-sentinel candidates make the two
    // numbers incommensurable. See `panic_callee_exemption_holds`. Vacuously
    // true for a function with no assert/panic block at all — there is then
    // nothing to exempt. Trust (NEW-TAILCALL): the rule also refuses the
    // exemption to a body with a tail call the refutation lane reads as a
    // target.
    let panic_calls_proven_unreachable = panic_callee_exemption_holds(
        func,
        refuted,
        left_unknown,
        &refutation_coverage,
        &candidate_proved,
    );
    if debug {
        let unproved: Vec<trust_types::BlockId> = refutation_coverage
            .target_blocks()
            .filter(|block| {
                !refutation_coverage.target_block_proved(*block, |index| {
                    candidate_proved.get(index).copied().unwrap_or(false)
                })
            })
            .collect();
        eprintln!(
            "[ASSERT_EXEMPT] fn={} exemption={panic_calls_proven_unreachable} \
             declined={:?} unproved={unproved:?}",
            func.def_path,
            refutation_coverage.declined_target_blocks(),
        );
    }

    // The whole-function panic-freedom CATCH-ALL obligation
    // (`synthesize_native_panic_freedom_*`, kind `Assertion`, message
    // "panic freedom: …", symbolic `panic_reachable:` formula) is what the trust-mc
    // CHC/PDR route left Unsupported -> Unknown. When the refute path has proven
    // EVERY assert/panic block unreachable AND every OTHER obligation of this
    // function is already definitively Proved (so every modeled overflow/bounds/div
    // panic site is also discharged), no modeled panic is reachable — so the
    // catch-all HOLDS. Discharge it IN PLACE (no duplicate per-block obligations),
    // keeping the obligation set 1:1 with the surfaced obligations. SOUNDNESS: this
    // upgrades the catch-all to Proved ONLY when the conjunction of its constituent
    // discharges (all asserts refuted-unreachable AND all other safety obligations
    // Proved) holds; if any assert is refuted it is surfaced as a real violation
    // above, and if any obligation is left open the catch-all stays Unknown.
    let is_panic_freedom = is_panic_freedom_assertion_vc;
    let is_definitive = |r: &VerificationResult| {
        matches!(r, VerificationResult::Proved { .. } | VerificationResult::Failed { .. })
    };
    let deadline_has_remaining = || !deadline.is_some_and(|at| std::time::Instant::now() >= at);

    // Trust (coroutine resume-state contract): the coroutine-sentinel proofs
    // must ALSO surface as their OWN rows, not only be folded into the
    // catch-all. The contract-publish seam's anti-silent-drop belt
    // (`rows_exhibit_coroutine_sentinel_proof`) accepts nothing but a PROVED
    // row per sentinel carrying the bridge marker and the exact live-state set:
    // "the catch-all is proved" is precisely the vacuous stand-in that belt
    // exists to refuse. Identified ONLY by the marker description, which
    // `coroutine_sentinel_refutation_vcs` mints exclusively from the pure
    // recognizer plus a compiler-generated `AssertMessage` — never from user
    // text — so no ordinary assert candidate can enter this set. Additive: real
    // ay `Proved` rows for real solved VCs; a sentinel that did NOT prove is
    // simply absent here, the count-equality gate then fails, and the
    // fail-closed `assumption:coroutine` row + strict abort stands.
    let sentinel_proof_rows: Vec<(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)> = proved_vcs
        .iter()
        .filter(|(vc, _)| {
            matches!(&vc.kind, VcKind::Assertion { message }
                if trust_types::assumption::
                    parse_coroutine_resume_contract_obligation_description(message).is_some())
        })
        .cloned()
        .collect();
    let mut sentinel_published = sentinel_proof_rows.is_empty();

    let mut proved = 0u32;
    if all_assert_panics_unreachable && deadline_has_remaining() {
        // Include an ALREADY-Proved-but-uncertified catch-all (e.g. one the
        // interprocedural `all_calls_target_proven_panic_free` seam discharged, or a
        // native path that proved it with the opaque `panic_reachable:` formula): its
        // result is definitive but it carries no CleanCic certificate. Re-pointing its
        // formula at the grounded residual violation below lets `certify_all`
        // kernel-certify it IN PLACE — so a `for _ in 0..n { assert!(x==x) }` whose
        // assert VC is now grounded (via the vcgen slice) certifies the catch-all
        // rather than surfacing a duplicate per-assert obligation. Exclude only a
        // genuinely-Failed panic-freedom (a real violation surfaced above).
        let pf_idxs: Vec<usize> = results
            .iter()
            .enumerate()
            .filter(|(_, (vc, r))| {
                is_panic_freedom(vc) && !matches!(r, VerificationResult::Failed { .. })
            })
            .map(|(i, _)| i)
            .collect();
        let others_all_proved = results.iter().enumerate().all(|(i, (_, r))| {
            pf_idxs.contains(&i) || matches!(r, VerificationResult::Proved { .. })
        });

        // FALSE PROOF #11 (discharge half — a hole the (A) carrier would OPEN if
        // left alone). This intraprocedural block discharges the whole-function
        // catch-all from "every assert/panic BLOCK of my own is unreachable AND
        // every OTHER row is Proved". That reasoning enumerates only the modeled
        // panic sites; it says NOTHING about `Terminator::Call`. It was survivable
        // before only because the catch-all was minted almost exclusively for
        // bodies whose calls had already forced an assumption row. Now that a
        // resolved bundled call mints the catch-all too, a function shaped
        //     fn f(i: u32) -> u32 {           // panic! below proven unreachable
        //         let j = always_panics(i);   // resolved, bundled, REALLY panics
        //         if impossible() { panic!() }
        //         j
        //     }
        // would take this branch and be falsely PROVED — a strictly worse #11.
        //
        // Require the SAME call gate block 2 uses, with the SAME
        // `panic_calls_proven_unreachable` exemption for the function's own
        // refuted assert/panic entrypoint blocks (which can never be registered
        // callees). Vacuously `true` for a function with no calls, so every
        // call-free function keeps its previous discharge byte-for-byte; a
        // function WITH calls now needs them discharged, which is the honest
        // requirement. Computed lazily so the common call-free path pays nothing.
        let mut calls_discharged_for_intraprocedural =
            || all_calls_discharge_panic_free(sess, owner, func, panic_calls_proven_unreachable, rows);
        if !pf_idxs.is_empty() && others_all_proved && calls_discharged_for_intraprocedural() {
            // Represent the discharge with the proof strength actually achieved for
            // the asserts (e.g. the bounded-finite exhaustive `ExhaustiveFinite` /
            // `Sound` from the in-process route), so the static-trust bar is met.
            let strength = match &proved_vcs[0].1 {
                VerificationResult::Proved { strength, .. } => strength.clone(),
                _ => trust_types::ProofStrength::smt_unsat_strict_checked(),
            };
            // KERNEL-CERTIFICATION of the catch-all. The catch-all's own formula is the
            // OPAQUE `panic_reachable:` Bool, which `trust_certify::certify_vc` cannot
            // re-check. But since EVERY assert block is refuted-unreachable (its
            // panic-path feasibility formula is UNSAT — ay just proved so) AND every
            // OTHER obligation is Proved, the catch-all's RESIDUAL violation is exactly
            // "some assert block IS reachable" = the OR of those per-block feasibility
            // formulas. Replace the opaque formula with that GROUNDED disjunction, so the
            // subsequent `certify_all`/`certify_vc` kernel re-check sees a linear-int-
            // refutable violation and upgrades the ay-`Proved` catch-all to a genuinely
            // KERNEL-CERTIFIED `CleanCic` (e.g. `for _ in 0..n { assert!(x==x) }` closes
            // via `Not(Eq(x,x))` reflexive disequality; a modulo/interval assert via its
            // arithmetic-UNSAT path). SOUND: the OR is the FAITHFUL residual violation
            // (all other panic sites are already discharged, `others_all_proved`); a
            // genuinely-reachable assert has a SAT feasibility, so its OR is SAT and
            // `certify_vc` returns `None` (fail-closed, stays ay-`Proved` uncertified) —
            // it can never mint a certificate for a reachable panic. `certify_all` runs
            // later over `results`, so setting the formula here suffices.
            let grounded_violation: Option<trust_types::Formula> = {
                let fs: Vec<trust_types::Formula> =
                    proved_vcs.iter().map(|(vc, _)| vc.formula.clone()).collect();
                match fs.len() {
                    0 => None,
                    1 => fs.into_iter().next(),
                    _ => Some(trust_types::Formula::Or(fs)),
                }
            };
            // Trust (grounded catch-all premise floor): the gate just read every
            // non-catch-all row's `Proved` LABEL (`others_all_proved`) and
            // discharged every call (tail calls included, NEW-TAILCALL) that is
            // not a disjunct below — and never looked at an opaque terminator. The
            // disjunction is the faithful residual of whole-function
            // panic-freedom only while those rows really are discharged and
            // the body's other panic sources really cannot panic — the
            // SOUNDNESS note above is exact about the disjunction and silent
            // about both. The kernel certificate `certify_all` mints re-checks
            // the disjunction, never the labels or the callees, so each
            // grounding records what it stood on for the authority phase to
            // grade it by.
            let gate_pf_idxs = pf_idxs.clone();
            let call_premise = grounded_call_premise(func, panic_calls_proven_unreachable);
            for i in pf_idxs {
                if !deadline_has_remaining() {
                    break;
                }
                if debug {
                    eprintln!(
                        "[ASSERT_PROVE] discharging whole-function panic-freedom @ {:?}",
                        results[i].0.location
                    );
                }
                // A catch-all the NATIVE lane already proved under a VALID
                // current binding is owned by that strict-accepted evidence
                // chain: the post-run proof-authority validation requires the
                // binding to still exist (`… has no exact current compiler
                // result binding` otherwise), so the kernel-cert grounding
                // BONUS must not revoke it. Before the aggregate recognizer's
                // two-anchor fix, a natively-proved catch-all carried the
                // placeholder `UnsupportedMir` VC and never matched
                // `is_panic_freedom`, so this collision could not occur; with
                // the intended `Assertion` shape restored, the alternative
                // considered here was to skip the rewrite for natively-owned
                // rows and keep grounding the rest.
                // GROUNDING PROCEEDS INSTEAD (measured: withholding it broke 79
                // fixtures — the kernel certification of the catch-all is what
                // most proved functions depend on). The rewrite necessarily
                // revokes this row's binding; the post-run native
                // proof-authority validation is reconciled instead, via the
                // grounded-obligation record below, so the aggregate is judged
                // on its GROUNDED row rather than on a binding the compiler
                // itself deliberately retired.
                let grounded_id = result_bindings
                    .as_deref()
                    .and_then(|bindings| bindings.get(i).and_then(Option::as_ref))
                    .filter(|binding| binding.matches_row(i, &results[i].0))
                    .map(|binding| binding.public_obligation_id.clone());
                if let Some(ref gv) = grounded_violation {
                    // Trust (NEW-EXEMPT-SPLIT, 2026-09-28): ... except where the
                    // grounded row could hold NO authority. A grounded row is
                    // authorized only by the kernel certificate `certify_all`
                    // mints over it (its exact identity is new, so no receipt of
                    // the native lane binds it). When `certify_vc` declines the
                    // disjunction, the rewrite would retire a native proof and
                    // put nothing in its place: the row is published
                    // `runtime_checked`. The twin pass
                    // (`ground_panic_freedom_catchall`) certifies BEFORE it
                    // rewrites and leaves the row untouched otherwise; this lane
                    // does the same for a row the native run proved. Measured
                    // (`tests/ui/trust/panic_callee_exemption_is_per_target_block.rs`):
                    // `two_single`, two single-path blocks the kernel does not
                    // certify together, was refused under certify on stage 1
                    // built from `76d76ee8d4` although trust_mc had proved it,
                    // and once the per-block exemption let this lane fire for
                    // `both_blocks_proved` too (three candidates, two blocks)
                    // it lost its trust_mc proof the same way. Withholding the
                    // rewrite here cannot cost a certificate (there is none to
                    // mint) and adds no authority: the row is judged on its
                    // native binding exactly as when this lane does not fire.
                    if grounded_id
                        .as_ref()
                        .is_some_and(|id| natively_proved_obligation_ids.contains(id))
                    {
                        let mut candidate = results[i].0.clone();
                        candidate.formula = gv.clone();
                        if trust_certify::certify_vc(&candidate).is_none() {
                            if debug {
                                eprintln!(
                                    "[ASSERT_PROVE] grounding withheld @ {:?}: the kernel does \
                                     not certify the grounded residual, so the native proof \
                                     keeps the row",
                                    results[i].0.location
                                );
                            }
                            continue;
                        }
                    }
                    if !rewrite_result_formula_revoking_binding(
                        i,
                        results,
                        result_bindings.as_deref_mut(),
                        gv.clone(),
                    ) {
                        // A stale/swapped binding must not be repaired by this
                        // rewrite or receive the refutation result below.
                        continue;
                    }
                    // Both records are sealed to the row AS REWRITTEN: the kernel
                    // token `cap_grounded_panic_freedom_authorities` later grades
                    // is minted over exactly that identity.
                    if let Some(row) = exact_result_row_identity(i, &results[i].0) {
                        if let Some(id) = grounded_id {
                            record_grounded_panic_freedom_obligation(sess, &id, row.clone());
                        }
                        grounded_catchalls.push(GroundedCatchallPremises::record(
                            row,
                            results,
                            |index| gate_pf_idxs.contains(&index),
                            call_premise,
                        ));
                    }
                }
                // Preserve an already-definitive result (counted upstream); only upgrade
                // an inconclusive catch-all to Proved. Either way the grounded formula
                // set above lets `certify_all` attach the CleanCic certificate in place.
                if !is_definitive(&results[i].1) {
                    results[i].1 = VerificationResult::Proved {
                        solver: trust_types::Symbol::intern("trust-assert-refute"),
                        time_ms: 0,
                        strength: strength.clone(),
                        proof_certificate: None,
                        solver_warnings: None,
                        native_proof_envelope: None,
                    };
                    proved += 1;
                }
            }
        } else if pf_idxs.is_empty() {
            // No catch-all to discharge (e.g. a non-`-full` lane): surface the
            // per-assert proofs directly so they still count.
            for entry in proved_vcs {
                results.push(entry);
                proved += 1;
            }
            // Already surfaced above (this branch pushes every proved
            // candidate, sentinels included); nothing left to publish.
            sentinel_published = true;
        }
        // else: some other obligation is still open — leave the catch-all Unknown
        // (the function stays inconclusive on that other gap) and do not inflate the
        // set with subsumed per-block duplicates.
    }
    // Trust (coroutine resume-state contract): publish the sentinel proofs as
    // their own rows (see `sentinel_proof_rows`). Deliberately OUTSIDE the
    // catch-all gate: the sentinel proof must be visible even when some OTHER
    // obligation of the body is still open, because the publish seam's
    // `coroutine_contract_rows_all_statically_proved` gate independently
    // refuses the contract row in exactly that case — so this can never
    // substitute for it.
    if !sentinel_published {
        for entry in sentinel_proof_rows {
            results.push(entry);
            proved += 1;
        }
    }

    // INTERPROCEDURAL panic-freedom discharge. A function whose own assert/panic
    // surface was NOT proved here (e.g. it has no `assert!`/`panic!` block at all, so
    // `proved_vcs` is empty and the block above did not fire) can still be panic-free:
    // if its ONLY residual panic risk is calls to functions already PROVEN fully
    // panic-free (`all_calls_target_proven_panic_free`) AND every OTHER obligation of
    // this function is already `Proved`, then no panic — modeled (arithmetic/bounds/
    // div, all Proved) or via-call (every callee cannot panic for any input) — is
    // reachable, so the whole-function panic-freedom catch-all HOLDS.
    //
    // SOUNDNESS: every panic site is one of (a) an obligation in `results` [required
    // Proved here], (b) a call — `Terminator::Call` OR (Trust, NEW-TAILCALL)
    // `Terminator::TailCall`, as `extracted_call_sites` enumerates them [required
    // to target a registered
    // panic-free callee — fail-closed for `unwrap`/`panic!`/foreign/unproved callees],
    // so the conjunction soundly entails panic-freedom. This NEVER fires when a real
    // assert was refuted (`refuted > 0` ⇒ the catch-all stays open and the refutation
    // is surfaced as a failure above) nor when any obligation is left open. The
    // callee facts come from this Session's panic-free registry, populated as
    // each function fully verifies and matched by exact `def_path`.
    //
    // Trust (coroutine resume-state contract) — THE SITE-ENUMERATION HOLE. That
    // soundness argument is a claim that (a) ∪ (b) COVERS every panic site, and
    // for a coroutine resume body it does not: rustc's `ResumedAfter*` sentinel
    // is a `Terminator::Assert` with no obligation of its own (`trust_vcgen`'s
    // safety lane deliberately emits none for those three messages), so it is
    // neither (a) nor (b). For a zero-await resume body — which has no calls at
    // all — `all_calls_target_proven_panic_free` is VACUOUSLY true, and this
    // rule stamped the whole-function catch-all `Proved` without anything ever
    // having looked at the sentinel or at the resume-state precondition it
    // depends on. DECLINE for any body carrying such a sentinel: its panic
    // surface is exactly what this rule cannot enumerate. The sentinel
    // candidates appended above are the honest route — if they prove, the
    // catch-all is discharged by the (non-vacuous) block above; if they do not,
    // the catch-all stays open.
    // MERGE (b62 <- origin/main; squash d24c671211, 2026-08-22) — THE HONEST 3-WAY COMPOSITION.
    // Base read `proved == 0 && refuted == 0 && deadline_has_remaining()`. The two
    // sides then made ORTHOGONAL edits to it:
    //   * OURS (R23) REMOVED `proved == 0`, so the call gate also runs for an
    //     already-`Proved` pf catch-all (see the R23 note below).
    //   * UPSTREAM ADDED the coroutine-sentinel decline, closing a real
    //     site-enumeration hole (see the note directly above).
    // Both are kept. Dropping either would silently revert one side: keeping only
    // upstream's would disable the InterproceduralCalleeClosure lane for exactly the
    // rows it exists to serve, and keeping only ours would reopen the coroutine hole.
    if refuted == 0
        && deadline_has_remaining()
        && !trust_vcgen::body_has_coroutine_protocol_sentinel(func)
    {
        // Full-lane restatement recognition needs each row's sealed binding
        // (read-only view; rows appended by the refute push have no binding and
        // index out of range yields `None` — fail-closed non-match).
        let binding_view: Option<&[Option<ResultObligationBinding>]> = result_bindings.as_deref();
        let row_binding = |i: usize| binding_view.and_then(|b| b.get(i)).and_then(Option::as_ref);
        // R23 (SELECTION widened; the RESULT OVERWRITE guard below is what stayed
        // narrow). This filter used to be `!is_definitive(r) && …`, which excluded
        // an ALREADY-`Proved` pf catch-all — and therefore never even RAN the call
        // gate for such a function (measured: `[ASSERT_P2] fn=P::c1_xv rows=1
        // pf_idxs=[] proved=0 refuted=0` with no `[PF_CALLGATE]` line at all). That
        // made a row the native lane had modeled panic-free STRICTLY WORSE OFF than
        // an Unknown one: with no gate run there is no seam fact, so
        // `install_interprocedural_callee_closure_authorities` never fires, the row
        // keeps `auth=None`, and the transport correctly demotes it to
        // `runtime_checked`. Mirrors the INTRAPROCEDURAL block's selection (`… &&
        // !matches!(r, Failed{..})`) — a genuinely-Failed panic-freedom is a real
        // violation and stays excluded here exactly as it does there.
        //
        // SOUND BECAUSE NOTHING IS OVERWRITTEN. Widening the SELECTION alone can
        // only (a) make `pf_idxs` non-empty so the gate RUNS, and (b) exempt the pf
        // row from `others_all_proved` — which it already passed, being `Proved`.
        // The verdict write below is still guarded by `!is_definitive`, so no
        // already-definitive result is silently rewritten and no certificate is
        // destroyed. The gate FACT reaches the authority installer through the
        // recorded `interprocedural_pf_call_closure_funcs` carry instead.
        let pf_idxs: Vec<usize> = results
            .iter()
            .enumerate()
            .filter(|(i, (vc, r))| {
                !matches!(r, VerificationResult::Failed { .. })
                    && (is_panic_freedom(vc)
                        || is_full_lane_panic_freedom_restatement_row(
                            func,
                            *i,
                            vc,
                            row_binding(*i),
                        ))
            })
            .map(|(i, _)| i)
            .collect();
        if debug {
            eprintln!(
                "[ASSERT_P2] fn={} rows={} pf_idxs={:?} proved={} refuted={}",
                func.def_path,
                results.len(),
                pf_idxs,
                proved,
                refuted,
            );
        }
        if !pf_idxs.is_empty() {
            // Trust (Part B — discharge-side pf/functional decouple). A FUNCTIONAL
            // `#[trust::ensures]` postcondition (`VcKind::Postcondition`) left
            // Unknown is a coverage gap, NOT a reachable panic, so it must not
            // block the whole-function panic-freedom discharge. EVERY other row —
            // every modeled panic site — must still be `Proved` (mirrors the
            // registration-side exemption in
            // `results_establish_unconditional_panic_freedom`). SOUND: only
            // `Postcondition` rows are exempted; an unproved panic site keeps the
            // catch-all open.
            let others_all_proved = results.iter().enumerate().all(|(i, (vc, r))| {
                pf_idxs.contains(&i)
                    || matches!(vc.kind, VcKind::Postcondition)
                    || matches!(r, VerificationResult::Proved { .. })
            });
            let calls_discharged = others_all_proved
                && all_calls_discharge_panic_free(sess, owner, func, panic_calls_proven_unreachable, rows);
            if debug {
                eprintln!(
                    "[ASSERT_P2] fn={} others_all_proved={} calls_discharged={}",
                    func.def_path, others_all_proved, calls_discharged,
                );
            }
            // Trust (Part B — under-precondition firewall), hoisted: a
            // `#[requires(P)]` function is only CONDITIONALLY panic-free.
            let under_precondition = has_declared_requires_contract(&func.contracts);
            // R23. Carry the seam's own gate result to the authority phase, where
            // `auth == None` is actually OBSERVABLE (every real lane has by then
            // had its chance to claim the row). Recorded on BOTH outcomes — never
            // only on success — so the AND-merge can be poisoned by a
            // monomorphization that DISAGREED. `true` requires the
            // UNCONDITIONAL tag: a `#[requires(P)]` discharge assumes `P` at entry
            // and must never mint the unconditional
            // `InterproceduralCalleeClosure` authority — the identical exclusion
            // the solver tag below already encodes.
            record_interprocedural_pf_call_closure(
                sess,
                &func.def_path,
                calls_discharged && !under_precondition,
            );
            if calls_discharged {
                // Trust (Part B — under-precondition firewall). A `#[requires(P)]`
                // function is only CONDITIONALLY panic-free (its callee-precondition
                // proofs, and possibly a Part-C `P[args]` proof, assume `P` at
                // entry). Tag its discharge distinctly so the UNCONDITIONAL
                // authority installer (`install_interprocedural_callee_closure_authorities`,
                // keyed on "trust-assert-refute") never seals it, and it registers
                // ONLY in the separate `panic_free_under_precondition` registry —
                // it can never launder into the unconditional registry.
                //
                // N1: keyed on the DECLARED `#[trust::requires]` contract list, NOT
                // `func.preconditions` — the latter also carries synthetic
                // type-range tautologies for by-value integer/char params, which
                // are not entry assumptions; a fn with only synthetic facts keeps
                // the unconditional tag exactly as before this seam existed.
                let solver_tag = if under_precondition {
                    "trust-assert-refute-under-precondition"
                } else {
                    "trust-assert-refute"
                };
                for i in pf_idxs {
                    if !deadline_has_remaining() {
                        break;
                    }
                    // R23. Preserve an ALREADY-definitive result, exactly as the
                    // intraprocedural block does: the widened selection above
                    // exists to RUN the gate, never to rewrite a verdict some
                    // other lane owns. A `Proved`-without-authority row keeps its
                    // own solver/strength/evidence and is authorized later from
                    // the recorded gate fact instead.
                    if is_definitive(&results[i].1) {
                        continue;
                    }
                    if debug {
                        eprintln!(
                            "[ASSERT_PROVE] interprocedural panic-freedom discharge @ {:?} (tag={solver_tag}; all callees proven/under-precondition panic-free)",
                            results[i].0.location
                        );
                    }
                    results[i].1 = VerificationResult::Proved {
                        solver: trust_types::Symbol::intern(solver_tag),
                        time_ms: 0,
                        strength: trust_types::ProofStrength::smt_unsat_strict_checked(),
                        proof_certificate: None,
                        solver_warnings: None,
                        native_proof_envelope: None,
                    };
                    proved += 1;
                }
            }
        }
    }
    (proved, refuted)
}

/// Trust (failed-rekey symmetry): re-key each VERIFIED `-full` assert/panic
/// refutation appended by [`refute_full_assert_obligations`] onto the
/// still-Unknown NATIVE DUPLICATE row restating the same source obligation.
///
/// The native bundle carries one Assertion-family obligation per assert/panic
/// block (`vc:<fn>:assertion:<i>`, round-tripped here as
/// `UnsupportedMir { kind: "FullVerification::Assertion", .. }` — user
/// `assert!` "assertion: panic call: …" and `unreachable!` "unreachable code
/// reached" alike). The native CHC route SOLVES such an obligation with a
/// counterexample but its transport rejects counterexample evidence as proof,
/// so the row stays Unknown — while the refute route just proved, from
/// function PARAMETERS, that a real input reaches the same panic block. A
/// concrete violation of the source obligation refutes its restatement, so
/// the duplicate must fail too instead of reporting a phantom coverage gap
/// beside a definite refutation.
///
/// FAIL-CLOSED pairing by obligation identity (function + exact source span +
/// aligned kinds), never by id-string parsing. Because every panic call in
/// one function can collapse onto the same macro-internal span
/// (`core/src/panic.rs`), a per-span refutation is credited to the duplicates
/// at that span ONLY when the counts match exactly — `#refutations ==
/// #Assertion-family duplicates` at that (function, span) key. vcgen emits
/// one Assertion VC per panic block and the refute route derives one
/// candidate per block, so equality means EVERY block at that span was
/// refuted and every duplicate restates a refuted obligation; any skew
/// (an unrefuted sibling block, a representation mismatch) skips the whole
/// key. SOUND: only Unknown -> Failed conversions on restatements of refuted
/// obligations; proof credit is never minted.
fn rekey_verified_assert_refutations_onto_unknown_duplicates(
    results: &mut [(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)],
    appended_from: usize,
) {
    if appended_from > results.len() {
        return;
    }
    // The verified refutations this call just appended, keyed by identity.
    let refutations: Vec<(trust_types::Symbol, SourceSpan, Option<Counterexample>)> = results
        [appended_from..]
        .iter()
        .filter(|(vc, result)| {
            matches!(vc.kind, VcKind::Assertion { .. })
                && matches!(result, VerificationResult::Failed { .. })
                && !vc.location.file.is_empty()
                && vc.location.line_start > 0
        })
        .map(|(vc, result)| {
            let counterexample = match result {
                VerificationResult::Failed { counterexample, .. } => counterexample.clone(),
                _ => None,
            };
            (vc.function, vc.location.clone(), counterexample)
        })
        .collect();
    if refutations.is_empty() {
        return;
    }
    let is_assertion_family_duplicate = |vc: &VerificationCondition| matches!(&vc.kind, VcKind::UnsupportedMir { kind, .. } if kind == "FullVerification::Assertion");
    let mut rekeyed_keys: Vec<(trust_types::Symbol, SourceSpan)> = Vec::new();
    for (function, location, _) in &refutations {
        if rekeyed_keys.iter().any(|(f, l)| f == function && l == location) {
            continue;
        }
        rekeyed_keys.push((*function, location.clone()));
        let refutations_at_key: Vec<&Option<Counterexample>> = refutations
            .iter()
            .filter(|(f, l, _)| f == function && l == location)
            .map(|(_, _, counterexample)| counterexample)
            .collect();
        let duplicates_at_key: Vec<usize> = results[..appended_from]
            .iter()
            .enumerate()
            .filter(|(_, (vc, _))| {
                is_assertion_family_duplicate(vc)
                    && vc.function == *function
                    && vc.location == *location
            })
            .map(|(index, _)| index)
            .collect();
        if duplicates_at_key.len() != refutations_at_key.len() {
            continue;
        }
        // Counts match: every block at this key was refuted, so every
        // duplicate restates a refuted obligation. Carry the refutations'
        // counterexample references across in deterministic carrier order.
        for (index, counterexample) in duplicates_at_key.into_iter().zip(refutations_at_key) {
            if matches!(results[index].1, VerificationResult::Unknown { .. }) {
                results[index].1 = VerificationResult::Failed {
                    solver: trust_types::Symbol::intern(TRUST_REFUTE_REKEY_SOLVER),
                    time_ms: 0,
                    counterexample: counterexample.clone(),
                };
            }
        }
    }
}

/// Trust: the v1 → fail-closed bridge.
///
/// The native full-verification (trust-mc typed-CHC) route cannot lower basic
/// arithmetic/bounds/div-by-zero obligations — it returns `unsupported MIR
/// FullVerification::...`. In fail-closed mode that left every such obligation
/// `unknown`, so no real safety obligation could prove. This runs the in-process
/// `ay` SMT backend—which emits a replayable, strict-checked UNSAT artifact—on
/// the precise function VCs and, for each obligation the native route left
/// non-definitive, substitutes a definitive `ay` Proved/Failed result.
///
/// Soundness: the substituted result comes from a real `ay` solve. A vacuous
/// proof is still rejected downstream by `apply_vacuity_gate`, and obligations
/// `ay` cannot decide remain unknown and fail closed. Postconditions and user
/// assertions accept only strict Proved results; preconditions and L0 safety
/// obligations retain symmetric Proved/Failed substitution. The two asymmetries
/// are KIND-based and are argued at their arms below.
///
/// WHY THERE IS NO ENTRY-GROUNDING GATE HERE (2026-08-29, measured).
/// `bc65b2ca7d` + `017a2a2af5` made every arm of this predicate additionally
/// require `trust_vcgen::composed_formula_is_caller_entry_grounded`. That
/// predicate belongs to the CALL-SITE REPLAY lane, where the formula is
/// assembled out of caller ENTRY state plus block defs, so "every free var is an
/// entry-stable parameter or `Eq`-pinned" is a standard a well-formed candidate
/// can meet. An ORDINARY body VC cannot meet it even in principle: its free
/// variables are the function's own modelled state — MIR temps (`_7`), versioned
/// SSA reads of reassigned locals (`remain#s0_3_s4_0`), discriminant model vars
/// (`__trust_mir__discr__6`), `__slice_len` companions of non-parameter locals
/// (`_4__slice_len#s0_1`), the BV companions of an integer parameter
/// (`__trust_ovf_bv_lhs_a`) and projections of all of these. What makes those
/// denote a real execution is the path condition and block defs already
/// conjoined into the VC, not an `Eq` pin the predicate can find. Widening the
/// predicate to accept those shapes is the same thing as not applying it.
///
/// MEASURED (`TRUST_GROUND_DEBUG=1`, frozen 154-fixture mutant corpus, gate
/// predicates copied from `scripts/trust_falsification_gate.sh`): the grounding
/// requirement cost 88 fixtures that refuted at `70a7534fd1` — 152 refuted / 2
/// incomplete became 64 / 90 — and protected NONE of the four `BRwitness`
/// controls it was written for. `w1_dynfn_dest_unguarded` reaches the predicate
/// with `free=[]`, `verdict=true`; `w1_opaque_dest_precond` and
/// `w3_extern_dest_unguarded` refuted THROUGH the gate anyway; and
/// `w2_dynfn_dest_guarded`, which must PROVE, was over-rejected by it. Dropping
/// the requirement and keeping only the kind polarity gives 143 refuted / 11
/// incomplete with all four controls exactly as `origin/main` has them and the
/// 134-fixture `proved/` lane unchanged at 126 proved / 8 incomplete / 0
/// refuted.
///
/// The refutation-side protection those commits wanted for a HAVOC'D CALL
/// DESTINATION is implemented, narrowly and at the right seam, by
/// `trust_vcgen::generate::havoc_decline` (landed `b4fd4c0e43`, two days
/// earlier): an obligation whose subject's ONLY value source is an unmodelled
/// callee's `Call` destination, which nothing in the hypotheses pins and whose
/// truth FLIPS as that value moves over its declared type range, is
/// reclassified to `VcKind::UnsupportedMir` before it ever reaches a solver.
/// That is a property of the MIR, checked once, and it cannot cost a decision
/// the verifier can actually make. It is not quite complete — it demotes the
/// `trust.vc` row for `w1`'s index check but not the sibling `assert` row that
/// restates the same violation — which is why the `Assertion` arm below is
/// load-bearing and stays.
///
/// Native `ArithmeticSafety` Unsupported rows are deliberately included. A
/// complete canonical VC is the primary bridge identity when it occurs exactly
/// once in both carriers. Source span is only a legacy lookup accelerator and is
/// permitted only for a one-native-row ↔ one-vc match. A row is re-keyed or
/// solved only when it already has an exact public/native binding and the v1 VC
/// has the byte-structurally identical violation formula and function identity.
/// A missing formula (the legacy `Bool(false)` placeholder), a binding-vector
/// skew, duplicate exact rows, or a same-span semantic mismatch stays Unknown.
///
/// Returns the refined results, counts of newly Proved and Failed obligations,
/// and an exact row-aligned S1 eligibility vector. Only a strict proof produced
/// by this function's fresh local in-process AY branch can populate that final
/// vector; every other producer is represented by `None`.
///
/// Compiler-authenticated contract catalog rows and constant-false bookkeeping
/// VCs are never bridge endpoints. They are excluded from match population,
/// re-keying, solving, and substitution; their dedicated sealed discharge lanes
/// remain the only way they can acquire authority.
fn bridge_ay_verdict_is_admissible(kind: &VcKind, result: &VerificationResult) -> bool {
    match kind {
        // Trust (contract-completeness inc-1): a `VcKind::Postcondition` is admitted to
        // ay to PROVE a self-contained body-fact-conjoined violation goal by strict
        // UNSAT — but its formula can be SATisfiable for a *valid* contract whose body
        // facts the formula under-approximates (e.g. the
        // `postcondition_references_mutated_param` fail-closed shape emits `Not(post)`
        // over FREE params). An ay `Failed` there is NOT a sound refutation of the
        // contract, so for `Postcondition` bridge ONLY an ay `Proved` (a strict-checked
        // ∀-inputs UNSAT proof); a SAT/Unknown stays fail-closed (Unknown), which the
        // downstream reclassify handles and which a genuinely-false contract also lands
        // in (never a false PROVE).
        //
        // A user `Assertion` is proof-only here for a DIFFERENT and MEASURED
        // reason (2026-08-29). Its generic body formula can range over a value
        // the body does not constrain — the `BRwitness/w1_dynfn_dest_unguarded`
        // shape, `let j = p(i); a[j]` through a `dyn Fn`. `havoc_decline`
        // demotes the `trust.vc` row for that index check to `UnsupportedMir`,
        // but the SIBLING `assert` row carries the same violation formula and
        // would cross this bridge as `ay-in-process` FAILED — a counterexample
        // over a value the unmodelled callee chooses, not one any execution
        // produces. Assertion REFUTATION is owned by
        // `refute_full_assert_obligations`, whose bidirectional and per-path
        // lanes establish exact grounding themselves. MEASURED on the frozen
        // 154-fixture mutant corpus: this arm alone is what keeps `w1` at
        // `incomplete`; its cost is 5 fixtures that refute without it
        // (`external_call_guarded`, `match_exhaustive_enum`,
        // `modulo_unreachable`, `try_convert_from_panic`,
        // `user_ord_btreemap_get`), each of which stays a build ERROR, just a
        // fail-closed one rather than a counterexample.
        // Trust (TINV, 2026-10-05): proof-only too. A satisfiable candidate conjunct is
        // dropped by Houdini, never a counterexample to the program. Defence in depth
        // only: other producers (constant folding, interval, incremental ay,
        // abstract interpretation) can still return `Failed`. Step 7 settled the rule:
        // a dropped conjunct is a note and never a row (TINV-9(c)), and published rows
        // are Houdini's kernel-certified ones (the kind gate admits a situated row since
        // 7.7). No compiler producer emits a TINV row until step 9.
        VcKind::Postcondition | VcKind::Assertion { .. } | VcKind::TypeInvariant { .. } => {
            matches!(result, VerificationResult::Proved { .. })
        }
        // Trust (falsification regression 2026-08-13): REFUTE-ONLY, the mirror
        // image of the `Postcondition` asymmetry above. ay SAT on an unreachable
        // trap's reachability formula is a REAL counterexample (a concrete input
        // reaches `unreachable_unchecked` — UB), so `Failed` substitutes. An ay
        // `Proved` is NOT substituted: a dead trap is discharged by
        // `promote_structurally_dead_unreachable` on the propositional-UNSAT
        // evidence it already requires, and routing proof authority for this kind
        // through the bridge would add a trust edge for no gain. Fail-closed:
        // Unknown/Timeout keep the existing verdict.
        VcKind::Unreachable => matches!(result, VerificationResult::Failed { .. }),
        // A caller-side `Precondition`'s formula is the negated precondition with
        // the call's ACTUAL arguments substituted (`Not(P[σ])`), a grounded goal
        // where ay SAT IS a real counterexample (a caller input violates the
        // callee's assumed precondition — #5-PRE-A). Suppressing that `Failed`
        // would let an unmet precondition (a latent callee L0 panic) ship green,
        // so `Precondition` keeps the full `definitive` substitution (both Proved
        // and Failed). Likewise every L0 safety kind at this kind-only gate.
        // The separate per-function dependency scope still withholds generic
        // SAT over an unsupported loop's ungrounded state.
        _ => {
            matches!(result, VerificationResult::Proved { .. } | VerificationResult::Failed { .. })
        }
    }
}

fn demote_inadmissible_generic_refutation(result: &mut VerificationResult, reason: &'static str) {
    let VerificationResult::Failed { solver, time_ms, .. } = result else {
        return;
    };
    *result = VerificationResult::Unknown {
        solver: *solver,
        time_ms: *time_ms,
        reason: reason.to_string(),
    };
}

/// Trust: `Unknown.reason` marker for a live solver SAT (a counterexample) on a
/// `VcKind::Assertion` row that the kind-polarity asymmetry
/// (`bridge_ay_verdict_is_admissible`, `bc65b2ca7d`'s Assertion proof-only arm)
/// holds below proof authority.
///
/// A held refutation is deliberately NOT reported as `Failed` — the formula may
/// range over a value no execution produces (the `w1_dynfn_dest_unguarded`
/// shape), so claiming a certified counterexample would be dishonest. But it is
/// not an ordinary "no proof yet" gap either: the ONLY evidence in hand says
/// the assertion is violable, so granting it the runtime-checked tolerance of
/// the default completeness-gap policy would let a claim the solver just
/// refuted compile clean. Measured regression this closes: the wrong-claim
/// soundness guards (`tests/ui/trust/clean_origin_caller_wrong_claim.rs`,
/// `clean_origin_wrapping_wrong_claim.rs`) went from the pinned strict build
/// error to exit 0 when `bc65b2ca7d` held their `ay-in-process` refutations and
/// the demoted rows fell into the tolerated runtime-checked bucket.
/// `residual_gap_tolerance_denial` and `strict_l0_verification_failure` key on
/// this exact string to keep such a row a fail-closed coverage gap.
const TRUST_HELD_ASSERT_REFUTATION_REASON: &str = "a solver counterexample for this assertion was held below proof authority (assertion \
     refutation is proof-only at the generic bridge); the obligation stays an unproved \
     coverage gap with no runtime-check tolerance";

/// Whether this row's `Unknown` records a generic SAT result held below
/// counterexample authority. The legacy name also covers unsupported-loop
/// dependency gaps; neither marker is positive evidence of any kind.
fn result_is_held_assert_refutation(result: &VerificationResult) -> bool {
    matches!(
        result,
        VerificationResult::Unknown { reason, .. }
            if reason.as_str() == TRUST_HELD_ASSERT_REFUTATION_REASON
                || reason.starts_with(trust_vcgen::GENERIC_REFUTATION_HELD_PREFIX)
    )
}

/// Trust: generic holds use a prefix; the legacy assertion hold above uses
/// exact equality and cannot take a suffix without restoring runtime tolerance.
fn result_is_held_generic_refutation(result: &VerificationResult) -> bool {
    matches!(result, VerificationResult::Unknown { reason, .. }
        if reason.starts_with(trust_vcgen::GENERIC_REFUTATION_HELD_PREFIX))
}

/// Whether an undecided row may take an ay count or resource-backstop stop into its
/// reason (`fold_lane_stop`). Never a held refutation: its marker is matched
/// exactly by [`result_is_held_assert_refutation`] and is what withholds the
/// runtime-check tolerance, so rewriting it would make a counterexample read
/// as a tolerated Unknown.
fn may_fold_count_stop(result: &VerificationResult) -> bool {
    matches!(result, VerificationResult::Unknown { .. })
        && !result_is_held_assert_refutation(result)
}

fn bridge_v1_ay_proofs_into_native_results_with_scope(
    refutation_scope: &trust_vcgen::GenericRefutationScope,
    per_vc_timeout_ms: u64,
    scale: trust_types::determinism::EffortScale,
    deadline: Option<std::time::Instant>,
    solver_vcs: &[VerificationCondition],
    result_bindings: &mut [Option<ResultObligationBinding>],
    // Determinism plan §3.7: the rows this bridge was going to decide and a
    // clock stopped first (see `bridge_clock_cuts` below).
    clock_stops: &mut ClockStops,
    // Whether to stamp TRUST_HELD_ASSERT_REFUTATION_REASON onto Assertion rows
    // whose ay/interval SAT the polarity asymmetry held. True exactly for the
    // fail-closed policies, where the stamp decides the build outcome; the
    // explicit advisory lane keeps its historical runtime-checked report text
    // (it never aborts, so the stamp would change wording and nothing else).
    stamp_held_assert_refutations: bool,
    results: Vec<(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)>,
    proof_artifact_root: Option<&std::path::Path>,
) -> (
    Vec<(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)>,
    u32,
    u32,
    Vec<Option<SolverRevalidationEligibility>>,
) {
    bridge_v1_ay_proofs_into_native_results_with_interval(
        refutation_scope,
        per_vc_timeout_ms,
        scale,
        deadline,
        solver_vcs,
        result_bindings,
        clock_stops,
        stamp_held_assert_refutations,
        results,
        proof_artifact_root,
        || {
            trust_router::Router::with_backends(vec![Box::new(
                trust_router::interval_backend::IntervalBackend,
            )])
        },
    )
}

// Trust: exercise the actual row-binding and authority path with an interval
// engine fault. AY and strict proof revalidation stay on their production paths.
fn bridge_v1_ay_proofs_into_native_results_with_interval(
    refutation_scope: &trust_vcgen::GenericRefutationScope,
    per_vc_timeout_ms: u64,
    scale: trust_types::determinism::EffortScale,
    deadline: Option<std::time::Instant>,
    solver_vcs: &[VerificationCondition],
    result_bindings: &mut [Option<ResultObligationBinding>],
    clock_stops: &mut ClockStops,
    stamp_held_assert_refutations: bool,
    mut results: Vec<(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)>,
    proof_artifact_root: Option<&std::path::Path>,
    make_interval: impl Fn() -> trust_router::Router,
) -> (
    Vec<(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)>,
    u32,
    u32,
    Vec<Option<SolverRevalidationEligibility>>,
) {
    if results.len() != result_bindings.len() {
        let ineligible = vec![None; results.len()];
        return (results, 0, 0, ineligible);
    }
    let definitive = |r: &VerificationResult| {
        matches!(r, VerificationResult::Proved { .. } | VerificationResult::Failed { .. })
    };
    // The native route labels an arithmetic obligation with a generic
    // `FullVerification` kind while vcgen labels it `overflow:add`, so VcKind is
    // not a cross-lane identity. Source location narrows the search only; exact
    // formula + function equality is the semantic authorization boundary.
    type LocKey = (String, u32, u32, u32, u32);
    #[derive(Clone, Hash, PartialEq, Eq)]
    enum BridgeKey {
        /// Complete, canonical serialized VC bytes. This is deliberately not a
        /// digest: a hash collision may cost a lookup, never transfer a proof.
        /// Trust (T3 L4, C09): held as the row's [`RowKey`], whose string decides.
        Exact(RowKey),
        /// Compatibility route for a native generic-kind row whose otherwise
        /// exact formula cannot have the same complete VC serialization.
        Location(LocKey),
    }
    let loc_key = |vc: &VerificationCondition| -> LocKey {
        let l = &vc.location;
        (l.file.clone(), l.line_start, l.col_start, l.line_end, l.col_end)
    };
    // A source span is not an obligation identity. Multiple arithmetic, bounds,
    // or temporal checks may originate at the same expression. The legacy
    // native row does not carry enough identity to choose among them, so the
    // location bridge is permitted only for a one-native-row ↔ one-vc match.
    // Ambiguity loses a proof and remains fail-closed; it must never smear one
    // definitive verdict across neighboring obligations.
    let is_temporal_family = |vc: &VerificationCondition| {
        matches!(
            vc.kind,
            VcKind::Temporal { .. }
                | VcKind::Liveness { .. }
                | VcKind::Fairness { .. }
                | VcKind::DeadState { .. }
                | VcKind::Deadlock
        )
    };
    // A generic full-verifier safety row can carry the exact arithmetic formula
    // while losing only its precise `VcKind`, which is the legacy fallback this
    // bridge exists to recover. Other UnsupportedMir rows describe a modeling
    // capability gap, often with a sentinel `Bool(true)` formula; solving that
    // sentinel would dishonestly relabel "unsupported" as a counterexample.
    let is_supported_bridge_kind = |vc: &VerificationCondition| {
        !matches!(
            &vc.kind,
            VcKind::UnsupportedMir { kind, .. }
                if !matches!(
                    kind.as_str(),
                    "FullVerification::ArithmeticSafety"
                        | "FullVerification::BoundsCheck"
                        | "FullVerification::MemorySafety"
                )
        )
    };
    let is_placeholder_vc = |vc: &VerificationCondition| matches!(vc.formula, Formula::Bool(false));
    let is_semantically_bridgeable = |vc: &VerificationCondition| {
        !is_temporal_family(vc) && !is_placeholder_vc(vc) && is_supported_bridge_kind(vc)
    };

    // Contract-origin rows are compiler catalog markers. Body-aware contract
    // VCs carry `VerificationCondition` origins instead. Derive the exclusion
    // from the exact compiler-owned obligation sealed into each binding, never
    // from a public label, ID string, or source-span coincidence. This is a
    // reject-only classification: malformed or stale context simply prevents a
    // marker exemption and the exact semantic gates below still fail closed.
    let catalog_marker_rows: FxHashSet<usize> = result_bindings
        .iter()
        .enumerate()
        .filter_map(|(index, binding)| {
            let obligation = &binding.as_ref()?.canonical_obligation;
            let context = exactly_one_obligation_context(obligation)?;
            (context.has_current_schema()
                && matches!(
                    context.producer,
                    trust_verifier_api::ObligationProducer::CompilerMirExtract
                )
                && matches!(
                    context.origin,
                    trust_verifier_api::ObligationOrigin::Contract { .. }
                        | trust_verifier_api::ObligationOrigin::UnsupportedContract { .. }
                ))
            .then_some(index)
        })
        .collect();
    let is_bridgeable_result_row = |index: usize, vc: &VerificationCondition| {
        !catalog_marker_rows.contains(&index) && is_semantically_bridgeable(vc)
    };
    // NOTE (2026-08-29): `017a2a2af5` ran an ingress-seam demotion loop here that
    // rewrote every PREEXISTING native `Failed` on a bridgeable row to `Unknown`
    // whenever the entry-grounding gate rejected its formula. Measured, that gate
    // rejects essentially every ordinary body VC (see
    // `bridge_ay_verdict_is_admissible`), so the loop demoted 25 further genuine
    // refutations on the frozen corpus (51 -> 26) without protecting any witness.
    // It is removed with the gate; the polarity asymmetries that remain are
    // applied where they always were, to results this bridge itself produces.
    let mut solver_vc_count_by_key: FxHashMap<LocKey, usize> = FxHashMap::default();
    let mut solver_vc_count_by_exact: FxHashMap<RowKey, usize> = FxHashMap::default();
    // Trust (T3 L4, C09): every exact key the counts were built from, in order, for the
    // identity partition below; collected only while the identity oracle runs.
    let mut keyed: Vec<RowKey> = Vec::new();
    for vc in solver_vcs.iter().filter(|vc| is_semantically_bridgeable(vc)) {
        *solver_vc_count_by_key.entry(loc_key(vc)).or_default() += 1;
        if let Some(payload) = RowKey::of(vc) {
            if row_key::oracle_runs() {
                keyed.push(payload.clone());
            }
            *solver_vc_count_by_exact.entry(payload).or_default() += 1;
        }
    }
    let mut native_result_count_by_key: FxHashMap<LocKey, usize> = FxHashMap::default();
    let mut native_result_count_by_exact: FxHashMap<RowKey, usize> = FxHashMap::default();
    for (index, (vc, _)) in results.iter().enumerate() {
        if !is_bridgeable_result_row(index, vc) {
            continue;
        }
        *native_result_count_by_key.entry(loc_key(vc)).or_default() += 1;
        if let Some(payload) = RowKey::of(vc) {
            if row_key::oracle_runs() {
                keyed.push(payload.clone());
            }
            *native_result_count_by_exact.entry(payload).or_default() += 1;
        }
    }
    // The counts' lookups compare only keys whose strings hash alike; a solver row and a
    // result row whose strings differ and whose identities are one are counted here.
    row_key::count_partition(&keyed);
    let is_one_to_one_location = |key: &LocKey| {
        solver_vc_count_by_key.get(key) == Some(&1)
            && native_result_count_by_key.get(key) == Some(&1)
    };
    let bridge_key = |vc: &VerificationCondition| -> Option<BridgeKey> {
        if let Some(payload) = RowKey::of(vc)
            && solver_vc_count_by_exact.get(&payload) == Some(&1)
            && native_result_count_by_exact.get(&payload) == Some(&1)
        {
            return Some(BridgeKey::Exact(payload));
        }
        let location = loc_key(vc);
        is_one_to_one_location(&location).then_some(BridgeKey::Location(location))
    };
    let solver_vc_by_key: FxHashMap<BridgeKey, &VerificationCondition> = solver_vcs
        .iter()
        .filter(|vc| is_semantically_bridgeable(vc))
        .filter_map(|vc| bridge_key(vc).map(|key| (key, vc)))
        .collect();
    let exact_semantic_match = |native: &VerificationCondition,
                                candidate: &VerificationCondition| {
        native.function == candidate.function
            && native.formula == candidate.formula
            // `legacy_vc_from_api_obligation` uses this as "formula unavailable".
            // Proving that placeholder says nothing about the public obligation.
            && !matches!(native.formula, Formula::Bool(false))
    };

    // Re-key a definitive native result only when the exact row-bound native
    // formula is also the precise v1 formula. A coincident span alone never
    // changes the VC that later receives kernel/native authority. Trust (F2): a
    // compiler-authenticated contract catalog MARKER row is never a re-key
    // target (`is_bridgeable_result_row` excludes it) — its span is shared
    // with the body-aware VC and it carries no independent formula.
    for (index, entry) in results.iter_mut().enumerate() {
        let key = is_bridgeable_result_row(index, &entry.0).then(|| bridge_key(&entry.0)).flatten();
        if result_bindings[index]
            .as_ref()
            .is_some_and(|binding| binding.matches_row(index, &entry.0))
            && definitive(&entry.1)
            && let Some(key) = key.as_ref()
            && let Some(vc) = solver_vc_by_key.get(key).copied()
            && exact_semantic_match(&entry.0, vc)
        {
            // A generic native label can be permissive while the precise v1
            // replacement reveals proof-only semantics (most importantly a
            // Postcondition). Re-keying must not carry the old SAT verdict
            // across that semantic boundary. Demote first; the normal bridge
            // solve below may still recover an admissible proof. This is the
            // one seam of `017a2a2af5` that survives the entry-grounding
            // revert: it now fires ONLY on the kind-polarity asymmetry, never
            // on a grounding judgement about an ordinary body VC.
            // Trust (M0(c), 2026-09-29): ...unless the compiler attested this very
            // postcondition row's encoding EXACT. That attestation exists to give a
            // trust-mc refutation authority, yet this blanket demotion (017a2a2af5,
            // 2026-08-28) predates it and discarded every such refutation anyway:
            // measured, `tests/trust-contract/contract_clause_sort_match.sh` declined all
            // six of its must-refute cases, `ensures false` among them, on main and on
            // seal 321aaeda7. The demotion's named hazard, vcgen's `Not(post)` placeholder
            // over a rebound parameter, can no longer be attested: a rebound parameter is
            // not an input of a postcondition payload
            // (`annotate_trust_mc_typed_chc_lowering_status`).
            if matches!(&entry.1, VerificationResult::Failed { .. })
                && !bridge_ay_verdict_is_admissible(&vc.kind, &entry.1)
                && !result_bindings[index].as_ref().is_some_and(
                    ResultObligationBinding::postcondition_refutation_is_attested_exact,
                )
            {
                demote_inadmissible_generic_refutation(
                    &mut entry.1,
                    "generic native SAT cannot be re-keyed onto a proof-only precise obligation",
                );
                continue;
            }
            if result_bindings[index]
                .as_mut()
                .is_some_and(|binding| binding.reseal_for_semantic_replacement(index, &entry.0, vc))
            {
                entry.0 = vc.clone();
            }
        }
    }
    // Only spend ay on obligations the native route did not already decide.
    let needs_bridge: FxHashSet<BridgeKey> = results
        .iter()
        .enumerate()
        .filter_map(|(index, (vc, result))| {
            if !is_bridgeable_result_row(index, vc) {
                return None;
            }
            let key = bridge_key(vc)?;
            (result_bindings[index].as_ref().is_some_and(|binding| binding.matches_row(index, vc))
                && !definitive(result)
                && solver_vc_by_key
                    .get(&key)
                    .is_some_and(|candidate| exact_semantic_match(vc, candidate)))
            .then_some(key)
        })
        .collect();
    let to_solve: Vec<VerificationCondition> = solver_vcs
        .iter()
        .filter(|vc| is_semantically_bridgeable(vc))
        .filter(|vc| bridge_key(vc).is_some_and(|key| needs_bridge.contains(&key)))
        // Trust: temporal-family VCs are NEVER ay-bridgeable. Their `formula`
        // is a deliberate symbolic Bool placeholder (see sep_engine's temporal
        // mmap emission) — the real claim is the model check of the carried
        // StateMachineMetadata, which only the native ty engine performs. ay
        // "solving" the unconstrained placeholder returns SAT and produces a
        // FALSE refutation of a proved temporal property (location-keyed
        // matching pulls the temporal VC in whenever any other obligation at
        // the same call span needs bridging).
        .filter(|vc| {
            !matches!(
                vc.kind,
                VcKind::Temporal { .. }
                    | VcKind::Liveness { .. }
                    | VcKind::Fairness { .. }
                    | VcKind::DeadState { .. }
                    | VcKind::Deadlock
            )
        })
        // Trust (F2): `Bool(false)` bookkeeping VCs were excluded from the
        // match population above; keep them out of the solve set too — the
        // trivial UNSAT of a contentless placeholder must never be substituted
        // as the "proof" of a real obligation sharing its span.
        .filter(|vc| !is_placeholder_vc(vc))
        .cloned()
        .collect();
    if env_flag_enabled("TRUST_BRIDGE_DEBUG") {
        eprintln!(
            "TRUST_BRIDGE: results={} needs_bridge={} solver_vcs={} to_solve={}",
            results.len(),
            needs_bridge.len(),
            solver_vcs.len(),
            to_solve.len(),
        );
    }
    if to_solve.is_empty() {
        let ineligible = vec![None; results.len()];
        return (results, 0, 0, ineligible);
    }
    // The caller supplies one per-function wall-clock deadline shared by the
    // bitmask probe, per-VC solver dispatch, and clean-kernel fallback. Work not
    // reached before it lapses remains Unknown/uncertified (fail-closed).
    let bridge_deadline_lapsed = || deadline.is_some_and(|at| std::time::Instant::now() >= at);
    // Keep the v1 VC alongside its result: it carries the precise obligation kind
    // (e.g. `overflow:add`) that the native route lost, which downstream
    // gap-accounting (strict_l0_verification_failure) relies on.
    let mut by_key: FxHashMap<
        BridgeKey,
        (VerificationCondition, VerificationResult, Option<StrictSmtRevalidation>),
    > = Default::default();
    // Trust: Assertion rows whose ay/interval verdict was a live SAT that the
    // kind-polarity asymmetry refused to certify. Applied to the surviving
    // Unknown rows after substitution so the default policy cannot grant them
    // runtime-check tolerance (see TRUST_HELD_ASSERT_REFUTATION_REASON).
    let mut held_assert_refutations: FxHashSet<BridgeKey> = FxHashSet::default();
    // Negative-only producer dependency findings. A satisfiable query over an
    // unsupported loop's free state is not an executable counterexample. Keep
    // the original obligation and withhold SAT before verdict substitution;
    // no invariant is assumed and independently grounded refute lanes below
    // are unaffected.
    let mut held_loop_refutations: FxHashMap<BridgeKey, String> = FxHashMap::default();
    // Determinism plan §3.3/§3.7 (review 2026-09-30): ay results the producer
    // below drops because they stopped on a COUNT (`effort`, or `function-
    // effort`, the cap's cut). The row that survives is the native one; it is
    // told about the count after substitution (`fold_lane_stop`), so a cap that
    // binds on this lane is not dropped without a trace.
    // Trust (2026-10-02, proof-loss class 6): ... and the ones that stopped on a
    // RESOURCE backstop (ay's process-memory ceiling, the router memory guard, a
    // solver fault — `StopCause::is_resource_backstop`). Dropped silently: on main
    // `d36c551fac` aterm-grid's ledger counted 532 in-process ay `rss-backstop`
    // stops, no function past ~60% of the crate carries any ay time, and 21 rows
    // the seal proves read the native bundle's typed-input refusal as their
    // reason (`trust_types::determinism::fold_lane_stop`).
    let mut ay_lane_stops: FxHashMap<BridgeKey, VerificationResult> = FxHashMap::default();
    // Determinism plan §3.7 (2026-10-01): candidates whose decision a CLOCK took
    // from this bridge — never dispatched because the function deadline had
    // passed, an ay solve its clock ended, or an ay proof whose strict S1
    // re-solve the clock withheld (no time left, its own clock, a late finish).
    // The row each one stands for is recorded as a clock stop after
    // substitution, so the deadline is visible on it instead of the row
    // silently keeping the native verdict, or a proof that can never earn
    // authority.
    let mut bridge_clock_cuts: FxHashMap<BridgeKey, StoppingClock> = FxHashMap::default();
    // Trust: sound `-full` refutation of a free-index bitmask out-of-bounds
    // access (`s[idx & mask]`, `arr[(col & mask) + k]`, …). vcgen lowers the
    // masked index to a SYMBOLIC mixed Int/BitVec round-trip
    // `bv2nat(int2bv(idx) & int2bv(mask))` that ay/trust-vc return Unknown on.
    // Full mode already rejects that gap; this refute-only probe additionally
    // recovers the concrete counterexample and honest `Failed` classification.
    // We concretize only the free index INPUT `idx := mask` (the value that
    // maximizes the masked index) and re-solve with ay: once `idx` is concrete
    // the round-trip collapses to a constant and ay decides the rest (the
    // dependent index var, the comparison flags, the unrelated range-bound
    // inputs). SAT => the OOB is genuinely reachable => Failed. SOUND: `idx =
    // mask` is a real program input, so any model of the concretized VC is a real
    // counterexample (identical to ay reporting SAT). It can never produce a
    // proof, and a guarded-safe access (`if idx < 16 { s[idx & 31] }`)
    // concretizes to UNSAT (the guard is false at `idx = 31`) -> not Failed ->
    // abandon. Seeded into `by_key` before the main ay solve so a recognized
    // refutation takes precedence (`or_insert`). See `bitmask_concretized_oob_formula`.
    {
        for vc in &to_solve {
            if bridge_deadline_lapsed() {
                break;
            }
            let Some(concretized) = bitmask_concretized_oob_formula(&vc.formula) else { continue };
            let Some(timeout_ms) =
                remaining_solver_timeout_ms(per_vc_timeout_ms, deadline, std::time::Instant::now())
            else {
                break;
            };
            let ay_only = trust_router::Router::deterministic_in_process_ay(timeout_ms, scale);
            let mut probe = vc.clone();
            probe.formula = concretized;
            let refuted = ay_only
                .verify_all_with_deadline(std::slice::from_ref(&probe), deadline)
                .iter()
                .any(|(_, r)| matches!(r, VerificationResult::Failed { .. }));
            if refuted {
                if env_flag_enabled("TRUST_BITMASK_DEBUG") {
                    eprintln!("TRUST_BITMASK: refuted free-index bitmask OOB at {:?}", loc_key(vc));
                }
                let Some(key) = bridge_key(vc) else { continue };
                by_key.entry(key).or_insert((
                    vc.clone(),
                    VerificationResult::Failed {
                        solver: trust_types::Symbol::intern("trust-bitmask-refute"),
                        time_ms: 0,
                        counterexample: None,
                    },
                    None,
                ));
            }
        }
    }
    for (position, candidate) in to_solve.iter().enumerate() {
        let Some(candidate_key) = bridge_key(candidate) else { continue };
        if by_key.contains_key(&candidate_key) {
            continue;
        }
        // Trust (2026-09-20): an admissible INTERVAL proof, held rather than
        // committed, so the ay producer below gets a chance to re-derive it and
        // carry S1 eligibility. Undecided or inadmissible AY outcomes preserve
        // this fallback; an admissible refutation must never be hidden by it.
        let mut interval_fallback: Option<(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)> = None;
        // Keep the cheap interval lane behavior, but keep its result explicitly
        // ineligible for S1. Running it in a separate router avoids inferring
        // provenance from the public `solver` string returned by a mixed
        // portfolio.
        if remaining_solver_timeout_ms(per_vc_timeout_ms, deadline, std::time::Instant::now())
            .is_none()
        {
            bridge_clock_cuts.extend(
                to_solve[position..]
                    .iter()
                    .filter_map(|vc| bridge_key(vc))
                    .map(|key| (key, StoppingClock::FunctionDeadline)),
            );
            break;
        }
        // A saved proof is useful before AY, not only at the later certificate
        // sweep. The lookup rechecks this current VC in the current kernel and
        // never starts a cold search. Preserve all bridge identity/kind gates
        // and the later authority sweep; no cached verdict is transported.
        if let Some(root) = proof_artifact_root {
            let (evidence, _) =
                trust_certify::proof_artifacts::replay_vc_with_artifacts_reporting_stop_until(
                    candidate, root, deadline,
                );
            if evidence.is_some() {
                let result = VerificationResult::Proved {
                    solver: trust_types::Symbol::intern("clean-kernel-certified"),
                    time_ms: 0,
                    strength: trust_types::ProofStrength::smt_unsat_strict_checked(),
                    proof_certificate: None,
                    solver_warnings: None,
                    native_proof_envelope: None,
                };
                if bridge_ay_verdict_is_admissible(&candidate.kind, &result) {
                    by_key.insert(candidate_key, (candidate.clone(), result, None));
                    continue;
                }
            }
        }
        let interval = make_interval();
        if let Some((vc, res)) = interval
            .verify_all_with_deadline(std::slice::from_ref(candidate), deadline)
            .into_iter()
            .next()
            && definitive(&res)
        {
            let loop_rejection = matches!(res, VerificationResult::Failed { .. })
                .then(|| refutation_scope.rejection_reason(&vc))
                .flatten();
            if let Some(reason) = &loop_rejection {
                held_loop_refutations.insert(candidate_key.clone(), reason.clone());
            }
            // Trust (2026-09-23, D3): only a PROOF may be held; an engine
            // refutation is never overridden by a later proof. The AY seam can
            // supersede a held proof, so holding an admissible interval `Failed`
            // would let AY silently erase an engine refutation.
            // `IntervalBackend::verify` returns only `Proved`/`Unknown` today,
            // so this changes nothing
            // now; it keeps that true if the interval engine ever refutes.
            if loop_rejection.is_none()
                && bridge_ay_verdict_is_admissible(&vc.kind, &res)
                && matches!(res, VerificationResult::Failed { .. })
            {
                // Trust (2026-09-23, D3): the pre-2026-09-20 commit, unchanged.
                by_key.entry(candidate_key).or_insert((vc, res, None));
                continue;
            } else if loop_rejection.is_none()
                && bridge_ay_verdict_is_admissible(&vc.kind, &res)
                && matches!(res, VerificationResult::Proved { .. })
            {
                // Trust (2026-09-20): HOLD it, do not commit it. This used to
                // `or_insert((vc, res, None)); continue;` — the `None` being the
                // producer-revalidation slot, so an interval-decided row could
                // never become S1-eligible and reported
                // "[...] UNKNOWN (interval): Proved label lacked exact
                // kernel/native proof authority" at the authority gate, while
                // the mint that would have credited it sits twenty lines below
                // and already credits every other L0 row in this same loop.
                //
                // THE `continue` WAS NOT A RULING. It arrived with `6d8eac36ea`,
                // a provenance refactor splitting one mixed-portfolio router in
                // two, and its own comment above states the reason: avoid
                // "inferring provenance from the public `solver` string returned
                // by a mixed portfolio". That rules on the interval lane's
                // LABEL. Whether an interval-decided row may be INDEPENDENTLY
                // re-derived by ay never came up.
                //
                // So fall through to the ay producer. If ay independently
                // reaches a strict-UNSAT proof it carries the row WITH its
                // revalidation; otherwise this fallback is committed exactly as
                // before, `None` slot included.
                interval_fallback = Some((vc, res));
            } else {
                // A held interval refutation of a user assertion: record it so the
                // row cannot earn runtime-check tolerance below. Fall through — the
                // ay solve may still recover an admissible proof.
                if matches!(vc.kind, VcKind::Assertion { .. })
                    && matches!(res, VerificationResult::Failed { .. })
                {
                    held_assert_refutations.insert(candidate_key.clone());
                }
            }
        }

        let Some(timeout_ms) =
            remaining_solver_timeout_ms(per_vc_timeout_ms, deadline, std::time::Instant::now())
        else {
            // Out of budget before the ay producer: commit the held interval
            // proof exactly as the old code did and stop. This candidate (its
            // held proof never reached the ay producer that carries S1
            // eligibility) and every later one were cut by the clock.
            bridge_clock_cuts.extend(
                to_solve[position..]
                    .iter()
                    .filter_map(|vc| bridge_key(vc))
                    .map(|key| (key, StoppingClock::FunctionDeadline)),
            );
            if let Some((ivc, ires)) = interval_fallback.take() {
                by_key.entry(candidate_key).or_insert((ivc, ires, None));
            }
            break;
        };
        // This single-backend invocation is the only ordinary producer whose
        // proof may become S1-eligible. We still require a separate strict-only
        // AY check at this producer seam: the normal backend can use a bounded
        // finite fallback, and such a result must never receive SMT-replay
        // eligibility.
        let ay_only = trust_router::Router::deterministic_in_process_ay(timeout_ms, scale);
        let Some((vc, res)) = ay_only
            .verify_all_with_deadline(std::slice::from_ref(candidate), deadline)
            .into_iter()
            .next()
        else {
            if let Some((ivc, ires)) = interval_fallback.take() {
                by_key.entry(candidate_key).or_insert((ivc, ires, None));
            }
            continue;
        };
        // Postconditions remain proof-only at this generic seam and
        // `Unreachable` remains refute-only (its proof direction stays owned by
        // `promote_structurally_dead_unreachable`). Preconditions and every L0
        // safety kind pass the kind-only Proved/Failed gate: `Not(P[σ])`
        // over the call's actuals is a grounded goal whose SAT is a real
        // caller-side counterexample, and an L0 body VC's SAT is the ordinary
        // refutation this whole lane exists to deliver. The havoc'd-call-dest
        // shape is declined upstream by `trust_vcgen`'s `havoc_decline`, not
        // here. The reject-only dependency scope below additionally withholds
        // SAT over an unsupported loop's over-approximated state, without
        // changing the original query or any proof-direction decision.
        let loop_rejection = matches!(res, VerificationResult::Failed { .. })
            .then(|| refutation_scope.rejection_reason(&vc))
            .flatten();
        if let Some(reason) = &loop_rejection {
            held_loop_refutations.insert(candidate_key.clone(), reason.clone());
        }
        let accept = loop_rejection.is_none() && bridge_ay_verdict_is_admissible(&vc.kind, &res);
        if !accept {
            // A held ay refutation of a user assertion (SAT with the polarity
            // asymmetry refusing certification): the verdict is dropped, but
            // the fact that the only evidence in hand refutes the assertion is
            // recorded so the surviving Unknown row keeps NO runtime-check
            // tolerance (fail-closed; the wrong-claim guards pin this).
            if matches!(vc.kind, VcKind::Assertion { .. })
                && matches!(res, VerificationResult::Failed { .. })
            {
                held_assert_refutations.insert(candidate_key.clone());
            }
            // An undecided solve that stopped on a count or a resource
            // backstop: keep the stop for the row that survives (folded in
            // after substitution).
            let ay_stop = trust_types::determinism::StopCause::of_result(&res);
            if ay_stop.is_solver_count() || ay_stop.is_resource_backstop() {
                ay_lane_stops.entry(candidate_key.clone()).or_insert_with(|| res.clone());
            }
            // One that stopped on a clock (the function deadline the router
            // enforces, or the solve's own timeout) left the row to a clock.
            if ay_stop == trust_types::determinism::StopCause::WallBackstop {
                let clock = if bridge_deadline_lapsed() {
                    StoppingClock::FunctionDeadline
                } else {
                    StoppingClock::SolveBudget(timeout_ms)
                };
                bridge_clock_cuts.entry(candidate_key.clone()).or_insert(clock);
            }
            if let Some((ivc, ires)) = interval_fallback.take() {
                by_key.entry(candidate_key).or_insert((ivc, ires, None));
            }
            continue;
        }
        // Trust: an admissible AY refutation takes precedence over a held
        // interval proof. Keeping that proof here would erase a counterexample
        // to the same obligation. Kind polarity and loop grounding have already
        // rejected inadmissible SAT above; undecided/rejected solves keep their
        // existing interval fallback. Only a proof can receive S1 eligibility.
        let producer_revalidation = if is_strict_smt_proved_result(&res) {
            let pre_solve_now = std::time::Instant::now();
            // Determinism plan §3.2, S1 row: the strict re-solve runs on the
            // same effort budget as the solve it re-checks, and keeps S1's own
            // four-second clock (`SOLVER_REVALIDATION_BUDGET_MS`) until plan
            // T2d demotes it.
            let strict_budget_ms = if deadline.is_some_and(|at| pre_solve_now >= at) {
                None
            } else {
                remaining_solver_timeout_ms(SOLVER_REVALIDATION_BUDGET_MS, deadline, pre_solve_now)
            };
            // Eligibility is reject-only. A bounded/fallback proof, a strict
            // miss, or a strict probe that finishes late keeps the ordinary AY
            // result exactly as produced while carrying no S1 capability.
            // Determinism plan §3.7: when a CLOCK is why it carries none — no
            // time left, the re-solve's own clock, or a late finish of a
            // re-solve that would have minted — the row is a clock stop; a
            // strict miss for any other reason is not.
            match strict_budget_ms {
                None => {
                    bridge_clock_cuts
                        .entry(candidate_key.clone())
                        .or_insert(StoppingClock::FunctionDeadline);
                    None
                }
                Some(budget_ms) => {
                    let strict = strict_smt_revalidate(&vc.formula, budget_ms, scale);
                    let late = bridge_deadline_lapsed();
                    if let Some(clock) = strict_revalidation_clock(&strict, late, budget_ms) {
                        bridge_clock_cuts.entry(candidate_key.clone()).or_insert(clock);
                    }
                    strict.ok().filter(|_| !late)
                }
            }
        } else {
            None
        };
        by_key.entry(candidate_key).or_insert((vc, res, producer_revalidation));
    }
    // Trust: clean-kernel certify fallback. The router's SMT/ay backends leave some
    // `to_solve` VCs Unknown that the clean CIC kernel PROVES outright — e.g. a
    // BV-modeled i128 guarded add/overflow whose bounded operands keep the result in
    // range (trust-certify's `conjuncts_carry_bv_overflow_safe`), which ay's QF_BV
    // leaves Unknown and trust-mc reports Unsupported. Run `certify_vc` on each
    // still-undecided `to_solve` VC — it carries the REAL vcgen formula + precise kind
    // (unlike the native `UnsupportedMir` placeholder whose formula lives only in
    // metadata) — and bridge a kernel proof in as a Certified `Proved`; the outer
    // `certify_all` re-derives the CleanCic certificate from the substituted VC.
    // SOUND + fail-closed: `certify_vc` returns `Some` ONLY on a clean-kernel-checked
    // (zero-trust) UNSAT of the violation formula; a genuinely-violable obligation has
    // a SAT violation, so it stays undecided (never a false Proved).
    for (position, vc) in to_solve.iter().enumerate() {
        // Deadline check per certify attempt: certify_vc runs a fresh
        // in-process ay solve (clean-auto AyProofBackend) per VC.
        if bridge_deadline_lapsed() {
            // Every still-undecided candidate this fallback had not reached
            // was left to the clock.
            for key in to_solve[position..].iter().filter_map(|vc| bridge_key(vc)) {
                if !by_key.contains_key(&key) {
                    bridge_clock_cuts.entry(key).or_insert(StoppingClock::FunctionDeadline);
                }
            }
            break;
        }
        let Some(key) = bridge_key(vc) else { continue };
        if by_key.contains_key(&key) {
            continue;
        }
        if trust_certify::certify_vc(vc).is_some() {
            by_key.insert(
                key,
                (
                    vc.clone(),
                    VerificationResult::Proved {
                        solver: trust_types::Symbol::intern("clean-kernel-certified"),
                        time_ms: 0,
                        strength: trust_types::ProofStrength::smt_unsat_strict_checked(),
                        proof_certificate: None,
                        solver_warnings: None,
                        native_proof_envelope: None,
                    },
                    None,
                ),
            );
        }
    }
    let mut bridged_proved = 0u32;
    let mut bridged_failed = 0u32;
    let mut revalidation_eligibilities = vec![None; results.len()];
    for (index, entry) in results.iter_mut().enumerate() {
        // Catalog markers, placeholders, temporal rows, and unsupported
        // sentinels must not receive a neighboring solved VC's verdict.
        // Trust (F2): in particular a def-site contract MARKER sharing the
        // solved VC's span aggregates ALL of its contract's VCs and is
        // promoted only by the post-bridge re-key, and only when every one
        // of them proved.
        if !is_bridgeable_result_row(index, &entry.0) {
            continue;
        }
        if definitive(&entry.1) {
            continue;
        }
        if let Some((bridged_vc, bridged_res, producer_revalidation)) =
            bridge_key(&entry.0).as_ref().and_then(|key| by_key.get(key))
        {
            if !exact_semantic_match(&entry.0, bridged_vc) {
                continue;
            }
            // Central backstop for every producer above, including specialized
            // seeds inserted directly into `by_key`: the kind-polarity
            // asymmetry (proof-only `Postcondition`, refute-only `Unreachable`)
            // holds at the substitution seam too, so a future backend branch
            // cannot bypass it by seeding `by_key` directly.
            if !bridge_ay_verdict_is_admissible(&bridged_vc.kind, bridged_res) {
                continue;
            }
            // This backstop also covers specialized bitmask/interval seeds:
            // choosing a concrete input does not ground a free loop state.
            if matches!(bridged_res, VerificationResult::Failed { .. })
                && let Some(reason) = refutation_scope.rejection_reason(bridged_vc)
            {
                if let Some(key) = bridge_key(&entry.0) {
                    held_loop_refutations.insert(key, reason);
                }
                continue;
            }
            if !result_bindings[index].as_mut().is_some_and(|binding| {
                binding.reseal_for_semantic_replacement(index, &entry.0, bridged_vc)
            }) {
                continue;
            }
            match bridged_res {
                VerificationResult::Proved { .. } => bridged_proved += 1,
                VerificationResult::Failed { .. } => bridged_failed += 1,
                _ => {}
            }
            // Mint the private exact-row eligibility only from the strict AY
            // outcome carried directly by the local producer branch above.
            // Recompute and byte-compare its problem before binding it to this
            // final carrier position; even an internal stale map entry fails
            // closed.
            if matches!(bridged_res, VerificationResult::Proved { .. })
                && let Some(producer_revalidation) = producer_revalidation
            {
                let expected_problem =
                    trust_router::in_process_ay_backend::problem_smt2(&bridged_vc.formula);
                if producer_revalidation.canonical_problem.as_bytes() == expected_problem.as_bytes()
                    && let Some(row) = exact_result_row_identity(index, bridged_vc)
                {
                    revalidation_eligibilities[index] = Some(SolverRevalidationEligibility {
                        row,
                        producer_canonical_problem: producer_revalidation.canonical_problem.clone(),
                    });
                }
            }
            *entry = (bridged_vc.clone(), bridged_res.clone());
        }
    }
    // Trust: stamp the held-refutation marker onto every still-undecided
    // Assertion row whose bridge solve produced a SAT the polarity asymmetry
    // refused to certify, and every exact row whose unsupported-loop scope
    // refused the generic SAT. The verdict stays Unknown (no counterexample is
    // claimed); only the runtime-check tolerance is withdrawn — a row the
    // solver actively refuted must not compile clean as "vanilla semantics"
    // under the default completeness-gap policy.
    if (stamp_held_assert_refutations && !held_assert_refutations.is_empty())
        || !held_loop_refutations.is_empty()
    {
        for (index, entry) in results.iter_mut().enumerate() {
            if !is_bridgeable_result_row(index, &entry.0) || definitive(&entry.1) {
                continue;
            }
            let Some(key) = bridge_key(&entry.0) else { continue };
            let held_loop = held_loop_refutations.get(&key);
            if held_loop.is_none()
                && !(stamp_held_assert_refutations && held_assert_refutations.contains(&key))
            {
                continue;
            }
            let Some(candidate) = solver_vc_by_key.get(&key).copied() else { continue };
            if !exact_semantic_match(&entry.0, candidate) {
                continue;
            }
            let (solver, time_ms) = match &entry.1 {
                VerificationResult::Unknown { solver, time_ms, .. } => (*solver, *time_ms),
                VerificationResult::Timeout { solver, .. } => (*solver, 0),
                _ => continue,
            };
            // Trust: the pure generic-SAT provenance screen must retain the
            // native lane's prior stop, including a bare Unknown's unclassified
            // cause. The legacy exact-string assertion marker stays unchanged.
            entry.1 = held_loop
                .and_then(|reason| trust_vcgen::hold_generic_refutation_result(&entry.1, reason))
                .unwrap_or_else(|| VerificationResult::Unknown {
                    solver,
                    time_ms,
                    reason: held_loop
                        .cloned()
                        .unwrap_or_else(|| TRUST_HELD_ASSERT_REFUTATION_REASON.to_string()),
                });
        }
    }
    // Determinism plan §3.7 (review 2026-09-30): a row the ay producer
    // tried and left undecided because it stopped on a COUNT (or, since
    // 2026-10-02, a resource backstop) keeps the native result, which now
    // states that stop too, combined by the stop-cause rule
    // (`trust_types::determinism::fold_lane_stop`). The less reproducible
    // cause still wins: a native clock stop stays `wall-backstop`, and a native
    // Unknown that states no cause stays `unclassified` (it may be a clock the
    // row cannot see). Before this the ay stop was dropped and the row read as
    // the native lane's cause alone — a deterministic `unsupported`, or a
    // `not-reproducible` stop with no trace of the count.
    if !ay_lane_stops.is_empty() {
        for (index, entry) in results.iter_mut().enumerate() {
            // Trust: a held generic SAT is not an execution counterexample.
            // Its prefix survives the shared reason-only fold. The legacy
            // assertion marker is exact-string and must still never be folded.
            if !is_bridgeable_result_row(index, &entry.0)
                || definitive(&entry.1)
                || (!may_fold_count_stop(&entry.1) && !result_is_held_generic_refutation(&entry.1))
            {
                continue;
            }
            let Some(key) = bridge_key(&entry.0) else { continue };
            let Some(stopped) = ay_lane_stops.get(&key) else { continue };
            let Some(candidate) = solver_vc_by_key.get(&key).copied() else { continue };
            if !exact_semantic_match(&entry.0, candidate) {
                continue;
            }
            if let VerificationResult::Unknown { reason, .. } = &mut entry.1
                && let Some(folded) =
                    trust_types::determinism::fold_lane_stop(reason, "the ay producer", stopped)
            {
                *reason = folded;
            }
        }
    }
    // Determinism plan §3.7 (2026-10-01): record the row each clock-cut
    // candidate stands for — the substituted row when the bridge adopted a
    // proof the clock kept from S1 eligibility, the native row otherwise. A row
    // that did earn eligibility was not cut. `stamp_clock_stopped_rows` later
    // publishes only a row that is still undecided once every lane has run.
    if !bridge_clock_cuts.is_empty() {
        for (index, entry) in results.iter().enumerate() {
            if !is_bridgeable_result_row(index, &entry.0)
                || revalidation_eligibilities[index].is_some()
            {
                continue;
            }
            let Some(key) = bridge_key(&entry.0) else { continue };
            let Some(&clock) = bridge_clock_cuts.get(&key) else { continue };
            let Some(candidate) = solver_vc_by_key.get(&key).copied() else { continue };
            if !exact_semantic_match(&entry.0, candidate) {
                continue;
            }
            clock_stops.record_provable(index, &entry.0, clock);
        }
    }
    if env_flag_enabled("TRUST_BRIDGE_DEBUG") {
        eprintln!(
            "TRUST_BRIDGE: substituted {} obligation(s) via v1/ay ({bridged_proved} proved, {bridged_failed} failed)",
            bridged_proved + bridged_failed
        );
    }
    (results, bridged_proved, bridged_failed, revalidation_eligibilities)
}

/// Trust: match `BvToInt(BvAnd(IntToBv(<var>), IntToBv(<const>)), w, false)` at
/// THIS node, in either operand order; return the (index variable name,
/// non-negative mask). This is exactly the unsigned `idx & mask` lowering
/// `chc::binop_to_formula` emits for a bitwise-AND index expression.
fn bitmask_match_here(f: &trust_types::Formula) -> Option<(String, i128)> {
    use trust_types::Formula;
    let Formula::BvToInt(inner, _w, signed) = f else { return None };
    if *signed {
        return None;
    }
    let Formula::BvAnd(a, b, _) = inner.as_ref() else { return None };
    let int_to_bv_var = |g: &Formula| -> Option<String> {
        if let Formula::IntToBv(x, _) = g { x.var_name().map(str::to_string) } else { None }
    };
    let int_to_bv_const = |g: &Formula| -> Option<i128> {
        let Formula::IntToBv(x, _) = g else { return None };
        match x.as_ref() {
            Formula::Int(n) => Some(*n),
            Formula::UInt(n) => i128::try_from(*n).ok(),
            _ => None,
        }
    };
    for (x, y) in [(a.as_ref(), b.as_ref()), (b.as_ref(), a.as_ref())] {
        if let (Some(idx), Some(mask)) = (int_to_bv_var(x), int_to_bv_const(y))
            && mask >= 0
        {
            return Some((idx, mask));
        }
    }
    None
}

/// Trust: collect every `(idx_var, mask)` bitmask source anywhere in `f`.
fn bitmask_collect_sources(f: &trust_types::Formula, out: &mut Vec<(String, i128)>) {
    if let Some(pair) = bitmask_match_here(f) {
        out.push(pair);
    }
    trust_types::for_each_formula_child(f, |child| {
        bitmask_collect_sources(child, out);
    });
}

/// Trust: substitute every variable named in `subst` with its concrete integer
/// value (handles both `Var` and interned `SymVar` via `var_name`).
fn bitmask_substitute(
    f: &trust_types::Formula,
    subst: &FxHashMap<String, i128>,
) -> trust_types::Formula {
    f.clone().map(&mut |node| {
        let replacement = node.var_name().and_then(|n| subst.get(n).copied());
        match replacement {
            Some(v) => trust_types::Formula::Int(v),
            None => node,
        }
    })
}

/// Trust: `-full`-scoped, refute-only concretizer for a free-index bitmask
/// out-of-bounds access (`s[idx & mask]`, `arr[(col & mask) + k]`, …).
///
/// vcgen lowers a masked index to a SYMBOLIC mixed Int/BitVec round-trip
/// `bv2nat(int2bv(idx) & int2bv(mask))` conjoined with the index-bounds
/// violation. ay/trust-vc return Unknown on that symbolic round-trip. Explicit
/// full verification rejects the unknown regardless; the concretizer recovers
/// the stronger, actionable `Failed` verdict and its counterexample.
///
/// If the VC contains a UNIQUE bitmask source `(idx, mask)`, this returns the VC
/// formula with the free index input concretized to `idx := mask` — the value
/// that maximizes the masked index (every mask bit set, so `idx & mask = mask`).
/// The caller re-solves that formula with ay: once `idx` is a constant the
/// round-trip collapses to a literal and ay decides everything else (the
/// dependent index var, the comparison flags, the unrelated range-bound inputs).
///
/// Soundness — the caller only acts on an ay SAT/`Failed` verdict, never a proof:
///   * `idx = mask` is a real, in-range program input, so ANY model of the
///     concretized VC extends to a model of the original (set `idx = mask`) —
///     a genuine counterexample, identical to ay reporting SAT on the original.
///   * Concretizing can only SHRINK the solution space, so a SAT concretized VC
///     implies a SAT original; the converse is not used, so completeness (not
///     soundness) is the only thing lost when `idx = mask` is not a witness.
///   * A guarded-safe access (`if idx < 16 { s[idx & 31] }`) concretizes to
///     UNSAT (the guard `idx < 16` is false at `idx = 31`) -> not `Failed` ->
///     the caller abandons. The guarded twin is never false-refuted; it stays
///     runtime_checked, valid drop-in Rust.
/// Returns `None` (no unique bitmask source / negative mask) to abandon. Only
/// reached from the native `-full` bridge; default mode is unaffected.
fn bitmask_concretized_oob_formula(formula: &trust_types::Formula) -> Option<trust_types::Formula> {
    let mut sources = Vec::new();
    bitmask_collect_sources(formula, &mut sources);
    sources.sort();
    sources.dedup();
    if sources.len() != 1 {
        return None;
    }
    let (idx, mask) = sources.into_iter().next().unwrap();
    if mask < 0 {
        return None;
    }
    let mut subst: FxHashMap<String, i128> = FxHashMap::default();
    subst.insert(idx, mask);
    Some(bitmask_substitute(formula, &subst))
}

/// Trust: E9 in-walk ensures-citation DISCHARGE (design note
/// 2026-07-15-e9-discharge-criterion §5d). For eligible postcondition rows whose
/// complete `ensures` surface is cited, import every recognized, facet-admitted
/// definition available to this body into a per-body CLONE of the
/// island-checked kernel env, then grade every cited theorem against its
/// postcondition goal. A result-bearing clause binds `result` to the admitted
/// self-definition; a result-free clause is ∀-closed over the parameters and
/// can cover an arbitrary body. A
/// `Certified` grade re-typechecks the proof term against that exact goal and
/// audits its full transitive closure sorry/axiom-free (`cert_meter::grade`),
/// i.e. a kernel proof of the postcondition for ALL inputs — rewrite the
/// obligation to `Proved` and report the discharged rows so the caller mints
/// the sealed `EnsuresCitationDischarge` authority.
///
/// FAIL-CLOSED bounded fragment, every miss leaves every obligation untouched:
/// - the island env is absent/tainted, or the theorem fails ANY gate;
/// - a result-bearing clause's self-function is not facet-admitted or its body
///   is not a recognized elaborator shape; calls in any clause are likewise
///   admitted only through recognized definitions;
/// - any citation misses, or any ensures clause has NO kernel route. Multiple
///   clauses are accepted only as an all-certified conjunction because exact
///   per-clause span attribution is not yet the authority key. Trust: an
///   UNCITED clause has a kernel route only when it names an admissible Clean
///   island definition and discharges by definitional equality; this lane then
///   reports its certified cited part and the island-defeq lane decides the
///   function (mixed surface). Any other uncited clause declines everything;
/// - a candidate row is neither solver-UNKNOWN nor an unsealed `Proved` label.
///   A solver REFUTATION or timeout is never
///   overridden: a kernel "proof" against a refuted VC would mean the
///   elaborated goal diverged from the VC — that must stay fatal.
/// Rewrite each whole-word occurrence of `<param>'` (a primed post-state
/// identifier for a by-value parameter) to `<param>` in a clause snippet. Sound
/// only for by-value parameters, whose post-state binding value equals the
/// entry value; callers pass only such names. Word-boundary matched so `xy'`
/// does not match for param `x`, and the trailing `'` must not be part of a
/// longer token (e.g. a char/lifetime) — it is consumed exactly.
///
/// Trust (CITE-PRIME-NONASCII-1, 2026-10-05): the word boundary is decided on
/// whole CHARACTERS with rustc's own identifier predicate
/// (`rustc_lexer::is_id_continue`, XID_Continue), never on bytes. The former
/// byte test (`is_ascii_alphanumeric() || b'_'`) read the UTF-8 continuation
/// byte of `é` in `éa'` as a boundary, so the pass for an immutable parameter
/// `a` rewrote the post-state of `mut éa` to its entry value: the false clause
/// `éa' >= éa` became the tautology `éa >= éa`, and a reflexivity theorem
/// kernel-certified it. With rustc's predicate, `p'` is rewritten only where
/// `p` is the WHOLE identifier token rustc lexed; any other spelling is left
/// primed and fails closed in elaboration. All four callers share this helper:
/// the E9 citation sweep (`trust_check_citations`), the in-walk and deferred
/// cited lanes (`discharge_ensures_citations_in_walk`,
/// `grade_pending_import_citation_goals`) and the uncited island-defeq lane
/// (`grade_uncited_island_ensures_clause`).
fn rewrite_by_value_prime(text: &str, param: &str) -> String {
    // Trust: rustc's identifier-continue predicate (XID_Continue already
    // contains `_`; it is spelled out so the ASCII reading stays obvious).
    let is_ident = |c: char| c == '_' || rustc_lexer::is_id_continue(c);
    let needle = format!("{param}'");
    let mut out = String::with_capacity(text.len());
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < text.len() {
        if text[i..].starts_with(&needle) {
            // Trust: `i` is always a char boundary (it advances by whole chars
            // or by the whole needle), so both slices below are valid.
            let before_ok = text[..i].chars().next_back().is_none_or(|c| !is_ident(c));
            let after = i + needle.len();
            // The char after `p'` must not extend the token (guards `p'a`,
            // `p'é`, and Rust lifetime/char forms adjacent to the name).
            let after_ok = text[after..].chars().next().is_none_or(|c| !is_ident(c));
            if before_ok && after_ok {
                out.push_str(param);
                i = after;
                continue;
            }
        }
        let ch = text[i..].chars().next().unwrap();
        out.push(ch);
        i += ch.len_utf8();
    }
    out
}

/// Recognize the native full-verifier representation of one `ensures` clause.
///
/// The description is user-controlled, so only the final `contract_id` suffix
/// appended by [`legacy_unsupported_kind_detail`] is authoritative. Parsing
/// the last suffix also prevents a source description containing an
/// ensures-shaped lookalike from being mistaken for compiler metadata when the
/// real transported contract is a `requires` clause.
fn is_full_verification_unsupported_ensures(kind: &str, detail: &str) -> bool {
    if kind != "FullVerification::trust.contract::unsupported" {
        return false;
    }
    let Some((_, transported)) = detail.rsplit_once("; contract_id=") else {
        return false;
    };
    let contract_id = transported
        .split_once("; metadata_keys=")
        .map_or(transported, |(contract_id, _)| contract_id);
    let Some((owner_and_kind, index)) = contract_id.rsplit_once(':') else {
        return false;
    };
    if index.is_empty() || !index.bytes().all(|byte| byte.is_ascii_digit()) {
        return false;
    }
    let Some((owner, clause_kind)) = owner_and_kind.rsplit_once(':') else {
        return false;
    };
    !owner.is_empty() && clause_kind == "ensures"
}

fn is_spec_ensures_unparseable_obligation(kind: &str, detail: &str) -> bool {
    kind == "SpecEnsuresUnparseable"
        || (kind == "FullVerification::trust.vc::unsupported_mir"
            && detail.starts_with("unsupported MIR `SpecEnsuresUnparseable`:"))
}

/// Trust (CITE-ENSURES-SUBSTRING-1, 2026-10-05): an `UnsupportedMir` row is an
/// ensures-clause stand-in ONLY through the two exact recognizers above. A
/// third arm used to accept any kind CONTAINING `trust.contract::unsupported`
/// whose detail CONTAINED `:ensures:` anywhere. The contract id is
/// `trust-contract:<def path>:<clause kind>:<index>`, so for any function whose
/// def path has an `ensures` segment (`mod ensures`, a fn named `ensures`, a
/// crate named `ensures`) the fail-closed `requires` marker
/// (`…::ensures::f:requires:0`) matched too: the E9 lane stamped the
/// kernel proof of the ENSURES goal onto it and `retired_ensures_sentinel`
/// published it as a Certified postcondition, turning a strict build green on
/// a module name. The only producer of that kind string is
/// [`legacy_unsupported_kind_detail`], which always spells it
/// `FullVerification::trust.contract::unsupported` with a final
/// `; contract_id=` suffix, and [`is_full_verification_unsupported_ensures`]
/// already parses exactly that, so the arm added nothing but the
/// misclassification and is deleted.
fn is_ensures_clause_obligation(vc: &VerificationCondition) -> bool {
    matches!(vc.kind, VcKind::Postcondition)
        || matches!(
            &vc.kind,
            VcKind::UnsupportedMir { kind, detail }
                if is_spec_ensures_unparseable_obligation(kind, detail)
                    || is_full_verification_unsupported_ensures(kind, detail)
        )
}

/// Trust: I0 (ref-self design §4.0) — the help line a clause author sees when
/// `name` is a Rust binding of the specified function that the clause tried to
/// resolve against a same-named ADMITTED item (a Clean island definition or an
/// E6-admitted program function).
///
/// The cases are exactly the readings a parameter can have in this lane, and
/// the help states which one applies. That is why it is computed here and not
/// in the elaborator: the elaborator sees only the flattened rows and cannot
/// tell a by-value struct parameter from a borrowed one, nor name the fields
/// that ARE binders. v7/D3: what was refused, why, and the concrete options.
///
/// Trust (2026-09-30): the parameter type is rendered under
/// `with_no_trimmed_paths!`. This line is NOT diagnostic text when it is
/// built: [`trust_fn_clause_shadow_idents`] computes it EAGERLY for every
/// named parameter of every body the E9 admission seam visits, and it reaches
/// a diagnostic only if a clause later names a shadowed binding. A bare `{ty}`
/// prints an ADT through `trimmed_def_paths`, which arms rustc's
/// `must_produce_diag`, so a compile that emitted no diagnostic ICEd at
/// session teardown (exit 101) AFTER writing its proved rows. MEASURED on
/// stage 1 of `bd8681c2628` on the raw-trustc JSON wire
/// (`-Ztrust-verify-output=json --error-format=human`), strict, default and
/// `certify` alike (and under `--emit=metadata`), for a fully proved
/// `ensures`-bearing fn with a parameter whose type names an ADT — a trait
/// impl method's `&self` (the reported shape),
/// `&mut self` and by-value `self`, an inherent method's `&self`, a by-value
/// or borrowed struct, an `Option<u32>` — while a scalar-only signature and a
/// trait DEFAULT method (`&Self` names no ADT) passed. Untrimmed is also the
/// deterministic spelling: the line rides in the clause-local facet table, and
/// a trimmed path varies with the crate graph in view.
/// `tests/ui/trust/ensures_adt_param_json_wire_no_ice.rs` pins it.
fn trust_clause_shadow_help<'tcx>(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>,
    name: &str,
    ty: Ty<'tcx>,
    // Trust (ref-self I1): this parameter's frozen-shared-pointee row list, when
    // it has one.
    frozen: Option<&trust_types::e6_param_view::FrozenPointee>,
) -> String {
    with_no_trimmed_paths!(trust_clause_shadow_help_text(tcx, name, ty, frozen))
}

/// [`trust_clause_shadow_help`]'s body; call that, never this, so the type is
/// always rendered untrimmed.
fn trust_clause_shadow_help_text<'tcx>(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>,
    name: &str,
    ty: Ty<'tcx>,
    frozen: Option<&trust_types::e6_param_view::FrozenPointee>,
) -> String {
    if trust_resolved_scalar_type(ty).is_some() {
        // Unreachable from the bare-identifier path (a scalar parameter HAS the
        // binder `name`, and `elab_term` consults binders first), but recorded
        // so the map is the honest set of Rust bindings rather than a curated
        // subset — and reachable from the CALL arm, where `name(…)` has no
        // binder reading at all.
        return format!(
            "`{name}: {ty}` is a by-value scalar parameter and already carries the clause \
             binder `{name}`; options: (1) use it; (2) if you meant the item `{name}`, rename \
             the parameter or the item"
        );
    }
    let rows = trust_param_scalar_rows(tcx, name, ty);
    if !rows.is_empty() {
        let fields = rows.iter().map(|(row, _)| format!("`{row}`")).collect::<Vec<_>>().join(", ");
        return format!(
            "`{name}: {ty}` is a by-value struct parameter, and a clause binds its SCALAR \
             FIELDS, never the aggregate; options: (1) name a field ({fields}); (2) keep the \
             clause solver-only (no island call, no `by <theorem>`); (3) if you meant the item \
             `{name}`, rename the parameter or the item"
        );
    }
    if let Some(pointee) = frozen {
        let fields = pointee
            .rows
            .iter()
            .map(|row| format!("`{name}.{}`", row.field_name))
            .collect::<Vec<_>>()
            .join(", ");
        return format!(
            "`{name}: {ty}` is a FROZEN shared borrow, and a clause binds the pointee's \
             scalar FIELDS AT ENTRY, never the borrow itself; options: (1) name a field \
             ({fields}); (2) keep the clause solver-only (no island call, no `by <theorem>`); \
             (3) if you meant the item `{name}`, rename the parameter or the item"
        );
    }
    if matches!(ty.kind(), ty::Ref(..)) {
        return format!(
            "`{name}: {ty}` is a BORROWED parameter that this lane cannot read — a shared \
             borrow contributes `{name}.<field>` clause binders only when the pointee is a \
             `Freeze` struct whose every declared field is a supported scalar, and this one \
             is not; options: (1) take the pointee by value if it is `Copy`, or read the \
             scalars out and put the Lean-citing clause on a by-value helper; (2) keep the \
             clause solver-only (no island call, no `by <theorem>`): the native verifier \
             reads a shared borrow's pointee as `*{name}`; (3) if you meant the item \
             `{name}`, rename the parameter or the item"
        );
    }
    format!(
        "`{name}: {ty}` has no scalar reading in this lane, so it contributes no clause \
         binder; options: (1) pass the scalars the contract needs by value and contract that \
         helper; (2) keep the clause solver-only (no island call, no `by <theorem>`); (3) if \
         you meant the item `{name}`, rename the parameter or the item"
    )
}

/// Trust: every identifier a function's own parameter patterns BIND, whatever
/// its type, plus the CONST GENERICS in scope — each mapped to the v7/D3 help
/// line [`trust_clause_shadow_help`] computes. ONE oracle with two consumers,
/// so the admission seam and the elaborator can never disagree about what a
/// signature binds.
///
/// `trust_fn_param_type_names` reports only the SCALAR-typed rows the clause
/// elaborator can type, so it is not a shadowing oracle: a `&T`, slice, closure
/// or fn-pointer parameter named like an island definition is missing from it,
/// and an identifier scan that consulted only that list would resolve the
/// clause's `f(a)` to the island definition `f` while the Rust reading is the
/// parameter.
///
/// A CONST GENERIC is an ordinary value name in the clause's Rust scope and can
/// coexist with a same-named admitted item for the same reason a parameter can:
/// it is not an ITEM, so `E0428` never fires for it, and
/// [`trust_rust_value_item_named`] does not match `DefKind::ConstParam` either.
/// `fn f<const N: usize>() -> u64 ensures result == N` beside
/// `clean { def N : UInt64 := 3 }` was unguarded before this list carried them.
///
/// SOUNDNESS: this set only ever REMOVES a resolution. A clause whose meaning
/// changes because of it was resolving a Rust binding's name to an unrelated
/// kernel constant. No name is excluded — not `result`, not the function's own
/// name — because an exclusion is a hole, and the E9 lanes key `result` and the
/// self-call in keyspaces this bare-name map does not reach
/// (`trust_spec_elab::self_fn_spelling` is the canonical def-path).
fn trust_fn_clause_shadow_idents(tcx: TyCtxt<'_>, local: LocalDefId) -> BTreeMap<String, String> {
    use rustc_hir::PatKind;

    let mut out: BTreeMap<String, String> = BTreeMap::new();
    let body = tcx.hir_body_owned_by(local);
    for param in body.params {
        // EVERY name the pattern binds, not only a top-level one: `&Point { x,
        // .. }: &Point` puts `x` in the clause's Rust scope exactly as `p:
        // &Point` puts `p` there, and a nested binding has no row under any
        // spelling, so it is precisely the shape a capture hides in.
        param.pat.each_binding(|_, _, _, ident| {
            let name = ident.name.as_str().to_string();
            let help = format!(
                "`{name}` is bound by a pattern in this function's parameter list and has no \
                 clause binder; options: (1) name a parameter this lane can read (a by-value \
                 scalar, or a scalar field of a by-value struct); (2) keep the clause \
                 solver-only (no island call, no `by <theorem>`); (3) if you meant the item \
                 `{name}`, rename the parameter or the item"
            );
            out.entry(name).or_insert(help);
        });
    }
    // A plain `p: T` is the case an author almost always means and the only one
    // whose TYPE this lane can state, so it replaces the pattern line with one
    // that names the rows that ARE binders. Typing needs a fn decl; a body
    // without one keeps the pattern-level help (membership is unchanged either
    // way, and membership is what the refusal is made of).
    if tcx.hir_fn_decl_by_hir_id(tcx.local_def_id_to_hir_id(local)).is_some() {
        let typeck_results = tcx.typeck(local);
        // Trust (ref-self I1): a FROZEN shared parameter now does contribute
        // clause binders (`p.x`), so the help for it must name them instead of
        // saying it contributes none. Membership in the shadow map is unchanged
        // — `each_binding` above enumerates every bound name independently —
        // and only the advice moves.
        let frozen = trust_frozen_shared_param_table(tcx, local);
        for (position, param) in body.params.iter().enumerate() {
            if let PatKind::Binding(_, _, ident, None) = param.pat.kind {
                let name = ident.name.as_str().to_string();
                let help = trust_clause_shadow_help(
                    tcx,
                    &name,
                    typeck_results.pat_ty(param.pat),
                    frozen.get(&position),
                );
                out.insert(name, help);
            }
        }
    }
    let mut generics = Some(tcx.generics_of(local.to_def_id()));
    while let Some(current) = generics {
        for param in &current.own_params {
            if matches!(param.kind, ty::GenericParamDefKind::Const { .. }) {
                let name = param.name.to_string();
                let help = format!(
                    "`{name}` is a const generic parameter of this function, not a clause \
                     binder; options: (1) pass the value as a run-time parameter the clause \
                     can name; (2) keep the clause solver-only (no island call, no `by \
                     <theorem>`); (3) if you meant the item `{name}`, rename the parameter \
                     or the item"
                );
                out.entry(name).or_insert(help);
            }
        }
        generics = current.parent.map(|parent| tcx.generics_of(parent));
    }
    out
}

/// Trust: a Rust VALUE item of this crate whose name is `name` — a `fn`
/// (free, associated or foreign), `const`, `static`, unit/tuple `struct`, or a
/// single `use` import. A clause identifier that names one has a Rust reading,
/// so it is never re-read as a Clean island definition of the same name.
///
/// The facet-key collision check inside `admit_certified_island_definition`
/// already refuses a same-named Rust `fn`, but only once that `fn` has a facet
/// record — in-walk that depends on walk order, and a spelling evicted as
/// ambiguous has none at all. This check is order-independent. A Clean-origin
/// (island-minted) item has only a SYNTHETIC HIR owner that no module lists, so
/// `hir_crate_items(()).definitions()` never yields it (see
/// `clean_origin_mir::clean_origin_defs`): the island definition's OWN minted
/// `fn` does not shadow it.
fn trust_rust_value_item_named(tcx: TyCtxt<'_>, name: &str) -> Option<LocalDefId> {
    tcx.hir_crate_items(()).definitions().find(|&def| match tcx.def_kind(def) {
        DefKind::Fn
        | DefKind::AssocFn
        | DefKind::Const { .. }
        | DefKind::AssocConst { .. }
        | DefKind::Static { .. }
        | DefKind::Struct => {
            tcx.opt_item_name(def.to_def_id()).is_some_and(|item| item.as_str() == name)
        }
        DefKind::Use => matches!(
            tcx.hir_expect_item(def).kind,
            rustc_hir::ItemKind::Use(rustc_hir::UseTree { kind: rustc_hir::UseKind::Single(ident), .. })
                if ident.name.as_str() == name
        ),
        _ => false,
    })
}

/// Trust: E9 evidence — which kernel route closed one `ensures` clause, and its
/// referent: the cited theorem's name, or the island definition the clause was
/// judged definitionally equal against.
#[derive(Clone)]
enum GradedEnsuresRoute {
    CitedTheorem(String),
    IslandDefeq(String),
}

/// Trust: E9 evidence — the inputs ONE `ensures` clause's kernel grade actually
/// received, kept so [`mint_ensures_discharge_certificate`] can re-judge exactly
/// that clause. `text` is the post-prime-rewrite string the grade call got and
/// `facets` the CLAUSE-LOCAL table it got (island definitions admitted for that
/// clause alone); re-elaborating from the composed table instead would certify a
/// different goal from the one the row rests on, or fail to elaborate at all.
#[derive(Clone)]
struct GradedEnsuresClause {
    text: String,
    facets: trust_spec_elab::FacetTable,
    route: GradedEnsuresRoute,
}

/// Trust: E9 evidence — mint the kernel-audited `CleanCic` certificate for a
/// function's COMPLETE discharged `ensures` surface, from the same environment,
/// clause texts, clause-local facet tables, binder types and self spelling the
/// grade calls used.
///
/// `graded` must be EVERY ensures clause of the function, because the
/// conjunction rule is what licenses discharging rows that are never attributed
/// to individual clauses: a certificate over a proper subset would misdescribe
/// what was proved. `expected_clauses` is that count, re-checked here rather
/// than assumed — a mismatch mints nothing.
///
/// `None` (fail closed) on any miss: elaboration, lookup, the producer's full
/// `Environment::audit_certification`, serialization or the size bound. The rows
/// then ship evidence-free and the authenticated publication boundary withholds
/// them, exactly as it did before this lane existed. No verdict depends on it.
fn mint_ensures_discharge_certificate(
    env: &trust_certify::clean_island::KernelEnvironment,
    graded: &[GradedEnsuresClause],
    expected_clauses: usize,
    params: &[(&str, &str)],
    self_ident: &str,
    def_path: &str,
    lane: &str,
    // Trust (nat-lift): the FIFTH E9 elaboration site. `rebuild_judgment`
    // re-elaborates every clause, so it must run at the SAME mode with the SAME
    // resolved `result` spelling the grade call used — otherwise a lifted clause
    // re-elaborates as `unsupported term shape` here and the function's WHOLE
    // surface (lifted and unlifted clauses alike) ships evidence-free.
    inputs: &trust_spec_elab::ProofClauseInputs,
) -> Option<trust_certify::clean_island::EnsuresDischargeCertificate> {
    use trust_certify::clean_island::{EnsuresDischargeClause, EnsuresDischargeRoute};
    let certificate = if graded.is_empty() || graded.len() != expected_clauses {
        None
    } else {
        let clauses: Vec<EnsuresDischargeClause<'_>> = graded
            .iter()
            .map(|clause| EnsuresDischargeClause {
                clause_src: clause.text.as_str(),
                route: match &clause.route {
                    GradedEnsuresRoute::CitedTheorem(theorem) => {
                        EnsuresDischargeRoute::CitedTheorem(theorem.as_str())
                    }
                    GradedEnsuresRoute::IslandDefeq(definition) => {
                        EnsuresDischargeRoute::IslandDefeq(definition.as_str())
                    }
                },
                facets: &clause.facets,
            })
            .collect();
        trust_certify::clean_island::certify_ensures_discharge(
            env, &clauses, params, self_ident, inputs,
        )
    };
    if certificate.is_none() && trust_env_var_present("TRUST_E9_DEBUG") {
        eprintln!(
            "TRUST_E9_DEBUG EVIDENCE-NOT-MINTED fn={def_path} lane={lane} \
             clauses={} expected={expected_clauses}",
            graded.len()
        );
    }
    certificate
}

/// Trust: what offering a clause's island definitions to its facet table did.
struct ClauseIslandAdmissions {
    /// Admitted definition names, in clause order (called spellings first, then
    /// bare zero-arity spellings).
    admitted: Vec<String>,
    /// `(name, reason)` for every identifier that DOES name a kernel-registered
    /// island definition and was nevertheless kept out of the clause. An
    /// identifier that names no island definition is not reported: it is an
    /// ordinary Rust name, and its refusal is noise.
    refused: Vec<(String, String)>,
}

/// Trust: E9 — admit the Clean island DEFINITIONS one `ensures` clause names
/// into that clause's OWN facet table, so the clause elaborator resolves
/// `spec(x)` (or a bare zero-arity `spec`) to the kernel constant. This is the
/// single admission seam of all four E9 consumers — the cited in-walk lane, the
/// deferred post-walk lane, the diagnostic sweep, and the uncited island-defeq
/// lane (whose inline scan this replaces, unchanged except for the two
/// name-resolution refusals below).
///
/// It grants NOTHING by itself. `admit_certified_island_definition` is the
/// authority: it re-runs the certification audit on the named declaration
/// (type, body and full provenance closure), refuses trust debt, unsafe/partial
/// declarations, the reserved `trust_import_*` namespace and any collision with
/// an existing program-function facet key. A definition admitted here only
/// changes WHICH goal the kernel is asked to check; the cited theorem (or
/// `Eq.refl`) must still prove that goal under `cert_meter::grade`.
///
/// Name resolution is where a wrong reading could enter, so two refusals sit in
/// front of the admitter, for called and bare spellings alike:
///  * a name BOUND BY THIS FUNCTION'S SIGNATURE is that binding
///    ([`trust_fn_clause_shadow_idents`] — every pattern binding whatever its
///    type, and every const generic in scope);
///  * a name a Rust value item of this crate also bears is the Rust item
///    ([`trust_rust_value_item_named`]).
/// `result`, `true` and `false` are never candidates.
///
/// I0 (ref-self design §4.0): those two refusals guard the ISLAND admissions
/// this function performs, and nothing else. A PROGRAM FUNCTION is admitted far
/// upstream — `trust_mint_program_admissions` and the in-walk mint key every
/// recognized crate function under its def-path AND every
/// `trust_spec_elab::spec_spellings` suffix, the BARE last segment first — with
/// no parameter-name filter anywhere, so `pub fn cap() -> u32` beside
/// `fn f(cap: C) -> u32 ensures result == cap` resolved the clause's `cap` to
/// the crate function's kernel constant while Rust reads it as the parameter.
/// The same shadow set is therefore INSTALLED on the clause-local table here,
/// where `local` and the clause are known together, and the elaborator refuses
/// a shadowed name against whatever admission reached it
/// (`trust_spec_elab::ambient_bare_admission` / `ambient_call_admission`). This
/// is the single seam all four E9 consumers pass through, which is why the
/// install lives here rather than at four call sites that could drift apart.
///
/// The map travels with the clause-local table into `GradedEnsuresClause::
/// facets`, so the FIFTH E9 site — `certify_ensures_discharge`'s
/// `rebuild_judgment`, which re-elaborates each clause to mint the publishable
/// `CleanCic` payload — builds the very goal that was graded. A clause that
/// grades cannot lose its evidence to this guard.
///
/// The caller passes a CLAUSE-LOCAL table: reusing an island facet across
/// clauses would trip the collision guard on a repeated call and would let one
/// clause's elaboration state reach another.
fn admit_clause_island_definitions(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'_>,
    local: LocalDefId,
    env: &trust_certify::clean_island::KernelEnvironment,
    def_path: &str,
    clause_text: &str,
    clause_table: &mut trust_spec_elab::FacetTable,
) -> ClauseIslandAdmissions {
    let bound = trust_fn_clause_shadow_idents(tcx, local);
    // I0: the elaborator's half of the same rule, for the admissions that never
    // pass through this function (program functions, reflected carriers). See
    // this function's doc comment.
    clause_table.set_shadowed_idents(bound.iter().map(|(name, help)| (name.clone(), help.clone())));
    let mut candidates = crate::trust_contract_query::called_identifiers(clause_text);
    candidates.extend(crate::trust_contract_query::bare_identifiers(clause_text));
    let mut seen = FxHashSet::default();
    let mut out = ClauseIslandAdmissions { admitted: Vec::new(), refused: Vec::new() };
    for name in candidates {
        if name == "result" || name == "true" || name == "false" || !seen.insert(name.clone()) {
            continue;
        }
        let names_island_definition =
            trust_certify::clean_island::island_definition_value(env, &name).is_some();
        let refusal = if bound.contains_key(&name) {
            Some(format!(
                "`{name}` is bound by the signature of `{def_path}` (a parameter pattern or a \
                 const generic), and a signature binding shadows an island definition of the \
                 same name in that function's clauses; rename the binding or the island \
                 definition"
            ))
        } else if names_island_definition
            && let Some(item) = trust_rust_value_item_named(tcx, &name)
        {
            Some(format!(
                "this crate also declares the Rust item `{}`, so `{name}` in a clause is read as \
                 the Rust item, never as the island definition; rename one of the two",
                trust_mir_extract::safe_def_path_str(tcx, item.to_def_id())
            ))
        } else {
            trust_certify::clean_island::admit_certified_island_definition(env, &name, clause_table)
                .err()
        };
        match refusal {
            None => out.admitted.push(name),
            Some(reason) => {
                if std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some() {
                    eprintln!(
                        "TRUST_E9_DEBUG ISLAND-ADMISSION-REFUSED fn={def_path} callee={name} \
                         reason={reason}"
                    );
                }
                if names_island_definition {
                    out.refused.push((name, reason));
                }
            }
        }
    }
    out
}

fn discharge_ensures_citations_in_walk<'tcx>(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>,
    body: &Body<'tcx>,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    results: &mut [(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)],
    // Trust: set to the `+`-joined theorem list when this is a MIXED surface
    // (some `ensures` clauses cited, some not) whose every CITED clause
    // Certified-graded. Nothing is discharged in that case: the caller hands
    // the list to `discharge_uncited_island_ensures_in_walk`, which discharges
    // the function only if every UNCITED clause also kernel-discharges.
    // Trust (OR-A1 H1): the list travels WITH its rung — the weakest
    // requires-link over the cited clauses, `None` when every one certified
    // unconditionally — so the uncited lane cannot seal a conditional credit
    // under the `Certified` variant.
    mixed_cited_part: &mut Option<(String, Option<trust_certify::clean_island::RequiresLink>)>,
    // Trust (OR-A1 H2): rows whose existing authority is already a static
    // proof (`trust_rows_with_static_proof`). A CONDITIONAL credit skips them;
    // an unconditional one is unaffected.
    static_proved_rows: &FxHashSet<usize>,
    // Trust: E9 evidence — the inputs every CITED clause's grade received, set
    // together with `mixed_cited_part` (and on the all-cited success path).
    // Empty on every fail-closed return.
    graded_cited: &mut Vec<GradedEnsuresClause>,
    // Trust: E9 evidence out-slot, written ONLY on the ALL-CITED success path
    // below (every fail-closed early return leaves it `None`): the
    // kernel-audited `CleanCic` certificate for the whole discharged surface,
    // bound per row by the caller. On a MIXED surface this lane mints nothing —
    // it owns only half the conjunction; it hands `graded_cited` to
    // `discharge_uncited_island_ensures_in_walk`, which mints over both halves.
    certificate: &mut Option<trust_certify::clean_island::EnsuresDischargeCertificate>,
    // Trust (OR-A1 H1): each discharged row carries the rung it was credited on
    // (`None` = unconditional) to the ONE authority constructor.
) -> Vec<(usize, String, Option<trust_certify::clean_island::RequiresLink>)> {
    let Some(local) = body.source.def_id().as_local() else {
        return Vec::new();
    };
    let bundle = tcx.trust_contracts(local.to_def_id());
    let cited: Vec<PendingImportCitationClause> = bundle
        .contracts
        .iter()
        .enumerate()
        .filter(|(_, contract)| contract.kind == TrustContractKind::Ensures)
        .filter_map(|(contract_index, contract)| {
            contract.citation.map(|citation| PendingImportCitationClause {
                contract_index: u64::try_from(contract_index)
                    .expect("a dense contract index fits u64"),
                clause: cited_ensures_clause_text(tcx, contract),
                theorem: citation.name,
            })
        })
        .collect();
    if cited.is_empty() {
        return Vec::new();
    }
    // The clause's obligation surfaces either as a real `Postcondition` VC (the
    // predicate was in the solver fragment) or as the fail-closed
    // `SpecEnsuresUnparseable` sentinel (a call-bearing predicate the VC lane
    // cannot represent — exactly the definitional-use case E6+E9 exist for).
    // Both are THE single ensures clause's stand-ins under the single-clause
    // pairing below, and the kernel proof of the clause covers them.
    // The sentinel appears in three compiler-owned representations:
    // direct `UnsupportedMir { kind: "SpecEnsuresUnparseable" }` on the default
    // lane, and round-tripped through the full-verifier as
    // `UnsupportedMir { kind: "FullVerification::trust.vc::unsupported_mir" }`
    // with the original kind preserved at the start of `detail`, plus a
    // compiler-contract `trust.contract::unsupported` row whose final
    // `contract_id` suffix names the exact ensures clause. Do not use substring
    // matching here: descriptions can contain source text.
    // Trust (CITE-ENSURES-SUBSTRING-1, 2026-10-05): that third spelling — a
    // query-lowering refusal round-tripped as
    // `FullVerification::trust.contract::unsupported` — is recognized ONLY by
    // `is_full_verification_unsupported_ensures`, which requires the clause-kind
    // segment of the final `contract_id` to be exactly `ensures`. A substring
    // `:ensures:` test also matched a `requires` marker whose owner def path has
    // an `ensures` segment, and was removed from `is_ensures_clause_obligation`.
    // The quarantine seam deliberately uses this same classifier when binding
    // indices to the finally selected E4/E5 carrier.
    let postcondition_indices: Vec<usize> = results
        .iter()
        .enumerate()
        .filter(|(_, (vc, _))| is_ensures_clause_obligation(vc))
        .map(|(index, _)| index)
        .collect();
    if std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some() {
        let kinds: Vec<String> = results
            .iter()
            .map(|(vc, r)| {
                let outcome = match r {
                    VerificationResult::Proved { .. } => "Proved",
                    VerificationResult::Failed { .. } => "Failed",
                    VerificationResult::Unknown { .. } => "Unknown",
                    VerificationResult::Timeout { .. } => "Timeout",
                    _ => "Other",
                };
                format!("{:?}/{}/{}@{:?}", vc.kind, r.solver_name(), outcome, vc.location)
            })
            .collect();
        eprintln!(
            "TRUST_E9_DEBUG fn={} cited={:?} results={:?} postcondition_indices={:?}",
            func.def_path,
            cited.iter().map(|clause| clause.theorem.as_str()).collect::<Vec<_>>(),
            kinds,
            postcondition_indices,
        );
    }
    // The single cited clause may surface as MORE THAN ONE Postcondition entry
    // (the legacy vcgen obligation and its native round-trip both appear in
    // `results`). All entries must be the SAME clause — one source location —
    // and every one solver-UNKNOWN; the one kernel proof of the clause then
    // discharges each of its representations. Distinct locations mean multiple
    // ensures clauses: pairing is ambiguous, fail closed (slice-1 fragment).
    if postcondition_indices.is_empty() {
        record_pending_import_citation(tcx, local, func, &cited, &[]);
        return Vec::new();
    }
    // Pairing (conjunction rule): EVERY ensures clause of the function must be
    // cited, and (below) EVERY citation must Certified-grade — then the
    // conjunction of all ensures clauses is kernel-proven, so every
    // clause-obligation entry in `results` (whatever span granularity it
    // carries: predicate span, whole-signature span, sentinel round-trip) is a
    // representation of some proven clause and may discharge. A partially
    // cited function fails closed: no per-entry span attribution is attempted,
    // so nothing discharges unless the whole postcondition surface is proven.
    //
    // Trust: a MIXED surface keeps that rule and changes only WHO may prove an
    // uncited clause. "Every ensures clause kernel-proven" is what licenses
    // discharging every clause-obligation row without attributing rows to
    // clauses; a cited theorem is one kernel route to a clause, and the
    // island-defeq route (`kernel_prove_ensures_by_defeq`, the same
    // `elaborate_ensures` goal judged by the kernel's own `Eq.refl` check) is
    // the other. So this lane grades the CITED clauses only, discharges
    // NOTHING itself on a mixed surface, and reports the certified cited part
    // through `mixed_cited_part`; `discharge_uncited_island_ensures_in_walk`
    // then discharges the function only if EVERY uncited clause also
    // kernel-discharges. An uncited clause with no kernel route — an ordinary
    // solver clause — still declines the whole function, exactly as before.
    let total_ensures_clauses =
        bundle.contracts.iter().filter(|c| c.kind == TrustContractKind::Ensures).count();
    let mixed_surface = total_ensures_clauses != cited.len();
    if mixed_surface && std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some() {
        eprintln!(
            "TRUST_E9_DEBUG MIXED-SURFACE fn={} total={total_ensures_clauses} cited={}",
            func.def_path,
            cited.len()
        );
    }
    for &index in &postcondition_indices {
        // Eligible: solver-UNKNOWN, or a bare Proved LABEL (the native lane's
        // public label without a static proof authority — the report demotes it
        // back to UNKNOWN; the discharge supplies the missing kernel authority).
        // A Failed/Refuted or Timeout entry is NEVER overridden — a kernel
        // "proof" against a refuted VC means the elaborated goal diverged from
        // the VC and must stay fatal.
        if !matches!(
            results[index].1,
            VerificationResult::Unknown { .. } | VerificationResult::Proved { .. }
        ) {
            if std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some() {
                eprintln!(
                    "TRUST_E9_DEBUG BAIL=ineligible-entry fn={} index={index}",
                    func.def_path
                );
            }
            record_pending_import_citation(tcx, local, func, &cited, &[]);
            return Vec::new();
        }
    }
    // Once a crate has a deferred island suffix, EVERY cited-ensures body waits
    // for the authoritative post-walk session. Restricting this to
    // `TheoremNotFound` is unsound: an earlier theorem can certify here and a
    // later rejected deferred island can subsequently taint the same session.
    // Record even the no-VC/mismatch/ineligible cases above (with no discharge
    // indices) so their goals remain inventoried and their body outcome is
    // published only after the final session state is known.
    if record_pending_import_citation(tcx, local, func, &cited, &postcondition_indices) {
        return Vec::new();
    }
    let Some(mut env) = session_island_env(tcx.sess) else {
        if std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some() {
            eprintln!("TRUST_E9_DEBUG BAIL=env-stash-none fn={}", func.def_path);
        }
        return Vec::new();
    };
    // Facets: the session's COMPOSED table as accumulated so far. Bodies are
    // walked callee-first (definition order), so every already-walked function
    // — including the clause's callees — has its facets recorded; the
    // composition is PARTIAL mid-walk but sound (an un-walked function simply
    // has no record and fails closed). Mint every admissible, recognized
    // accumulated function into the per-body env CLONE so a call inside the
    // clause resolves definitionally (the E6 flagship), keyed under the
    // def-path and every spec spelling exactly like
    // `trust_mint_program_admissions`.
    let (accumulated, mut table) =
        tcx.sess.with_trust_compiler_state::<SessionFnFacets, _>(|state| {
            (state.accumulated.clone(), state.composed_table())
        });
    let mut candidates: Vec<&trust_types::VerifiableFunction> = accumulated.iter().collect();
    // The CURRENT body may not be in `accumulated` yet depending on intra-pass
    // ordering; always include self.
    if !accumulated.iter().any(|f| f.def_path == func.def_path) {
        candidates.push(func);
    }
    for candidate in candidates {
        let Some((path, admission)) =
            trust_certify::clean_island::admit_function_into(&mut env, candidate, &table)
        else {
            continue;
        };
        // Keyed under the def-path AND every spec spelling of it, exactly like
        // `trust_mint_program_admissions` and `record_fn_facets`. An
        // ambiguity-evicted spelling carries no facet record, so
        // `FacetTable::admitted` still fails closed for it.
        for spelling in trust_spec_elab::spec_spellings(&path) {
            if spelling != path {
                table.admit(spelling, admission.clone());
            }
        }
        table.admit(path, admission);
    }
    // Trust (ref-self I1): the SELF function's borrow-erased PARAMETER VIEW,
    // after the ordinary loop so it can never displace an ordinary admission.
    //
    // ONLY the self function's view is minted, and that is exact rather than a
    // scope cut: a view import carries `self_result_only`, so no clause may
    // ever CALL one — the single position it can occupy is the self-call
    // `elaborate_ensures` splices in for `result` in THIS function's own
    // `ensures`. Minting another function's view could therefore only add an
    // unnameable constant to the environment.
    trust_admit_param_view_into(tcx, local, func, &mut env, &mut table);
    // NOTE: no self-mint requirement here — `elaborate_ensures` enforces the
    // self-admission gate exactly when the clause mentions `result` (the
    // substitution target); a result-free clause is a ∀-params statement that
    // discharges on ANY body, admitted or not.
    // SELF IDENTITY (2026-08-26): the CANONICAL def-path, not the bare last
    // segment. The bare name re-enters the ambiguity keyspace
    // `record_fn_facets` deliberately evicts, so a contract on a function whose
    // bare name is shared with any other item could never bind `result`; the
    // def-path is the very string `record_fn_facets` keys the record by. See
    // `trust_spec_elab::self_fn_spelling` for the soundness argument.
    let self_ident = trust_spec_elab::self_fn_spelling(&func.def_path);
    // Trust: `usize`/`isize` rows resolved at the target's width, so they bind
    // at the carrier the import minted just above carries.
    let params_typed = trust_fn_discharge_binder_types(tcx, local);
    // `result` AND its FIELD rows. `trust_fn_param_type_names` emits
    // `result.<field>` entries so a clause on an aggregate return can be TYPED;
    // they are not function parameters. Filtering only the exact name `result`
    // left them in this list, and the self-call `elaborate_ensures` builds from
    // it then applied the function to its own output -- which is why the goal
    // treated `result.coeff` as a free binder and the cited theorem could never
    // match it.
    let params: Vec<(&str, &str)> = params_typed
        .iter()
        .filter(|(n, _)| n != "result" && !n.starts_with("result."))
        .map(|(n, t)| (n.as_str(), t.as_str()))
        .collect();
    // Trust (SOUNDNESS — the `p'`→`p` collapse below): only a provably-immutable
    // by-value binding may have its post-state prime collapsed to entry. A `mut`
    // by-value param can be reassigned in the body (`bad(mut x){ x = 0; x }`), so
    // its `p'` is NOT definitionally equal to entry `p`; collapsing it would let
    // reflexivity (`u64_ge_refl`) falsely discharge `x' >= x`.
    let collapsible = trust_fn_collapsible_by_value_params(tcx, local);
    // EVERY cited clause must Certified-grade (conjunction rule) — a single
    // miss discharges NOTHING for this function.
    use trust_certify::clean_island::CitationVerdict;
    let mut theorems = Vec::new();
    // Trust: E9 evidence — the exact clause text and CLAUSE-LOCAL facet table
    // each grade call received, index-aligned with `theorems`.
    let mut graded: Vec<GradedEnsuresClause> = Vec::new();
    // Trust (OR-A1, Inc 1a): the rung the conjunction rests on — the WEAKEST
    // requires-link over the cited clauses credited conditionally, `None` while
    // every clause certified unconditionally. Rows are not attributed to
    // clauses, so one conditional clause makes the whole discharge conditional.
    let mut function_link: Option<trust_certify::clean_island::RequiresLink> = None;
    // The `requires` candidates, derived once and only if some clause needs the
    // conditional rungs.
    let mut requires_candidates: Option<RequiresHypothesisCandidates> = None;
    for cited_clause in &cited {
        let authored_clause = match &cited_clause.clause {
            Ok(clause) => clause,
            Err(reason) => {
                record_refused_cited_goal_once(
                    tcx,
                    local,
                    cited_clause.contract_index,
                    &func.def_path,
                    "ensures-citation",
                    cited_clause.theorem.as_str(),
                    reason,
                );
                return Vec::new();
            }
        };
        let theorem = cited_clause.theorem;
        let mut clause_text = authored_clause.clone();
        // E2 primes (by-value slice): a primed post-state identifier `p'` for a
        // by-value parameter `p` denotes p's value in the post-state. A
        // provably-immutable (non-`mut`) by-value binding is NEVER reassigned
        // (Rust rejects reassigning it), so `p'` is DEFINITIONALLY EQUAL to `p`
        // — rewrite it to `p` before elaboration. A `mut` by-value param may be
        // reassigned, so it is EXCLUDED from `collapsible` and its `p'` is left
        // intact to fail closed in elaboration (unbound `p'`), never falsely
        // discharged. A prime on ANY OTHER identifier (a `&mut` deref post-state,
        // an unknown name) is likewise left untouched and fails closed downstream
        // — the two-state `&mut` semantics are the separate deep lane.
        for (pname, _) in &params {
            if collapsible.contains(*pname) {
                clause_text = rewrite_by_value_prime(&clause_text, pname);
            }
        }
        // Trust: a CITED clause may name a Clean island definition
        // (`ensures result == spec(x) by impl_correct`), exactly as an uncited
        // one may. Admit the definitions this clause names into a CLAUSE-LOCAL
        // table before elaborating its goal; the cited theorem must still
        // prove that goal under the full certification audit, so the admission
        // decides only which kernel constant `spec` denotes. See
        // `admit_clause_island_definitions` for the name-resolution refusals.
        let mut clause_table = table.clone();
        admit_clause_island_definitions(
            tcx,
            local,
            &env,
            &func.def_path,
            &clause_text,
            &mut clause_table,
        );
        // Trust (nat-lift): a user `ensures` clause is a PROOF position.
        let lift_inputs = trust_proof_clause_inputs(&params_typed);
        let verdict = trust_certify::clean_island::kernel_discharge_ensures_citation_in(
            &env,
            theorem.as_str(),
            &clause_text,
            &params,
            &self_ident,
            &clause_table,
            &lift_inputs,
        );
        // Trust (OR-A1, Inc 1a): the unconditional goal did not certify — climb
        // the CONDITIONAL rungs (`G1★`, then `G1`) in THIS lane's own
        // environment, facet table and clause mode, and let the one shared rule
        // (`trust_grade_conditional_citation`) decide the credit.
        let certified_unconditionally =
            matches!(verdict, CitationVerdict::KernelStatementMatchCertified);
        let conditional = if certified_unconditionally {
            None
        } else {
            let candidates = requires_candidates.get_or_insert_with(|| {
                // The UNRESOLVED signature table, as the sweep passes it: the
                // query-owned cross-check compares Rust spellings (`usize`).
                cited_requires_hypothesis_inputs(
                    tcx,
                    bundle,
                    &trust_fn_param_type_names(tcx, local),
                    &collapsible,
                )
            });
            trust_grade_conditional_citation(tcx, local, candidates, |hyps| {
                trust_certify::clean_island::kernel_discharge_ensures_citation_conditional_in(
                    &env,
                    theorem.as_str(),
                    &clause_text,
                    &params,
                    &self_ident,
                    &clause_table,
                    hyps,
                    &lift_inputs,
                )
            })
        };
        // The credit also requires that the goal it rests on can be DISCLOSED
        // (the spec-clean row prints the conditional goal): no row, no credit.
        let goal_row = conditional.as_ref().and_then(|grade| {
            grade.credited.and_then(|link| trust_conditional_goal_row(&grade.ladder, link))
        });
        let credited = if goal_row.is_some() {
            conditional.as_ref().and_then(|grade| grade.credited)
        } else {
            None
        };
        // The row stores the exact grade that controls this ordinary in-walk
        // discharge — the conditional one when that is what is credited. The
        // post-walk diagnostic sweep fills only identities not reached here, so
        // a later, richer facet table cannot rewrite history.
        let recorded_verdict = match (&conditional, credited) {
            (Some(grade), Some(_)) => grade.ladder.verdict.clone(),
            _ => verdict.clone(),
        };
        record_graded_cited_goal_once(
            tcx,
            local,
            cited_clause.contract_index,
            &func.def_path,
            "ensures-citation",
            &clause_text,
            &params,
            &self_ident,
            &clause_table,
            theorem.as_str(),
            &recorded_verdict,
            &lift_inputs,
            goal_row.as_ref(),
        );
        if !certified_unconditionally && credited.is_none() {
            if std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some() {
                eprintln!(
                    "TRUST_E9_DEBUG NOT-CERTIFIED fn={} clause={clause_text:?} thm={theorem} \
                     verdict={verdict:?} conditional={:?}",
                    func.def_path,
                    conditional.as_ref().map(|grade| (grade.ladder.rung, &grade.withheld)),
                );
            }
            return Vec::new();
        }
        if let Some(link) = credited {
            function_link = Some(function_link.map_or(link, |previous| previous.weakest(link)));
            if std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some() {
                eprintln!(
                    "TRUST_E9_DEBUG CONDITIONAL-CREDIT fn={} clause={clause_text:?} thm={theorem} \
                     requires-link={}",
                    func.def_path,
                    link.as_str()
                );
            }
        }
        theorems.push(theorem.to_string());
        graded.push(GradedEnsuresClause {
            text: clause_text,
            facets: clause_table,
            route: GradedEnsuresRoute::CitedTheorem(theorem.to_string()),
        });
    }
    *graded_cited = graded;
    let theorem_list = theorems.join("+");
    if mixed_surface {
        // Trust: the cited part is kernel-proven; the uncited part is not this
        // lane's to judge. Discharge nothing here (see the conjunction rule
        // above) and hand the certified theorem list to the island-defeq lane.
        if std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some() {
            eprintln!(
                "TRUST_E9_DEBUG MIXED-CITED-PART-CERTIFIED fn={} theorems={theorem_list} \
                 requires-link={:?}",
                func.def_path,
                function_link.map(|link| link.as_str())
            );
        }
        *mixed_cited_part = Some((theorem_list, function_link));
        return Vec::new();
    }
    // Trust: E9 evidence — an ALL-CITED surface certified, so the cited clauses
    // ARE the whole conjunction the rows below rest on. Mint the payload from
    // THE SAME environment, clause texts, clause-local facet tables, binder
    // types and self spelling the grade calls above used. A miss changes no
    // verdict: the rows then ship without a payload and publication withholds
    // them, as it did before this lane existed.
    //
    // Trust (OR-A1): NOT for a conditional discharge. The certificate producer
    // re-judges the UNCONDITIONAL goal, which a conditional theorem does not
    // prove, and the conditional authority hands out no evidence anyway
    // (`kernel_evidence_for`); minting over `G1★` is Inc 2.
    *certificate = if function_link.is_some() {
        None
    } else {
        mint_ensures_discharge_certificate(
            &env,
            graded_cited,
            total_ensures_clauses,
            &params,
            &self_ident,
            &func.def_path,
            "citation",
            &trust_proof_clause_inputs(&params_typed),
        )
    };
    // Trust (ref-self I1): a row whose goal rests on the borrow-erased
    // PARAMETER VIEW carries its own solver label and a strength BELOW
    // `Certified` — owner ruling R1. The label is distinct rather than a suffix
    // so no consumer counting `(clean-kernel-citation` picks it up by accident.
    // Trust (OR-A1 H1): so does a CONDITIONAL discharge, decided from the rung
    // carried as data by the one label helper the authority constructor mirrors.
    let view = trust_e6_view_marker(tcx, local);
    let (solver, strength) = trust_ensures_discharge_label(view, function_link, false);
    let mut discharged = Vec::new();
    for &index in &postcondition_indices {
        // Trust (OR-A1 H2): a conditional credit never replaces an existing
        // static proof — above all never a `KernelCertified` one.
        if function_link.is_some() && static_proved_rows.contains(&index) {
            if std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some() {
                eprintln!(
                    "TRUST_E9_DEBUG CONDITIONAL-KEEPS-EXISTING-PROOF fn={} index={index}",
                    func.def_path
                );
            }
            continue;
        }
        results[index].1 = VerificationResult::Proved {
            solver: trust_types::Symbol::intern(solver),
            time_ms: 0,
            strength: strength.clone(),
            proof_certificate: None,
            solver_warnings: None,
            native_proof_envelope: None,
        };
        discharged.push((index, theorem_list.clone(), function_link));
    }
    if std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some() {
        eprintln!(
            "TRUST_E9_DEBUG DISCHARGED fn={} indices={:?} view={view:?} requires-link={:?}",
            func.def_path,
            discharged.iter().map(|(index, ..)| *index).collect::<Vec<_>>(),
            function_link.map(|link| link.as_str())
        );
    }
    discharged
}

/// Record a body whose ensures-citation must be adjudicated after the walk.
/// See [`SessionPendingImportCitations`] for why post-walk is the only sound
/// placement.
///
/// No-op unless the crate actually carries a deferred island suffix. Recording
/// is idempotent per body — the discharge lane can run more than once for one
/// body during E4→E5 feedback. Its per-pass row indices are only provisional;
/// [`quarantine_pending_import_citation_body`] recomputes them from the finally
/// selected carrier before attaching the artifacts.
fn record_pending_import_citation(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'_>,
    local: LocalDefId,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    cited: &[PendingImportCitationClause],
    postcondition_indices: &[usize],
) -> bool {
    // Trust: the UNCITED `ensures` clauses of a mixed surface ride with the
    // record so the post-walk conjunction covers them too. Read from the same
    // dense contract lane `cited` was built from; empty for an all-cited body.
    let uncited: Vec<(u64, rustc_span::Span)> = tcx
        .trust_contracts(local.to_def_id())
        .contracts
        .iter()
        .enumerate()
        .filter(|(_, contract)| {
            contract.kind == TrustContractKind::Ensures && contract.citation.is_none()
        })
        .map(|(contract_index, contract)| {
            (u64::try_from(contract_index).expect("a dense contract index fits u64"), contract.span)
        })
        .collect();
    let record = PendingImportCitation {
        func_path: trust_mir_extract::safe_def_path_str(tcx, local.to_def_id()),
        func: func.clone(),
        cited: cited.to_vec(),
        uncited,
        postcondition_indices: postcondition_indices.to_vec(),
        artifacts: None,
    };
    tcx.sess.with_trust_compiler_state::<SessionPendingImportCitations, _>(|state| {
        if !state.crate_has_deferred_islands {
            return false;
        }
        if !state.bodies.contains_key(&local) {
            state.bodies.insert(local, record);
        }
        true
    })
}

/// The post-walk E9 install into a row's slot, which may hold a provisional
/// authority from the in-walk pass: the minted `authority`, or NOTHING (the
/// slot is cleared) when none was minted — the KR0 switch's reference entry
/// refused the surface's judgment — or the TINV policy refuses it. Returns
/// whether an authority was installed.
fn install_post_walk_e9_authority(
    slot: &mut Option<ResultProofAuthority>,
    authority: Option<ResultProofAuthority>,
    premised: bool,
    vc: &VerificationCondition,
) -> bool {
    *slot = authority.filter(|authority| !tinv_refuses_post_cap_authority(premised, vc, authority));
    slot.is_some()
}

/// Trust (two-language design item 10, phase 2): adjudicate every quarantined
/// ensures-citation against the COMPLETE crate environment, then publish each
/// affected body's single verification outcome.
///
/// Called from `trust_ensure_whole_crate_verification` after
/// `trust_mint_program_admissions` and after the deferred island suffix has been
/// kernel-checked in authored order — i.e. exactly where the AUTHORITATIVE
/// whole-crate check runs. That placement is the whole design: it supplies
/// context, environment, facet, admission and inventory parity BY CONSTRUCTION,
/// which is what two in-walk attempts could not do at any level of care (the walk
/// holds a partial admission inventory; see [`SessionPendingImportCitations`]).
///
/// The criterion is the in-walk criterion, unchanged and unweakened:
/// `kernel_discharge_ensures_citation` must return
/// `KernelStatementMatchCertified` for EVERY ensures clause of the function (the
/// conjunction rule), and no row a solver refuted or timed out is ever touched.
/// A citation that still does not discharge leaves the body's rows exactly as the
/// walk computed them, so the honest unproved verdict and its strict abort are
/// published here rather than lost.
#[allow(rustc::potential_query_instability)]
fn finalize_pending_import_citations(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'_>,
    clean_session: &trust_certify::clean_island::CleanIslandSession,
) {
    let mut pending: Vec<(LocalDefId, PendingImportCitation)> =
        tcx.sess.with_trust_compiler_state::<SessionPendingImportCitations, _>(|state| {
            state.bodies.drain().collect()
        });
    if pending.is_empty() {
        return;
    }
    // Deterministic diagnostic order, independent of hash iteration order.
    pending.sort_by_key(|(local, _)| {
        let span = tcx.def_span(*local);
        (span.lo(), span.hi())
    });
    let policy = TrustVerifyPolicy::from_session(tcx.sess);
    let table =
        tcx.sess.with_trust_compiler_state::<SessionFnFacets, _>(|state| state.composed_table());
    for (local, mut record) in pending {
        // Grade and publish the complete cited-clause population before looking
        // at provisional verification artifacts. Spec-clean rows disclose the
        // citation obligations themselves, not whether this body happened to
        // reach the ordinary outcome-publication seam. A tainted deferred suffix
        // therefore still emits one explicit `session-tainted` row per clause,
        // and an internal eligibility refusal cannot silently shrink the ledger.
        // Trust: E9 evidence — minted by the grading pass from the COMPLETE
        // (untainted) session environment, only when every clause certified.
        let mut ensures_certificate = None;
        let certified_theorems = grade_pending_import_citation_goals(
            tcx,
            local,
            &record,
            clean_session,
            &table,
            &mut ensures_certificate,
        );
        // Trust (T3 L4, C07r; L4-E9-1): the amendment takes the provisional artifacts out of
        // the record; their hold, and with it the body's VC context, ends when they drop
        // once the amended artifacts are published, below. The clone shares that context.
        let Some(provisional) = record.artifacts.take() else {
            continue;
        };
        // Trust (T3 L4, C09): the amendment compares the keys the body's collection sealed, so
        // their identity oracle's context is installed again for it, and what it compared is
        // written as a second `PARITY <fn> identity` row when the amendment ends.
        let _row_keys = row_key::Installed::resume(tcx, local.to_def_id(), &provisional.ctx);
        let mut artifacts = (*provisional).clone();
        // Trust (OR-A1 H1): the theorem list travels WITH its rung.
        let requires_link = certified_theorems.as_ref().and_then(|(_, link)| *link);
        let discharged =
            certified_theorems.as_ref().map_or_else(Vec::new, |(theorem_list, link)| {
                apply_pending_import_citation_discharge(
                    tcx,
                    local,
                    &record,
                    theorem_list,
                    *link,
                    &mut artifacts,
                )
            });
        if !discharged.is_empty() {
            // Rebuild exactly the derived views the in-walk pipeline builds after
            // its own citation lane, in the same order, from the same inputs.
            for (index, theorem) in &discharged {
                if let Some(row) = exact_result_row_identity(*index, &artifacts.results[*index].0) {
                    // Trust (ref-self I1): same disclosure as the in-walk
                    // twin; a deferred discharge must not grade a view-borne
                    // row any higher.
                    // Trust (OR-A1 H1): nor a conditional one.
                    let authority = trust_ensures_discharge_authority(
                        trust_e6_view_marker(tcx, local),
                        requires_link,
                        row,
                        theorem.clone(),
                        // Trust: bind the surface certificate to THIS row.
                        ensures_certificate.as_ref().and_then(|certificate| {
                            certificate.evidence_for_row(&artifacts.results[*index].0)
                        }),
                    );
                    // KR0 switch: `None` when the reference entry did not accept
                    // the surface's judgment, so no Certified authority.
                    let minted = authority.is_some();
                    // Trust (TINV step 10.4, review fold-in 3): the cap pass already
                    // ran; apply its E9 policy here, so both lanes agree.
                    let premised = type_invariant::body_carries_premise(tcx, local.to_def_id());
                    let installed = install_post_walk_e9_authority(
                        &mut artifacts.proof_authorities[*index],
                        authority,
                        premised,
                        &artifacts.results[*index].0,
                    );
                    if std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some() {
                        if !minted {
                            eprintln!("TRUST_E9_DEBUG NO-REFERENCE-CERTIFICATE index={index}");
                        } else if installed {
                            eprintln!("TRUST_E9_DEBUG AUTHORITY-INSTALLED-POST-WALK index={index}");
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            // (The whole-function panic-freedom subsumption used to re-run here,
            // after the citation authority existed, so a deferred-theorem body
            // earned the same credit as one citing an ordinary island. That lane
            // was removed as a false proof; nothing replays here now, and the
            // post-walk verdict is exactly what the in-walk pipeline reached.)
            let cleancic = certify_all(&artifacts.results, None);
            artifacts.proof_results = build_proof_results(
                tcx.sess,
                &artifacts.results,
                &[],
                &artifacts.result_bindings,
                &artifacts.proof_authorities,
                Some(&record.func),
            );
            artifacts.telemetry = build_proof_telemetry(
                tcx.sess,
                &artifacts.results,
                &artifacts.result_bindings,
                &artifacts.proof_authorities,
            );
            artifacts.transport_results = build_transport_results_bound(
                tcx.sess,
                &artifacts.results,
                artifacts.full_verification.as_ref(),
                &cleancic,
                &artifacts.result_bindings,
                &artifacts.proof_authorities,
            );
        }
        // `optimized_mir` is already computed for every eligible body by the eager
        // walk, so this is a cache read, not a second verification.
        //
        // The body read here differs from the one `run_pass` publishes against in
        // exactly one way: `inject_certified_test_monitors` has already run on it.
        // Both body-derived predicates inside `publish_body_verification_outcome`
        // are insensitive to that — `body_has_coroutine_protocol_assert` looks for
        // `ResumedAfter*` assert terminators, and `function_uses_unsafe` reads the
        // SOURCE def plus inlined source scopes, neither of which monitor
        // injection creates. Trust (D9): the third, the `termination-unmeasured`
        // self-call census (`termination_unmeasured::termination_unmeasured_charge`),
        // counts only calls resolving to the body's OWN `DefId`, and every injected
        // call targets the `trust_certified_monitor_check` diagnostic item. A change
        // that makes any of the three depend on injected calls must capture them
        // in-walk instead.
        let body = tcx.optimized_mir(local.to_def_id());
        publish_body_verification_outcome(
            tcx,
            body,
            &record.func_path,
            &Arc::new(artifacts),
            &policy,
        );
        // The hold ends here (`CitationHold`'s drop).
        drop(provisional);
    }
}

/// Grade and publish every deferred citation against the complete environment.
/// Returns the conjunction's theorem-list spelling only when every clause
/// Certified-grades. Row emission is intentionally independent of provisional
/// body artifacts: a tainted session or later discharge-eligibility refusal is
/// evidence to disclose, never a reason to omit the obligation.
fn grade_pending_import_citation_goals(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'_>,
    local: LocalDefId,
    record: &PendingImportCitation,
    clean_session: &trust_certify::clean_island::CleanIslandSession,
    table: &trust_spec_elab::FacetTable,
    // Trust: E9 evidence out-slot, written only when EVERY clause certified.
    certificate: &mut Option<trust_certify::clean_island::EnsuresDischargeCertificate>,
    // Trust (OR-A1 H1): the theorem list is returned WITH its rung — the
    // weakest requires-link over the cited clauses credited conditionally, or
    // `None` when every one certified unconditionally.
) -> Option<(String, Option<trust_certify::clean_island::RequiresLink>)> {
    use trust_certify::clean_island::CitationVerdict;
    if record.cited.is_empty() {
        return None;
    }
    // SELF IDENTITY (2026-08-26): the CANONICAL def-path spelling, not the
    // bare last segment. The bare name re-enters the ambiguity keyspace
    // `record_fn_facets` deliberately evicts, so a contract on a function
    // whose bare name is shared with any other item (`veripb_core_proved::
    // cap_coeff` beside the `CoeffDomain::cap_coeff` method and its impls)
    // could never bind `result`; `record.func.def_path` is the very string
    // `record_fn_facets` keys the record by. See
    // `trust_spec_elab::self_fn_spelling` for the soundness argument. Both
    // uses below feed `elaborate_ensures`-shaped lookups (the discharge and
    // the graded row), so both take the key, keeping the recorded grade
    // consistent with the verdict it records.
    let self_key = trust_spec_elab::self_fn_spelling(&record.func.def_path);
    // Trust: the SAME target-resolved binder table the in-walk discharge uses —
    // a deferred citation must not read `usize` differently from an ordinary one.
    let params_typed = trust_fn_discharge_binder_types(tcx, local);
    let params: Vec<(&str, &str)> = params_typed
        .iter()
        .filter(|(n, _)| n != "result")
        .map(|(n, t)| (n.as_str(), t.as_str()))
        .collect();
    let collapsible = trust_fn_collapsible_by_value_params(tcx, local);
    let mut theorems = Vec::new();
    // Trust: E9 evidence — the inputs each grade call received, in clause order
    // (the cited clauses first, then this lane's defeq clauses), so the
    // certificate minted after BOTH loops covers the whole conjunction.
    let mut graded: Vec<GradedEnsuresClause> = Vec::new();
    let mut all_certified = true;
    // Trust (OR-A1, Inc 1a): see the in-walk twin — the weakest link over the
    // clauses credited conditionally, and the lazily derived candidates.
    let mut function_link: Option<trust_certify::clean_island::RequiresLink> = None;
    let mut requires_candidates: Option<RequiresHypothesisCandidates> = None;
    let bundle = tcx.trust_contracts(local.to_def_id());
    for cited_clause in &record.cited {
        let authored_clause = match &cited_clause.clause {
            Ok(clause) => clause,
            Err(reason) => {
                all_certified = false;
                record_refused_cited_goal_once(
                    tcx,
                    local,
                    cited_clause.contract_index,
                    &record.func.def_path,
                    "ensures-citation-deferred",
                    cited_clause.theorem.as_str(),
                    reason,
                );
                if std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some() {
                    eprintln!(
                        "TRUST_E9_DEBUG POST-WALK-PREPARATION-REFUSED fn={} thm={} reason={reason}",
                        record.func.def_path, cited_clause.theorem
                    );
                }
                continue;
            }
        };
        let theorem = cited_clause.theorem;
        let mut clause_text = authored_clause.clone();
        for (pname, _) in &params {
            if collapsible.contains(*pname) {
                clause_text = rewrite_by_value_prime(&clause_text, pname);
            }
        }
        // Trust: a cited clause may name a Clean island definition; admit the
        // ones this clause names into a CLAUSE-LOCAL table, exactly as the
        // in-walk lane does (`admit_clause_island_definitions`). The
        // environment is the session's own and only while it is untainted:
        // `environment()` is `None` after any rejection, so a tainted session
        // admits nothing here and the grading call below still answers
        // `SessionTainted`.
        let mut clause_table = table.clone();
        if let Some(env) = clean_session.environment() {
            admit_clause_island_definitions(
                tcx,
                local,
                env,
                &record.func.def_path,
                &clause_text,
                &mut clause_table,
            );
        }
        // Keep the session's taint gate on the only grading path. Consulting a
        // cloned partial environment here would let a rejected deferred suffix
        // mint positive evidence; the session method instead returns the
        // explicit `SessionTainted` verdict that this ledger must disclose.
        // Trust (nat-lift): a user `ensures` clause is a PROOF position.
        let lift_inputs = trust_proof_clause_inputs(&params_typed);
        let verdict = clean_session.check_ensures_discharge_in(
            theorem.as_str(),
            &clause_text,
            &params,
            &self_key,
            &clause_table,
            &lift_inputs,
        );
        // Trust (OR-A1, Inc 1a): the conditional rungs, through the SESSION's
        // taint gate (a tainted session answers `SessionTainted` on every rung),
        // decided by the one rule the in-walk lane and the sweep share.
        let certified_unconditionally =
            matches!(verdict, CitationVerdict::KernelStatementMatchCertified);
        let conditional = if certified_unconditionally {
            None
        } else {
            let candidates = requires_candidates.get_or_insert_with(|| {
                cited_requires_hypothesis_inputs(
                    tcx,
                    bundle,
                    &trust_fn_param_type_names(tcx, local),
                    &collapsible,
                )
            });
            trust_grade_conditional_citation(tcx, local, candidates, |hyps| {
                clean_session.check_ensures_discharge_conditional_in(
                    theorem.as_str(),
                    &clause_text,
                    &params,
                    &self_key,
                    &clause_table,
                    hyps,
                    &lift_inputs,
                )
            })
        };
        let goal_row = conditional.as_ref().and_then(|grade| {
            grade.credited.and_then(|link| trust_conditional_goal_row(&grade.ladder, link))
        });
        let credited = if goal_row.is_some() {
            conditional.as_ref().and_then(|grade| grade.credited)
        } else {
            None
        };
        let recorded_verdict = match (&conditional, credited) {
            (Some(grade), Some(_)) => grade.ladder.verdict.clone(),
            _ => verdict.clone(),
        };
        // Trust (v6/D1): same goal row, deferred-island adjudication lane.
        record_graded_cited_goal_once(
            tcx,
            local,
            cited_clause.contract_index,
            &record.func.def_path,
            "ensures-citation-deferred",
            &clause_text,
            &params,
            &self_key,
            &clause_table,
            theorem.as_str(),
            &recorded_verdict,
            &lift_inputs,
            goal_row.as_ref(),
        );
        if !certified_unconditionally && credited.is_none() {
            all_certified = false;
            if std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some() {
                eprintln!(
                    "TRUST_E9_DEBUG POST-WALK-NOT-CERTIFIED fn={} clause={clause_text:?} \
                     thm={theorem} verdict={verdict:?}",
                    record.func.def_path
                );
            }
        } else {
            if let Some(link) = credited {
                function_link = Some(function_link.map_or(link, |previous| previous.weakest(link)));
            }
            theorems.push(theorem.to_string());
            graded.push(GradedEnsuresClause {
                text: clause_text,
                facets: clause_table,
                route: GradedEnsuresRoute::CitedTheorem(theorem.to_string()),
            });
        }
    }
    // Trust: MIXED surface — the conjunction rule covers the UNCITED clauses
    // too, by the one criterion the in-walk island-defeq lane uses
    // (`grade_uncited_island_ensures_clause`): each must name an admissible
    // Clean island definition and kernel-discharge by definitional equality.
    // Mirrors the in-walk order: the uncited clauses are judged only once
    // every cited clause has Certified-graded, the first miss stops the
    // function, and a miss is reported exactly as it is in-walk. A tainted
    // session has no environment, so nothing is judged and nothing certifies.
    let mut defeq_clauses: Vec<(rustc_span::Span, String)> = Vec::new();
    // Trust: E9 evidence — the two halves of this lane resolve their binder
    // tables through two different filters (a `result.<field>` row survives the
    // cited one), so a surface whose halves were NOT graded against the same
    // table mints nothing rather than certify a goal no grade call ever saw.
    let mut binder_tables_agree = true;
    if all_certified && !record.uncited.is_empty() {
        let uncited_params: Vec<(&str, &str)> = params_typed
            .iter()
            .filter(|(n, _)| n != "result" && !n.starts_with("result."))
            .map(|(n, t)| (n.as_str(), t.as_str()))
            .collect();
        binder_tables_agree = uncited_params == params;
        let Some(env) = clean_session.environment() else {
            return None;
        };
        for &(contract_index, clause_span) in &record.uncited {
            let grade = grade_uncited_island_ensures_clause(
                tcx,
                local,
                &record.func.def_path,
                env,
                table,
                &uncited_params,
                &collapsible,
                &self_key,
                contract_index,
                clause_span,
                // Trust (nat-lift): the SAME resolved binder table the clause
                // elaborates with, so the `result` spelling the lift's
                // self-function cross-check reads is the one the goal binds.
                &trust_proof_clause_inputs(&params_typed),
            );
            match grade {
                UncitedIslandClauseGrade::SourceUnavailable => return None,
                UncitedIslandClauseGrade::NoIslandDefinition { clause_text } => {
                    let clause_row_unknown = record.artifacts.as_ref().is_some_and(|artifacts| {
                        artifacts.results.iter().any(|(vc, result)| {
                            is_ensures_clause_obligation(vc)
                                && matches!(result, VerificationResult::Unknown { .. })
                        })
                    });
                    decline_uncited_clause_without_island_definition(
                        tcx,
                        &record.func.def_path,
                        clause_span,
                        &clause_text,
                        !defeq_clauses.is_empty(),
                        clause_row_unknown,
                    );
                    return None;
                }
                UncitedIslandClauseGrade::Graded {
                    island_name,
                    verdict,
                    clause_text,
                    clause_table,
                } => {
                    if !matches!(verdict, CitationVerdict::KernelStatementMatchCertified) {
                        emit_uncited_island_defeq_refusal(
                            tcx,
                            local,
                            clause_span,
                            &island_name,
                            &verdict,
                            &clause_text,
                            &record.func.def_path,
                            &self_key,
                            table,
                        );
                        return None;
                    }
                    theorems.push(format!("defeq:{island_name}"));
                    graded.push(GradedEnsuresClause {
                        text: clause_text,
                        facets: clause_table,
                        route: GradedEnsuresRoute::IslandDefeq(island_name.clone()),
                    });
                    defeq_clauses.push((clause_span, island_name));
                }
            }
        }
    }
    if all_certified {
        for (clause_span, island_name) in &defeq_clauses {
            emit_island_defeq_discharge_note(tcx, *clause_span, island_name, &record.func.def_path);
        }
        // Trust: E9 evidence — the whole deferred surface certified; mint from
        // the session's COMPLETE (untainted) environment, which is `None` while
        // tainted, so a partial environment can mint no payload either.
        // Trust (OR-A1): never for a conditional surface (see the in-walk twin).
        if let Some(env) = clean_session.environment()
            && binder_tables_agree
            && function_link.is_none()
        {
            *certificate = mint_ensures_discharge_certificate(
                env,
                &graded,
                record.cited.len() + record.uncited.len(),
                &params,
                &self_key,
                &record.func.def_path,
                "citation-deferred",
                &trust_proof_clause_inputs(&params_typed),
            );
        }
    }
    all_certified.then(|| (theorems.join("+"), function_link))
}

/// Apply a successfully graded deferred conjunction to eligible provisional
/// rows. All goal rows were already published by
/// [`grade_pending_import_citation_goals`]; every malformed or ineligible
/// artifact shape therefore refuses discharge without dropping disclosure.
fn apply_pending_import_citation_discharge(
    // Trust (ref-self I1): `tcx`/`local` are here only so this lane can read
    // the same frozen-shared-parameter table the in-walk one does; a deferred
    // discharge must not grade a view-borne row differently from an ordinary
    // one.
    tcx: TyCtxt<'_>,
    local: LocalDefId,
    record: &PendingImportCitation,
    theorem_list: &str,
    // Trust (OR-A1 H1): the rung the grading pass credited, `None` when every
    // cited clause certified unconditionally.
    requires_link: Option<trust_certify::clean_island::RequiresLink>,
    artifacts: &mut VerificationArtifacts,
) -> Vec<(usize, String)> {
    if record.postcondition_indices.is_empty() {
        return Vec::new();
    }
    for &index in &record.postcondition_indices {
        // The provisional artifacts are the walk's, so the row set is the same
        // one the indices were computed against; re-check the bound and the
        // eligibility anyway. A Failed/Refuted or Timeout entry is NEVER
        // overridden — a kernel proof against a refuted VC means the elaborated
        // goal diverged from the VC and must stay fatal.
        let Some((_, result)) = artifacts.results.get(index) else {
            return Vec::new();
        };
        if !matches!(result, VerificationResult::Unknown { .. } | VerificationResult::Proved { .. })
        {
            return Vec::new();
        }
    }
    if artifacts.results.len() != artifacts.proof_authorities.len() {
        return Vec::new();
    }
    // Trust (ref-self I1): same rule as the in-walk lane — a view-borne goal
    // takes the distinct label and the below-`Certified` strength.
    // Trust (OR-A1 H1): and so does a conditional one, from the carried rung.
    let (solver, strength) =
        trust_ensures_discharge_label(trust_e6_view_marker(tcx, local), requires_link, false);
    // Trust (OR-A1 H2): the rows the walk already proved keep their proof.
    let static_proved_rows = trust_rows_with_static_proof(
        &artifacts.results,
        &artifacts.result_bindings,
        &artifacts.proof_authorities,
    );
    let mut discharged = Vec::new();
    for &index in &record.postcondition_indices {
        if requires_link.is_some() && static_proved_rows.contains(&index) {
            continue;
        }
        let proved = VerificationResult::Proved {
            solver: trust_types::Symbol::intern(solver),
            time_ms: 0,
            strength: strength.clone(),
            proof_certificate: None,
            solver_warnings: None,
            native_proof_envelope: None,
        };
        // Trust (T3 L4, C07r): the verdict changes; the row's VC, and so its id, stays.
        // Every index was bounds-checked against these results above (the loop that
        // returns no discharge for a missing row), so the row is always there.
        artifacts.results.set_result(index, proved);
        discharged.push((index, theorem_list.to_string()));
    }
    if std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some() {
        // Trust (OR-A1 review, round 2): the rows this lane DISCHARGED, as its
        // in-walk and island-defeq twins print — not every candidate index. A
        // conditional surface skips the rows another lane already proved (H2),
        // so the candidate list overstated what was installed.
        eprintln!(
            "TRUST_E9_DEBUG POST-WALK-DISCHARGED fn={} indices={:?} requires-link={:?}",
            record.func.def_path,
            discharged.iter().map(|(index, _)| *index).collect::<Vec<_>>(),
            requires_link.map(|link| link.as_str())
        );
    }
    discharged
}

/// Attach this body's provisional artifacts to its pending-citation record and
/// report whether publication must be withheld. Returns `false` for every
/// ordinary body, so the non-deferred path is byte-identical to before.
fn quarantine_pending_import_citation_body(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'_>,
    body: &Body<'_>,
    artifacts: &Arc<VerificationArtifacts>,
) -> bool {
    if body.source.promoted.is_some() {
        return false;
    }
    let Some(local) = body.source.def_id().as_local() else { return false };
    // Full/native E4→E5 feedback may rebuild the result carrier, changing row
    // positions even though the authored citations are unchanged. Bind the
    // discharge indices HERE, after the caller has selected first or second
    // artifacts. Reusing whichever pass happened to record first could apply a
    // kernel proof to a different second-pass row. Every mismatch or ineligible
    // selected row clears the list and therefore fails closed.
    let bundle = tcx.trust_contracts(local.to_def_id());
    let total_ensures = bundle
        .contracts
        .iter()
        .filter(|contract| contract.kind == TrustContractKind::Ensures)
        .count();
    let cited_ensures = bundle
        .contracts
        .iter()
        .filter(|contract| {
            contract.kind == TrustContractKind::Ensures && contract.citation.is_some()
        })
        .count();
    let mut selected_indices: Vec<usize> = artifacts
        .results
        .iter()
        .enumerate()
        .filter(|(_, (vc, _))| is_ensures_clause_obligation(vc))
        .map(|(index, _)| index)
        .collect();
    // Trust: a MIXED surface (`cited_ensures < total_ensures`) keeps its row
    // indices too. That grants nothing: the indices are consumed only if
    // `grade_pending_import_citation_goals` certifies EVERY ensures clause, and
    // it judges the uncited ones by island defeq or certifies nothing. The
    // record must account for exactly the uncited clauses the bundle has
    // (checked below), so no clause can fall between the two lists.
    let selected_is_eligible = cited_ensures > 0
        && !selected_indices.is_empty()
        && selected_indices.iter().all(|&index| {
            matches!(
                artifacts.results[index].1,
                VerificationResult::Unknown { .. } | VerificationResult::Proved { .. }
            )
        });
    if !selected_is_eligible {
        selected_indices.clear();
    }
    tcx.sess.with_trust_compiler_state::<SessionPendingImportCitations, _>(|state| {
        match state.bodies.get_mut(&local) {
            Some(pending) => {
                if pending.cited.len() != cited_ensures
                    || pending.cited.len() + pending.uncited.len() != total_ensures
                {
                    selected_indices.clear();
                }
                pending.postcondition_indices = selected_indices;
                // Trust (T3 L4, C07r; L4-E9-1): the record holds the body's provisional
                // artifacts, and so its VC context, until its post-walk amendment.
                state.citation_holds.attach(&mut pending.artifacts, artifacts);
                true
            }
            None => false,
        }
    })
}

/// R4 §1 typed-citation discharge (the composed lane; design note
/// 2026-07-22-r4-remaining-lanes-design.md §1a, discharge core
/// `kernel_prove_ensures_by_defeq`): UNCITED ensures clauses whose predicate
/// CITES an island DEFINITION — a call (`c42(0)`) or the bare name of a
/// zero-arity def (`result == forty_two_isl`, e6 probe f04) — discharge by
/// kernel definitional equality: E6 admission supplies the self constant,
/// the island environment supplies the cited def, and the kernel judges the
/// constructed `Eq.refl` term against the elaborated goal. Fail-closed
/// mirrors of every cited-lane gate apply: ALL uncited ensures clauses must
/// be island-citing (a non-island surface discharges nothing; Trust: a MIXED
/// surface is judged only under the cited lane's `cited_part` handoff, i.e.
/// after every cited clause Certified-graded), every clause obligation
/// must be solver-UNKNOWN or bare-Proved, and a single defeq failure —
/// including the wrap-vs-Int divergence class, which is structurally not
/// defeq — discharges NOTHING (`e9_island_call_divergence_battery` stays
/// failed by construction).
fn discharge_uncited_island_ensures_in_walk<'tcx>(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>,
    body: &Body<'tcx>,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    results: &mut [(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)],
    // Trust: `Some(theorems)` on a MIXED surface whose every CITED clause the
    // cited lane just Certified-graded (`discharge_ensures_citations_in_walk`'s
    // `mixed_cited_part`). This lane then judges the UNCITED clauses only, and
    // a full success means every ensures clause of the function is
    // kernel-proven by one of the two routes — the conjunction rule's premise.
    // `None` is the all-uncited surface, unchanged.
    // Trust (OR-A1 H1): the cited half's RUNG rides with it — `Some(link)` when
    // some cited clause was credited only under the function's own `requires`
    // — and decides this lane's label, strength and authority variant exactly
    // as it does in the cited lane. Without it a mixed surface would seal a
    // conditional credit as the `Certified` variant.
    cited_part: Option<(&str, Option<trust_certify::clean_island::RequiresLink>)>,
    // Trust (OR-A1 H2): see `discharge_ensures_citations_in_walk`.
    static_proved_rows: &FxHashSet<usize>,
    // Trust: E9 evidence — the cited half of a MIXED surface, exactly as the
    // cited lane graded it. Empty on an all-uncited surface. The certificate
    // minted below covers `graded_cited` followed by this lane's own clauses,
    // i.e. the WHOLE conjunction the discharged rows rest on; a certificate
    // over a proper subset of the surface would misdescribe what was proved.
    graded_cited: &[GradedEnsuresClause],
    // Trust: E9 evidence out-slot — same contract as the cited lane's: written
    // only on the success path, `None` on every fail-closed return.
    certificate: &mut Option<trust_certify::clean_island::EnsuresDischargeCertificate>,
) -> Vec<(usize, String, Option<trust_certify::clean_island::RequiresLink>)> {
    let Some(local) = body.source.def_id().as_local() else {
        return Vec::new();
    };
    let bundle = tcx.trust_contracts(local.to_def_id());
    // The DENSE function-level contract index rides with each clause so the
    // kernel grade this lane obtains can be published as a spec-clean goal row
    // (v6/D1: `targo trust spec --emit clean` prints what the kernel graded).
    // `ensures` clauses live only in the dense `contracts` lane — loop clauses
    // are `invariant`/`decreases` — so this enumerates the same population
    // `iter_all()` did, now with its index.
    let ensures: Vec<(u64, rustc_span::Span)> = bundle
        .contracts
        .iter()
        .enumerate()
        // Trust: the UNCITED clauses only. On the all-uncited surface that is
        // every ensures clause, as before; on a mixed surface the cited ones
        // were graded by the cited lane and arrive as `cited_part`.
        .filter(|(_, c)| c.kind == TrustContractKind::Ensures && c.citation.is_none())
        .map(|(contract_index, c)| {
            (u64::try_from(contract_index).expect("a dense contract index fits u64"), c.span)
        })
        .collect();
    // Trust: a function with cited clauses is this lane's business ONLY when
    // the cited lane certified all of them and said so. Cited clauses without
    // that handoff (a citation missed, the body is quarantined for the
    // deferred post-walk stage, a row was ineligible) discharge nothing, and a
    // handoff for a function with no cited clause is a caller bug — both fail
    // closed.
    let any_cited =
        bundle.iter_all().any(|c| c.kind == TrustContractKind::Ensures && c.citation.is_some());
    if ensures.is_empty() || any_cited != cited_part.is_some() {
        return Vec::new();
    }
    let postcondition_indices: Vec<usize> = results
        .iter()
        .enumerate()
        .filter(|(_, (vc, _))| is_ensures_clause_obligation(vc))
        .map(|(index, _)| index)
        .collect();
    if postcondition_indices.is_empty() {
        return Vec::new();
    }
    for &index in &postcondition_indices {
        if !matches!(
            results[index].1,
            VerificationResult::Unknown { .. } | VerificationResult::Proved { .. }
        ) {
            return Vec::new();
        }
    }
    let Some(mut env) = session_island_env(tcx.sess) else {
        return Vec::new();
    };
    let (accumulated, mut table) =
        tcx.sess.with_trust_compiler_state::<SessionFnFacets, _>(|state| {
            (state.accumulated.clone(), state.composed_table())
        });
    let mut candidates: Vec<&trust_types::VerifiableFunction> = accumulated.iter().collect();
    if !accumulated.iter().any(|f| f.def_path == func.def_path) {
        candidates.push(func);
    }
    for candidate in candidates {
        let Some((path, admission)) =
            trust_certify::clean_island::admit_function_into(&mut env, candidate, &table)
        else {
            continue;
        };
        // Keyed under the def-path AND every spec spelling of it, exactly like
        // `trust_mint_program_admissions` and `record_fn_facets`. An
        // ambiguity-evicted spelling carries no facet record, so
        // `FacetTable::admitted` still fails closed for it.
        for spelling in trust_spec_elab::spec_spellings(&path) {
            if spelling != path {
                table.admit(spelling, admission.clone());
            }
        }
        table.admit(path, admission);
    }
    // Trust (ref-self I1): the SELF function's borrow-erased PARAMETER VIEW.
    // See the twin at the in-walk cited-discharge seam for why only the self
    // function's view is minted.
    trust_admit_param_view_into(tcx, local, func, &mut env, &mut table);
    // SELF IDENTITY (2026-08-26): the CANONICAL def-path, not the bare last
    // segment. The bare name re-enters the ambiguity keyspace
    // `record_fn_facets` deliberately evicts, so a contract on a function whose
    // bare name is shared with any other item could never bind `result`; the
    // def-path is the very string `record_fn_facets` keys the record by. See
    // `trust_spec_elab::self_fn_spelling` for the soundness argument.
    let self_ident = trust_spec_elab::self_fn_spelling(&func.def_path);
    // Trust: `usize`/`isize` rows resolved at the target's width, so they bind
    // at the carrier the import minted just above carries.
    let params_typed = trust_fn_discharge_binder_types(tcx, local);
    let params: Vec<(&str, &str)> = params_typed
        .iter()
        .filter(|(n, _)| n != "result" && !n.starts_with("result."))
        .map(|(n, t)| (n.as_str(), t.as_str()))
        .collect();
    let collapsible = trust_fn_collapsible_by_value_params(tcx, local);
    use trust_certify::clean_island::CitationVerdict;
    let mut cited_defs = Vec::new();
    // Clause span + the island definition that closed it, for the per-clause
    // discharge note emitted once the WHOLE surface certifies. Collected rather
    // than emitted in the loop: a clause that certifies before a later clause
    // declines discharges nothing, and a note claiming otherwise would be the
    // exact diagnostic dishonesty this lane is being hardened against.
    let mut certified_clauses: Vec<(rustc_span::Span, String)> = Vec::new();
    // Trust: E9 evidence — the inputs each defeq grade call received, in clause
    // order. On a MIXED surface the cited lane's half is prepended below, so the
    // certificate covers the whole conjunction.
    let mut graded: Vec<GradedEnsuresClause> = graded_cited.to_vec();
    for (contract_index, clause_span) in &ensures {
        // Trust: clause preparation, island admission, the kernel defeq
        // judgment and the published goal row are ONE shared step
        // (`grade_uncited_island_ensures_clause`), because the deferred
        // post-walk stage must judge a mixed surface's uncited clauses by
        // exactly this criterion. What stays here is what is this lane's own:
        // the all-or-nothing policy and its diagnostics.
        let grade = grade_uncited_island_ensures_clause(
            tcx,
            local,
            &func.def_path,
            &env,
            &table,
            &params,
            &collapsible,
            &self_ident,
            *contract_index,
            *clause_span,
            // Trust (nat-lift): the SAME resolved binder table the clause
            // elaborates with, so the `result` spelling the lift's
            // self-function cross-check reads is the one the goal binds.
            &trust_proof_clause_inputs(&params_typed),
        );
        let (island_name, verdict, clause_text, clause_table) = match grade {
            UncitedIslandClauseGrade::SourceUnavailable => return Vec::new(),
            UncitedIslandClauseGrade::Graded {
                island_name,
                verdict,
                clause_text,
                clause_table,
            } => (island_name, verdict, clause_text, clause_table),
            UncitedIslandClauseGrade::NoIslandDefinition { clause_text } => {
                decline_uncited_clause_without_island_definition(
                    tcx,
                    &func.def_path,
                    *clause_span,
                    &clause_text,
                    !certified_clauses.is_empty(),
                    cited_part.is_some()
                        && postcondition_indices.iter().any(|&index| {
                            matches!(results[index].1, VerificationResult::Unknown { .. })
                        }),
                );
                return Vec::new();
            }
        };
        if !matches!(verdict, CitationVerdict::KernelStatementMatchCertified) {
            emit_uncited_island_defeq_refusal(
                tcx,
                local,
                *clause_span,
                &island_name,
                &verdict,
                &clause_text,
                &func.def_path,
                &self_ident,
                &table,
            );
            return Vec::new();
        }
        cited_defs.push(format!("defeq:{island_name}"));
        graded.push(GradedEnsuresClause {
            text: clause_text,
            facets: clause_table,
            route: GradedEnsuresRoute::IslandDefeq(island_name.clone()),
        });
        certified_clauses.push((*clause_span, island_name));
    }
    // Trust: E9 evidence — see the cited lane. The producer re-judges the whole
    // surface under the FULL certification audit, independently of the same
    // audit each clause's grade required (TG9), so a payload can be absent for a discharged row
    // but never present for a judgment the audit refuses. On a MIXED surface
    // `graded` opens with the cited lane's clauses, so the certificate covers
    // the same conjunction the discharge below rests on — `expected` is the
    // function's TOTAL ensures count, checked rather than assumed.
    // Trust (OR-A1): the RUNG of the cited half, `None` on an all-uncited
    // surface or when every cited clause certified unconditionally.
    let requires_link = cited_part.and_then(|(_, link)| link);
    // Trust (OR-A1): no payload for a conditional surface — the producer
    // re-judges the unconditional goal, and the conditional authority hands out
    // no evidence (Inc 2 mints over `G1★`).
    *certificate = if requires_link.is_some() {
        None
    } else {
        mint_ensures_discharge_certificate(
            &env,
            &graded,
            bundle.contracts.iter().filter(|c| c.kind == TrustContractKind::Ensures).count(),
            &params,
            &self_ident,
            &func.def_path,
            if cited_part.is_some() { "mixed-defeq" } else { "defeq" },
            &trust_proof_clause_inputs(&params_typed),
        )
    };
    // Trust (2026-08-26): name what was proved, and against what. The function
    // note this lane produces reads `N proved ... of which N kernel-certified`,
    // which is a COUNT with no referent — nothing in it says which clause closed
    // or which island definition closed it. The cited lane has always emitted
    // its per-clause `note: citation X kernel-discharges this postcondition
    // statement`; the uncited lane emitted nothing, so a reader had no way to
    // tell an enforced clause from a coincidence. Same shape as the decline
    // above: the report must state what constrained the body.
    for (clause_span, island_name) in &certified_clauses {
        emit_island_defeq_discharge_note(tcx, *clause_span, island_name, &func.def_path);
    }
    // Trust: on a mixed surface the sealed authority records BOTH halves of the
    // conjunction — the cited theorems, then the defeq referents — and the row
    // carries the citation lane's solver label, as the deferred post-walk
    // discharge of the same surface does.
    let def_list = match cited_part {
        Some((theorems, _)) => format!("{theorems}+{}", cited_defs.join("+")),
        None => cited_defs.join("+"),
    };
    // Trust (ref-self I1): the view marker is decided by the SIGNATURE, so it
    // applies to the uncited island-defeq route exactly as it does to the cited
    // one — an uncited `ensures self.x <= 5` names a pointee value at entry and
    // rests on R1 whether or not `result` appears in it.
    // Trust (OR-A1 H1): the label, strength and (at the install seam) the
    // authority variant are decided from `(view, requires_link, mixed)` by the
    // one helper every E9 seam uses, so a conditional cited half makes the
    // whole mixed discharge conditional here too.
    let view = trust_e6_view_marker(tcx, local);
    let (solver, strength) =
        trust_ensures_discharge_label(view, requires_link, cited_part.is_none());
    let mut discharged = Vec::new();
    for &index in &postcondition_indices {
        // Trust (OR-A1 H2): see the cited lane — a conditional surface never
        // replaces an existing static proof.
        if requires_link.is_some() && static_proved_rows.contains(&index) {
            continue;
        }
        results[index].1 = VerificationResult::Proved {
            solver: trust_types::Symbol::intern(solver),
            time_ms: 0,
            strength: strength.clone(),
            proof_certificate: None,
            solver_warnings: None,
            native_proof_envelope: None,
        };
        discharged.push((index, def_list.clone(), requires_link));
    }
    if std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some() {
        eprintln!(
            "TRUST_E9_DEBUG DEFEQ-DISCHARGED fn={} indices={:?} mixed={} requires-link={:?}",
            func.def_path,
            discharged.iter().map(|(index, ..)| *index).collect::<Vec<_>>(),
            cited_part.is_some(),
            requires_link.map(|link| link.as_str())
        );
    }
    discharged
}

/// Trust: the kernel grade of ONE uncited `ensures` clause on the island-defeq
/// route (see [`grade_uncited_island_ensures_clause`]).
enum UncitedIslandClauseGrade {
    /// The authored clause text could not be recovered from the source map.
    SourceUnavailable,
    /// The clause names no ADMISSIBLE Clean island definition, so it has no
    /// kernel route: it belongs to the ordinary solver lanes.
    NoIslandDefinition { clause_text: String },
    /// `island_name` (the first admitted definition, in clause order) was
    /// admitted and the kernel judged the clause by definitional equality.
    /// `clause_text` is the prime-collapsed clause the kernel was asked about —
    /// carried out so a refusal diagnostic can name the binder carriers it
    /// mentions. Trust: `clause_table` is the CLAUSE-LOCAL facet table that
    /// judgment used, carried out so the E9 evidence producer re-elaborates the
    /// very goal the kernel was asked about and not a differently-resolved one.
    Graded {
        island_name: String,
        verdict: trust_certify::clean_island::CitationVerdict,
        clause_text: String,
        clause_table: trust_spec_elab::FacetTable,
    },
}

/// Trust: R4 §1 — judge ONE uncited `ensures` clause on the island-defeq route:
/// recover its text, collapse the primes of provably-immutable by-value
/// parameters, admit the Clean island definitions it names into a CLAUSE-LOCAL
/// table, ask the kernel the `Eq.refl` question, and publish the goal row.
///
/// This lane owns ONLY island-def-citing clauses: at least one cited identifier
/// must be an unfoldable island Definition; everything else stays with the
/// ordinary lanes. A citation is a CALL (`c42(0)`) or — e6 probe f04's
/// spelling — the BARE name of a zero-arity definition (`result ==
/// forty_two_isl`). Names a Rust binding or a Rust item of this crate already
/// bears are never candidates: the clause means the Rust name, so proposing the
/// island definition would mislabel an ordinary clause as island-citing (see
/// [`admit_clause_island_definitions`]). Every island-def citation is
/// independently certification-audited, then installed with the exact
/// facet/admission pair that lets the clause elaborator resolve its reference
/// against the kernel constant. The admitter refuses trust provenance and name
/// collisions, so this cannot overwrite a Rust function's identity.
///
/// Island-call authority is clause-local. Reusing an inserted island facet in a
/// later clause would make the collision guard reject an otherwise valid
/// repeated call, and would unnecessarily let one clause's elaboration state
/// influence another — hence the per-call clone of `table`.
///
/// One criterion, two callers: the in-walk island-defeq lane and the deferred
/// post-walk stage (a mixed surface in a crate with a deferred island suffix).
/// Neither may judge an uncited clause any other way.
#[allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]
fn grade_uncited_island_ensures_clause(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'_>,
    local: LocalDefId,
    def_path: &str,
    env: &trust_certify::clean_island::KernelEnvironment,
    table: &trust_spec_elab::FacetTable,
    params: &[(&str, &str)],
    collapsible: &FxHashSet<String>,
    self_ident: &str,
    contract_index: u64,
    clause_span: rustc_span::Span,
    lift_inputs: &trust_spec_elab::ProofClauseInputs,
) -> UncitedIslandClauseGrade {
    let Ok(mut clause_text) = tcx.sess.source_map().span_to_snippet(clause_span) else {
        return UncitedIslandClauseGrade::SourceUnavailable;
    };
    for (pname, _) in params {
        if collapsible.contains(*pname) {
            clause_text = rewrite_by_value_prime(&clause_text, pname);
        }
    }
    let mut clause_table = table.clone();
    let admissions =
        admit_clause_island_definitions(tcx, local, env, def_path, &clause_text, &mut clause_table);
    let Some(island_name) = admissions.admitted.into_iter().next() else {
        return UncitedIslandClauseGrade::NoIslandDefinition { clause_text };
    };
    // Trust (nat-lift): a user `ensures` clause is a PROOF position, so the
    // kernel question is asked at `ClauseMode::ProofLift` — `e as nat` /
    // `e as int` elaborate into the exact integer injection. Every other
    // elaboration in this file stays `Strict`.
    let verdict = trust_certify::clean_island::kernel_prove_ensures_by_defeq_in(
        env,
        &clause_text,
        params,
        self_ident,
        &clause_table,
        lift_inputs,
    );
    // Trust (2026-09-12, `reports/2026-09-11-island-probe-level-is-zero.md`
    // §6): publish the grade EXACTLY as the cited lane does. Before this,
    // `targo trust spec --emit clean` on an island-defeq crate printed
    // "0 kernel-graded goal(s) … authority none / ungraded" for the very
    // clause the compiler's text report called kernel-certified — the
    // defeq lane graded a goal and told nobody. Same arguments as the
    // kernel call above (v6/D1: never an offline re-elaboration); the
    // `theorem` slot carries the defeq referent, `defeq:<island def>`,
    // which is also what the sealed `EnsuresCitationDischarge` authority
    // records for this row.
    record_graded_cited_goal_once(
        tcx,
        local,
        contract_index,
        def_path,
        "ensures-island-defeq",
        &clause_text,
        params,
        self_ident,
        &clause_table,
        &format!("defeq:{island_name}"),
        &verdict,
        lift_inputs,
        // An uncited clause has no citation, so no conditional rung.
        None,
    );
    if !matches!(
        verdict,
        trust_certify::clean_island::CitationVerdict::KernelStatementMatchCertified
    ) && std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some()
    {
        eprintln!(
            "TRUST_E9_DEBUG DEFEQ-NOT-CERTIFIED fn={def_path} clause={clause_text:?} verdict={verdict:?}"
        );
    }
    UncitedIslandClauseGrade::Graded { island_name, verdict, clause_text, clause_table }
}

/// Trust: an uncited `ensures` that names no admissible island definition
/// declines its whole function on the island-defeq route. Say so when the
/// author has demonstrably written a kernel-backed contract.
///
/// The uncited route is ALL-OR-NOTHING across a function's clauses — one clause
/// that calls no admissible island definition declines the whole function,
/// including clauses that would have discharged on their own. That is the
/// fail-closed direction and is intended, but it used to happen in complete
/// silence: a user with one island clause and one ordinary clause saw no
/// diagnostic at all, only an unproved obligation. Say which clause declined
/// and why.
///
/// An ordinary uncited `ensures` that calls no island definition is the COMMON
/// case and belongs to other lanes, so declining it is not a diagnostic event.
/// But once an EARLIER clause of this same function has already certified
/// against an island definition (`earlier_clause_certified`), the author has
/// demonstrably written an island-backed contract, and the all-or-nothing rule
/// is about to throw that certified clause away. Say so, for the same reason
/// the defeq refusal does.
///
/// Known limit, stated rather than hidden: that error fires only when the
/// island clause precedes the ordinary one. In the reverse order nothing has
/// certified yet and this lane cannot distinguish an island-backed surface
/// from an ordinary one without running the whole admission pass twice. The
/// obligations still stay unproved in both orders — no verdict is ever claimed
/// — so what the reverse order loses is the explanation, not the enforcement.
///
/// MIXED surface (`cited_part_discarded`): the cited lane kernel-certified every
/// cited clause of this function, and this clause is why none of that reaches a
/// verdict row. The partially-cited decline used to be visible only as
/// `TRUST_E9_DEBUG BAIL=partially-cited`. It is a NOTE, not an error, and the
/// caller raises it only while a clause obligation of the function is still
/// solver-UNKNOWN: the solver lanes may well prove an ordinary clause on their
/// own (`e9_multi_clause_undischarged`), and then there is nothing to explain.
fn decline_uncited_clause_without_island_definition(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'_>,
    def_path: &str,
    clause_span: rustc_span::Span,
    clause_text: &str,
    earlier_clause_certified: bool,
    cited_part_discarded: bool,
) {
    if std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some() {
        eprintln!(
            "TRUST_E9_DEBUG NO-ISLAND-CALL fn={def_path} clause={clause_text:?} \
             (uncited defeq route declines the whole function)"
        );
    }
    if earlier_clause_certified {
        tcx.dcx()
            .struct_span_err(
                clause_span,
                "this uncited `ensures` calls no admissible Clean island definition, \
                 so the whole function's island-defeq discharge is declined",
            )
            .with_note(
                "the uncited island route is all-or-nothing across a function's \
                 clauses: an earlier clause of this function DID kernel-discharge, and \
                 that discharge is discarded because this one cannot",
            )
            // Trust: citing a theorem for THIS clause is now enough (a mixed
            // surface discharges when every clause has a kernel route); the
            // old text asked for the whole surface to be cited.
            .with_help(
                "options: (1) give this clause an island-definition call too; (2) cite a \
                 theorem for this clause with `by <theorem>`; (3) move it to its own function",
            )
            .emit();
    } else if cited_part_discarded {
        tcx.dcx()
            .struct_span_note(
                clause_span,
                format!(
                    "this uncited `ensures` has no Clean-kernel route, so the `by` citations on \
                     the other `ensures` clauses of `{def_path}` discharge nothing"
                ),
            )
            .with_note(
                "a citation discharges a function's postcondition rows only when EVERY \
                 `ensures` clause of the function is kernel-proven — cited clauses by their \
                 theorem, uncited clauses by definitional equality against a Clean island \
                 definition they name — because a verdict row is not attributed to a single \
                 clause; this clause names no admissible island definition, so the cited \
                 clauses were certified and then set aside, and every clause is left to the \
                 solver lanes",
            )
            .with_help(
                "options: (1) cite a theorem for this clause too (`ensures <clause> by \
                 <theorem>`); (2) state it against a Clean island definition the body is \
                 definitionally equal to (`ensures result == spec(args)`); (3) move the cited \
                 clauses to a wrapper function of their own",
            )
            .emit();
    }
}

/// Trust (2026-08-26): SAY SO. The island-defeq route is the ONLY thing that
/// can close an island-def-calling `ensures`. The predicate is outside the
/// native verifier's vocabulary by construction — its callee is a Clean
/// definition, not a Rust function — so the obligations it leaves behind are
/// `Unsupported` sentinels, and `-Ztrust-policy=advisory` downgrades those to
/// warnings. Declining in SILENCE therefore turned a contract the author
/// believes is enforced into one that is not: the body could be mutated
/// arbitrarily and the build stayed green, with nothing in the output naming
/// the clause that stopped holding. The same mutation under a CITED clause has
/// always produced a hard `error: citation ... failed the strict Clean
/// statement/certification audit` (`trust_check_citations`), regardless of
/// policy. That asymmetry — cited fails loudly, uncited fails silently — is the
/// defect; the two lanes now fail the same way.
///
/// Policy-independent for the same reason the cited lane is. Survey mode exists
/// to tolerate prover-STRENGTH gaps while the solver is improved; this is not
/// one. The author named a definition the compiler had already
/// certification-audited and admitted, the kernel was asked the identity, and
/// it did not hold. There is no stronger solver to wait for and no runtime
/// check underneath.
fn emit_uncited_island_defeq_refusal(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'_>,
    local: LocalDefId,
    clause_span: rustc_span::Span,
    island_name: &str,
    verdict: &trust_certify::clean_island::CitationVerdict,
    clause_text: &str,
    // Trust (E6 expansion I0): what the shortfall needs to name the gate that
    // refused the function's own E6 import — the def path, its facet key, and
    // the table `admit_function_into` read.
    def_path: &str,
    self_key: &str,
    facet_table: &trust_spec_elab::FacetTable,
) {
    use trust_certify::clean_island::CitationVerdict;
    let mut reason = match verdict {
        CitationVerdict::ClauseOutsideFragment { reason } => reason.clone(),
        CitationVerdict::StatementOrCertificationRejected { detail } => detail.clone(),
        other => format!("{other:?}"),
    };
    // Trust (E6 expansion I0): the cited path has always appended WHY the
    // function is not E6-admitted (`trust_e6_self_admission_shortfall`); this
    // uncited path printed the bare "it is not" for every refused body, facet
    // gate and shape gate alike, so only `TRUST_E6_DEBUG` told them apart.
    // The two lanes now say the same thing. Diagnostics only.
    if trust_spec_elab::is_self_not_e6_admitted_refusal(&reason) {
        reason.push_str("; ");
        reason.push_str(&trust_e6_self_admission_shortfall(
            tcx,
            local,
            def_path,
            self_key,
            facet_table,
        ));
    }
    let mut diag = tcx
        .dcx()
        .struct_span_err(
            clause_span,
            format!(
                "uncited `ensures` naming the Clean island definition \
                 `{island_name}` was not discharged by definitional equality: {reason}"
            ),
        )
        .with_note(
            "an uncited island-calling clause has exactly one discharge route — the \
             kernel judging it against this function's E6-admitted body — so a refusal \
             here leaves the clause constraining nothing",
        )
        .with_help(
            "make the body definitionally equal to the island definition, or cite a \
             theorem for the clause with `by <theorem>`",
        );
    // Trust: name the carrier a `usize`/`isize` binder took.
    if let Some(note) = trust_pointer_sized_binder_note(tcx, local, clause_text) {
        diag.note(note);
    }
    diag.emit();
}

/// Trust: the per-clause note of a successful island-defeq discharge — which
/// clause closed, and against which island definition.
fn emit_island_defeq_discharge_note(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'_>,
    clause_span: rustc_span::Span,
    island_name: &str,
    def_path: &str,
) {
    let mut diag = tcx.dcx().struct_span_note(
        clause_span,
        format!(
            "island definition `{island_name}` kernel-discharges this postcondition by \
             definitional equality: the kernel judged this clause against the imported \
             defining equation of `{def_path}`",
        ),
    );
    diag.note(
        "this is the whole proof of the clause — change the body so it is no longer \
         definitionally equal to the island definition and the discharge becomes a \
         build error, not a downgraded unknown",
    );
    // Trust (GREEN THAT LIES, 2026-09-18): what that kernel check was relative
    // TO. The per-island note says the same thing beside the `import`; this
    // says it beside the ROW, because the row is what a report file, a
    // downstream gate or a summarizer reads. Nothing here is a refusal and
    // nothing is downgraded: the declaration really was kernel-checked.
    for disclosure in trust_island_standin_disclosures(tcx) {
        diag.note(disclosure);
    }
    diag.emit();
}

/// Trust (T3 L4, C07r; L4-C07p-2): a full-verification run's artifacts before the body's VC
/// context is frozen into them. Their results' ids name rows of the body's context, which is
/// still being written while a run (or the E5 rerun) is under way, so they are not
/// [`VerificationArtifacts`] yet: their own `ctx` is not that context. They read only as
/// the parts the run selection needs, and become artifacts only by [`UnfrozenArtifacts::freeze`]
/// with the context the ids were interned into, so artifacts whose results name rows of
/// another context than their own cannot leave `collect_full_verification_artifacts`.
mod unfrozen_artifacts {
    use std::sync::Arc;

    use trust_types::vc_context::VcContext;

    use super::{ResultObligationBinding, ResultProofAuthority, RowResults, VerificationArtifacts};

    pub(super) struct UnfrozenArtifacts(VerificationArtifacts);

    impl UnfrozenArtifacts {
        /// `artifacts`, whose results' ids name rows of a context other than their `ctx`,
        /// which [`Self::freeze`] replaces.
        pub(super) fn new(artifacts: VerificationArtifacts) -> Self {
            Self(artifacts)
        }

        pub(super) fn results(&self) -> &RowResults {
            &self.0.results
        }

        pub(super) fn result_bindings(&self) -> &[Option<ResultObligationBinding>] {
            &self.0.result_bindings
        }

        pub(super) fn proof_authorities(&self) -> &[Option<ResultProofAuthority>] {
            &self.0.proof_authorities
        }

        /// The artifacts, with `ctx` frozen into them: the context their results' ids
        /// were interned into, which nothing writes again.
        pub(super) fn freeze(self, ctx: VcContext) -> VerificationArtifacts {
            let mut artifacts = self.0;
            artifacts.ctx = Arc::new(ctx);
            artifacts
        }
    }
}
use unfrozen_artifacts::UnfrozenArtifacts;

fn collect_full_verification_artifacts<'tcx>(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>,
    body: &Body<'tcx>,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    compiler_contracts: &trust_types::CompilerContractBundle,
    solver_vcs: &[VerificationCondition],
    discharged: &[(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)],
    policy: &TrustVerifyPolicy,
    function_deadline: Option<std::time::Instant>,
    vcgen_context: &trust_vcgen::VcgenContext,
    overflow_check_origins: &OverflowCheckOrigins,
    // Trust (D9): the level `filter_vcs_by_level` applied to `solver_vcs`,
    // recorded on the artifacts (`VerificationArtifacts::max_proof_level`).
    max_level: trust_types::ProofLevel,
    // Trust (TINV step 10.4): the record of the generation `solver_vcs` came from.
    tinv_record: &trust_vcgen::type_invariant::TinvGenerationRecord,
    // Trust (2026-10-17): the body's premise-free rows, which grade an argument
    // premise per row (`argument_premise::PremiseFreeRows`); shared by both passes.
    premise_free: &argument_premise::PremiseFreeRows,
    // Trust (T3 L4, C07p): the body's VC context. Every producer below writes its rows
    // into it, both runs included, and it is frozen into the artifacts this selects.
    mut rows: trust_types::vc_context::VcContext,
) -> VerificationArtifacts {
    // Trust (2026-10-18, gate-rca deadline NESTED-CLOCK): the function deadline as of
    // each read below — moved by any other body's verification a pass forced
    // (`trust_types::verify_budget::credited`), so the E5 rerun and its checks run on
    // this function's own time.
    let lane_credit = trust_types::verify_budget::clock_credit_mark();
    let deadline_now = || trust_types::verify_budget::credited(function_deadline, lane_credit);
    let first = collect_full_verification_artifacts_once(
        tcx,
        body,
        func,
        compiler_contracts,
        solver_vcs,
        discharged,
        &[],
        policy,
        function_deadline,
        vcgen_context,
        overflow_check_origins,
        max_level,
        tinv_record,
        premise_free,
        &mut rows,
    );

    // Full/native E4→E5 feedback is a complete second verification run, never
    // an in-place formula splice under first-pass native bindings. The second
    // run rebuilds its public bundle, native request identities, evidence,
    // result bindings, Clean certificates, and private row authorities from
    // the refined solver input under the SAME absolute function deadline.
    let selected = (|| -> Result<Option<UnfrozenArtifacts>, String> {
        if deadline_now().is_some_and(|at| std::time::Instant::now() >= at) {
            return Err("the shared full/native deadline expired before feedback reconstruction"
                .to_string());
        }
        let feedback = proof_gated_loop_invariant_feedback_tokens(
            func,
            first.results(),
            first.result_bindings(),
            first.proof_authorities(),
        )?;
        if feedback.is_empty() {
            return Ok(None);
        }

        let mut current_vcs = solver_vcs.to_vec();
        current_vcs.extend(discharged.iter().map(|(vc, _)| vc.clone()));
        let plan = proof_gated_loop_e5_replacement_plan(func, &current_vcs, &feedback, &mut rows)?;
        if !plan.iter().any(|replacement| replacement.changed) {
            return Ok(None);
        }
        if deadline_now().is_some_and(|at| std::time::Instant::now() >= at) {
            return Err("the shared full/native deadline expired before the E5 rerun".to_string());
        }

        let mut refined_solver_vcs = solver_vcs.to_vec();
        let mut moved_discharged = FxHashSet::default();
        for replacement in plan.iter().filter(|replacement| replacement.changed) {
            if replacement.row_index < solver_vcs.len() {
                refined_solver_vcs[replacement.row_index] = replacement.replacement.clone();
            } else {
                let discharged_index = replacement.row_index - solver_vcs.len();
                if discharged_index >= discharged.len()
                    || !moved_discharged.insert(discharged_index)
                {
                    return Err(
                        "a changed E5 row escaped the full/native solver+discharged carrier"
                            .to_string(),
                    );
                }
                // A first-pass interval discharge is not reusable after the
                // formula changes. Move the regenerated row into the second
                // pass solver carrier so both the native engine and AY bridge
                // see and solve it anew.
                refined_solver_vcs.push(replacement.replacement.clone());
            }
        }
        let refined_discharged = discharged
            .iter()
            .enumerate()
            .filter(|(index, _)| !moved_discharged.contains(index))
            .map(|(_, result)| result.clone())
            .collect::<Vec<_>>();
        let second = collect_full_verification_artifacts_once(
            tcx,
            body,
            func,
            compiler_contracts,
            &refined_solver_vcs,
            &refined_discharged,
            &feedback,
            policy,
            deadline_now(),
            vcgen_context,
            overflow_check_origins,
            max_level,
            tinv_record,
            premise_free,
            &mut rows,
        );
        // A late pass may contain valid proofs, but accepting it after the one
        // function budget would make the feedback lane a budget multiplier.
        if deadline_now().is_some_and(|at| std::time::Instant::now() >= at) {
            return Err("the shared full/native deadline expired during the E5 rerun".to_string());
        }
        validate_proof_gated_loop_feedback_candidate(
            func,
            second.results(),
            second.result_bindings(),
            second.proof_authorities(),
            &feedback,
            &mut rows,
        )?;
        if deadline_now().is_some_and(|at| std::time::Instant::now() >= at) {
            return Err(
                "the shared full/native deadline expired during final E5 validation".to_string()
            );
        }
        Ok(Some(second))
    })();

    let selected = match selected {
        Ok(Some(second)) => second,
        Ok(None) => first,
        Err(reason) => {
            debug!(
                "Trust: full/native proof-gated loop feedback for {} failed closed: {}",
                func.def_path, reason,
            );
            first
        }
    };
    // Trust (T3 L4, C07p, C07r): every producer for the body has written its rows, so the
    // body's context is frozen here, into the run this selected (L4-CTX-1); only now are
    // its artifacts `VerificationArtifacts`.
    let mut selected = selected.freeze(rows);

    // The first pass is deliberately side-effect-free with respect to this
    // function's interprocedural panic registry. Publish at most once, and only
    // from the finally-selected, fully-revalidated carrier.
    // Trust (2026-09-16): decided ONCE here and threaded into both the
    // zero-obligation gate (R40 candidacy is poisoned for a charged body, so the
    // fixpoint cannot bless it either) and the registration gate below.
    // Trust (2026-10-08, review round 82): a body with NO rows has no verdict that
    // could rest on an argument premise, so its marker is cleared and it keeps its
    // zero-obligation blessing (measured: a premise-installed zero-row callee
    // otherwise failed its callers under `certify`, which passed before D15).
    // Only D15's marker: D14 REMOVES rows, so a zero-row body may owe its
    // emptiness to it and keeps the marker (`argument_premise` module doc).
    if selected.results.is_empty() {
        argument_premise::clear_noalias_premise(tcx, body);
    }
    // Trust (2026-10-08, review round 81): a body carrying an argument premise
    // (D15) is CONDITIONAL in exactly the same sense, so it rides the same flag.
    // Without this the zero-obligation fixpoint blessed such a body and a caller
    // spent the verdict with no trace of the premise.
    let charged_global_alloc_contract = body_is_charged_global_alloc_contract(tcx, body)
        || argument_premise::body_carries_premise(tcx.sess, body.source.def_id());
    // Trust (2026-09-29): a verdict whose native bundle kept a bundled std member
    // under the runtime-checked disposition is CONDITIONAL on that member's own
    // runtime checks (`RuntimeCheckedStdMember`): its rows say nothing about the
    // member's Asserts. It must never become registry authority by any route —
    // `register_proven_panic_free` (whose entries let a caller EVICT this body
    // from its bundle and discharge the call, dropping the member rows out of the
    // caller too), the Part-B conditional registry, or the R40 zero-obligation
    // fixpoint. Threaded into the zero-obligation gate exactly like the charged
    // global-alloc contract, so the R40 candidacy is poisoned as well.
    let conditional_std = !selected.runtime_checked_std_members.is_empty();
    // Trust (OD-1 phase 1): a body carrying an instance hypothesis claims
    // panic-freedom only under it, so it is CONDITIONAL exactly like `conditional_std`
    // above: never registered by any route, never an R40 candidate. (Its bundle was
    // built above, which is where the hypothesis was recorded.)
    let conditional_instance_hypothesis =
        verdict_carries_instance_hypothesis(tcx.sess, body.source.def_id());
    // Trust (SAFETY-ASSUME-1, ruled 2026-10-17): a body one of whose unsafe blocks is
    // ASSUMED sound on its `// SAFETY:` comment (some row of it was withheld at the
    // generation above, which wrote the ledger before these artifacts were built) is
    // CONDITIONAL on that declaration exactly like an instance hypothesis: never
    // registered by any route, never an R40 candidate.
    let conditional_safety_comment =
        safety_comment_assumption::body_carries_declaration(tcx.sess, body.source.def_id());
    let conditional_std_member = conditional_std;
    let conditional_std =
        conditional_std || conditional_instance_hypothesis || conditional_safety_comment;
    let established = results_establish_unconditional_panic_freedom(
        &selected.results,
        &selected.result_bindings,
        &selected.proof_authorities,
    ) || postcondition_only_run_establishes_unconditional_panic_freedom(
        tcx.sess, zero_obligation_source_owner(body.source), func, &selected,
    ) || zero_obligation_run_establishes_unconditional_panic_freedom(
        tcx,
        body,
        func,
        &selected,
        charged_global_alloc_contract || conditional_std,
    );
    // Diagnostic-only registration probe (TRUST_PF_REG_TRACE). Reads only; the
    // registration decision below is unchanged. Reveals, per function, whether
    // it registered as unconditionally panic-free and — per obligation — the
    // exact clause (proved / entry-assumption / assumed-total / authority
    // variant / unconditional-static-proof) so the interprocedural frontier is
    // observable without altering any verdict.
    if std::env::var_os("TRUST_PF_REG_TRACE").is_some() {
        eprintln!(
            "[PF_REG] fn={} established={} nrows={}",
            func.def_path,
            established,
            selected.results.len()
        );
        let is_target = func.def_path.contains("rational")
            || func.def_path.contains("PartialOrd")
            || func.def_path.contains("Ord>::cmp")
            || func.def_path.contains("err_barrier");
        if is_target || !established {
            for (i, ((vc, r), a)) in
                selected.results.iter().zip(&selected.proof_authorities).enumerate()
            {
                let binding = selected.result_bindings.get(i).and_then(Option::as_ref);
                let uncond = a
                    .as_ref()
                    .is_some_and(|auth| auth.is_unconditional_static_proof_for(i, vc, r, binding));
                eprintln!(
                    "[PF_REG]   row {i} kind={} proved={} entry_disc={} assumed_disc={} uncond={} auth={}",
                    display_obligation_kind_key(&vc.kind),
                    r.is_proved(),
                    is_entry_assumption_discharge(r),
                    is_assumed_total_discharge(r),
                    uncond,
                    a.as_ref().map(pf_authority_name).unwrap_or("None"),
                );
            }
        }
    }
    // Coroutine resume-state safety is published as an explicit protocol
    // assumption/contract after artifact collection. It is therefore never
    // unconditional registry authority, even when all data-safety rows are
    // independently proved. Belt (D1 coupling): bridge contract evidence also
    // blocks registration — it witnesses that the solved module carries the
    // entry resume-state Assume, so every proof in it is CONDITIONAL on
    // callers, even in the (structurally excluded) case of a sentinel-free
    // MIR body.
    if established
        && !body_has_coroutine_protocol_assert(body)
        && selected.coroutine_resume_contract.is_none()
        // §5 STEP 5: a body charged the `contract:global-alloc-dealloc-panic-
        // free` premise (it drops an allocation-owning std container) is
        // CONDITIONAL — even when the contract discharged vacuously for
        // `Global`, the discharge is a statement about the crate graph in
        // view, and a final link may register an allocator this compilation
        // never saw. Registering it would let the interprocedural route spend
        // the verdict in a caller whose bundle carries no trace of the
        // premise — the exact axiom-vanishing class `verdict_rests_on_user_
        // axiom` documents. THE SAME detector as the publication seam
        // (`body_is_charged_global_alloc_contract`, both sides of the charge),
        // so producer and consumer cannot drift — they did once (2026-09-16).
        && !charged_global_alloc_contract
        // Trust (2026-09-29): see `conditional_std` above.
        && !conditional_std
    {
        // MERGE (b62 <- origin/main; squash d24c671211): OUR surrounding logic (the R43 parametric
        // registry and the Part-B conditional panic-freedom branch) is kept, but the
        // call itself takes UPSTREAM's new 3-arg signature, which threads the DefId
        // so the registry is keyed by identity and not by printed path alone.
        register_proven_panic_free(tcx, body.source.def_id(), &func.def_path);
        // R43 — and, when this body's panic-freedom is a property of its
        // control-flow SHAPE rather than of its type arguments, also record it in
        // the strictly narrower parametric registry, which is the ONLY thing that
        // licenses a MONOMORPHIZED call key to be discharged against this
        // argument-free registration. Nested inside the branch above so membership
        // is by construction a subset of `panic_free_proven_funcs`.
        if body_is_parametrically_panic_free(tcx, body) {
            register_parametric_panic_free(tcx.sess, &func.def_path);
        }
    } else if !established
        // Trust (2026-10-08, review round 82): nor the conditional registry for a
        // body whose verdict rests on an argument premise (D15).
        && !argument_premise::body_carries_premise(tcx.sess, body.source.def_id())
        // Trust (2026-09-29): the Part-B registry is a panic-freedom claim too, and
        // a caller discharges a call to a registered body there; see
        // `conditional_std` above.
        && !conditional_std
        && results_establish_panic_freedom_under_precondition(
            func,
            has_declared_requires_contract(&func.contracts),
            &selected.results,
            &selected.result_bindings,
            &selected.proof_authorities,
        )
    {
        // Trust (Part B): the function did not establish UNCONDITIONAL
        // panic-freedom (it carries a declared `#[requires(P)]` entry
        // assumption), but its panic-freedom IS established UNDER `P`. Record it
        // in the SEPARATE conditional registry so callers can discharge a call to
        // it ONLY by proving `P[args]` at the call site (Part-C). `None` (no
        // faithfully-extracted declared `P`) registers nothing — fail closed.
        if let Some(summary) =
            panic_free_under_precondition_summary(tcx, &func.def_path, func, body)
        {
            register_panic_free_under_precondition(tcx.sess, summary);
        }
    }
    // R40 — the well-founded zero-obligation blessing. The coroutine carve-out
    // above applies identically here: a body whose resume-state safety is
    // published as a protocol assumption is panic-free only for callers that
    // honour it, so it must never become unconditional registry authority by any
    // route. Withdraw it BEFORE the fixpoint runs; poison is permanent.
    if body_has_coroutine_protocol_assert(body) || selected.coroutine_resume_contract.is_some() {
        revoke_zero_obligation_blessing_candidacy(tcx.sess, zero_obligation_source_owner(body.source));
    }
    // Trust (SAFETY-ASSUME-1): the same withdrawal for a SAFETY-comment declared body
    // (belt: the zero-obligation gate above already recorded a poisoned candidacy).
    if conditional_safety_comment {
        revoke_zero_obligation_blessing_candidacy(tcx.sess, zero_obligation_source_owner(body.source));
    }
    // Trust (2026-09-29): the same withdrawal for a verdict conditional on a
    // runtime-checked std member (belt: the zero-obligation gate above already
    // recorded a poisoned candidacy for it).
    if conditional_instance_hypothesis {
        // OD-1 phase 1: the premise authenticator already declines it
        // (`instance_hypothesis_verdicts`, recorded with the hypothesis).
        revoke_zero_obligation_blessing_candidacy(tcx.sess, zero_obligation_source_owner(body.source));
        // The transport half of a zero-obligation body: when every call it makes is
        // discharged except its admitted sites, those calls are accounted for by its
        // own hypothesis rows, so it publishes them instead of the gap row.
        if selected.proof_results.summary.total == 0 {
            let accounted =
                instance_hypothesis::admitted_site_call_keys(tcx.sess, body.source.def_id());
            let only_hypotheses = !accounted.is_empty()
                && all_calls_target_proven_panic_free_modulo(
                    tcx.sess, zero_obligation_source_owner(body.source), func, &accounted,
                );
            with_crate_verification_state(tcx.sess, |state| {
                if let Some(owner) = zero_obligation_source_owner(body.source) {
                    if only_hypotheses {
                        state.zero_obligation_calls_only_hypotheses.insert(owner);
                    } else {
                        state.zero_obligation_calls_only_hypotheses.remove(&owner);
                    }
                }
            });
        }
    }
    if conditional_std_member {
        revoke_zero_obligation_blessing_candidacy(tcx.sess, zero_obligation_source_owner(body.source));
        // ... and every premise lane that would spend this verdict for another
        // body declines it (`verdict_rests_on_user_axiom`).
        record_runtime_checked_std_member_verdict(tcx.sess, body.source.def_id());
        if std::env::var_os("TRUST_PF_REG_TRACE").is_some() {
            eprintln!(
                "[PF_REG] fn={} registration withheld: conditional on {} bundled std member runtime check(s)",
                func.def_path,
                selected.runtime_checked_std_members.len()
            );
        }
    }
    // Re-decide every recorded candidate against the registry as it now stands.
    // This is what makes the zero-obligation lane order-independent: a candidate
    // blocked only because its callee had not registered YET is blessed as soon
    // as that callee does, instead of being written off by its single evaluation.
    propagate_zero_obligation_blessings(tcx.sess);
    calculus_pending::finish_inputs(&mut selected, deadline_now());
    selected
}

/// Trust (T3 L4, C07r; plan C07r change 1): the full lane's results carrier, built where
/// the run's evidence rebuilds its rows. [`full_verification_legacy_results_bound_with_fresh_vcs`]
/// rebuilds each row with its binding, the function budget is stamped on the rows' evidence
/// timeouts, and each row joins the body's context `rows` through the row oracle.
fn full_verification_row_results_bound(
    rows: &mut trust_types::vc_context::VcContext,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    bundle: &trust_verifier_api::TrustContractBundle,
    result: &trust_verifier_api::VerificationRunResult,
    fresh_vcs: &[VerificationCondition],
    fresh_vc_rekey_snapshot: &ExactFreshVcRekeySnapshot,
    function_budget_ms: u64,
) -> (RowResults, Vec<Option<ResultObligationBinding>>) {
    let (mut rebuilt, result_bindings) = full_verification_legacy_results_bound_with_fresh_vcs(
        func,
        bundle,
        result,
        fresh_vcs,
        fresh_vc_rekey_snapshot,
    );
    // Trust ('0ms' timeout honesty): the legacy conversion stamps a `0`
    // placeholder on every evidence-status Timeout (the evidence carries no
    // duration). Here — the one production seam where the session options are
    // reachable — replace the placeholder with the resolved per-function
    // budget on exactly the rows whose evidence names the wall-clock budget,
    // so the strict message reports the budget that actually expired.
    stamp_function_budget_on_evidence_timeouts(
        function_budget_ms,
        result,
        &result_bindings,
        &mut rebuilt,
    );
    (RowResults::intern(rows, rebuilt), result_bindings)
}

/// Trust (T3 L4, C07r; plan C07r change 1): the ay bridge on the full lane's results
/// carrier ([`bridge_v1_ay_proofs_into_native_results_with_scope`], whose arguments these
/// are). The bridged rows replace the carrier's in one rewrite, so each keeps its id in the
/// body's context `rows` where it still reads back as the row, and joins it again where it
/// does not (`RowResults::rewrite`). Returns the bridge's proved and failed counts and its
/// revalidation eligibilities.
fn bridge_v1_ay_proofs_into_row_results(
    refutation_scope: &trust_vcgen::GenericRefutationScope,
    per_vc_timeout_ms: u64,
    scale: trust_types::determinism::EffortScale,
    deadline: Option<std::time::Instant>,
    solver_vcs: &[VerificationCondition],
    result_bindings: &mut [Option<ResultObligationBinding>],
    clock_stops: &mut ClockStops,
    stamp_held_assert_refutations: bool,
    results: &mut RowResults,
    rows: &mut trust_types::vc_context::VcContext,
    proof_artifact_root: Option<&std::path::Path>,
) -> (u32, u32, Vec<Option<SolverRevalidationEligibility>>) {
    results.rewrite(rows, |results, _| {
        let (bridged, proved, failed, eligibilities) =
            bridge_v1_ay_proofs_into_native_results_with_scope(
                refutation_scope,
                per_vc_timeout_ms,
                scale,
                deadline,
                solver_vcs,
                result_bindings,
                clock_stops,
                stamp_held_assert_refutations,
                std::mem::take(results),
                proof_artifact_root,
            );
        *results = bridged;
        (proved, failed, eligibilities)
    })
}

fn collect_full_verification_artifacts_once<'tcx>(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>,
    body: &Body<'tcx>,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    compiler_contracts: &trust_types::CompilerContractBundle,
    solver_vcs: &[VerificationCondition],
    discharged: &[(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)],
    loop_feedback: &[ProofGatedLoopInvariant],
    policy: &TrustVerifyPolicy,
    function_deadline: Option<std::time::Instant>,
    vcgen_context: &trust_vcgen::VcgenContext,
    overflow_check_origins: &OverflowCheckOrigins,
    max_level: trust_types::ProofLevel,
    tinv_record: &trust_vcgen::type_invariant::TinvGenerationRecord,
    premise_free: &argument_premise::PremiseFreeRows,
    // Trust (T3 L4, C07p): the body's VC context, which this run's probes write into.
    rows: &mut trust_types::vc_context::VcContext,
) -> UnfrozenArtifacts {
    // Trust (2026-10-17, gate-rca deadline): the native lane — bundle construction, then
    // the live dispatch — may spend only its share of what is left of the function clock
    // (`native_lane_cutoff`); the certifying lanes after it keep the rest.
    let native_cutoff = native_lane_cutoff(std::time::Instant::now(), function_deadline);
    // Trust (2026-10-18, gate-rca deadline NESTED-CLOCK): construction reads bundled
    // members' and destructors' optimized MIR, which can verify another body in full,
    // synchronously. That body's verification is its own time, not this function's
    // (`trust_types::verify_budget`, "Another function's verification is not this
    // function's time"), so the deadline and the cutoff the rest of the lane runs on are
    // moved by the credit construction earned, BEFORE the context every live receipt is
    // bound to is built from them.
    let construction_credit = trust_types::verify_budget::clock_credit_mark();

    let mut all_vcs = solver_vcs.to_vec();
    all_vcs.extend(discharged.iter().map(|(vc, _)| vc.clone()));
    let (
        bundle,
        mut native_trust_ir_bundle,
        definition_entry_markers,
        mut coroutine_resume_contract,
        runtime_checked_std_members,
        source_question_bundle,
        decline_reference,
        source_reference_sites,
    ) = build_full_verification_input(
        tcx,
        body,
        func,
        compiler_contracts,
        &all_vcs,
        loop_feedback,
        policy,
        native_cutoff,
    );
    let function_deadline =
        trust_types::verify_budget::credited(function_deadline, construction_credit);
    let native_cutoff = trust_types::verify_budget::credited(native_cutoff, construction_credit);
    let context = full_verifier_execution_context(
        tcx.sess,
        full_verifier_run_id(&func.def_path),
        function_deadline,
    );
    // This is the one audited semantic-TCB issuer in the root constellation:
    // the native bundle has just returned from the final live MIR lowering and
    // all compiler-owned rewrites are complete. A mint failure poisons the
    // native result, so no ordinary or replayed bundle can silently fall
    // through to the source-authorized TrustMC lane.
    let source_generation_authority = match mint_source_generation_authority_for_live_native_bundle(
        &mut native_trust_ir_bundle,
    ) {
        Ok(authority) => authority,
        Err(reason) => {
            coroutine_resume_contract = None;
            native_trust_ir_bundle = Err(reason);
            None
        }
    };
    // Trust (coroutine resume-state contract): the bridge's authenticated
    // sentinel-conversion evidence was minted INSIDE the bundle builder, from
    // the live module while it still carried the bridge's private proof
    // inventory (see the extraction site in
    // `build_native_trust_ir_bundle_from_verifiable_functions`). Extracting it
    // HERE — the previous shape — could only ever answer `None`: by this point
    // `append_verifier_api_obligations_to_native_trust_ir` has reset the module
    // inventory to the public bundle and the marker-stamped obligations the
    // extractor authenticates against are gone. `None` whenever the bundle
    // failed to build, the authority mint failed, or the contract lane did not
    // (fully) engage — the publish seam then keeps the fail-closed
    // `assumption:coroutine` + strict abort. Belt: the evidence is about THIS
    // function (the extractor recomputes the recognizer decision from `func`'s
    // own body) and is re-filtered on `function_def_path` at the publish seam.
    //
    // A definition-site `requires(P)` is the callee's modular entry
    // assumption, not a proposition to prove for arbitrary inputs. Keep its
    // exact compiler-authored row in `bundle` for catalog/transport reporting,
    // but do not route it to a proof engine. Caller-side precondition VCs have
    // `VerificationCondition` origin and remain in this dispatched subset.
    let dispatched_obligations = full_verification_dispatched_obligations_for_function(
        func,
        &bundle,
        &definition_entry_markers,
    );
    // Freeze the exact public claims after every legitimate compiler-owned
    // synthetic contract rewrite and before the verifier sees the bundle.
    let expected_function = trust_verifier_api::FunctionContext {
        crate_name: tcx.crate_name(body.source.def_id().krate).to_string(),
        path: trust_mir_extract::safe_def_path_str(tcx, body.source.def_id()),
    };
    let function_authority = CompilerFunctionAuthority::exact(
        expected_function,
        tcx.stable_crate_id(body.source.def_id().krate).as_u64(),
    );
    let fresh_vc_rekey_snapshot =
        exact_fresh_vc_rekey_snapshot_with_dispatched_obligations_and_source_reference(
            func,
            compiler_contracts,
            &bundle,
            &dispatched_obligations,
            &all_vcs,
            definition_entry_markers,
            &function_authority,
            context.snapshot(),
            decline_reference.as_ref(),
        );
    // Trust (#10): thread the per-function wall-clock budget into the
    // full-verification path so a large native obligation set
    // degrades to Timeout instead of stalling the build unbounded. A disabled
    // budget (`-Ztrust-verify-function-budget-ms=0`) leaves the deadline `None` and
    // preserves the original unbounded behaviour exactly. Timeout is sound:
    // `FunctionVerdict::from_summary` maps any Timeout obligation to TimedOut
    // before it ever considers Proved.
    if env_flag_enabled("TRUST_NATIVE_DEBUG") {
        let outcome = match &native_trust_ir_bundle {
            Ok(Some(_)) => "Ok(Some) — native bundle built".to_string(),
            Ok(None) => "Ok(None) — native bundle skipped".to_string(),
            Err(reason) => format!("Err — {reason}"),
        };
        eprintln!("[NATIVE_TRUSTMC_BUNDLE] func={} outcome={outcome}", func.def_path);
    }
    // Trust: match on a REFERENCE so the native bundle stays alive past the
    // solve — the bridge-lane evidence emission below binds its proofs to the
    // SAME bundle/request digests the native engines would have attached, and
    // those digests may only be computed from bundle bytes we actually hold.
    // Trust: capture the exact live public/request/native triple only under
    // the existing opt-in dump. Canonical bytes are diagnostic inputs, never
    // authority, and this does not extend the verification deadline.
    if let Some(dump_dir) = tcx.sess.opts.unstable_opts.trust_dump.native_bundle.as_deref()
        && let Ok(Some(native)) = &native_trust_ir_bundle
    {
        publish_paired_verifier_input_dump(tcx, &bundle, &dispatched_obligations, native, dump_dir);
    }
    // Trust: candidates come only from the complete compiler-frozen current
    // claim/VC association. The router retains all request rows and returns
    // owned current-kernel evidence separately from pending public status.
    let early_replay_candidates = early_kernel_replay_candidates(
        &bundle,
        &dispatched_obligations,
        &all_vcs,
        &fresh_vc_rekey_snapshot,
    );
    let (live_dispatch, early_kernel_replays) = match &native_trust_ir_bundle {
        // The dispatch context is `context` with the native cutoff set: solving stops at
        // the cutoff, while its deadline (what every live receipt is bound to and every
        // mint below checks), its snapshot and its liveness are `context`'s own.
        Ok(native_trust_ir_bundle) => {
            // Trust (C1, 2026-11-06): the one audited issuer of live source reference
            // facts, right after the authority mint and over the same final bundle
            // (nothing between the mint and this dispatch mutates it). The facts
            // borrow the bundle and the authority and live only for this dispatch;
            // the whole-function receipt consumer below re-translates nothing (its
            // receipts carry the rows translated here), so it needs no facts. `None`
            // (no claim survived, or the constructor refused) is today's fail-closed
            // behaviour.
            let source_reference_facts = match (
                native_trust_ir_bundle.as_ref(),
                source_generation_authority.as_ref(),
                source_reference_sites.as_ref(),
            ) {
                (Some(native), Some(authority), Some(sites)) => {
                    source_reference_facts::source_reference_facts_for_live_native_bundle(
                        native, authority, sites,
                    )
                }
                _ => None,
            };
            verify_full_bundle_with_live_receipts_and_kernel_replays(
                &bundle,
                &dispatched_obligations,
                native_trust_ir_bundle.as_ref(),
                source_generation_authority.as_ref(),
                source_reference_facts.as_ref(),
                &context.clone().with_native_solve_cutoff(native_cutoff),
                vc_timeout_ms(tcx.sess),
                early_replay_candidates,
                tcx.sess.opts.unstable_opts.trust_proof_artifact_root.as_deref(),
            )
        }
        Err(reason) => (
            LiveFullVerificationDispatch {
                result: native_trust_ir_bundle_build_failure_result(
                    &bundle,
                    &dispatched_obligations,
                    &context,
                    reason,
                ),
                live_receipts: None,
            },
            Vec::new(),
        ),
    };
    let mut early_kernel_replays = early_kernel_replays
        .into_iter()
        .map(|proof| (proof.obligation().obligation_id.clone(), proof))
        .collect::<BTreeMap<_, _>>();
    let mut live_receipts = live_dispatch.live_receipts;
    let live_full_result = live_dispatch.result;
    // Trust (T3 L4, C07r; plan C07r change 1): the results carrier is built here, where
    // the run's evidence rebuilds its rows, and every writer below works on it, so every
    // results row is on an id in the body's context from its first write on.
    let (mut results, mut result_bindings) = full_verification_row_results_bound(
        rows,
        func,
        &bundle,
        &live_full_result,
        &all_vcs,
        &fresh_vc_rekey_snapshot,
        tcx.sess.opts.unstable_opts.trust_verify_function_budget_ms,
    );
    // Determinism plan §3.7 (2026-10-01): the rows a clock leaves unproved in
    // this run — a lane below that would have decided a row, or minted the
    // authority its verdict needs, and stopped on the function deadline or on a
    // solve's own clock. Published as timeouts by `stamp_clock_stopped_rows` at
    // the end of the authority phase, once every lane has had its chance.
    // Trust: this is before the entire bridge, including its bitmask/interval
    // probes. A checked proof changes only its exact compiler-bound pending
    // row; the ordinary final vacuity/assumption/authority gates still run.
    results.rewrite(rows, |results, _| {
        for (index, (vc, result)) in results.iter_mut().enumerate() {
            if !matches!(result, VerificationResult::Unknown { .. }) {
                continue;
            }
            let Some(binding) = result_bindings.get(index).and_then(Option::as_ref) else {
                continue;
            };
            if binding.matches_row(index, vc)
                && let Some(proof) =
                    early_kernel_replays.get(&binding.public_obligation_id).filter(|proof| {
                        proof.matches(&bundle, &binding.canonical_obligation, vc, &context)
                    })
            {
                *result = current_kernel_replay_result(proof.elapsed_ms());
            }
        }
    });
    let mut clock_stops = ClockStops::default();
    record_deadline_declined_bundle_clock_stops(
        &mut clock_stops,
        &live_full_result,
        &results,
        &result_bindings,
    );
    // Trust: bridge — the native CHC route can't lower arithmetic/bounds, so let
    // the artifact-backed in-process ay backend discharge what it left unproved.
    // The preclassified carrier is historically named `discharged`, but also
    // holds UnsupportedMir rows as Unknown. Those rows are deliberately not
    // sent to the solver; omitting them here hides the unresolved loop that
    // makes a satisfiable body query insufficient as an execution witness.
    // Trust (D5-A): plus the same-crate call-destination screen, evaluated
    // under the SAME function-owned context the VCs were generated under.
    let refutation_scope = trust_vcgen::GenericRefutationScope::for_function_with_call_dests(
        func,
        solver_vcs,
        discharged,
        vcgen_context,
    );
    let (_bridged_proved, _bridged_failed, mut revalidation_eligibilities) =
        bridge_v1_ay_proofs_into_row_results(
            &refutation_scope,
            vc_timeout_ms(tcx.sess),
            effort_scale(tcx.sess),
            context.deadline,
            solver_vcs,
            &mut result_bindings,
            &mut clock_stops,
            TrustVerifyPolicy::from_session(tcx.sess).fail_closed(),
            &mut results,
            rows,
            tcx.sess.opts.unstable_opts.trust_proof_artifact_root.as_deref(),
        );
    // Trust: `-full`-only, refute-only user-`assert!` refutation. vcgen builds the
    // path-feasibility formula of each `assert!`/`panic!` failure block, emitting
    // it only when grounded in function PARAMETERS (no computed intermediate left
    // free); an ay SAT verdict on such a formula is a genuine counterexample (a
    // real input reaches the panic). See `refute_full_assert_obligations`.
    // Obligation ids the NATIVE run holds Proved evidence for. The refute
    // lane's grounding rewrite must not revoke those rows' bindings even when
    // the compiler-side import DOWNGRADED the verdict (strict-accept declined):
    // the post-run proof-authority validation walks native-Proved evidence and
    // requires the binding to still exist.
    let natively_proved_obligation_ids: FxHashSet<String> = live_full_result
        .evidence
        .iter()
        .filter(|evidence| evidence.status == trust_verifier_api::EvidenceStatus::Proved)
        .map(|evidence| evidence.obligation_id.clone())
        .collect();
    // Trust (grounded catch-all premise floor): every catch-all a grounding
    // rewrite re-points, with the sibling rows its gate read as labels. Graded
    // by `cap_grounded_panic_freedom_authorities` at the end of the authority
    // phase.
    let mut grounded_catchalls: Vec<GroundedCatchallPremises> = Vec::new();
    // Trust (T3 L4, C07r): the refutation, replay, structural and kernel lanes below append
    // and rewrite rows, so they run as one rewrite of the carrier, whose rows are paired
    // with their ids again when it returns (`RowResults::rewrite`). They write the body's
    // context as before, through the rewrite's `rows`.
    let panic_grounding = results.rewrite(rows, |results, rows| {
        let result_count_before_assert_refutation = results.len();
        let (_assert_proved, _assert_refuted) = refute_full_assert_obligations(
            tcx.sess,
            zero_obligation_source_owner(body.source),
            func,
            results,
            Some(&mut result_bindings),
            context.deadline,
            vcgen_context,
            &natively_proved_obligation_ids,
            &mut grounded_catchalls,
            rows,
        );
        result_bindings
            .extend((result_count_before_assert_refutation..results.len()).map(|_| None));
        // Trust (failed-rekey symmetry): a refutation just appended is a VERIFIED
        // counterexample reaching a panic block; the native duplicate of the same
        // source obligation must not stay Unknown beside it. Only Unknown ->
        // Failed conversions; the reconciled native summary is deliberately left
        // untouched (the native evidence honestly WAS unsupported, and a function
        // with a refutation already fails on the authoritative bridged summary).
        rekey_verified_assert_refutations_onto_unknown_duplicates(
            results,
            result_count_before_assert_refutation,
        );
        revalidation_eligibilities
            .extend((result_count_before_assert_refutation..results.len()).map(|_| None));
        // Trust (#5-PRE-A follow-through): call-site L0 replay for solver-witnessed
        // unmet preconditions — refutation-only, appends only `Failed` rows with the
        // callee's REAL L0 kind so `strict_l0_verification_failure` counts them in
        // its `failed` arm and the refutation-proper header fires. Runs AFTER the
        // bridge (which is what substitutes the ay `Failed { counterexample }`
        // verdict onto the Precondition row this lane keys off) and BEFORE
        // `certify_all`/`build_result_proof_authorities_with_revalidations`, so the
        // appended rows are covered by the (None) binding/eligibility/authority
        // slots below and can never acquire evidence or authority. See
        // `refute_callsite_l0_replays_for_unmet_preconditions` for the full
        // soundness argument.
        let result_count_before_callsite_replay = results.len();
        refute_callsite_l0_replays_for_unmet_preconditions(
            tcx,
            body,
            func,
            results,
            context.deadline,
            rows,
        );
        result_bindings.extend((result_count_before_callsite_replay..results.len()).map(|_| None));
        revalidation_eligibilities
            .extend((result_count_before_callsite_replay..results.len()).map(|_| None));
        // Trust (BMC bounded-reduction refutation): the same refutation-only,
        // append-only discipline for a fixed-size array reduction whose accumulator
        // genuinely overflows — the complete unroll's SAT model IS the counterexample.
        // Placed beside the callsite replay so the appended rows are covered by the
        // (None) binding/eligibility/authority slots and can never acquire evidence.
        // See `refute_bounded_reduction_bmc_overflows`.
        let result_count_before_bmc_reduction = results.len();
        refute_bounded_reduction_bmc_overflows(tcx.sess, func, results, context.deadline, rows);
        result_bindings.extend((result_count_before_bmc_reduction..results.len()).map(|_| None));
        revalidation_eligibilities
            .extend((result_count_before_bmc_reduction..results.len()).map(|_| None));
        // Trust (`[unreach]`/`[divzero]`): the legacy/full-verification path also gets
        // the structural propositional-UNSAT discharge — an exhaustive `Unreachable`
        // trap or a guarded division whose violation reduces to a trivial Boolean/
        // equality contradiction ay cannot strict-prove (a guarded division lowers here
        // with a bool-temp-indirected `[divzero]` formula). Mirrors the default-lane
        // call in `collect_verification_artifacts_inner_with_policy`.
        // Trust (verifier-perf, 2026-08-31): a structural search cut short by its work
        // bound used to be indistinguishable from one that completed and found nothing —
        // both were a bare `false` — so a fan-out blowup in the case-split degraded with
        // NOTHING SAID, which is how it reached a production stack sample before anyone
        // knew it was there. A non-zero count is now a note.
        let structural_unsat_declined =
            promote_structurally_dead_unreachable(results, context.deadline);
        if structural_unsat_declined > 0 {
            tcx.sess.dcx().note(format!(
                "Trust structural propositional-UNSAT: {structural_unsat_declined} obligation(s) \
                 declined UNDECIDED — the deterministic case-split work bound was reached \
                 before the search completed, so no structural discharge was attempted for \
                 them; each keeps the verdict its solver gave it. This is a completeness limit \
                 of the shortcut, never a refutation, and it is reported rather than silent so \
                 a fan-out blowup in the resolution search shows up as a number instead of as \
                 wall-clock time"
            ));
        }
        // Trust: promote an obligation the native typed-CHC (trust-mc PDR) path left
        // Unknown/Unsupported that the clean CIC kernel PROVES directly (e.g. a BV-modeled
        // i128 guarded add whose bounded result-range fits the type — trust-mc leaves the
        // bounded 128-bit sign-bit overflow test Unsupported, but trust-certify's
        // `conjuncts_carry_bv_overflow_safe` refutes it). `certify_vc` only runs on
        // already-`Proved` obligations downstream, so this is the seam that lets the kernel
        // rescue such a coverage gap. SOUND/fail-closed (see `promote_kernel_certifiable`).
        //
        // Reuse `context.deadline` — the same per-function
        // wall-clock deadline already threaded into the solver dispatch above
        // (`full_verifier_execution_context`), so the certify grind shares the
        // function's total budget rather than getting an unbounded window of its own.
        // A row the deadline kept from the kernel is a clock stop (the lane records
        // it). This sweep also stands for the structural promotion and the
        // catch-all discharges above: a deadline that cut one of them has passed
        // before this lane starts.
        //
        // Trust: 2026-10-02 (review of the referent-fold fix) — a separate, later
        // window for these lanes (`authority_phase_deadline`) was tried and withdrawn
        // before it was built: the AMBIENT budget installed at function entry
        // (`VerifyBudgetGuard::install(function_deadline, ..)`) keeps the function's
        // deadline, so any budget poll reached from the lanes would still trip on it, and the
        // window roughly doubled a function's worst-case wall time. Which phase may
        // spend the function's budget is a resource-policy decision, taken 2026-10-17
        // (DECISION_QUEUE DEADLINE-SHARE-1): the native lane before this point stops at
        // `native_lane_cutoff`, half of what was left when it started, and these lanes keep
        // the function's own deadline, so they always have at least the other half.
        promote_kernel_certifiable(results, context.deadline, &mut clock_stops);
        let panic_grounding = ground_panic_freedom_catchall(
            results,
            Some(&mut result_bindings),
            context.deadline,
            every_catchall_panic_source_has_a_site_row(func),
        );
        if let Some(grounding) = &panic_grounding {
            grounded_catchalls.push(grounding.premises.clone());
            if let Some(id) = &grounding.public_obligation_id {
                record_grounded_panic_freedom_obligation(tcx.sess, id, grounding.row.clone());
            }
        }
        // Trust (P0 OOM native-route gap): the native typed-CHC path can report a
        // forced over-budget bulk allocation `Proved` SAFE; refute it structurally so
        // the native route matches the structural VC route (which `Failed` it). SOUND: fires
        // ONLY when the count folds to a ground constant >= the budget ceiling.
        refute_forced_unbounded_allocations(results);
        // Trust (SOUNDNESS — surviving-mutant fix): a fail-closed Assertion FINDING
        // carries an always-SAT GROUND violation (`Formula::Bool(true)`) — the
        // `[unsafe:unmodeled-call]` and non-modeled-unsafe-op demands, "always a
        // finding: no model of this construct's effects". SAT is trivially witnessed,
        // so no sound prover may discharge it — but the native typed-CHC lane cannot
        // encode a bare ground assertion and defaults it to Proved, which laundered an
        // undocumented-unsafe call into a false PROVE (`undocumented_unsafe_sig_call`
        // surviving mutant). Force it Failed structurally, exactly as
        // `refute_forced_unbounded_allocations` locks the OOM route to the structural
        // verdict. Fail-closed: a `Proved` verdict is overridden ONLY for a ground
        // `Bool(_)` violation — `Bool(true)` because it is unprovable by construction,
        // `Bool(false)` because a proof of the reconstruction placeholder is vacuous
        // (see the pass doc for why the two classes carry different labels); a
        // discharged unsafe obligation carrying a REAL formula — the sep-engine
        // null / in-bounds / alignment / `[unsafe:sep:backing]` ESTABLISH lanes —
        // keeps its proof, so this only ADDS refutations.
        refute_unsafe_demand_findings(results);
        panic_grounding
    });
    // Trust #540 (R1): apply whole-program caller-propagation verdict flips on the FULL
    // strict path too — same sound helper as the boundary-policy seam, EXTENDED with the
    // per-row native flip keys: on this lane `results` rows are native transport rows
    // whose reconstructed placeholder VC kind (`unsupported MIR \`FullVerification::…\``)
    // can never match the sealed certificate's exact kind key, so the isolated duplicate
    // of an R1-covered obligation — honestly REFUTED by the isolated typed-CHC/PDR
    // re-check — would otherwise survive as an independent refutation beside the sealed
    // whole-program discharge. See `apply_r1_flips_with_native_row_keys` for the
    // soundness argument and fail-closed boundaries.
    // Apply R1 on the private result carrier. Native/public summary and evidence
    // remain unchanged; strict compiler consumers use the exact row authority.
    let r1_flip_native_keys =
        r1_native_transport_flip_keys(&live_full_result, &result_bindings, &all_vcs);
    // Trust (T3 L4, C07r): a verdict-only writer, on the carrier's `set_result`.
    let r1_flipped = apply_r1_flips_with_native_row_keys(
        tcx.sess,
        func.def_path.as_str(),
        &mut results,
        &r1_flip_native_keys,
    );
    let mut full_result = live_full_result.clone();

    // Generic structural/kernel promotions run over all legacy rows and do not
    // know that an exact source marker's `Bool(false)` is only a catalog
    // placeholder. Restore the marker boundary before any evidence publication
    // or certification step can observe a synthetic Proved label.
    // Trust (T3 L4, C07r): a list writer on the carrier, so a rewrite of it.
    results.rewrite(rows, |results, _| {
        keep_exact_source_clause_markers_pending(&bundle, results, &result_bindings);
    });
    // Kernel-certify and perform the mandatory fresh S1 replay before publishing
    // any bridge evidence into the public run. A producer-side LRAT/check report
    // is useful attribution, but it may change public status only after the
    // compiler gate independently re-solves this exact private row and mints a
    // `SolverRevalidated` authority. A replay miss, timeout, carrier skew, or
    // kernel-only proof therefore leaves the original public evidence/summary
    // untouched while the private strict result remains fail-closed.
    // Both lanes record the rows a clock kept from them (determinism plan §3.7).
    // Trust (TINV step 10.4): this body's type-invariant disclosure (the installed use
    // set and its generation record). Empty until step 10.5 installs a set.
    let tinv_disclosure = type_invariant::TinvRowDisclosure::for_generation(tcx.sess, tinv_record);
    let (cleancic, mut proof_authorities) = certify_and_revalidate_full_lane_rows(
        &results,
        &result_bindings,
        Some(&full_result),
        panic_grounding,
        context.deadline,
        &mut clock_stops,
        &tinv_disclosure,
        tcx.sess.opts.unstable_opts.trust_proof_artifact_root.as_deref(),
        Some((&bundle, &context, &mut early_kernel_replays)),
        |cleancic| {
            revalidate_all_solver_proofs(
                &results,
                &revalidation_eligibilities,
                cleancic,
                context.deadline,
                effort_scale(tcx.sess),
            )
        },
    );
    // Trust (hardened evidence gate): the bridge substituted honest ay-in-process
    // proofs into `results`, but the run result still carried NO per-obligation
    // evidence for them — so downstream transport rows surfaced Proved with no
    // publishable proof evidence and the hardened evidence gate failed 0/N on
    // every ay-discharged obligation. Append the bridge lane's own evidence:
    // status Proved, an exact owner-bound input -> LRAT transcript -> strict
    // check-report DAG, and the obligation's typed native-TrustIr binding
    // artifacts recomputed from the bundle we hold. Emission is
    // fail-closed per obligation: any missing, oversized, or invalid artifact
    // byte or binding skips that obligation (its row keeps failing the gate) —
    // evidence is never fabricated. Keep the original native run internally
    // valid until the helper transactionally replaces the exact non-definitive
    // evidence, recomputes summary/status from that final typed carrier, and
    // rolls back on any structural or derived-state mismatch. Private kernel,
    // structural, assertion-refutation, and R1 authorities deliberately leave
    // the public native run unchanged; strict compiler consumers use their exact
    // private result-row capabilities instead of fabricated public counters.
    if let Err(error) = append_bridge_lane_full_verification_evidence(
        &mut full_result,
        native_trust_ir_bundle.as_ref().ok().and_then(|bundle| bundle.as_ref()),
        &results,
        &result_bindings,
        &proof_authorities,
    ) {
        debug!("Trust: bridge evidence publication for {} failed closed: {}", func.def_path, error,);
    }
    // Live direct TrustVC authority is minted only now, after the final bridge
    // publication transaction has committed or rolled back. The opaque router
    // receipt is consumed exactly once against the exact Ownership row and
    // final accepted composite evidence. Public/cache state cannot recreate it.
    let direct_trust_vc_report = install_direct_trust_vc_live_authorities(
        &bundle,
        &dispatched_obligations,
        &context,
        &full_result,
        live_receipts.as_mut(),
        &results,
        &result_bindings,
        &mut proof_authorities,
    );
    record_live_mint_clock_stops(
        &mut clock_stops,
        &results,
        &result_bindings,
        &direct_trust_vc_report.clock_rejected,
    );
    if !direct_trust_vc_report.rejected.is_empty() {
        debug!(
            "Trust: direct TrustVC live authority for {} minted {} and rejected: {}",
            func.def_path,
            direct_trust_vc_report.minted,
            direct_trust_vc_report.rejected.join("; "),
        );
    }
    // S3 authority is minted at the same final boundary. Its affine router
    // sidecars are consumed exactly once and checked against this final public
    // carrier; neither pass-one artifacts nor serialized/cache state can
    // recreate them.
    let fresh_exact_direct_report = install_fresh_exact_direct_chc_pdr_authorities(
        &bundle,
        &dispatched_obligations,
        &context,
        &full_result,
        live_receipts.as_mut(),
        &results,
        &result_bindings,
        &mut proof_authorities,
    );
    record_live_mint_clock_stops(
        &mut clock_stops,
        &results,
        &result_bindings,
        &fresh_exact_direct_report.clock_rejected,
    );
    if !fresh_exact_direct_report.rejected.is_empty() {
        debug!(
            "Trust: fresh exact-direct CHC/PDR authority for {} minted {} and rejected: {}",
            func.def_path,
            fresh_exact_direct_report.minted,
            fresh_exact_direct_report.rejected.join("; "),
        );
    }
    // Whole-function safety has its own affine family. Consumption rechecks
    // current live source authentication and freezes the complete native
    // snapshot; no standalone formula or sibling proof is inferred.
    let fresh_whole_function_report = install_fresh_whole_function_chc_pdr_authorities(
        func,
        &bundle,
        &dispatched_obligations,
        native_trust_ir_bundle.as_ref().ok().and_then(|bundle| bundle.as_ref()),
        source_generation_authority.as_ref(),
        &context,
        &full_result,
        live_receipts.as_mut(),
        &results,
        &result_bindings,
        &mut proof_authorities,
    );
    record_live_mint_clock_stops(
        &mut clock_stops,
        &results,
        &result_bindings,
        &fresh_whole_function_report.clock_rejected,
    );
    if !fresh_whole_function_report.rejected.is_empty() {
        debug!(
            "Trust: fresh whole-function CHC/PDR authority for {} minted {} and rejected: {}",
            func.def_path,
            fresh_whole_function_report.minted,
            fresh_whole_function_report.rejected.join("; "),
        );
    }
    if env_flag_enabled("TRUST_BRIDGE_DEBUG") {
        let summary = &full_result.summary;
        eprintln!(
            "TRUST_BRIDGE: canonical public summary -> requested={} proved={} failed={} unknown={} unsupported={} skipped={}",
            summary.requested_obligations,
            summary.proved,
            summary.failed,
            summary.unknown,
            summary.unsupported,
            summary.skipped,
        );
    }
    // INTERPROCEDURAL panic-freedom registry. When EVERY obligation of this function
    // discharged to `Proved` (no unknown / failed / timed-out / runtime-checked gap),
    // the function is statically panic-free for ALL inputs — including its
    // whole-function panic-freedom catch-all when present. Register its `def_path` so a
    // later-verified CALLER can discharge its own catch-all via
    // `all_calls_target_proven_panic_free`. Functions are verified callee-first
    // (definition order), so this is populated before any caller is processed.
    // Non-empty guard: a zero-obligation result is a coverage gap, not a proof.
    // A definition-site `requires` discharge is an ASSUMPTION, however: the body
    // is panic-free only for callers that establish that precondition, not for
    // all inputs. Such a conditional result must never enter this unconditional
    // registry (doing so let an unrelated caller suppress its panic obligation).
    // Source markers are deliberately absent from the base authority pass.
    // Derive them only now, after bridge evidence has been published and every
    // body row has either received an exact private Clean-kernel capability or
    // remained untrusted. This is the sole phase that may rewrite
    // a recognized marker from Unknown to Proved.
    // Trust (T3 L4, C07r): a list writer on the carrier, so a rewrite of it.
    results.rewrite(rows, |results, _| {
        apply_exact_source_clause_discharges(
            &bundle,
            &full_result,
            &all_vcs,
            &fresh_vc_rekey_snapshot,
            results,
            &result_bindings,
            &mut proof_authorities,
        );
        source_clause_certificate::attach(
            tcx,
            body,
            func,
            compiler_contracts,
            &bundle,
            source_question_bundle.as_ref(),
            &all_vcs,
            results,
            &result_bindings,
            &mut proof_authorities,
        );
        native_wp_source::apply_authorities(
            &bundle,
            native_trust_ir_bundle.as_ref().ok().and_then(|bundle| bundle.as_ref()),
            &full_result,
            live_receipts.as_ref().map(|receipts| receipts.checked_native_wp_proofs()),
            results,
            &result_bindings,
            &mut proof_authorities,
        );
        source_body_decline::apply(
            &bundle,
            &live_full_result,
            &full_result,
            &all_vcs,
            &fresh_vc_rekey_snapshot,
            results,
            &result_bindings,
            &proof_authorities,
        );
    });
    // R1 cannot participate in source-clause or E4/E5 derivations. Install its
    // exact private row authority only after those lanes have finished
    // rebuilding the selected carrier.
    install_r1_flip_authorities(&r1_flipped, &results, &mut proof_authorities);
    // Trust: E9 §5d — in-walk ensures-citation discharge (bounded fragment). In
    // this pipeline it runs LAST in the authority phase — after the
    // source-marker lanes (whose pending-reset must not undo a kernel
    // discharge) and after the R1 install — because a citation discharge
    // OUTRANKS every one of them (kernel-certified clause proof): the sealed
    // citation authority is installed over any earlier authority for the same
    // row and nothing may overwrite it afterwards. A solver REFUTATION or
    // Timeout is never touched (`discharge_ensures_citations_in_walk` fails
    // closed on any non-UNKNOWN/non-Proved entry, and discharges nothing
    // unless EVERY ensures clause of the function Certified-grades).
    let mut mixed_cited_part: Option<(String, Option<trust_certify::clean_island::RequiresLink>)> =
        None;
    // Trust: E9 evidence — the lane that discharges also mints the
    // kernel-audited `CleanCic` certificate for the surface (or leaves `None`).
    // On a MIXED surface the cited lane discharges nothing and instead hands
    // its GRADED cited clauses to the defeq lane, which mints one certificate
    // over the complete conjunction the rows actually rest on.
    let mut ensures_certificate = None;
    let mut graded_cited_clauses: Vec<GradedEnsuresClause> = Vec::new();
    // Trust (OR-A1 H2): the rows that already carry a static proof, read from
    // the authority vector BEFORE either E9 lane writes a row, so a conditional
    // credit can never replace one (and never demote a `KernelCertified` row).
    let static_proved_rows =
        trust_rows_with_static_proof(&results, &result_bindings, &proof_authorities);
    // Trust (T3 L4, C07r): both E9 lanes are list writers on the carrier, so one rewrite of
    // it, which hands back the rows they discharged.
    let discharged_citations = results.rewrite(rows, |results, _| {
        let mut discharged_citations = discharge_ensures_citations_in_walk(
            tcx,
            body,
            func,
            results,
            &mut mixed_cited_part,
            &static_proved_rows,
            &mut graded_cited_clauses,
            &mut ensures_certificate,
        );
        // R4 §1: the composed defeq lane runs only when the cited lane discharged
        // nothing. Trust: on an all-uncited surface it owns every clause. On a
        // MIXED surface it runs only with the cited lane's handoff — every cited
        // clause Certified — and discharges only if every UNCITED clause
        // kernel-discharges by island defeq too, so the function's whole
        // postcondition conjunction is kernel-proven. Anything less discharges
        // nothing, as before.
        if discharged_citations.is_empty() {
            discharged_citations.extend(discharge_uncited_island_ensures_in_walk(
                tcx,
                body,
                func,
                results,
                mixed_cited_part.as_ref().map(|(theorems, link)| (theorems.as_str(), *link)),
                &static_proved_rows,
                &graded_cited_clauses,
                &mut ensures_certificate,
            ));
        }
        discharged_citations
    });
    // Summary-partition semantics: a discharged `SpecEnsuresUnparseable`
    // sentinel deliberately stays `unsupported` in the PUBLIC native run —
    // private authorities leave public counters unchanged (rewriting them here
    // would also invalidate the sealed live-receipt final-run seals minted
    // above). Strict compiler consumers read the sealed row authority instead.
    for (index, theorem, requires_link) in &discharged_citations {
        if let Some(row) = exact_result_row_identity(*index, &results[*index].0) {
            // Trust (ref-self I1): the disclosure that demotes this row below
            // `Certified` when its goal rests on the parameter view. Read off
            // the SAME `LocalDefId` the discharge lanes read (a non-local body
            // carries no contract, so the `map_or` arm is unreachable — it is
            // spelled as `None` rather than left implicit so the fail-open
            // direction is never the default).
            // Trust (OR-A1 H1): and the RUNG the lane credited, carried as data
            // from the grade — a conditional discharge is never sealed as the
            // `Certified` variant.
            // KR0 switch: `None` when the reference entry did not accept the
            // surface's judgment; the slot then holds no authority.
            proof_authorities[*index] = trust_ensures_discharge_authority(
                body.source.def_id().as_local().and_then(|local| trust_e6_view_marker(tcx, local)),
                *requires_link,
                row,
                theorem.clone(),
                // Trust: bind the surface certificate to THIS row's identity.
                ensures_certificate
                    .as_ref()
                    .and_then(|certificate| certificate.evidence_for_row(&results[*index].0)),
            );
            let unminted = proof_authorities[*index].is_none();
            if unminted && std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some() {
                eprintln!("TRUST_E9_DEBUG NO-REFERENCE-CERTIFICATE index={index}");
            }
            if std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some() {
                eprintln!("TRUST_E9_DEBUG AUTHORITY-INSTALLED index={index}");
            }
        } else if std::env::var_os("TRUST_E9_DEBUG").is_some() {
            eprintln!("TRUST_E9_DEBUG AUTHORITY-ROW-NONE index={index}");
        }
    }
    // Trust (interprocedural panic-freedom closure): seal the pf catch-all row(s)
    // that the `all_calls_discharge_panic_free` seam discharged with a bare
    // `Proved` and no authority. Runs after every real lane
    // (kernel/native/source/body-bound/R1/E9) has had its chance to claim the
    // row — so the `None`-slot guard installs only where no independent proof
    // exists, and the sibling-unconditionality gate makes the single pf row
    // carrying this authority all-input panic-free in isolation.
    // Trust (TINV step 10.4, TINV-26): every lane has claimed its rows; strip every
    // authority a type-invariant premise cannot be disclosed through, BEFORE the
    // callee closure and the grounded premise floor read the authorities as premises.
    // A body carrying a premise is recorded (TINV-27: no registry, no elision).
    let tinv_stripped =
        cap_type_invariant_authorities(&results, &mut proof_authorities, &tinv_disclosure);
    if tinv_disclosure.body_carries_premise() {
        record_tinv_premised_verdict(
            tcx.sess,
            body.source.def_id(),
            &func.def_path,
            &proof_authorities,
        );
    }
    if tinv_stripped > 0 {
        debug!(
            "Trust: {tinv_stripped} authorit(ies) of {} stripped by the type-invariant cap",
            func.def_path
        );
    }
    // Trust (VF5): a "proved (calculus)" certificate for this function claims
    // the L0 rows no lane above claimed, after the type-invariant cap (it covers
    // no tagged row) and before the callee closure and the premise floor (which
    // read it as a conditional premise).
    if let Some((symbol, artifact)) = calculus_row_binding_of_body(tcx, body) {
        let minted = install_calculus_row_authorities(
            &symbol,
            &artifact,
            tcx.sess.overflow_checks(),
            &results,
            &mut proof_authorities,
        );
        for index in &minted {
            results.set_result(*index, calculus_row_result());
        }
        if !minted.is_empty() {
            debug!("Trust: {} calculus row(s) of {} (VF5)", minted.len(), func.def_path);
        }
    }
    install_interprocedural_callee_closure_authorities(
        tcx.sess,
        func,
        &results,
        &result_bindings,
        &mut proof_authorities,
    );
    // Trust (grounded catch-all premise floor, 2026-09-24): LAST in the
    // authority phase. A grounded catch-all's `KernelCertified` token re-checks
    // the residual disjunction its rewrite installed, and that disjunction is
    // the faithful residual of whole-function panic-freedom only under the
    // sibling `Proved` LABELS the rewrite read before any authority existed
    // (and, on the refute lane, the calls its gate discharged or never
    // examined). Now that every lane has claimed its rows, grade each grounded
    // catch-all by the weakest premise it stands on — never higher. Measured
    // before this pass: see
    // `tests/ui/trust/grounded_catchall_inherits_weakest_premise.rs`.
    let lowered_catchalls = cap_grounded_panic_freedom_authorities(
        &results,
        &result_bindings,
        &mut proof_authorities,
        Some(&full_result),
        &grounded_catchalls,
    );
    if lowered_catchalls > 0 {
        debug!(
            "Trust: {} grounded panic-freedom catch-all(s) of {} graded below Certified by their \
             weakest premise",
            lowered_catchalls, func.def_path,
        );
    }
    // Trust (2026-10-08, argument premise; R1's rule): LAST in the authority
    // phase, after every lane has spent its `KernelCertified` premises within this
    // body: a row whose VC rested on an unproved premise about the body's
    // reference arguments reports no `Certified` grade. Graded per row since
    // 2026-10-17: a token whose certified VC the premise-free generation also
    // produces keeps its grade (`argument_premise` module doc, # Grading).
    if let Some(premise) = argument_premise::demotion_premise(tcx.sess, body.source.def_id()) {
        let (kept, demoted) = demote_argument_premise_authorities(
            &mut proof_authorities,
            premise,
            premise_free,
            &grounded_catchalls,
        );
        debug!(
            "Trust: argument premise {premise} of {}: {kept} kernel token(s) certify a VC the \
             premise-free generation also produces (kept), {demoted} demoted",
            func.def_path,
        );
    }
    // NOTE (upstream 2026-08-14, kept on merge): the whole-function panic-freedom
    // STRUCTURAL SUBSUMPTION ran here, last in the authority phase, deriving the
    // synthesized `assertion:panic-freedom:0` aggregate from "every other row is a
    // static proof". It was a FALSE PROOF and is gone — the aggregate exists only
    // for panic sources that have NO per-site row, so no conjunction over the
    // siblings can entail it. The aggregate's sound discharge is the per-site one
    // in `refute_full_assert_obligations`, which already ran upstream.
    //
    // MERGE (b62 <- origin/main; squash d24c671211): our side had GUARDED that same lane (false
    // proof #12: a non-empty premise set plus a call-discharge conjunct) rather than
    // removing it. Upstream's removal is strictly stronger — it showed premise (1)
    // is false BY CONSTRUCTION, so no guard can rescue the lane — and upstream's
    // removal is what this merge keeps. The interprocedural callee-closure seal
    // above is a DIFFERENT authority (it discharges a pf row from registered
    // panic-free callees, not from sibling rows) and survives.
    // Trust: 2026-09-19 — GROUND every refuted row's counterexample against THIS
    // function's own definitions before any consumer is built from `results`.
    //
    // A refutation is delivered with a model of the row's VIOLATION FORMULA, and
    // that formula can be weaker than the program: the signed-multiply core is a
    // QF_BV query over FRESH `__trust_ovf_bv_*` operand vars, disjoint from the
    // Int-sorted guard rendering, and `trust_router::disjoint_components` composes
    // the two components' models into one witness. When the guard bound cannot be
    // mined exactly, nothing constrains either half, and the composed witness
    // contradicted the program — measured on this tree: `lim = 1` for a `lim` the
    // entry block defines as 4294967295, beside `a = 0, b = 0` for a MULTIPLY
    // overflow. The verdict was right and the witness was impossible.
    //
    // `ground_delivered_counterexamples` replays each model through the function
    // and rewrites ONLY the counterexample of a `Failed` row: to a repaired,
    // replayable input assignment where one exists, and otherwise to a single
    // explicit `UNGROUNDED` entry naming the contradiction and the fix (v7/D3).
    // It never moves a verdict — see `trust_vcgen::counterexample_grounding`.
    //
    // Placed HERE, before `build_proof_results` and `build_transport_results_bound`,
    // so the human diagnostic and the JSON transport carry the SAME witness; a
    // grounding applied at render time would leave the two surfaces disagreeing
    // about the same row.
    //
    // Determinism plan §3.7 (2026-10-01): LAST in the authority phase, with
    // every lane done, publish each row a clock left unproved as a timeout —
    // `timed_out` with the `wall-backstop` stop cause on every surface built
    // below — instead of a silent `unknown` with no cause and `0ms`.
    //
    // Trust (T3 L4, C07r): both are list writers on the carrier, so one rewrite of it.
    let clock_stopped = results.rewrite(rows, |results, _| {
        trust_vcgen::counterexample_grounding::ground_delivered_counterexamples(&func, results);
        stamp_clock_stopped_rows(
            results,
            &proof_authorities,
            &clock_stops,
            tcx.sess.opts.unstable_opts.trust_verify_function_budget_ms,
        )
    });
    if clock_stopped > 0 {
        debug!(
            "Trust: {} obligation(s) of {} left unproved by a clock are published as timeouts",
            clock_stopped, func.def_path,
        );
    }
    let proof_results = build_proof_results(
        tcx.sess,
        &results,
        &[],
        &result_bindings,
        &proof_authorities,
        Some(&func),
    );
    let telemetry = build_proof_telemetry(tcx.sess, &results, &result_bindings, &proof_authorities);
    let transport_results = build_transport_results_bound(
        tcx.sess,
        &results,
        Some(&full_result),
        &cleancic,
        &result_bindings,
        &proof_authorities,
    );

    // Trust (T3 L4, C07r; L4-C07p-2): the body's context, which holds this run's ids, is still
    // being written (the E5 rerun writes into it too), so the run's artifacts leave here
    // unfrozen; `collect_full_verification_artifacts` freezes the context into the run it
    // selects (`UnfrozenArtifacts::freeze`).
    UnfrozenArtifacts::new(VerificationArtifacts {
        proof_results,
        telemetry,
        // Trust (T3 L4, C07r): the carrier every writer above worked on, each row on its id
        // in the body's context.
        results,
        transport_results,
        full_verification: Some(full_result),
        expanded_function_identity: body.source.def_id().as_local().and_then(|owner| {
            expanded_function_identity::capture(tcx, owner, func, compiler_contracts)
        }),
        result_bindings,
        proof_authorities,
        overflow_check_origins: overflow_check_origins.clone(),
        coroutine_resume_contract,
        // Trust (D9): the level the caller's `filter_vcs_by_level` applied.
        max_proof_level: Some(max_level),
        // Trust (2026-09-29): the debit the native bundle's Assert gate recorded,
        // carried whether or not that bundle survived (see the builder).
        runtime_checked_std_members,
        // Replaced at the freeze; `UnfrozenArtifacts` exposes no context.
        calculus_reporting: calculus_pending::capture_inputs(
            trust_types::verify_budget::current_deadline(),
            matches!(&native_trust_ir_bundle, Ok(Some(_))),
        ),
        ctx: Arc::new(trust_types::vc_context::VcContext::new()),
    })
}

/// Whether every row has a real static proof capability. Empty rows satisfy
/// this universal predicate; callers that require non-vacuity add that guard.
fn all_results_have_static_proof_authority(
    results: &[(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)],
    result_bindings: &[Option<ResultObligationBinding>],
    proof_authorities: &[Option<ResultProofAuthority>],
) -> bool {
    (result_bindings.is_empty() || results.len() == result_bindings.len())
        && results.len() == proof_authorities.len()
        && results.iter().zip(proof_authorities).enumerate().all(
            |(index, ((vc, result), authority))| {
                result.is_proved()
                    && authority.as_ref().is_some_and(|authority| {
                        authority.is_static_proof_for(
                            index,
                            vc,
                            result,
                            result_bindings.get(index).and_then(Option::as_ref),
                        )
                    })
            },
        )
}

/// Trust (Part B — conditional panic-freedom registration). Returns the callee
/// summary to record in `panic_free_under_precondition` iff `func` (a
/// `#[trust::requires(P)]` function) is proven panic-free UNDER `P`:
///   * the whole-function panic-freedom catch-all was discharged by the Part-B
///     under-precondition seam (the DISTINCT `trust-assert-refute-under-precondition`
///     tag), so it can never launder into the unconditional registry;
///   * every OTHER row is accounted for WITHOUT any open PANIC obligation —
///     a functional `Postcondition` (permitted open), the assumed-`P` entry
///     assumption(s), or a proved UNCONDITIONAL static proof (every modeled panic
///     site). An unproved panic site, an `assume_total` callee assumption, or a
///     `CallerPropagation` token fails closed.
/// SOUNDNESS: this NEVER registers into the unconditional `panic_free_proven_funcs`
/// (that is `results_establish_unconditional_panic_freedom`, guarded by the
/// entry-assumption firewall). A member of the conditional registry only ever
/// discharges a caller whose per-call-site proof of `P[args]` holds.
fn results_establish_panic_freedom_under_precondition(
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    has_declared_requires: bool,
    results: &[(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)],
    result_bindings: &[Option<ResultObligationBinding>],
    proof_authorities: &[Option<ResultProofAuthority>],
) -> bool {
    // Only a fn with a DECLARED `#[trust::requires]` contract is a candidate; a
    // function with no declared entry assumption is handled by the UNCONDITIONAL
    // registry (N1: synthetic type-range preconditions never route here).
    if !has_declared_requires {
        return false;
    }
    if results.is_empty() || results.len() != proof_authorities.len() {
        return false;
    }
    if !result_bindings.is_empty() && results.len() != result_bindings.len() {
        return false;
    }
    let is_panic_freedom = is_panic_freedom_assertion_vc;
    // Both authenticated catch-all shapes: the vcgen/rekeyed Assertion row and
    // the full-lane transport restatement (identity sealed by the row binding's
    // compiler-held canonical obligation — recomputed id/marker/digests).
    let is_pf_row = |i: usize, vc: &VerificationCondition| {
        is_panic_freedom(vc)
            || is_full_lane_panic_freedom_restatement_row(
                func,
                i,
                vc,
                result_bindings.get(i).and_then(Option::as_ref),
            )
    };
    let is_under_pre_pf_discharge =
        |i: usize, vc: &VerificationCondition, r: &VerificationResult| {
            is_pf_row(i, vc)
                && matches!(r, VerificationResult::Proved { solver, .. }
                if solver.as_str() == "trust-assert-refute-under-precondition")
        };
    // The whole-function panic-freedom catch-all must have been discharged by the
    // Part-B under-precondition seam (distinct tag).
    if !results.iter().enumerate().any(|(i, (vc, r))| is_under_pre_pf_discharge(i, vc, r)) {
        return false;
    }
    // No `assume_total` callee assumption or `CallerPropagation` token may be
    // present — those are DIFFERENT, non-registerable forms of conditionality.
    if results.iter().any(|(_, r)| is_assumed_total_discharge(r)) {
        return false;
    }
    if proof_authorities.iter().any(|auth| {
        matches!(
            auth,
            Some(
                ResultProofAuthority::AssumedTotalCalleeAssumption { .. }
                    | ResultProofAuthority::CallerPropagation { .. }
                    // Trust (TINV step 10.4, TINV-27): conditional on a type-invariant
                    // certificate; never a registry premise.
                    | ResultProofAuthority::TypeInvariantComposed { .. }
                    // Trust (merge 2026-10-14): nor a row resting on an argument
                    // premise (D14/D15).
                    | ResultProofAuthority::ArgumentPremiseKernelDischarge { .. }
                    // Trust (VF5): nor a calculus row (closed-world entry premise).
                    | ResultProofAuthority::CalculusRowProved { .. }
            )
        )
    }) {
        return false;
    }
    // Every row must be one of: a functional `Postcondition` (permitted open),
    // this function's own `#[requires]` entry assumption(s), the under-precondition
    // pf discharge, or a proved UNCONDITIONAL static proof (every modeled panic
    // site). Anything else — crucially an UNPROVED panic site — fails closed.
    results.iter().zip(proof_authorities).enumerate().all(|(i, ((vc, r), auth))| {
        matches!(vc.kind, VcKind::Postcondition)
            || is_entry_assumption_discharge(r)
            || is_under_pre_pf_discharge(i, vc, r)
            || (r.is_proved()
                && auth.as_ref().is_some_and(|a| {
                    a.is_unconditional_static_proof_for(
                        i,
                        vc,
                        r,
                        result_bindings.get(i).and_then(Option::as_ref),
                    )
                }))
    })
}

/// Build the callee summary recorded in `panic_free_under_precondition`: the
/// DECLARED `#[trust::requires]` clauses `P` plus the param names/types a caller
/// substitutes its actual arguments into when rendering and PROVING `P[args]` at
/// each call site.
///
/// N1: `P` comes from `extract_contract_formulas_for_instance` — the declared
/// contract clauses ONLY, exactly the entry assumptions the callee's pf proof
/// relied on (its `DefinitionEntryAssumption` rows). The synthetic type-range
/// tautologies in `func.preconditions` are deliberately excluded: they are not
/// entry assumptions, and including them would only add render-failure surface.
/// FAIL-CLOSED alignment: the extractor never silently drops a declared requires
/// clause — an unparseable/unbindable clause becomes `Formula::Bool(false)`, an
/// impossible caller obligation, so a caller can never discharge a call to a
/// callee whose assumed contract could not be faithfully rendered. Returns
/// `None` (register nothing) when extraction yields ZERO requires clauses for a
/// declared-requires fn — a summary with an empty `P` would make Part-C's
/// callsite pass emit no obligation and vacuously discharge (fail closed here
/// instead).
fn panic_free_under_precondition_summary<'tcx>(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>,
    def_path: &str,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    body: &Body<'tcx>,
) -> Option<trust_vcgen::FunctionSummary> {
    let def_id = body.source.def_id();
    let args = ty::GenericArgs::identity_for_item(tcx, def_id);
    let (declared_requires, _postconditions) =
        trust_mir_extract::extract_contract_formulas_for_instance(tcx, def_id, args);
    if declared_requires.is_empty() {
        return None;
    }
    let summary = trust_vcgen::FunctionSummary::new(def_path.to_string())
        .with_param_names(mir_param_names(body))
        .with_param_types(mir_param_types_from_vf(func));
    Some(declared_requires.into_iter().fold(summary, |summary, p| summary.with_precondition(p)))
}

/// Whether result rows establish panic-freedom without relying on a function
/// entry assumption. A proved body under `requires(P)` is conditional on `P`;
/// it cannot authorize the global, all-input callee registry.
fn results_establish_unconditional_panic_freedom(
    results: &[(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)],
    result_bindings: &[Option<ResultObligationBinding>],
    proof_authorities: &[Option<ResultProofAuthority>],
) -> bool {
    // Trust (Part A — panic-freedom / functional-correctness decoupling).
    // Panic-freedom is INDEPENDENT of functional correctness: an unproved
    // `#[trust::ensures]` postcondition (`VcKind::Postcondition`) is a
    // functional-coverage gap, NOT a reachable panic — a logical assertion about
    // the return value, never executed code that can trap. A function whose every
    // PANIC-SITE obligation is a proved UNCONDITIONAL static proof (arithmetic /
    // shift / div-or-rem-by-zero / bounds / cast / negation overflow,
    // `Unreachable`, user `assert!`/`panic!`, the whole-function panic-freedom
    // catch-all, callee-precondition `Precondition` rows, memory/ownership
    // safety, and EVERY other non-`Postcondition` row) is unconditionally
    // panic-free for ALL inputs, even when a functional `Postcondition` row is
    // left Unknown. This lets a `#[trust::ensures(|_r| true)]` function whose
    // vacuous postcondition the transport necessarily downgrades (its violation
    // folds to a constant-false the vacuity gate rejects, and its source-clause
    // marker carries no clean-CIC / native evidence) still REGISTER its
    // panic-freedom for interprocedural callee discharge.
    //
    // SOUNDNESS: ONLY `VcKind::Postcondition` rows are exempted — the narrowest
    // functional kind. EVERY other row (every modeled panic site AND the
    // panic-freedom catch-all itself) must still be a proved unconditional static
    // proof, so a genuinely-panicking function — one carrying an unproved
    // panic-site or catch-all row — never registers. At least one
    // non-`Postcondition` row must itself be proved, so a function whose only
    // rows are (unproved) postconditions carries zero positive panic evidence and
    // fails closed. The entry-assumption / assumed-total firewall is kept intact,
    // so a `#[trust::requires(P)]` (conditionally panic-free) function still never
    // enters this UNCONDITIONAL registry. NB: the exemption is gated on the VC
    // KIND being `Postcondition`, never on a violation folding to constant-false
    // — which would conflate a benign vacuous postcondition with a
    // contradictory-premise (dead-code) vacuity the project rejects.
    let is_functional_postcondition =
        |vc: &VerificationCondition| matches!(vc.kind, VcKind::Postcondition);
    !results.is_empty()
        && (result_bindings.is_empty() || results.len() == result_bindings.len())
        && results.len() == proof_authorities.len()
        && results.iter().zip(proof_authorities).enumerate().all(
            |(index, ((vc, result), authority))| {
                is_functional_postcondition(vc)
                    || (result.is_proved()
                        && authority.as_ref().is_some_and(|authority| {
                            authority.is_unconditional_static_proof_for(
                                index,
                                vc,
                                result,
                                result_bindings.get(index).and_then(Option::as_ref),
                            )
                        }))
            },
        )
        // Fail-closed positive-evidence guard: never register a function whose
        // ONLY rows are (unproved) functional postconditions. Requiring at least
        // one proved non-postcondition row preserves the original predicate's
        // "every row proved" invariant across the panic-relevant rows.
        && results
            .iter()
            .any(|(vc, result)| !is_functional_postcondition(vc) && result.is_proved())
        && !results.iter().any(|(_, result)| {
            is_entry_assumption_discharge(result) || is_assumed_total_discharge(result)
        })
        && !proof_authorities.iter().any(|authority| {
            matches!(
                authority,
                Some(
                    ResultProofAuthority::DefinitionEntryAssumption { .. }
                        | ResultProofAuthority::AssumedTotalCalleeAssumption { .. }
                        | ResultProofAuthority::CallerPropagation { .. }
                        // Trust (TINV step 10.4, TINV-27): conditional on a
                        // type-invariant certificate; never a registry premise.
                        | ResultProofAuthority::TypeInvariantComposed { .. }
                        // Trust (merge 2026-10-14): nor a row resting on an
                        // argument premise (D14/D15).
                        | ResultProofAuthority::ArgumentPremiseKernelDischarge { .. }
                        // Trust (VF5): nor a calculus row (closed-world entry).
                        | ResultProofAuthority::CalculusRowProved { .. }
                )
            )
        })
}

/// Trust (strict-lane regression, 2026-09-12): the PURE half of the
/// postcondition-only registration lane — every row is a functional
/// `Postcondition` AND every row is a proved UNCONDITIONAL static proof.
///
/// This is exactly the pre-b62 predicate ("every row proved under an
/// unconditional authority") restricted to the shape b62's positive-evidence
/// guard started refusing. That guard was written for a function whose only
/// rows are UNPROVED postconditions ("zero positive panic evidence") — but it
/// keyed on the ROW KIND, not on the exemption having been USED, so a function
/// whose every row is a proved, kernel-certified `ensures` and whose body has no
/// panic site at all (`clamp_upper(v, hi) -> u32 ensures result <= hi { if v >
/// hi { hi } else { v } }` — the fragment's two-call building block) stopped
/// registering. Every caller of such a leaf then grew a `panic freedom` catch-all
/// row it could not discharge. Measured with `TRUST_PF_REG_TRACE=1` on
/// `trust-ratio` under `25ccaf01f` and main's `f25bd2783`:
/// `[PF_REG] fn=trust_ratio::clamp_upper established=false nrows=3` with every
/// row `kind=Postcondition proved=true uncond=true`, then
/// `[PF_CALLGATE] fn=trust_ratio::span_width UNREGISTERED callee blocks
/// discharge: trust_ratio::clamp_upper`. 16 of the 19 reference crates failed
/// on that one shape.
///
/// The exemption itself is untouched: an UNPROVED postcondition still needs a
/// proved panic-site row beside it (`only_unproved_postconditions_fails_closed`).
/// Registration through this lane additionally needs the SESSION conjuncts in
/// `postcondition_only_run_establishes_unconditional_panic_freedom` — this
/// helper alone is not a registration verdict.
fn results_are_only_proved_unconditional_postconditions(
    results: &[(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)],
    result_bindings: &[Option<ResultObligationBinding>],
    proof_authorities: &[Option<ResultProofAuthority>],
) -> bool {
    !results.is_empty()
        && (result_bindings.is_empty() || results.len() == result_bindings.len())
        && results.len() == proof_authorities.len()
        && results.iter().zip(proof_authorities).enumerate().all(
            |(index, ((vc, result), authority))| {
                matches!(vc.kind, VcKind::Postcondition)
                    && result.is_proved()
                    && authority.as_ref().is_some_and(|authority| {
                        authority.is_unconditional_static_proof_for(
                            index,
                            vc,
                            result,
                            result_bindings.get(index).and_then(Option::as_ref),
                        )
                    })
            },
        )
        && !results.iter().any(|(_, result)| {
            is_entry_assumption_discharge(result) || is_assumed_total_discharge(result)
        })
        && !proof_authorities.iter().any(|authority| {
            matches!(
                authority,
                Some(
                    ResultProofAuthority::DefinitionEntryAssumption { .. }
                        | ResultProofAuthority::AssumedTotalCalleeAssumption { .. }
                        | ResultProofAuthority::CallerPropagation { .. }
                        // Trust (TINV step 10.4, TINV-27): conditional on a
                        // type-invariant certificate; never a registry premise.
                        | ResultProofAuthority::TypeInvariantComposed { .. }
                        // Trust (merge 2026-10-14): nor a row resting on an
                        // argument premise (D14/D15).
                        | ResultProofAuthority::ArgumentPremiseKernelDischarge { .. }
                        // Trust (VF5): nor a calculus row (closed-world entry).
                        | ResultProofAuthority::CalculusRowProved { .. }
                )
            )
        })
}

/// Trust (strict-lane regression, 2026-09-12): register a function whose
/// obligation set is NON-empty but whose PANIC-RELEVANT subset is empty — every
/// row is a proved unconditional `Postcondition`
/// (`results_are_only_proved_unconditional_postconditions`).
///
/// Such a function is, for panic-freedom purposes, the zero-obligation case
/// with extra (discharged) functional rows, so it is gated the way
/// `zero_obligation_run_establishes_unconditional_panic_freedom` gates the
/// empty set — NOT the way the pre-b62 predicate did (which asked nothing about
/// calls). Every conjunct there that is meaningful for a non-empty run is
/// required here, for the same reason it is required there:
///
///   * FALSE PROOF #11 — every call (`Terminator::Call` and, Trust NEW-TAILCALL,
///     `Terminator::TailCall`) must target a callee this
///     Session has ALREADY established as unconditionally panic-free
///     (`all_calls_target_proven_panic_free`, the authenticated registries only;
///     cycles fail closed by construction, see the twin's doc). A postcondition
///     row says nothing about a call, and the bridge does not charge a resolved
///     call to an unregistered callee as a counted row in every shape.
///   * no unproved assumption-panic transport row (an absent/extern callee);
///   * the native run's `Proved` authorities validate exactly;
///   * the typed-TrustIr lowering did not fail (#47) — an unmodeled construct
///     is a panic path the row set cannot see.
///
/// `native_run_is_vacuously_clean` is deliberately NOT reused: it asserts
/// `requested_obligations == 0`, which is false here by construction. What it
/// guards beyond that (no failed/unknown/unsupported/skipped rows) is already
/// implied by every row being proved plus the lowering conjunct.
///
/// Monotone-safe: this lane only ADDS registrations for a shape the pre-b62
/// verifier registered with FEWER conjuncts, and each of the 19 strict
/// reference crates' gates was recorded green on that verifier.
fn postcondition_only_run_establishes_unconditional_panic_freedom(
    sess: &Session,
    owner: Option<DefId>,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    artifacts: &VerificationArtifacts,
) -> bool {
    if !results_are_only_proved_unconditional_postconditions(
        &artifacts.results,
        &artifacts.result_bindings,
        &artifacts.proof_authorities,
    ) {
        return false;
    }
    let native_authority_valid = native_proved_authority_validation_failures(
        sess,
        artifacts.full_verification.as_ref(),
        &artifacts.results,
        &artifacts.result_bindings,
        &artifacts.proof_authorities,
    )
    .is_empty();
    let assumption_panic_free = !artifacts_have_unproved_assumption_panic(artifacts);
    let lowering_complete = artifacts
        .full_verification
        .as_ref()
        .is_some_and(|run| !native_run_trust_ir_lowering_failed(run));
    let all_calls_proven_panic_free = all_calls_target_proven_panic_free(sess, owner, func);
    let accepted = native_authority_valid
        && assumption_panic_free
        && lowering_complete
        && all_calls_proven_panic_free;
    if std::env::var_os("TRUST_PF_REG_TRACE").is_some() {
        eprintln!(
            "[PF_REG] fn={} postcondition-only gate: native_authority_valid={} assumption_panic_free={} lowering_complete={} all_calls_proven={} accepted={}",
            func.def_path,
            native_authority_valid,
            assumption_panic_free,
            lowering_complete,
            all_calls_proven_panic_free,
            accepted,
        );
    }
    accepted
}

fn zero_obligation_registry_gate(
    summary_is_empty: bool,
    assumption_panic_free: bool,
    native_authority_valid: bool,
    native_run_vacuously_clean: bool,
    // FALSE PROOF #11 (propagation half). "Zero obligations" is NOT a proof — it
    // is the ABSENCE of a question. Before this conjunct, a function whose body
    // is nothing but `leaf(a, 0)` for a same-crate `leaf` that divides by zero
    // produced an empty obligation set (the bridge's resolved-call arm charged
    // nothing) and this gate read that emptiness as "trivially panic-free",
    // registering it in the UNCONDITIONAL `panic_free_proven_funcs`. Measured:
    //   [PF_REG] fn=fa11::fa_leaf_div       established=false   (correct)
    //   [PF_REG] fn=fa11::fa_caller_div0    established=true  nrows=0  (FALSE)
    //   [PF_REG] fn=fa11::fa_caller_of_cycle established=true nrows=0  (FALSE —
    //       and this is the CALLER of a mutually-recursive pair, so the poison
    //       also bypassed the SCC oracle)
    // Every later consumer of that registry — `all_calls_discharge_panic_free`
    // and `collect_proven_total_panic_free_callees` — then flipped OTHER
    // callers' rows to Proved off a fact that was never established.
    //
    // The fix is to require POSITIVE evidence for the one panic source an empty
    // obligation set structurally cannot cover: the function's calls.
    all_calls_proven_panic_free: bool,
) -> bool {
    summary_is_empty
        && assumption_panic_free
        && native_authority_valid
        && native_run_vacuously_clean
        && all_calls_proven_panic_free
}

/// Register the genuinely empty case accepted by the strict verifier. The
/// nonempty result-row gate above deliberately rejects `[]`; without this twin,
/// a clean leaf function could pass strict verification but never become usable
/// as panic-freedom authority for a caller.
fn zero_obligation_run_establishes_unconditional_panic_freedom<'tcx>(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>,
    body: &Body<'tcx>,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    artifacts: &VerificationArtifacts,
    charged_global_alloc_contract: bool,
) -> bool {
    let sess = tcx.sess;
    let owner = zero_obligation_source_owner(body.source);
    let identity = owner.and_then(|owner| trust_mir_extract::item_identity(tcx, owner));
    let registration_admissible = owner.is_some_and(|owner| {
        panic_free_registration_refusal(tcx, owner).is_none()
    });
    let native_authority_valid = native_proved_authority_validation_failures(
        sess,
        artifacts.full_verification.as_ref(),
        &artifacts.results,
        &artifacts.result_bindings,
        &artifacts.proof_authorities,
    )
    .is_empty();
    // FALSE PROOF #11: every call in this body — `Terminator::Call` and (Trust,
    // NEW-TAILCALL) `Terminator::TailCall`, enumerated by `extracted_call_sites` —
    // must target a callee this Session has ALREADY established as
    // unconditionally panic-free.
    //
    // AUTHENTICATED REGISTRIES ONLY. Ordinary callees need exact
    // `panic_free_proven_identities`; the separate ABI/type-total, bridge and
    // owner-bound typed arms retain their own rules. It does NOT accept
    // `panic_free_under_precondition`: a conditionally-proved callee must never
    // discharge an UNGUARDED call site, and this zero-obligation lane has no
    // call-site precondition VC to discharge it with (that is Path-2b, which
    // lives in `all_calls_discharge_panic_free` and proves `P[args]` per site).
    // A display name never substitutes for an absent ordinary identity.
    //
    // CYCLES FAIL CLOSED BY CONSTRUCTION, and require no separate oracle.
    // Ordinary registration exports an identity only AFTER a function's own
    // verification has completed and `established` came out true; deferred R40
    // candidates use frozen, strictly earlier identity rounds. So for any
    // call cycle A → B → A: A's gate needs B ∈ registry, and B enters the
    // registry only after B's own gate needed A ∈ registry. Neither can be first,
    // so NO cycle member — and no CALLER of a cycle, since a caller's gate needs
    // a cycle member that never registers — can ever be established through this
    // lane, in ANY verification order. That is strictly stronger than a
    // syntactic SCC test: it is order-independent and needs no whole-crate graph.
    //
    // ORDERING (requirement 4): the registry is monotone-append and consulted
    // read-only here. A callee not yet verified is simply absent ⇒ `false` ⇒ this
    // function does not register. Absence of evidence fails closed; a late-
    // arriving callee proof can only ever help a LATER function, never
    // retroactively license this one.
    let all_calls_proven_panic_free = all_calls_target_proven_panic_free(sess, owner, func);
    // The FOUR non-call conjuncts, bound once. R40's blessing candidacy is
    // recorded from EXACTLY these values — the same positively-computed facts the
    // gate below consumes — so the deferred re-decision can never rest on a
    // separate, drift-prone re-derivation of the premises.
    let summary_is_empty = artifacts.proof_results.summary.total == 0;
    let assumption_panic_free = !artifacts_have_unproved_assumption_panic(artifacts);
    let native_run_vacuously_clean =
        artifacts.full_verification.as_ref().is_some_and(native_run_is_vacuously_clean);
    if summary_is_empty {
        if trust_env_var_present("TRUST_PF_REG_TRACE") {
            eprintln!(
                "[PF_REG] fn={} zero-obligation gate: all_calls_proven={}",
                func.def_path, all_calls_proven_panic_free,
            );
        }
        // Record the NEGATIVE finding for the transport half (part C). Written
        // only from this positively-computed result; see the field's doc.
        if !all_calls_proven_panic_free {
            with_crate_verification_state(sess, |state| {
                if let Some(owner) = owner {
                    state.zero_obligation_undischarged_call_funcs.insert(owner);
                }
            });
        }
    }
    // R40: candidacy retains the four non-call conjuncts and the ordinary
    // registry's premise refusal. The fifth
    // (every call discharged) is deliberately left out — re-deciding it against a
    // later, strictly larger registry is the entire point of the fixpoint. Both a
    // pass and a failure are recorded, because a failing walk POISONS the key.
    // Trust (2026-09-16): a body charged the global-alloc contract is
    // CONDITIONAL whatever its calls resolve to, so it is not a candidate — the
    // recorded `false` POISONS the key, and the R40 fixpoint (which registers
    // directly, bypassing the gate below) can never bless it.
    record_zero_obligation_blessing_candidacy(
        sess,
        owner,
        identity,
        func,
        summary_is_empty
            && assumption_panic_free
            && native_authority_valid
            && native_run_vacuously_clean
            && !charged_global_alloc_contract
            && registration_admissible,
    );
    let accepted = zero_obligation_registry_gate(
        summary_is_empty,
        assumption_panic_free,
        native_authority_valid,
        native_run_vacuously_clean,
        all_calls_proven_panic_free,
    ) && !charged_global_alloc_contract;
    if summary_is_empty {
        info!(
            "Trust zero-obligation registry gate: fn={} calls_authenticated={} accepted={}",
            func.def_path, all_calls_proven_panic_free, accepted,
        );
    }
    accepted
}

/// Trust (hardened evidence gate): per-obligation proof evidence for the
/// v1/ay bridge lane.
///
/// `bridge_v1_ay_proofs_into_native_results` substitutes honest in-process ay
/// proofs into the legacy results, but the native run result carried no
/// `ObligationEvidence` for them — so the transport row surfaced `proved` with
/// no publishable structured proof evidence, and `targo trust`'s hardened
/// evidence gate correctly failed 0/N. This appends the bridge lane's own
/// evidence, mirroring the artifact policy of the one lane that already passes
/// the gate (the native trust-mc CHC lane):
///
///   * status `Proved`, strength `smt_unsat` (the transport-representable
///     floor of the result's actual `smt_unsat_strict_checked` — the label
///     never outruns the proof);
///   * one owner/native-bound `NormalizedObligation` containing the canonical
///     SMT-LIB problem asserted to ay; one `SolverTranscript` containing the
///     exact retained LRAT bytes and explicitly referencing that input; and one
///     `ProofCheckReport` recording the strict consumer checker identity/result
///     and explicitly referencing the transcript. Every public hash covers the
///     complete role/owner/proof-binding envelope;
///   * the obligation's typed native-TrustIr binding artifact triple
///     (bundle / request / proof `trust_ir-native://` URIs), recomputed from the
///     compiler-held `NativeVerificationBundle` via
///     `NativeTrustIrBindingIndex` — the identical digests the native engines
///     attach.
///
/// HONESTY BOUNDARY — fail-closed per obligation, never fabricate:
///   * only `ay-in-process` `Proved` results qualify (the sole bridge solver
///     that retains a strict-checked proof artifact); interval /
///     kernel-certified / structural discharges are skipped — a
///     `clean-kernel-certified` obligation has NO solver transcript, so it
///     keeps failing the gate rather than shipping a forged one;
///   * a `Proved` without retained LRAT bytes is skipped;
///   * an ay bridge row without an exact private `SolverRevalidated` authority
///     is skipped, even when its producer evidence is otherwise well formed;
///   * an obligation without a full native identity
///     (`trust.trust_ir.native.{verifier_suite,request_id,proof_obligation_id}`)
///     or whose declared identity the held bundle cannot back is skipped.
///
/// Evidence replacement and all derived public fields are committed as one
/// transaction. If the candidate cannot pass verifier-api structural and
/// derived-state validation, the original canonical run is retained and the
/// bridge row receives no public evidence authority. The public engine field
/// remains the enclosing composite verifier, exactly like every accepted child
/// proof wrapped by `FullVerificationEngine`; the concrete ay producer remains
/// explicit in the evidence ID, diagnostics, checker payload, and artifact
/// URIs. Publishing the child engine directly would create an engine-provenance
/// conflict and invalidate the whole strict-evidence index.
fn append_bridge_lane_full_verification_evidence(
    full_result: &mut trust_router::VerificationRunResult,
    native_trust_ir_bundle: Option<&trust_ir_bridge::NativeVerificationBundle>,
    results: &[(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)],
    result_bindings: &[Option<ResultObligationBinding>],
    proof_authorities: &[Option<ResultProofAuthority>],
) -> Result<bool, String> {
    // A no-op must not launder a malformed source carrier as success. Validate
    // before cloning or inspecting bridge rows so every return path preserves
    // the same canonical public-run precondition transactionally.
    full_result.validate_derived_state()?;
    let mut candidate = full_result.clone();
    append_bridge_lane_full_verification_evidence_unchecked(
        &mut candidate,
        native_trust_ir_bundle,
        results,
        result_bindings,
        proof_authorities,
    );
    if candidate == *full_result {
        return Ok(false);
    }
    candidate.try_reconcile_derived_state().map_err(|error| {
        format!("bridge evidence candidate failed public carrier reconciliation: {error}")
    })?;
    *full_result = candidate;
    Ok(true)
}

fn append_bridge_lane_full_verification_evidence_unchecked(
    full_result: &mut trust_router::VerificationRunResult,
    native_trust_ir_bundle: Option<&trust_ir_bridge::NativeVerificationBundle>,
    results: &[(VerificationCondition, VerificationResult)],
    result_bindings: &[Option<ResultObligationBinding>],
    proof_authorities: &[Option<ResultProofAuthority>],
) {
    let Some(native_trust_ir_bundle) = native_trust_ir_bundle else {
        return;
    };
    // Cheap pre-check: nothing to do unless some result is an ay bridge proof.
    if !results.iter().any(|(_, result)| {
        matches!(result, VerificationResult::Proved { solver, .. } if solver.as_str() == "ay-in-process")
    }) || results.len() != result_bindings.len()
        || results.len() != proof_authorities.len()
    {
        return;
    }

    let binding_index = trust_router::full_verification::NativeTrustIrBindingIndex::from_bundle(
        native_trust_ir_bundle,
    );
    let requested_by_id = unique_requested_obligations(full_result);
    let mut appended: Vec<trust_verifier_api::ObligationEvidence> = Vec::new();
    for (index, ((vc, result), row_binding)) in results.iter().zip(result_bindings).enumerate() {
        let VerificationResult::Proved { solver, proof_certificate, .. } = result else {
            continue;
        };
        if solver.as_str() != "ay-in-process" {
            continue;
        }
        // The LRAT certificate ay's strict checker produced. No retained
        // bytes -> no exact certificate artifact -> no evidence (fail-closed).
        let Some(certificate) = proof_certificate.as_deref().filter(|bytes| !bytes.is_empty())
        else {
            continue;
        };
        let Some(row_binding) = row_binding.as_ref() else {
            continue;
        };
        if !row_binding.matches_row(index, vc) {
            continue;
        }
        if !proof_authorities[index].as_ref().is_some_and(|authority| {
            authority.is_solver_revalidated_for(index, vc, Some(row_binding))
        }) {
            continue;
        }
        let Some(obligation) =
            requested_by_id.get(row_binding.public_obligation_id.as_str()).copied()
        else {
            continue;
        };
        // The full native identity must already be declared on the obligation;
        // the appended evidence only BINDS to it, it never invents one.
        if !row_binding.matches_public_obligation(obligation) {
            continue;
        }
        let identity = native_transport_identity(obligation);
        if !native_identity_is_complete_and_canonical(&identity) {
            continue;
        }
        let Some(proof_binding_id) = identity.native_id.as_deref() else { continue };
        // Binding artifacts recomputed from the held bundle. `Err` means the
        // declared identity is not backed by the bundle -> fail closed.
        let mut artifacts = match binding_index.binding_artifacts_for_obligation(obligation) {
            Ok(Some(artifacts)) => artifacts,
            Ok(None) | Err(_) => continue,
        };
        let input_payload = trust_router::in_process_ay_backend::problem_smt2(&vc.formula);
        let input_kind = trust_verifier_api::EvidenceArtifactKind::NormalizedObligation;
        let Some((input_materialization, input_hash)) =
            trust_verifier_api::EvidenceArtifactMaterialization::new_bound(
                input_kind,
                input_payload.as_bytes(),
                proof_binding_id,
                &obligation.obligation_id,
                Vec::new(),
            )
        else {
            continue;
        };
        let input_artifact = trust_verifier_api::EvidenceArtifact {
            kind: input_kind,
            uri: format!("trust-bridge://ay-in-process/normalized-input/{}", input_hash.value),
            hash: input_hash,
            materialization: Some(input_materialization),
        };

        let transcript_kind = trust_verifier_api::EvidenceArtifactKind::SolverTranscript;
        let Some((transcript_materialization, transcript_hash)) =
            trust_verifier_api::EvidenceArtifactMaterialization::new_bound(
                transcript_kind,
                certificate,
                proof_binding_id,
                &obligation.obligation_id,
                vec![trust_verifier_api::EvidenceArtifactReference {
                    kind: input_artifact.kind,
                    hash: input_artifact.hash.clone(),
                }],
            )
        else {
            continue;
        };
        let transcript_artifact = trust_verifier_api::EvidenceArtifact {
            kind: transcript_kind,
            uri: format!("trust-bridge://ay-in-process/lrat-transcript/{}", transcript_hash.value),
            hash: transcript_hash,
            materialization: Some(transcript_materialization),
        };

        let check_payload = serde_json::to_vec(&serde_json::json!({
            "schema": "trust.bridge-ay-strict-proof-check.v1",
            "solver": "ay-in-process",
            "checker": "ay::UnsatProofArtifact::accept_for_consumer",
            "acceptance_mode": "strict",
            "strict_verdict": "verified",
            "input_artifact_sha256": input_artifact.hash.value.clone(),
            "lrat_transcript_sha256": transcript_artifact.hash.value.clone(),
            "lrat_payload_sha256": trust_types::stable_sha256_hex(certificate),
        }))
        .ok();
        let Some(check_payload) = check_payload else {
            continue;
        };
        let check_kind = trust_verifier_api::EvidenceArtifactKind::ProofCheckReport;
        let Some((check_materialization, check_hash)) =
            trust_verifier_api::EvidenceArtifactMaterialization::new_bound(
                check_kind,
                &check_payload,
                proof_binding_id,
                &obligation.obligation_id,
                vec![trust_verifier_api::EvidenceArtifactReference {
                    kind: transcript_artifact.kind,
                    hash: transcript_artifact.hash.clone(),
                }],
            )
        else {
            continue;
        };
        let check_artifact = trust_verifier_api::EvidenceArtifact {
            kind: check_kind,
            uri: format!("trust-bridge://ay-in-process/strict-proof-check/{}", check_hash.value),
            hash: check_hash,
            materialization: Some(check_materialization),
        };
        artifacts.extend([input_artifact, transcript_artifact, check_artifact]);

        appended.push(trust_verifier_api::ObligationEvidence {
            evidence_id: format!("bridge-ay:{}", obligation.obligation_id),
            obligation_id: obligation.obligation_id.clone(),
            engine: full_result.engine.clone(),
            status: trust_verifier_api::EvidenceStatus::Proved,
            decline: None,
            proof_strength: Some(trust_verifier_api::ProofStrength::smt_unsat()),
            artifacts,
            counterexample: None,
            publication: trust_verifier_api::EvidencePublicationMetadata::default(),
            diagnostics: vec![
                "primary owner ay-in-process@bridge-v1 produced accepted strict evidence for the \
                 enclosing trust-full-verifier composite"
                    .to_string(),
                "in-process ay strict-checked UNSAT proof bridged onto this obligation as an \
                 exact owner/native-bound normalized-input -> LRAT-transcript -> strict-check DAG"
                    .to_string(),
                "typed TrustIr native binding artifacts recomputed from the compiler-held \
                 NativeVerificationBundle"
                    .to_string(),
            ],
        });
    }
    if appended.is_empty() {
        return;
    }

    // A bridge proof may replace only a non-definitive native carrier. If any
    // existing Proved/Failed evidence already targets the same obligation,
    // appending a second verdict would create a conflicting/double-counted
    // public carrier. Reject that obligation before taking either evidence or
    // skips out of the candidate run; the transactional wrapper then observes
    // a true no-op when no bridge rows remain.
    let definitive_obligation_ids = full_result
        .evidence
        .iter()
        .filter(|evidence| {
            matches!(
                evidence.status,
                trust_verifier_api::EvidenceStatus::Proved
                    | trust_verifier_api::EvidenceStatus::Failed
            )
        })
        .map(|evidence| evidence.obligation_id.as_str())
        .collect::<FxHashSet<_>>();
    appended.retain(|entry| !definitive_obligation_ids.contains(entry.obligation_id.as_str()));
    if appended.is_empty() {
        return;
    }

    // A non-definitive native row may also have produced one typed skip entry.
    // Duplicate skips are malformed ambiguity: do not publish a bridge proof
    // that would require silently choosing which skip it supersedes.
    let mut skipped_counts = BTreeMap::<String, usize>::new();
    for skipped in &full_result.skipped {
        *skipped_counts.entry(skipped.obligation_id.clone()).or_default() += 1;
    }
    appended
        .retain(|entry| skipped_counts.get(&entry.obligation_id).copied().unwrap_or_default() <= 1);
    if appended.is_empty() {
        return;
    }

    // Supersede the stale NON-definitive evidence rows (Unknown / Unsupported /
    // Canceled / Timeout) the native lane recorded for obligations the bridge
    // resolved. The transactional wrapper reconciles the summary only after
    // this exact carrier replacement; leaving both rows would be contradictory
    // (and the first-entry-wins transport index would keep surfacing the stale
    // one). Definitive native rows (Proved / Failed) are never displaced, and
    // the supersession is recorded on the appended evidence — history is
    // annotated, not erased.
    let mut retained = Vec::with_capacity(full_result.evidence.len());
    for evidence in std::mem::take(&mut full_result.evidence) {
        let superseded = !matches!(
            evidence.status,
            trust_verifier_api::EvidenceStatus::Proved | trust_verifier_api::EvidenceStatus::Failed
        ) && appended
            .iter()
            .any(|entry| entry.obligation_id == evidence.obligation_id);
        if superseded {
            if let Some(entry) =
                appended.iter_mut().find(|entry| entry.obligation_id == evidence.obligation_id)
            {
                // Trust: no engine NAME here — a native engine name can carry
                // marker text that targo's row-text classifiers key on;
                // transport-row text must stay marker-free. The evidence id
                // still identifies the superseded entry unambiguously.
                entry.diagnostics.push(format!(
                    "supersedes non-definitive native evidence `{}` (status {:?}) that the \
                     bridge lane resolved",
                    evidence.evidence_id, evidence.status
                ));
            }
        } else {
            retained.push(evidence);
        }
    }
    full_result.evidence = retained;

    // Supersede the corresponding typed skip as part of the same atomic
    // carrier update. Keeping it would make verifier-api classify the new proof
    // as simultaneously skipped, and the compiler's strict index correctly
    // rejects that contradiction. Preserve the exact old reason in the new
    // evidence diagnostics so audit history is not erased.
    let mut retained_skipped = Vec::with_capacity(full_result.skipped.len());
    let mut resolved_skip_ids = BTreeSet::new();
    for skipped in std::mem::take(&mut full_result.skipped) {
        if let Some(entry) =
            appended.iter_mut().find(|entry| entry.obligation_id == skipped.obligation_id)
        {
            resolved_skip_ids.insert(skipped.obligation_id.clone());
            entry.diagnostics.push(format!(
                "supersedes native skipped row for obligation `{}` (reason {:?}) that the \
                 strict bridge proof resolved",
                skipped.obligation_id, skipped.reason
            ));
        } else {
            retained_skipped.push(skipped);
        }
    }
    full_result.skipped = retained_skipped;
    // Resource-limit skips also install a derived release-blocking diagnostic
    // on the run envelope. Once the exact skip is superseded, keep its history
    // only on the replacement evidence above; retaining the blocking marker on
    // the run would make reports disagree with the now-proved typed carrier.
    let resolved_gap_prefixes = resolved_skip_ids
        .iter()
        .map(|id| format!("release-blocking proof gap: obligation {id} "))
        .collect::<Vec<_>>();
    full_result.diagnostics.retain(|diagnostic| {
        !resolved_gap_prefixes.iter().any(|prefix| diagnostic.starts_with(prefix))
    });
    full_result.evidence.extend(appended);
}

fn monitor_contract_source_index(contract_id: &str) -> Option<usize> {
    trust_types::canonical_contract_source_index(contract_id)
}

fn unsupported_monitor_obligation_source_index(obligation_id: &str) -> Option<usize> {
    let rest = obligation_id.strip_prefix("unsupported:")?;
    let (function_component, index_text) = rest.rsplit_once(':')?;
    let index = index_text.parse::<usize>().ok()?;
    (index.to_string() == index_text
        && !function_component.is_empty()
        && function_component.len() <= 384
        && function_component.bytes().all(|byte| byte.is_ascii_alphanumeric() || byte == b'_'))
    .then_some(index)
}

fn compiler_monitor_contract_kind_label(
    kind: rustc_middle::mir::trust_contract::TrustContractKind,
) -> &'static str {
    use rustc_middle::mir::trust_contract::TrustContractKind as CompilerKind;

    match kind {
        CompilerKind::Requires => "requires",
        CompilerKind::Ensures => "ensures",
        CompilerKind::Invariant => "invariant",
        CompilerKind::LoopInvariant => "loop_invariant",
        CompilerKind::Decreases => "decreases",
        CompilerKind::Assumes => "assumes",
        CompilerKind::Asserts => "asserts",
        CompilerKind::Refinement => "refine",
        CompilerKind::Temporal => "temporal",
    }
}

/// `Contract::stable_source_id` is the only public id constructor authorized
/// to bind a compiler-private monitor record. Merely ending an arbitrary id in
/// the right integer is not enough: otherwise public metadata could redirect a
/// certified record to a different clause with the same dense index.
fn canonical_monitor_contract_id_matches_record(
    record: &ClauseMonitorRecord,
    contract_id: &str,
    function_path: &str,
) -> bool {
    contract_id
        == trust_types::canonical_contract_source_id(
            function_path,
            compiler_monitor_contract_kind_label(record.kind),
            record.source_index,
        )
}

fn exactly_one_monitor_contract<'a>(
    contracts: &'a [trust_verifier_api::TrustContract],
    contract_id: &str,
) -> Option<&'a trust_verifier_api::TrustContract> {
    let mut matches = contracts.iter().filter(|contract| contract.contract_id == contract_id);
    let contract = matches.next()?;
    matches.next().is_none().then_some(contract)
}

fn stamp_clause_monitor_metadata(
    metadata: &mut Vec<trust_verifier_api::MetadataEntry>,
    record: &ClauseMonitorRecord,
) {
    let (status, reason) = match &record.evidence {
        ClauseMonitorEvidence::Monitored(_) => (
            "monitored",
            "Clean kernel certified monitor=true iff proposition under the strict rooted certification audit (canonical foundations only; no trust markers)"
                .to_string(),
        ),
        ClauseMonitorEvidence::MonitorCertified(_) => (
            "unmonitored",
            "Clean kernel certified the Boolean evaluator, but exact MIR binding and placement were not authenticated for this body"
                .to_string(),
        ),
        ClauseMonitorEvidence::Measured(_) => (
            "measured",
            "Clean kernel certified the scalar evaluator against the exact E5 measure and the compiler authenticated its exact MIR transition placement; monitor-enabled test artifacts check strict descent there (runtime evidence, not static proof)"
                .to_string(),
        ),
        ClauseMonitorEvidence::MeasureCertified(_) => (
            "unmonitored",
            "Clean kernel certified the scalar evaluator, but exact MIR transition placement was not authenticated for this body"
                .to_string(),
        ),
        ClauseMonitorEvidence::Unmonitored { reason } => {
            ("unmonitored", bounded_monitor_reason(reason))
        }
    };
    stamp_monitor_metadata(metadata, status, &reason, &record.predicate_digest);
}

fn stamp_monitor_metadata(
    metadata: &mut Vec<trust_verifier_api::MetadataEntry>,
    status: &str,
    reason: &str,
    predicate_digest: &str,
) {
    metadata.retain(|entry| {
        !matches!(
            entry.key.as_str(),
            TRUST_MONITOR_STATUS_METADATA_KEY
                | TRUST_MONITOR_REASON_METADATA_KEY
                | TRUST_MONITOR_PREDICATE_DIGEST_METADATA_KEY
        )
    });
    metadata.push(trust_verifier_api::MetadataEntry {
        key: TRUST_MONITOR_STATUS_METADATA_KEY.to_string(),
        value: status.to_string(),
    });
    metadata.push(trust_verifier_api::MetadataEntry {
        key: TRUST_MONITOR_PREDICATE_DIGEST_METADATA_KEY.to_string(),
        value: predicate_digest.to_string(),
    });
    metadata.push(trust_verifier_api::MetadataEntry {
        key: TRUST_MONITOR_REASON_METADATA_KEY.to_string(),
        value: bounded_monitor_reason(reason),
    });
}

const TRUST_MONITOR_DIGEST_UNAVAILABLE: &str = "unavailable";

fn public_monitor_digest_from_payload(
    domain: &str,
    payload: Result<Vec<u8>, String>,
) -> Result<String, String> {
    let payload = payload?;
    Ok(format!("sha256:{}", domain_length_bound_sha256_hex(domain, &payload)))
}

/// Stamp the fail-closed public row for an unmatched certified-monitor
/// candidate. A serialization failure is evidence unavailability, never a
/// license to substitute an identity field as though it described the
/// predicate.
fn stamp_unmatched_public_monitor_metadata(
    metadata: &mut Vec<trust_verifier_api::MetadataEntry>,
    unmatched_reason: &str,
    predicate_digest: Result<String, String>,
) {
    let (digest, reason) = match predicate_digest {
        Ok(digest) => (digest, unmatched_reason.to_string()),
        Err(error) => (
            TRUST_MONITOR_DIGEST_UNAVAILABLE.to_string(),
            format!("{unmatched_reason}; monitor predicate digest unavailable: {error}"),
        ),
    };
    stamp_monitor_metadata(metadata, "unmonitored", &reason, &digest);
}

fn fallback_public_contract_monitor_digest(
    contract: &trust_verifier_api::TrustContract,
) -> Result<String, String> {
    if let Some(digest) = contract
        .metadata
        .iter()
        .find(|entry| entry.key == TRUST_CONTRACT_PREDICATE_DIGEST_METADATA_KEY)
        .map(|entry| entry.value.as_str())
    {
        return Ok(if digest.starts_with("sha256:") {
            digest.to_string()
        } else {
            format!("sha256:{digest}")
        });
    }

    public_monitor_digest_from_payload(
        "trust.monitor.public-predicate.v1",
        serde_json::to_vec(&contract.predicate)
            .map_err(|error| format!("public contract predicate serialization failed: {error}")),
    )
}

fn fallback_public_obligation_monitor_digest(
    obligation: &trust_verifier_api::TrustObligation,
) -> Result<String, String> {
    if let Some(digest) = obligation
        .metadata
        .iter()
        .find(|entry| entry.key == TRUST_CONTRACT_PREDICATE_DIGEST_METADATA_KEY)
        .map(|entry| entry.value.as_str())
    {
        return Ok(if digest.starts_with("sha256:") {
            digest.to_string()
        } else {
            format!("sha256:{digest}")
        });
    }

    public_monitor_digest_from_payload(
        "trust.monitor.public-obligation.v1",
        serde_json::to_vec(obligation)
            .map_err(|error| format!("public obligation serialization failed: {error}")),
    )
}

fn monitor_record_matches_public_contract(
    record: &ClauseMonitorRecord,
    contract: &trust_verifier_api::TrustContract,
    function_path: &str,
) -> bool {
    use rustc_middle::mir::trust_contract::TrustContractKind as CompilerKind;
    use trust_verifier_api::ContractKind as PublicKind;

    let kind_matches = matches!(
        (record.kind, contract.kind),
        (CompilerKind::Requires, PublicKind::Requires)
            | (CompilerKind::Ensures, PublicKind::Ensures)
            | (CompilerKind::Invariant, PublicKind::Invariant)
            | (CompilerKind::LoopInvariant, PublicKind::LoopInvariant)
            | (CompilerKind::Decreases, PublicKind::Asserts)
            | (CompilerKind::Assumes, PublicKind::Assumes)
            | (CompilerKind::Asserts, PublicKind::Asserts)
            | (CompilerKind::Refinement, PublicKind::Refinement)
            | (CompilerKind::Temporal, PublicKind::Temporal)
    );
    if !kind_matches
        || !canonical_monitor_contract_id_matches_record(
            record,
            &contract.contract_id,
            function_path,
        )
    {
        return false;
    }

    let kind_label = compiler_monitor_contract_kind_label(record.kind);
    let predicate_digest = exactly_one_metadata_value(
        &contract.metadata,
        TRUST_CONTRACT_PREDICATE_DIGEST_METADATA_KEY,
    );
    let source_digest =
        exactly_one_metadata_value(&contract.metadata, TRUST_SOURCE_DIGEST_METADATA_KEY);
    let typed_proposition_matches = match record.typed_proposition_digest.as_deref() {
        Some(expected) => {
            exactly_one_metadata_value(
                &contract.metadata,
                TRUST_CONTRACT_TYPED_PROPOSITION_DIGEST_METADATA_KEY,
            ) == Some(expected)
        }
        None => !matches!(
            &record.evidence,
            ClauseMonitorEvidence::MonitorCertified(_)
                | ClauseMonitorEvidence::Monitored(_)
                | ClauseMonitorEvidence::MeasureCertified(_)
                | ClauseMonitorEvidence::Measured(_)
        ),
    };
    typed_proposition_matches
        && exactly_one_metadata_value(&contract.metadata, "trust.contract.kind") == Some(kind_label)
        && exactly_one_metadata_value(&contract.metadata, "trust.contract.lowering").is_some()
        && source_digest.is_some_and(canonical_sha256_hex_segment)
        && predicate_digest.is_some_and(canonical_sha256_hex_segment)
        && record.predicate_digest.strip_prefix("sha256:").is_some_and(canonical_sha256_hex_segment)
        && contract.metadata.iter().all(|entry| {
            matches!(
                entry.key.as_str(),
                "trust.contract.kind"
                    | "trust.contract.lowering"
                    | "trust.contract.unsupported_reason"
                    | "trust.contract.predicate.schema"
                    | TRUST_SOURCE_DIGEST_METADATA_KEY
                    | TRUST_CONTRACT_PREDICATE_DIGEST_METADATA_KEY
                    | TRUST_CONTRACT_TYPED_PROPOSITION_DIGEST_METADATA_KEY
                    | TRUST_MONITOR_STATUS_METADATA_KEY
                    | TRUST_MONITOR_REASON_METADATA_KEY
                    | TRUST_MONITOR_PREDICATE_DIGEST_METADATA_KEY
            )
        })
        && ["trust.contract.unsupported_reason", "trust.contract.predicate.schema"]
            .into_iter()
            .all(|key| contract.metadata.iter().filter(|entry| entry.key == key).count() <= 1)
}

/// Bind the compiler-private record to the one marker emitted when a
/// compiler-supported contract kind has a predicate the static public lane
/// cannot represent. The marker may inherit `Monitored` only through this
/// complete identity: current typed origin, compiler producer and kind, dense
/// source index, canonical contract id/row, unsupported predicate/reason, and
/// one matching canonical predicate digest on both public rows.
fn unsupported_contract_marker_matches_monitor_record(
    record: &ClauseMonitorRecord,
    obligation: &trust_verifier_api::TrustObligation,
    contracts: &[trust_verifier_api::TrustContract],
    function: &trust_verifier_api::FunctionContext,
) -> bool {
    let function_path = function.path.as_str();
    if !matches!(
        &obligation.kind,
        trust_verifier_api::ObligationKind::Custom { namespace, name }
            if namespace == trust_verifier_api::TRUST_CONTRACT_OBLIGATION_NAMESPACE
                && name == "unsupported"
    ) {
        return false;
    }

    let Some(context) = exactly_one_obligation_context(obligation) else { return false };
    if !context.has_current_schema()
        || context.producer != trust_verifier_api::ObligationProducer::CompilerMirExtract
    {
        return false;
    }
    let trust_verifier_api::ObligationOrigin::UnsupportedContract {
        contract_index,
        compiler_contract_kind,
        reason,
    } = context.origin
    else {
        return false;
    };
    let kind_label = compiler_monitor_contract_kind_label(record.kind);
    if contract_index != record.source_index
        || compiler_contract_kind != kind_label
        || reason.trim().is_empty()
        || obligation.description != reason
        || exactly_one_metadata_value(&obligation.metadata, "trust.contract.kind")
            != Some(kind_label)
    {
        return false;
    }

    let Some(contract_id) = obligation.contract_id.as_deref() else { return false };
    if !canonical_monitor_contract_id_matches_record(record, contract_id, function_path) {
        return false;
    }
    let Some(context_function) = context.function.as_ref() else { return false };
    let canonical_contract_id = trust_types::canonical_contract_source_id(
        &context_function.path,
        kind_label,
        record.source_index,
    );
    if context_function != function
        || contract_id != canonical_contract_id
        || obligation.obligation_id
            != format!(
                "unsupported:{}:{}",
                trust_types::canonical_artifact_id_component(function_path),
                record.source_index,
            )
    {
        return false;
    }

    let Some(contract) = exactly_one_monitor_contract(contracts, contract_id) else { return false };
    if !monitor_record_matches_public_contract(record, contract, function_path)
        || obligation.source != contract.source
    {
        return false;
    }
    let trust_verifier_api::ContractPredicate::Unsupported { reason: predicate_reason } =
        &contract.predicate
    else {
        return false;
    };
    if predicate_reason != &reason
        || exactly_one_metadata_value(&contract.metadata, "trust.contract.unsupported_reason")
            != Some(reason.as_str())
    {
        return false;
    }

    let Some(contract_digest) = exactly_one_metadata_value(
        &contract.metadata,
        TRUST_CONTRACT_PREDICATE_DIGEST_METADATA_KEY,
    ) else {
        return false;
    };
    let Some(obligation_digest) = exactly_one_metadata_value(
        &obligation.metadata,
        TRUST_CONTRACT_PREDICATE_DIGEST_METADATA_KEY,
    ) else {
        return false;
    };
    let typed_proposition_matches = match record.typed_proposition_digest.as_deref() {
        Some(expected) => {
            exactly_one_metadata_value(
                &obligation.metadata,
                TRUST_CONTRACT_TYPED_PROPOSITION_DIGEST_METADATA_KEY,
            ) == Some(expected)
        }
        None => exactly_one_metadata_value(
            &obligation.metadata,
            TRUST_CONTRACT_TYPED_PROPOSITION_DIGEST_METADATA_KEY,
        )
        .is_none(),
    };
    typed_proposition_matches
        && obligation.proof_item_id.is_none()
        && obligation.required_strength == Some(trust_verifier_api::ProofStrength::deductive())
        && obligation.summary_facts.is_empty()
        && contract_digest == obligation_digest
        && canonical_sha256_hex_segment(contract_digest)
}

fn monitor_record_matches_public_obligation(
    record: &ClauseMonitorRecord,
    obligation: &trust_verifier_api::TrustObligation,
    contracts: &[trust_verifier_api::TrustContract],
    function: &trust_verifier_api::FunctionContext,
) -> bool {
    use rustc_middle::mir::trust_contract::TrustContractKind as CompilerKind;
    use trust_verifier_api::ObligationKind as PublicKind;

    if unsupported_contract_marker_matches_monitor_record(record, obligation, contracts, function) {
        return true;
    }

    let obligation_kind_label = match (&record.kind, &obligation.kind) {
        (CompilerKind::Requires, PublicKind::Precondition) => "precondition",
        (CompilerKind::Ensures, PublicKind::Postcondition) => "postcondition",
        (CompilerKind::Invariant, PublicKind::Invariant) => "invariant",
        (CompilerKind::LoopInvariant, PublicKind::LoopInvariant) => "loop_invariant",
        (CompilerKind::Decreases, PublicKind::Termination) => "termination",
        (CompilerKind::Asserts, PublicKind::Assertion) => "assertion",
        (CompilerKind::Refinement, PublicKind::Refinement) => "refinement",
        (CompilerKind::Temporal, PublicKind::TemporalSafety) => "temporal_safety",
        (CompilerKind::Temporal, PublicKind::Liveness) => "liveness",
        _ => return false,
    };
    let kind_matches = matches!(
        (&record.kind, &obligation.kind),
        (CompilerKind::Requires, PublicKind::Precondition)
            | (CompilerKind::Ensures, PublicKind::Postcondition)
            | (CompilerKind::Invariant, PublicKind::Invariant)
            | (CompilerKind::LoopInvariant, PublicKind::LoopInvariant)
            | (CompilerKind::Decreases, PublicKind::Termination)
            | (CompilerKind::Asserts, PublicKind::Assertion)
            | (CompilerKind::Refinement, PublicKind::Refinement)
            | (CompilerKind::Temporal, PublicKind::TemporalSafety | PublicKind::Liveness)
    );
    if !kind_matches {
        return false;
    }

    let Some(contract_id) = obligation.contract_id.as_deref() else { return false };
    let Some(contract) = exactly_one_monitor_contract(contracts, contract_id) else { return false };
    let expected_obligation_id = format!(
        "obligation:{}:{obligation_kind_label}:{}",
        trust_types::canonical_artifact_id_component(&function.path),
        record.source_index,
    );
    if obligation.obligation_id != expected_obligation_id
        || obligation.proof_item_id.is_some()
        || obligation.source != contract.source
        || obligation.description
            != format!("prove {} contract", compiler_monitor_contract_kind_label(record.kind))
        || obligation.required_strength != Some(trust_verifier_api::ProofStrength::deductive())
        || !obligation.summary_facts.is_empty()
    {
        return false;
    }

    let Some(context) = exactly_one_obligation_context(obligation) else { return false };
    if !context.has_current_schema()
        || context.producer != trust_verifier_api::ObligationProducer::CompilerMirExtract
        || context.function.as_ref() != Some(function)
    {
        return false;
    }
    let trust_verifier_api::ObligationOrigin::Contract {
        contract_id: origin_contract_id,
        contract_kind,
        contract_index,
        predicate_schema,
    } = context.origin
    else {
        return false;
    };
    let contract_schema =
        exactly_one_metadata_value(&contract.metadata, "trust.contract.predicate.schema");
    if origin_contract_id != contract_id
        || contract_kind != contract.kind
        || contract_index != record.source_index
        || predicate_schema.as_deref() != contract_schema
    {
        return false;
    }

    let contract_source_digest =
        exactly_one_metadata_value(&contract.metadata, TRUST_SOURCE_DIGEST_METADATA_KEY);
    let obligation_source_digest =
        exactly_one_metadata_value(&obligation.metadata, TRUST_SOURCE_DIGEST_METADATA_KEY);
    monitor_record_matches_public_contract(record, contract, &function.path)
        && public_contract_matches_monitor_obligation(contract, obligation)
        && contract_source_digest == obligation_source_digest
        && contract_source_digest.is_some_and(canonical_sha256_hex_segment)
}

fn public_contract_matches_monitor_obligation(
    contract: &trust_verifier_api::TrustContract,
    obligation: &trust_verifier_api::TrustObligation,
) -> bool {
    // Trust: shared constructor vocabulary; live certificate/reference gates stay above.
    trust_source_contract::source_monitor_metadata_matches_contract(contract, obligation)
}

fn monitor_metadata_stripped(
    metadata: &[trust_verifier_api::MetadataEntry],
) -> Vec<trust_verifier_api::MetadataEntry> {
    metadata
        .iter()
        .filter(|entry| {
            !matches!(
                entry.key.as_str(),
                TRUST_MONITOR_STATUS_METADATA_KEY
                    | TRUST_MONITOR_REASON_METADATA_KEY
                    | TRUST_MONITOR_PREDICATE_DIGEST_METADATA_KEY
            )
        })
        .cloned()
        .collect()
}

fn contract_matches_monitor_reference(
    actual: &trust_verifier_api::TrustContract,
    expected: &trust_verifier_api::TrustContract,
) -> bool {
    actual.contract_id == expected.contract_id
        && actual.kind == expected.kind
        && actual.predicate == expected.predicate
        && actual.source == expected.source
        && monitor_metadata_stripped(&actual.metadata) == expected.metadata
}

fn obligation_matches_monitor_reference(
    actual: &trust_verifier_api::TrustObligation,
    expected: &trust_verifier_api::TrustObligation,
) -> bool {
    actual.obligation_id == expected.obligation_id
        && actual.kind == expected.kind
        && actual.contract_id == expected.contract_id
        && actual.proof_item_id == expected.proof_item_id
        && actual.source == expected.source
        && actual.description == expected.description
        && actual.required_strength == expected.required_strength
        && actual.summary_facts == expected.summary_facts
        && monitor_metadata_stripped(&actual.metadata) == expected.metadata
}

fn exactly_one_monitor_reference_contract<'a>(
    reference: &'a trust_verifier_api::TrustContractBundle,
    actual: &trust_verifier_api::TrustContract,
) -> Option<&'a trust_verifier_api::TrustContract> {
    let mut matches = reference
        .contracts
        .iter()
        .filter(|expected| contract_matches_monitor_reference(actual, expected));
    let expected = matches.next()?;
    matches.next().is_none().then_some(expected)
}

fn exactly_one_monitor_reference_obligation<'a>(
    reference: &'a trust_verifier_api::TrustContractBundle,
    actual: &trust_verifier_api::TrustObligation,
) -> Option<&'a trust_verifier_api::TrustObligation> {
    let mut matches = reference
        .obligations
        .iter()
        .filter(|expected| obligation_matches_monitor_reference(actual, expected));
    let expected = matches.next()?;
    matches.next().is_none().then_some(expected)
}

fn obligation_contract_matches_monitor_reference(
    reference: &trust_verifier_api::TrustContractBundle,
    contracts: &[trust_verifier_api::TrustContract],
    obligation: &trust_verifier_api::TrustObligation,
) -> bool {
    let Some(contract_id) = obligation.contract_id.as_deref() else { return false };
    let Some(actual) = exactly_one_monitor_contract(contracts, contract_id) else { return false };
    exactly_one_monitor_reference_contract(reference, actual).is_some()
}

/// Compiler-private authority for attaching verifier evidence to one rustc
/// function. Public `FunctionContext` intentionally remains a display/routing
/// schema and is not globally unique across same-name crate instances.
enum CompilerFunctionAuthority {
    Exact {
        function: trust_verifier_api::FunctionContext,
        stable_crate_id: u64,
        bundle_id: String,
    },
    #[cfg(test)]
    CompatibilityForTest {
        function: trust_verifier_api::FunctionContext,
    },
}

impl CompilerFunctionAuthority {
    fn exact(function: trust_verifier_api::FunctionContext, stable_crate_id: u64) -> Self {
        let bundle_id = format!(
            "trust-contracts:{}:rustc-crate:{stable_crate_id:016x}",
            trust_types::canonical_artifact_id_component(&function.path),
        );
        Self::Exact { function, stable_crate_id, bundle_id }
    }

    #[cfg(test)]
    fn compatibility_for_test(function: trust_verifier_api::FunctionContext) -> Self {
        Self::CompatibilityForTest { function }
    }

    fn function(&self) -> &trust_verifier_api::FunctionContext {
        match self {
            Self::Exact { function, .. } => function,
            #[cfg(test)]
            Self::CompatibilityForTest { function } => function,
        }
    }

    fn matches_bundle(&self, bundle: &trust_verifier_api::TrustContractBundle) -> bool {
        let function = self.function();
        if function.crate_name.trim().is_empty()
            || function.path.is_empty()
            || bundle.subject
                != (trust_verifier_api::BundleSubject::Function {
                    crate_name: function.crate_name.clone(),
                    path: function.path.clone(),
                })
        {
            return false;
        }
        match self {
            Self::Exact { stable_crate_id, bundle_id, .. } => {
                let expected_stable_id = format!("{stable_crate_id:016x}");
                bundle.bundle_id == *bundle_id
                    && bundle
                        .metadata
                        .iter()
                        .filter(|entry| entry.key == TRUST_COMPILER_STABLE_CRATE_ID_METADATA_KEY)
                        .map(|entry| entry.value.as_str())
                        .eq(std::iter::once(expected_stable_id.as_str()))
            }
            #[cfg(test)]
            Self::CompatibilityForTest { .. } => true,
        }
    }
}

/// Persist invocation-scoped monitor certification into the public verifier
/// bundle. `contract_id` ends in the canonical compiler source index by schema:
/// dense function contracts followed by first-class loop clauses. The mapping
/// therefore does not depend on source text, spans, or vector position after
/// unsupported-contract filtering. Contract-derived obligations receive the
/// same metadata, preserving the status when a public contract row cannot
/// represent the compiler contract kind.
fn stamp_certified_monitor_metadata<'tcx>(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>,
    body: &Body<'tcx>,
    reference: &trust_verifier_api::TrustContractBundle,
    bundle: &mut trust_verifier_api::TrustContractBundle,
) {
    let Some(local) = body.source.def_id().as_local() else { return };
    let expected_function = trust_verifier_api::FunctionContext {
        crate_name: tcx.crate_name(body.source.def_id().krate).to_string(),
        path: trust_mir_extract::safe_def_path_str(tcx, body.source.def_id()),
    };
    let authority = CompilerFunctionAuthority::exact(
        expected_function,
        tcx.stable_crate_id(body.source.def_id().krate).as_u64(),
    );
    let records = tcx.sess.with_trust_compiler_state::<SessionCertifiedMonitors, _>(|state| {
        state.functions.get(&local).cloned().unwrap_or_default()
    });
    stamp_certified_monitor_metadata_from_records(&records, &authority, reference, bundle);
}

fn stamp_certified_monitor_metadata_from_records(
    records: &[ClauseMonitorRecord],
    authority: &CompilerFunctionAuthority,
    reference: &trust_verifier_api::TrustContractBundle,
    bundle: &mut trust_verifier_api::TrustContractBundle,
) {
    let function = (authority.matches_bundle(reference) && authority.matches_bundle(bundle))
        .then(|| authority.function());
    let mut by_index = FxHashMap::default();
    let mut ambiguous_indices = FxHashSet::default();
    for record in records {
        if by_index.insert(record.source_index, record).is_some() {
            ambiguous_indices.insert(record.source_index);
        }
    }

    // A canonical compiler source index denotes one clause. Multiple public
    // rows claiming it are ambiguous even if only one has an otherwise
    // plausible id.
    let mut public_contract_indices = FxHashSet::default();
    let mut ambiguous_public_contract_indices = FxHashSet::default();
    for contract in &bundle.contracts {
        if let Some(index) = exactly_one_monitor_reference_contract(reference, contract)
            .and_then(|_| monitor_contract_source_index(&contract.contract_id))
            && !public_contract_indices.insert(index)
        {
            ambiguous_public_contract_indices.insert(index);
        }
    }

    for contract in &mut bundle.contracts {
        if let Some(record) = function.as_ref().and_then(|function| {
            exactly_one_monitor_reference_contract(reference, contract)
                .and_then(|_| monitor_contract_source_index(&contract.contract_id))
                .filter(|index| {
                    !ambiguous_indices.contains(index)
                        && !ambiguous_public_contract_indices.contains(index)
                })
                .and_then(|index| by_index.get(&index).copied())
                .filter(|record| {
                    monitor_record_matches_public_contract(record, contract, &function.path)
                })
        }) {
            stamp_clause_monitor_metadata(&mut contract.metadata, record);
        } else {
            let predicate_digest = fallback_public_contract_monitor_digest(contract);
            stamp_unmatched_public_monitor_metadata(
                &mut contract.metadata,
                "no kernel-certified monitor evidence matched this public contract",
                predicate_digest,
            );
        }
    }
    let mut exact_obligation_match_counts = FxHashMap::default();
    for obligation in &bundle.obligations {
        let Some(function) = function.as_ref() else { break };
        let Some(_) = exactly_one_monitor_reference_obligation(reference, obligation) else {
            continue;
        };
        if !obligation_contract_matches_monitor_reference(reference, &bundle.contracts, obligation)
        {
            continue;
        }
        let Some(source_index) = obligation
            .contract_id
            .as_deref()
            .and_then(monitor_contract_source_index)
            .or_else(|| unsupported_monitor_obligation_source_index(&obligation.obligation_id))
        else {
            continue;
        };
        let Some(record) = by_index.get(&source_index).copied() else { continue };
        if !ambiguous_indices.contains(&source_index)
            && !ambiguous_public_contract_indices.contains(&source_index)
            && monitor_record_matches_public_obligation(
                record,
                obligation,
                &bundle.contracts,
                function,
            )
        {
            *exact_obligation_match_counts.entry(source_index).or_insert(0usize) += 1;
        }
    }

    for obligation in &mut bundle.obligations {
        let source_index = obligation
            .contract_id
            .as_deref()
            .and_then(monitor_contract_source_index)
            .or_else(|| {
                obligation
                    .metadata
                    .iter()
                    .any(|entry| entry.key == "trust.contract.kind")
                    .then(|| unsupported_monitor_obligation_source_index(&obligation.obligation_id))
                    .flatten()
            });
        if let Some(record) = source_index
            .filter(|index| {
                !ambiguous_indices.contains(index)
                    && !ambiguous_public_contract_indices.contains(index)
                    && exact_obligation_match_counts.get(index) == Some(&1)
            })
            .and_then(|index| by_index.get(&index).copied())
            .filter(|record| {
                exactly_one_monitor_reference_obligation(reference, obligation).is_some()
                    && obligation_contract_matches_monitor_reference(
                        reference,
                        &bundle.contracts,
                        obligation,
                    )
                    && function.as_ref().is_some_and(|function| {
                        monitor_record_matches_public_obligation(
                            record,
                            obligation,
                            &bundle.contracts,
                            function,
                        )
                    })
            })
        {
            stamp_clause_monitor_metadata(&mut obligation.metadata, record);
        } else if source_index.is_some()
            || obligation.contract_id.is_some()
            || obligation.metadata.iter().any(|entry| entry.key == "trust.contract.kind")
        {
            let predicate_digest = fallback_public_obligation_monitor_digest(obligation);
            stamp_unmatched_public_monitor_metadata(
                &mut obligation.metadata,
                "no kernel-certified monitor evidence matched this contract-derived obligation",
                predicate_digest,
            );
        }
    }
}

/// Retain the complete pre-routing question for authored ensures clauses.
/// This is data retention, not semantic admission: the shared source/body
/// dispatcher and checked certificates decide whether any clause may publish.
/// Do not duplicate the dispatcher's function-signature or body predicates here.
fn capture_source_clause_question(
    contracts: &trust_types::CompilerContractBundle,
    bundle: &trust_verifier_api::TrustContractBundle,
) -> Option<trust_verifier_api::TrustContractBundle> {
    contracts
        .contracts
        .iter()
        .any(|contract| contract.kind == trust_types::ContractKind::Ensures)
        .then(|| bundle.clone())
}

fn build_full_verification_input<'tcx>(
    tcx: TyCtxt<'tcx>,
    body: &Body<'tcx>,
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    compiler_contracts: &trust_types::CompilerContractBundle,
    all_vcs: &[VerificationCondition],
    loop_feedback: &[ProofGatedLoopInvariant],
    policy: &TrustVerifyPolicy,
    // Trust (2026-10-17, gate-rca deadline): the NATIVE LANE's cutoff
    // (`native_lane_cutoff`), not the function deadline: construction is the first half
    // of the native lane and may spend only its share of the function clock.
    deadline: Option<std::time::Instant>,
) -> (
    trust_verifier_api::TrustContractBundle,
    Result<Option<trust_ir_bridge::NativeVerificationBundle>, String>,
    ExactDefinitionEntryMarkerSet,
    // Trust (coroutine resume-state contract): the bridge's authenticated
    // sentinel-conversion evidence, minted from the live PRE-RESET module (see
    // the extraction site). `None` whenever the bundle failed to build or the
    // contract lane did not fully engage.
    Option<trust_ir_bridge::CoroutineResumeContractModuleEvidence>,
    // Trust (2026-09-29): the members the native bundle's Assert gate kept under
    // the runtime-checked std-member disposition. A DEBIT: unlike the coroutine
    // evidence above it is NOT cleared when the bundle does not survive (a
    // deadline, `Ok(None)`, an `Err`), because the rows it owes are refusals.
    Vec<RuntimeCheckedStdMember>,
    // Complete canonical source question before monitor/native routing adds
    // independent obligations. Those additions are never proof premises here.
    Option<trust_verifier_api::TrustContractBundle>,
    // Trust: independently constructed source reference, carried through the
    // actual query-binding constructor before the final fresh seal.
    Option<source_body_decline::MonitorReference>,
    // Trust (C1, 2026-11-06): the source reference side table of the exact module
    // the native bundle above was built from; `None` whenever that bundle did not
    // survive (`Err`, `Ok(None)`, a deadline decline).
    Option<trust_ir_bridge::LoweredSourceReferenceSites>,
) {
    // verifier-perf: degrade over-budget fat recursive `Ty::Adt` declared types ONCE, up
    // front, so BOTH the verifier-api bundle AND the native trust-ir bundle builders
    // below see the cheap fail-closed type shape. This is the bundle-path analogue of the
    // mid-generation work-bound `trust_vcgen` applies to the VC-generation walk: the
    // kernel's `Expr`/`ExprKind`/`Name` lower to deeply-nested `Ty::Adt` trees
    // (build_recursor_type: a 464 MB serialized type), and
    // `trust_mir_extract::function_to_verifier_api_bundle` / `trust_ir_bridge::lower`'s
    // many O(datatype-size) `Ty::Adt` walks over an un-degraded tree are what drive the
    // multi-GB, never-completing `build_ind_app` stall. The cooperative function
    // deadline is checked around bundle construction, but cannot asynchronously
    // preempt one in-process tree walk; degrading first deterministically bounds
    // those walks as well.
    //
    // SOUNDNESS: DROP-ONLY. `enforce_datatype_budget` returns the BORROWED function
    // unchanged for the in-budget common case (no clone), and for an over-budget function
    // an owned clone whose every recursive `Ty::Adt` is the fail-closed
    // `Ty::Unsupported { kind: "TyKind::Adt", detail: "recursive…" }` marker — the SAME
    // shape trust-mir-extract already produces for genuinely recursive ADTs. So the
    // bundle reverts to its sound, fail-closed verdict for that one function; it can only
    // LOSE a would-be proof, never false-prove, never a guaranteed-violation.
    let func = trust_vcgen::enforce_datatype_budget(func);
    let func = func.as_ref();
    let feedback_candidates = loop_feedback_candidates(loop_feedback);
    let crate_name = tcx.crate_name(body.source.def_id().krate).to_string();
    let stable_crate_id = tcx.stable_crate_id(body.source.def_id().krate).as_u64();
    // Pointer-sized spec binders (`forall i: usize, …`) take their exact
    // domain from THIS compilation target's pointer width — the same
    // `tcx.data_layout` authority the certified-monitor lane already uses. An
    // unnameable/unsupported width stays `None` and those binders keep failing
    // closed inside the bundle constructors. Both constructors below MUST see
    // the same width, or reference/bundle predicate digests would diverge.
    let target_pointer_width_bits =
        monitor_target_pointer_width(tcx).ok().map(|width| width.bits());
    // Build an independent, compiler-owned contract-only reference before any
    // monitor metadata is stamped. Positive monitor authority requires an
    // exact row match against this fresh constructor output; the full bundle's
    // subject, predicate carrier, typed context, IDs, and metadata therefore
    // cannot authenticate themselves circularly.
    let monitor_reference =
        trust_mir_extract::contract_bundle_to_verifier_api_with_compiler_identity(
            func,
            compiler_contracts,
            &crate_name,
            stable_crate_id,
            target_pointer_width_bits,
        );
    // Trust (2026-09-25, mutual-recursion termination): the constructor re-derives
    // this function's source-bound recursion `decreases` rows and admits the
    // authored clause only on an exact bijection with the rows generation
    // supplied. A recursion-SCC member's rows depend on the compiler's SCC facts,
    // so the regeneration must see the same facts generation saw — recomputed
    // here from the same `(tcx, body)` — or no mutual-call row could ever
    // discharge a sound measure.
    //
    // Trust (ARGS-FINGERPRINT-READ-1 round 3): and the `DefId`-decided self-call
    // blocks, so the regeneration classifies self calls as generation did.
    let mut bundle = trust_vcgen::with_termination_identity_context(
        &func.def_path,
        recursion_scc_termination_context(tcx, body),
        termination_unmeasured::direct_self_call_identity_blocks(tcx, body),
        || {
            trust_mir_extract::function_to_verifier_api_bundle_with_loop_feedback_candidates_and_compiler_identity(
                func,
                compiler_contracts,
                all_vcs,
                &feedback_candidates,
                &crate_name,
                stable_crate_id,
                target_pointer_width_bits,
            )
        },
    );
    let mut decline_monitor_reference = source_body_decline::MonitorReference::capture(
        func,
        compiler_contracts,
        &bundle,
        all_vcs,
        &CompilerFunctionAuthority::exact(
            trust_verifier_api::FunctionContext {
                crate_name: crate_name.clone(),
                path: func.def_path.clone(),
            },
            stable_crate_id,
        ),
    );
    let source_question_bundle = capture_source_clause_question(compiler_contracts, &bundle);
    let definition_entry_markers =
        ExactDefinitionEntryMarkerSet::freeze_from_compiler_reference(&monitor_reference, &bundle);
    stamp_certified_monitor_metadata(tcx, body, &monitor_reference, &mut bundle);
    if let Some(reference) = decline_monitor_reference.as_ref() {
        source_body_decline::stamp_monitors(tcx, body, reference, &mut bundle);
    }
    // Trust: bind catalog claims to complete fresh body violations only after
    // the independent monitor reference has been checked, and before native
    // source/formula/request identities commit the public claim.
    if !deadline.is_some_and(|at| std::time::Instant::now() >= at) {
        let _native_share = trust_types::verify_budget::DeadlineShareGuard::narrow(deadline);
        bind_exact_source_clause_native_formulas(
            func,
            compiler_contracts,
            all_vcs,
            &monitor_reference,
            &mut bundle,
            target_pointer_width_bits,
        );
    }
    let mut coroutine_resume_contract = None;
    let mut runtime_checked_std_members = Vec::new();
    let mut source_reference_sites = None;
    // Trust (assumption-root gate): this early-out skips
    // `build_native_trust_ir_bundle_for_verifier_api`, which is where the user-axiom
    // census is written — so a body that times out HERE gets no census record and
    // `verdict_rests_on_user_axiom` reads it as UNANSWERED. That is the fail-closed
    // direction (a timed-out verdict is unspendable as a premise, its own verdict
    // untouched) and it is not the only thing refusing such a body: with no bundle
    // there is no `full_verification`, so conjunct 4 of the shared bar already
    // refuses it before conjunct 5 is consulted. Not exercised by any fixture —
    // recorded so a future reader does not mistake a deadline decline for an axiom.
    let native_trust_ir_bundle = if deadline.is_some_and(|at| std::time::Instant::now() >= at) {
        Err(format!(
            "{NATIVE_BUNDLE_DEADLINE_DECLINE} (the native lane's share of it, `native_lane_cutoff`) before native trust-ir bundle construction"
        ))
    } else {
        // Trust (2026-10-17, gate-rca deadline): construction is synchronous vcgen and
        // bridge work whose loops poll the AMBIENT budget, and that budget's clock was
        // the function deadline, so the only bound on construction was the check after
        // it: measured on the candidate gate's `scan_frames`, construction ran ~2.5 min
        // past the 120 s function clock before this decline fired. While it runs, the
        // ambient deadline is the native cutoff (the step counter stays the function's,
        // `DeadlineShareGuard`), so a passed share trips the polls inside the fixpoints,
        // the regenerations discard what followed (`step_by_constructor::
        // reconcile_bundle`, the Assert gate) and the decline below fires at the share.
        let _native_share = trust_types::verify_budget::DeadlineShareGuard::narrow(deadline);
        // NESTED-CLOCK (2026-10-18): another body this construction verified (a bundled
        // member's optimized MIR) moved the share; the decline below reads it moved too.
        let construction_credit = trust_types::verify_budget::clock_credit_mark();
        let built = build_native_trust_ir_bundle_for_verifier_api(
            tcx,
            body,
            func,
            &mut bundle,
            policy,
            all_vcs,
            &mut coroutine_resume_contract,
            &mut runtime_checked_std_members,
            decline_monitor_reference.as_mut(),
            &mut source_reference_sites,
        );
        if trust_types::verify_budget::credited(deadline, construction_credit)
            .is_some_and(|at| std::time::Instant::now() >= at)
        {
            coroutine_resume_contract = None;
            Err(format!(
                "{NATIVE_BUNDLE_DEADLINE_DECLINE} (the native lane's share of it, `native_lane_cutoff`) during native trust-ir bundle construction"
            ))
        } else {
            built
        }
    };
    if !matches!(&native_trust_ir_bundle, Ok(Some(_))) {
        decline_monitor_reference = None;
        // Evidence about a module that did not survive to become the solved
        // bundle must never publish — the coroutine evidence, and the
        // gap-class attestation the transport builder would otherwise read
        // off the session ledger for this function.
        coroutine_resume_contract = None;
        // Trust (C1, 2026-11-06): nor may its source reference claims.
        source_reference_sites = None;
        record_session_gap_class_attestation(
            tcx.sess,
            &func.def_path,
            PanicFreedomFoldAttestation::default(),
        );
    }
    let definition_entry_markers = definition_entry_markers.seal_final_bundle(&bundle);
    (
        bundle,
        native_trust_ir_bundle,
        definition_entry_markers,
        coroutine_resume_contract,
        runtime_checked_std_members,
        source_question_bundle,
        decline_monitor_reference,
        source_reference_sites,
    )
}

/// Trust: supply semantic formulas for retained authored ensures markers.
/// This does not discharge a marker or mint any proof authority. Its formula
/// is the complete fresh body-violation disjunction, never the bare positive
/// contract predicate or the catalog's legacy `Bool(false)` placeholder.
fn bind_exact_source_clause_native_formulas(
    func: &trust_types::VerifiableFunction,
    compiler_contracts: &trust_types::CompilerContractBundle,
    all_vcs: &[VerificationCondition],
    reference: &trust_verifier_api::TrustContractBundle,
    bundle: &mut trust_verifier_api::TrustContractBundle,
    target_pointer_width_bits: Option<u32>,
) -> usize {
    let trust_verifier_api::BundleSubject::Function { crate_name, path } = &reference.subject
    else {
        return 0;
    };
    if path != &func.def_path
        || bundle.subject != reference.subject
        || bundle.bundle_id != reference.bundle_id
    {
        return 0;
    }
    let eligible = bundle
        .obligations
        .iter()
        .filter_map(|obligation| {
            let identity = source_clause_marker_identity(bundle, obligation)?;
            if identity.kind != SourceClauseKind::Ensures
                || bundle
                    .obligations
                    .iter()
                    .filter(|row| row.obligation_id == obligation.obligation_id)
                    .count()
                    != 1
                || exactly_one_monitor_reference_obligation(reference, obligation).is_none()
            {
                return None;
            }
            let contract = exactly_one_original_contract(bundle, &identity.contract_id)?;
            exactly_one_monitor_reference_contract(reference, contract)?;
            Some((obligation.obligation_id.clone(), identity.index))
        })
        .collect::<BTreeMap<_, _>>();
    if eligible.is_empty() {
        return 0;
    }
    let formulas = trust_mir_extract::verifier_api_source_clause_body_formula_metadata(
        func,
        compiler_contracts,
        all_vcs,
        crate_name,
        target_pointer_width_bits,
    );
    let mut bound = 0;
    for obligation in &mut bundle.obligations {
        let Some(index) = eligible.get(&obligation.obligation_id) else { continue };
        let Some(metadata) = formulas.get(index) else { continue };
        obligation.metadata.extend(metadata.iter().cloned());
        bound += 1;
    }
    bound
}

/// Mint source-generation authority only for the exact live native bundle
/// returned by the final compiler lowering seam.
///
/// Keeping the issuer in this small helper lets the static call-site gate count
/// one production mint while focused tests exercise one-shot and clone-erasure
/// behavior without introducing a second authority issuer.
fn mint_source_generation_authority_for_live_native_bundle(
    native_bundle: &mut Result<Option<trust_ir_bridge::NativeVerificationBundle>, String>,
) -> Result<Option<trust_ir_bridge::SourceGenerationAuthority>, String> {
    let Ok(Some(native_bundle)) = native_bundle else { return Ok(None) };
    trust_ir_bridge::SourceGenerationAuthority::mint_from_live_lowering(native_bundle)
        .map(Some)
        .map_err(|error| {
            format!("live compiler source-generation authority mint failed closed: {error}")
        })
}

